window.REVISED_CYCLES = {"c1":{"title":"Cycle 1: Understanding the Questions","html":"<div class=\"revised-cycle\"><header class=\"intro guide-heading\" id=\"top\"><h1>Cycle 1: Understanding the Questions</h1><p class=\"lead\">Current template questions, plain-language explanations, and a critique of the interpolated example.</p><p>The template and handbook used here are Version 09. Each question is followed by its meaning, relevant rubric components, interpolated response, and critique.</p></header><details class=\"contents-index\"><summary>Questions and reference</summary><nav aria-label=\"Cycle 1 questions\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/top\" aria-current=\"location\">Introduction</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/preparation\">Before you begin</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A-data\">I.1 / Data and student-group selection</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A-qualitative\">II / Qualitative evidence table</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A-mission\">III.1 / School vision and mission</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A1\">IV.1 / Selecting the equity gap</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A2\">IV.2 / Explaining the qualitative sources</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A3\">IV.3 / Connecting qualitative and quantitative findings</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A4\">IV.4 / Defining the gap and using research</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A5\">IV.5 / Connecting the gap to school commitments</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/B1\">I.1 / Identifying potential contributing factors</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/B2\">I.2 / Explaining the impact of the factors</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/B3\">I.3 / Using research to examine factors</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/B4\">I.4 / Identifying the educational need</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/B5\">II.5 / Developing a feasible problem statement</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/C1\">1 / Describing proposed strategies</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/C2\">2 / Explaining how strategies address the problem</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/C3\">3 / Connecting strategies to factors and school goals</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/C4\">4 / Applying targeted feedback</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/C5\">5 / Building participation and anticipating implications</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/D1\">1 / Explaining the choice and influence of feedback</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/D2\">2 / Learning, strengths, and areas for growth</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/D3\">3 / Planning professional learning</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/version-alignment\">Template and handbook</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/A-sources\">Evidence sources</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-record\">Source notes</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/score-summary\">Example status</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Rubric 1.1: Quantitative inquiry</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Rubric 1.2: Qualitative inquiry</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Rubric 1.3: Equity gap analysis</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Rubric 1.4: Contributing factors</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Rubric 1.5: Problem statement</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Rubric 1.6: Strategies and alignment</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Rubric 1.7: Feedback and feasibility</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Rubric 1.8: Leadership learning</a></nav></details><section id=\"preparation\" class=\"major-section\"><span id=\"foundation\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span>\n<h2>Before you begin</h2>\n<p>Read Parts A-D and their rubrics before collecting evidence. Part A establishes the equity issue; Part B develops contributing factors and a focused problem; Part C proposes strategies and uses feedback to refine them; Part D reflects on leadership learning. Arrange access to evidence, relevant perspectives, and strategy feedback early enough to inform the writing.</p>\n<p>Keep one student group and one state indicator connected across the cycle. The questions below follow the current template. Plain-language explanations and critiques are teaching guidance. The interpolated examples are newly worded teaching parallels. They are not official scores, candidate submissions, or templates to copy.</p>\n</section>\n<article id=\"A-data\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"1\"><span id=\"a-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-a\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.1 / Data and student-group selection</h2><section class=\"question\"><span id=\"A-group\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tIdentify the one specific student group you have chosen to further investigate.</p><p class=\"task-directions\">Directions: Complete the following table. While this template reflects the categories found on the California School Dashboard, it is neither an exclusive nor a mandatory list of categories, and you may add or delete rows as needed.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Choose one state indicator, compare the school and its student groups across the three most recent years, and identify one group for further investigation. Make the table understandable: the measure, years, groups, and reporting populations should be clear. The short identification answer names the group; Part A IV.1 develops the reason for choosing it.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Reading the data accurately</summary><p>A rate is a count divided by the population eligible for that measure. Keep the numerator, denominator, reporting year, and definition together; enrollment may not be the reporting denominator. Subtract two rates to describe a percentage-point difference.</p><p>Compare both the group's trend and its difference from the comparison group. Schoolwide results include the selected group; overlapping group rows cannot be added. Annual results may involve different students, so they are not automatically a matched-cohort comparison. Missing or suppressed results are not zero.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.1</h3><ul><li>Analyze three years of quantitative data for one state indicator.</li><li>Identify relevant patterns and explain the selected student group.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how additional data clarify group differences within the indicator.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Full Rubric 1.1</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Selected California indicator: Chronic Absenteeism. The attendance figures below are from the LAUSD Whole Child Integrated Data record used in this instructional scenario.</p><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Three-year chronic absence percentages\"><table class=\"source-table\"><caption>Three-year chronic absence percentages</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Student population</th><th scope=\"col\">Latest reporting year 2024-2025</th><th scope=\"col\">Previous reporting year 2023-2024</th><th scope=\"col\">Earlier reporting year 2022-2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Schoolwide</td><td>33.78%</td><td>36.39%</td><td>35.91%</td></tr><tr><td>English learners</td><td>51.09% of 167 (53% NC)</td><td>51.71% of 170 (59% NC)</td><td>47.27% of 162 (56% NC)</td></tr><tr><td>Socioeconomically disadvantaged</td><td>36.53%</td><td>38.23%</td><td>40.03%</td></tr><tr><td>Students with disabilities</td><td>40.61%</td><td>45.56%</td><td>48.08%</td></tr><tr><td>Asian</td><td>20%</td><td>14.29%</td><td>11.54%</td></tr><tr><td>African American</td><td>42.86% (23 students)</td><td>58.62% (24 students)</td><td>53.85% (25 students)</td></tr><tr><td>Filipino</td><td>20%</td><td>24.14%</td><td>25.71%</td></tr><tr><td>Hispanic</td><td>33.66%</td><td>36.79%</td><td>36.78%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"School Experience Survey: enjoyment of classroom learning\"><table class=\"source-table\"><caption>School Experience Survey: enjoyment of classroom learning</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Student population</th><th scope=\"col\">Latest reporting year 2024-2025</th><th scope=\"col\">Previous reporting year 2023-2024</th><th scope=\"col\">Earlier reporting year 2022-2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Schoolwide</td><td>46%</td><td>41%</td><td>37%</td></tr><tr><td>English learners</td><td>50%</td><td>53%</td><td>45%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"School Experience Survey: participation in school extracurricular activities\"><table class=\"source-table\"><caption>School Experience Survey: participation in school extracurricular activities</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Student population</th><th scope=\"col\">Latest reporting year 2024-2025</th><th scope=\"col\">Previous reporting year 2023-2024</th><th scope=\"col\">Earlier reporting year 2022-2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Schoolwide</td><td>49%</td><td>46%</td><td>52%</td></tr><tr><td>English learners</td><td>37%</td><td>35%</td><td>48%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"California Dashboard: summative ELPAC and English Learner Progress Indicator\"><table class=\"source-table\"><caption>California Dashboard: summative ELPAC and English Learner Progress Indicator</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Population</th><th scope=\"col\">Latest year 2024</th><th scope=\"col\">Previous year 2023</th><th scope=\"col\">Earlier year 2022</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All English learners</td><td>51% advanced one ELPI level; 44.4% stayed at their ELPI level; 4.6% moved down one ELPI level.</td><td>46.7% advanced one ELPI level; 46.2% stayed at their ELPI level; 7.1% moved down one ELPI level.</td><td>45.6% advanced one ELPI level; 41.1% stayed at their ELPI level; 12.8% moved down one ELPI level.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>I selected English learners at Riverview High for further investigation of chronic absence. In each of the three reporting years, their rate exceeds the schoolwide rate. This continuing difference makes the group a relevant focus for examining attendance and access to support. This population remains the focus throughout the analysis and the development of the proposed response.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Report the annual differences in percentage points and explain both the persistent disparity and the final-year improvement in the EL rate.</li><li>Identify the year, population, denominator, and source for each measure; reconcile apparent inconsistencies.</li><li>Keep the selected group consistent and explain the contribution of the additional measures in Part A IV.1.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A-qualitative\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"\"><span id=\"qualitative-analysis\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II / Qualitative evidence table</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">Directions: Identify each of the three qualitative sources you have selected, and record and analyze the qualitative data you have collected from each source.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Record what each qualitative source revealed and explain what it contributes to understanding the selected group and indicator. A source is the person or material; an interview is a method; a finding is what was learned; analysis explains its significance and limits. Listing interview participants completes only the first part of this task.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Turning sources into findings</summary><p>Choose sources that can illuminate the selected population's experiences and the school conditions related to the indicator. Ask about concrete events: what happened, what support was available, whether it was usable, and what happened next. For documents, compare written expectations with available evidence of follow-through.</p><p>Record each source's contribution before comparing accounts. Look for recurring themes, differences, and counterexamples. A theme is an interpretation supported by evidence, not a count of interview labels. Explain whose experience is represented and what remains unknown; one account does not establish how common a condition is.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.2</h3><ul><li>Collect and analyze at least three different, relevant qualitative sources.</li><li>Explain relationships among their findings, the quantitative data, and the equity issue.</li><li>For Level 4, deepen understanding with additional qualitative data as appropriate and provide a complete connected analysis.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Full Rubric 1.2</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Interview evidence\"><table class=\"source-table\"><caption>Interview evidence</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Source</th><th scope=\"col\">Method</th><th scope=\"col\">Findings</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ELD teacher</td><td>Interview</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Chronically absent EL student</td><td>Interview</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>PSA counselor</td><td>Interview</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>ELAC parent</td><td>Interview</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Qualitative findings and their contribution\"><table class=\"audit evidence-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Reported finding</th><th>Contribution to the inquiry</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">ELD teacher</th><td>Frequent enrollment interrupts instructional continuity and requires repeated review.</td><td>Investigate entry routines and instructional support; this is not evidence of teacher incompetence.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Student</th><td>The student reports a difficult placement experience and improved attendance after placement changed.</td><td>Examine the individual experience without treating it as a groupwide effect.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">PSA counselor</th><td>Unstable housing interferes with a student reaching school.</td><td>Examine attendance access and support; the account does not establish how common the barrier is.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">ELAC parent</th><td>Bus-route changes and less frequent service make travel to school more difficult.</td><td>Investigate transportation conditions and who is affected.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Populate each findings cell with the actual evidence already described in the narrative.</li><li>Compare the accounts to identify themes, differences, and questions that remain unresolved.</li><li>Use a focused follow-up, where needed, to examine an unresolved condition rather than collecting another source merely to increase the count.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A-mission\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"1\"><span id=\"a-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / III.1 / School vision and mission</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tInvestigate the vision, mission, and/or goals at your school and document your findings.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Investigate the school's adopted vision, mission, or goals and document the commitments relevant to your selected population. Explain why those commitments matter for that group. This question establishes the school's intentions; Part A IV.5 develops the comparison between those intentions and the equity-gap evidence.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><ul><li>Document specific school commitments that can inform the equity analysis.</li><li>Connect those commitments to the selected group and indicator in the Part A analysis.</li><li>Rubric 1.3 evaluates the integrated analysis across the relevant responses, including the thorough synthesis required at Level 4.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Riverview High's vision centers on an education shaped around individual learners so that graduates are ready to enter college, begin a career, or pursue both. Its mission identifies five responsibilities: (1) set ambitious expectations and offer demanding, standards-aligned learning connected to situations beyond school; (2) maintain a secure, personal learning setting with the relationships associated with a small school; (3) strengthen the knowledge and abilities of teachers and other staff to meet every student's needs; (4) connect students and families with community services that reduce barriers to learning; and (5) give educational partners meaningful influence and opportunities to participate in improvements to their school and surrounding community. These responsibilities apply across the full student population.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the source and date of the school's adopted vision, mission, or goals.</li><li>Select the commitments most relevant to EL students' instructional experience and attendance access.</li><li>Develop the evidence-based comparison in Part A IV.5, distinguishing intended provision from students' reported experiences.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"1\"><span id=\"a-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.1 / Selecting the equity gap</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tReflecting upon the data collected and analyzed, what supports your decision to select this specific equity gap for your identified group of students? Share your analysis for the specific quantitative data you included in Section II of the Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis template and your rationale for collecting it. How are these data relevant to understanding equity issues at the school for student groups?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why this student group and equity gap deserve investigation. Interpret the three-year quantitative pattern, explain why you collected the additional numerical data, and show what those data contribute to understanding equity at the school. A list of percentages is incomplete without the reasoning that connects them to your choice.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/version-alignment\">Template and handbook cross-reference note</a>.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.1</h3><ul><li>Explain the three-year pattern and the rationale for selecting the group.</li><li>Show how additional quantitative findings clarify the indicator and group differences.</li><li>Distinguish observed patterns from explanations the available data do not establish.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Full Rubric 1.1</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I selected Chronic Absenteeism as the California indicator for this investigation. The opening table compares three reporting years for the entire school and several demographic groups. Across those years, the schoolwide rate at Riverview High averages approximately 35%, providing a reference point for examining differences among groups. The comparable averages are about 50% for English learners, 38% for socioeconomically disadvantaged students, and 45% for students with disabilities. Asian students have the lowest average, approximately 15%. The averages for African American, Filipino, and Hispanic students are approximately 51%, 23%, and 36%, respectively. These figures describe overlapping populations rather than separate portions of the student body.</p>\n<p>Most groups show lower chronic absence rates by 2024-2025 than they did in 2022-2023. The year-to-year movements are not identical, however. English learners, Asian students, and African American students experienced an increase in the middle year, followed by some improvement in the final year. For English learners, the first-to-last-year comparison remains unfavorable even though their final-year rate is slightly lower than the previous year's. Comparing the rounded three-year averages gives a difference of roughly 15 percentage points between English learners and all students. I chose the English learner population for further inquiry because the disparity has continued and because the group represents approximately 166 students, a substantial part of this school. The annual figures also show that the schoolwide improvement has outpaced improvement for this group.</p>\n<p>To investigate what might accompany this attendance pattern, I examined the School Experience Survey and the California Dashboard's English Learner Progress Indicator. These additional measures concern classroom experience, participation in school life, and language development. I selected them to explore possible explanations for the attendance difference, recognizing that a relationship between measures would need further investigation before it could be treated as a cause.</p>\n<p>The Academic Focus item in the survey asks whether students experience their teachers' lessons as enjoyable. I considered this because a positive classroom experience may support a student's willingness to attend. Across the three years, approximately 49% of English learners responded favorably, compared with about 41% of students overall. English learners therefore reported more favorable experiences on this item than the schoolwide population. At the same time, the remaining approximately 51% prompted questions about students who may not experience that connection. The survey alone does not tell me whether those respondents are the students with frequent absences, and the nonpositive responses could include neutral views.</p>\n<p>The school-climate item addresses participation in extracurricular activities. In secondary schools, athletics and clubs can give students additional reasons to attend: athletic eligibility may depend on attendance, while clubs involve responsibilities to peers and projects. I wanted to know whether English learners had comparable opportunities for these connections. Their average participation rate was approximately 40%, compared with 49% schoolwide. That difference raises a question about access to school activities and the sense of belonging those activities can provide. It may be relevant to attendance, but participation and attendance records would need to be examined together to establish who experiences both conditions.</p>\n<p>Finally, I examined language development because missed instruction and academic progress can influence one another. The ELPI table shows that, on average, approximately 48% of English learners moved to a higher reported proficiency level. About half of the school's English learner population consists of newcomers. These students may be developing initial English skills while adjusting to a new culture, housing arrangements, and, in some cases, earlier traumatic experiences. I wondered whether the students not advancing a reported level were predominantly newcomers, although the aggregate figures cannot answer that question. Remaining at a level also does not necessarily mean that no learning occurred.</p>\n<p>The additional tables therefore serve different purposes in the inquiry. The classroom-experience item concerns how students perceive learning, the participation item concerns their involvement beyond lessons, and the language indicator concerns reported progress. I selected each because it might clarify a different dimension of school connection. Their value depends on keeping those distinctions visible as I compare them with attendance.</p>\n<p>One possible explanation is that insufficient language support contributes to academic frustration, weakened engagement, and eventually absence. Another is that social, emotional, or economic circumstances interrupt attendance and thereby reduce opportunities to develop English. Both directions deserve attention. The relationship between attendance and achievement discussed by Liu and colleagues (2021) provides a reason to investigate these connections, but does not identify the explanation at Riverview. My working concern is that newcomers may face several of these challenges at once. I would need student-level attendance, language-development, and support information before concluding how much of the chronic absence pattern belongs to that subgroup.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use annual percentage-point differences and acknowledge the final-year improvement in the EL rate.</li><li>Remove unsupported claims that identify absent students from separate survey or newcomer percentages.</li><li>Explain how each additional finding changes the interpretation of the attendance disparity and identify what remains uncertain.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"2\"><span id=\"a-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.2 / Explaining the qualitative sources</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tDescribe the three different qualitative data sources you included in Section II of the Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis template. How did these sources provide more information about this student group for the California state indicator?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>For each qualitative source, explain the perspective it provides, what you learned, and how that finding adds to your understanding of the selected group and indicator. Explain why the source was useful. A biography of each participant or a list of interviews does not show the contribution of the evidence.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.2</h3><ul><li>Describe at least three different qualitative sources and analyze their relevant findings.</li><li>Explain what each source adds to understanding the group and state indicator.</li><li>Use the findings together to deepen the inquiry, with further qualitative evidence as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Full Rubric 1.2</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>To understand English learners' attendance experiences from several positions, I interviewed an English language development teacher, a student with a history of chronic absence, a Pupil Services and Attendance counselor, and a parent who participates in the English Learner Advisory Committee. Each person could describe a different part of the student's relationship with school: instruction, placement, attendance support, or family access.</p>\n<p>The ELD teacher is early in her career and teaches the newcomer course that begins the district's English learner instructional sequence. Newly enrolled students enter her classes throughout the year, sometimes more than two in an almost alternate-week pattern. She wants each arrival to feel included, but repeatedly introducing students and revisiting objectives interrupts the continuity of lessons. She also described irregular attendance among students already enrolled. Some miss class for vaccination appointments; others take part-time jobs when a parent is ill. Several are adjusting to unstable housing or difficult experiences in their countries of origin. Her account helped me see how circumstances outside the classroom can weaken continuity inside it. It also connects with Bryk, Gomez, and colleagues' discussion of the increasing complexity of heterogeneous classrooms (p. 62). The account suggests a systemic instructional challenge, not simply a question of one teacher's effort or competence.</p>\n<p>The student I interviewed had recently been reclassified after previously being chronically absent. He explained that he missed much of the preceding year because he disliked his courses and relationships with teachers. Although he spoke English fluently, his English learner designation from public elementary school had remained in place through private middle school. On entering Riverview, he was assigned to ELD classes. He described feeling discouraged, depressed, and disconnected in that placement. The English learner coordinator subsequently helped him complete reclassification, and he entered mainstream English at the end of that year. He reported attending more consistently and enjoying both his classes and teachers afterward. His experience made timely reclassification and appropriate scheduling important questions for this inquiry. It does not establish that all English learners with frequent absences have the same placement problem.</p>\n<p>The attendance counselor described how a parent's substance use and homelessness had affected one student's ability to reach school. The student and his parents were living in a vehicle. On some mornings he awoke in a different city, far from campus, because the family had moved overnight. After learning of the circumstances, the counselor contacted the child welfare agency, and the student subsequently entered foster care. The counselor used this example to explain how traumatic and unstable living circumstances can interfere with daily attendance and a student's connection to school. I understood it as an account of a serious access barrier, while recognizing that it does not indicate how many English learners experience that barrier.</p>\n<p>The source choices were deliberate. The teacher could describe instructional conditions that an attendance report cannot show, while the student could explain the meaning of a placement experience. The counselor understood the coordination of attendance and family assistance, and the parent understood community travel conditions. Comparing these perspectives helped me avoid relying on only the school office or only a classroom account.</p>\n<p>The ELAC parent added a community transportation perspective. Two bus routes had formerly served the school area at roughly fifteen-minute intervals. Following a service change, families depended on one route with less frequent service and stops farther from some homes. Students sometimes faced long waits or could not board as expected. For families without the resources for private transportation, missing a bus could mean remaining home rather than walking three to five miles. This interview broadened my understanding of the conditions surrounding attendance. Taken together, the accounts suggest that instructional continuity, appropriate placement, housing stability, and transportation all merit examination. They offer distinct explanations to investigate rather than a single explanation that can be assumed to apply to every English learner.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Organize each source around its perspective, finding, contribution to the attendance inquiry, and limitation.</li><li>Qualify the placement conclusion as an individual account and identify the further evidence needed for a broader claim.</li><li>Explain how sources were selected and how collection arrangements supported relevant, accessible participation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"3\"><span id=\"a-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.3 / Connecting qualitative and quantitative findings</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tWhat patterns and/or trends related to the California state indicator and student group equity issues did you find in the qualitative data? How do they relate to the quantitative data patterns and/or trends you identified?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Move from individual accounts to patterns across the qualitative evidence. Identify which sources support each pattern, then explain how it relates to the numerical trend. Include evidence that complicates your explanation. The task is to connect different kinds of evidence, not repeat every interview or assume that all sources confirm the same story.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubrics 1.2 and 1.3</h3><ul><li>Rubric 1.2: analyze relationships among qualitative findings and quantitative patterns.</li><li>Rubric 1.3: integrate the findings into a clear understanding of the group's equity issue.</li><li>A stronger synthesis explains both supporting evidence and the limits of the proposed interpretation.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Full Rubric 1.2</a> / <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Three related concerns emerged when I compared the interviews. The first involved students' opportunities to develop English and progress through their courses. The newcomer teacher described frequent changes in class membership and repeated review that made instruction difficult to sustain. The wider concern was that some students continued to struggle with communication, reclassification, and other content courses after several years of ELD instruction. The ELPI figures add a related question: approximately 48% of English learners advanced a reported proficiency level on average across the three years. The remaining students cannot simply be described as learning nothing, because maintaining a reporting category may include growth within that category. Nevertheless, the combination of reported classroom interruptions and the progression figures makes instructional continuity worth examining.</p>\n<p>A second concern was connection to school. Newcomers may find it difficult to build relationships when they cannot easily communicate with teachers or classmates. The recently reclassified student's account showed a different experience: feeling confined to an inappropriate placement despite speaking English fluently. His frustration and disengagement suggest that belonging can be affected by placement as well as by language access. The approximately 40% average extracurricular participation rate for English learners provides another reason to ask how students become involved in school life. It does not establish that the interviewed student, newcomers, and nonparticipants are the same students.</p>\n<p>The third concern involved material conditions outside school. The attendance counselor described housing instability, while the ELAC parent explained reduced access to public transportation. Both accounts point to difficulties reaching campus, regardless of a student's interest in learning. Students managing these circumstances may have to prioritize immediate family needs and basic stability. The roughly 50% average chronic absence rate among English learners makes these accounts relevant to the inquiry, but it does not quantify how common each barrier is. Together, the three concerns connect language-learning opportunities, belonging, and access to attendance. They also show why an explanation based entirely on student motivation would leave important parts of the evidence unaddressed.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Name each theme and identify the specific accounts supporting or complicating it.</li><li>Correct the interpretation of ELPI categories before connecting them to instructional experience.</li><li>Explain the relationship to the numerical pattern without claiming that separate datasets identify the same students.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"4\"><span id=\"a-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.4 / Defining the gap and using research</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">4.\tDefine the equity gap you identified for a student group through your quantitative and qualitative data analyses. Discuss related research and explain how this research informs and/or supports your equity gap analysis finding for the California state indicator and student group you identified.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>State precisely what the equity gap is for the selected group and indicator. Bring together the numerical disparity and the qualitative findings that help interpret it. Then explain how relevant research informs that interpretation. Research should contribute an idea or finding you can apply and examine, not serve as a quotation added after the conclusion.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><ul><li>Define the group's equity gap through integrated quantitative and qualitative analysis.</li><li>Explain patterns and the relationship between resources, access, and outcomes.</li><li>Discuss the research required by the prompt and explain its relevance to the selected group and indicator.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The equity concern at Riverview High is the sustained difference in chronic absenteeism between English learners and the school as a whole. Over the three reporting years, the rounded averages are approximately 50% and 35%, respectively, a difference of about 15 percentage points. This pattern calls for examining whether the supports available to English learners fit the different circumstances within that population.</p>\n<p>Finn and colleagues (2023) discuss the importance of considering long-term English learners and newcomers separately when studying secondary-school attendance. In addition to common reasons for missing school, such as illness, moving, or reduced academic motivation, attendance may reflect a mismatch between students' needs and the services they receive. The interview accounts make that distinction useful here. Newcomers may need help understanding instruction while also adjusting to a new country, limited family income, or housing insecurity. Language access and connections to social services could both matter to their ability to attend regularly.</p>\n<p>Long-term English learners may face a different set of concerns. The student interview raises questions about reclassification support and course placement. When students remain in language-development courses that they experience as inappropriate, their schedules can also restrict elective choices or participation in athletics. Taking two English periods each year may reduce access to the parts of school they find most engaging. These are potential mechanisms to investigate, rather than effects established for the entire group by one interview.</p>\n<p>Understanding those differences is necessary when considering how support systems can be developed and maintained over time.</p>\n<p>The research discussion of reclassification is relevant because students designated as fluent English proficient generally enter a more mainstream educational program and may encounter fewer forms of tracking, labeling, and exclusion associated with continued English learner designation. Carlson and colleagues (2016) are cited in that discussion of academic outcomes, while the source also acknowledges that findings are not uniformly positive. This literature helps frame questions about services; it does not supply a local estimate of their effect on attendance. The two broad directions for further inquiry are therefore newcomer access to instruction and social support, and long-term English learners' access to appropriate reclassification and programming. Sustained systems would need to respond to both, with local evidence showing which students need which forms of assistance.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Define the disparity with the selected group, indicator, years, and correct numerical units.</li><li>Connect each interpretive claim to an actual quantitative or qualitative finding.</li><li>Explain the research connection and retain uncertainty where local evidence is incomplete.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\" data-current-question=\"5\"><span id=\"a-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.5 / Connecting the gap to school commitments</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">5.\tHow is the equity gap you identified for a student group at your school related to specific components of the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Compare the equity-gap findings with specific school commitments documented in Part A III. Explain what the school promises for this population, what the evidence suggests about students' experiences, and where the two align or appear to differ. Repeating the mission or stating that the gap conflicts with it leaves the comparison unfinished.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><ul><li>Connect the selected group's equity gap to specific school commitments.</li><li>Use quantitative and qualitative findings to explain the relationship.</li><li>For Level 4, provide a thorough synthesis of the disparity and the conditions that help interpret it.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Riverview's vision commits the school to an individualized education that prepares students for college, careers, or both. The English learner experiences described in this inquiry raise questions about how fully that commitment is realized. Under the district's newer programming policy, placement follows grade level rather than language proficiency. That arrangement may make it harder to match ELD instruction to individual needs. Reclassification assistance also needs examination for whether it responds to the particular requirements each student has yet to meet.</p>\n<p>Students who speak English fluently but remain classified as English learners may experience their assigned courses as limiting. The student interview illustrates how that frustration can be associated with disengagement and absence. Newcomers face a different concern: after several years of high school, limited English proficiency or uncertainty about college readiness may lead them to question the value of completing school and to consider other future options. These possibilities connect directly to the mission's emphasis on a secure, personal, small-school learning environment that addresses academic and social-emotional needs.</p>\n<p>The attendance disparity remained across the three years despite existing school systems. That persistence warrants reviewing how individualized support reaches English learners, but it does not by itself demonstrate that every service had no effect. The school needs to examine what was offered, who received it, and whether the support matched different subgroup circumstances. A more coordinated approach to placement, reclassification, and attendance assistance would help the school assess its progress toward the mission. The central comparison is between the promised individualized experience and the instructional and access difficulties reported by students, staff, and families.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Pair each relevant commitment with the particular finding that helps assess it.</li><li>Replace claims of no effect with an evidence-based account of the unresolved access or outcome concern.</li><li>Identify the service or implementation evidence needed to distinguish planned support from support received.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\" data-current-question=\"1\"><span id=\"b-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-b\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.1 / Identifying potential contributing factors</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tWhat potential contributing factors are suggested by the data you have collected and analyzed that may have created or added to the equity gap you identified for a student group at your school?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Identify plausible conditions that may have created or contributed to the single equity gap. Draw each factor from the Part A findings and explain the school practice, arrangement, or process involved. 'Potential' matters: the evidence should justify investigating the factor without pretending that the inquiry has proved its causal effect.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><ul><li>Use the equity analysis to identify relevant contributing factors, including institutional or structural factors.</li><li>Connect factors to supporting inquiry evidence and relevant research across Part B.</li><li>For Level 4, explain several factors in detail using supporting evidence from Step 1.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Full Rubric 1.4</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The Step 1 analysis identified a continuing attendance disparity for English learners at Riverview High. Their rounded three-year chronic absence rate is approximately 50%, compared with 35% for the school overall. This difference of about 15 percentage points led me to consider three possible contributing conditions, using both the numerical evidence and the interviews. I distinguish conditions suggested by the evidence from causes that would require further verification.</p>\n<p>The first possible factor is structural: newcomers may not have consistent access to demanding instruction that is appropriately supported for their language development. The ELD teacher described limited preparation for the newcomer course and difficulty maintaining a coherent pace while students enter throughout the year. Frequent arrivals require her to revisit content and welcome new learners while continuing instruction for students already present. Her relative inexperience and the demands of the enrollment pattern may affect the learning opportunities available in that classroom. The ELPI figures, with about 48% of English learners advancing a reported level on average, provide a related reason to examine instructional support. They do not measure this teacher's competence or establish that the students maintaining a level made no progress. My concern is that limited access to English can also make other courses difficult, leading students to fall behind in credits. Academic frustration may then weaken their connection to school or make employment seem more immediately worthwhile than continuing their education.</p>\n<p>The second possible factor concerns an institutional decision: the revised district English learner course-placement policy. The former long-term English learner course supplemented secondary English language arts and supported English-fluent students in meeting reclassification requirements. That course was removed, and students are now assigned to ELD courses by grade rather than by proficiency. The resulting classrooms include a wider range of language needs. Some students may interpret work below their perceived proficiency as a remedial placement and feel that the school underestimates their abilities. The recently reclassified student described precisely that reaction. He associated his ELD assignment with feeling unintelligent, reduced self-esteem, disengagement, and frequent absence. The survey's approximately 49% favorable average on enjoyable learning raises a related question about classroom experience, although the other 51% cannot be assumed to be the students who are absent. His account supports examining the placement process and differentiated instruction rather than generalizing his experience to everyone.</p>\n<p>The third possible factor is structural access to school, particularly housing instability and unreliable public transportation. The attendance counselor described newly arrived students whose families face unstable employment and difficult family relationships. In the example she shared, homelessness made reaching campus unpredictable. Such experiences can interrupt attendance and make it difficult to sustain relationships with classmates or continuity in coursework. The ELAC parent described a separate but related barrier: fewer bus routes, less frequent service, and stops farther from some homes. Students unable to arrange another ride may remain home after a missed or delayed bus.</p>\n<p>The distinction among these factors matters for planning. Professional learning could address aspects of instruction, while a placement policy requires attention to institutional decisions. Housing and transportation concerns require coordination with services beyond a classroom. Treating them as interchangeable would obscure the different responsibilities and resources involved in responding to students.</p>\n<p>These access conditions also raise questions about participation beyond the school day. English learners reported extracurricular participation averaging about 40%, compared with 49% for students overall. Students who must leave immediately to secure transportation, or who are absent altogether, may have fewer opportunities to join clubs and sports. However, the survey does not reveal the reason for each student's nonparticipation. Together, the three possible factors concern instructional access, placement and reclassification, and the practical ability to attend and participate. They suggest where the school could investigate its systems, while leaving open how strongly each condition contributes to the overall attendance disparity.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Trace each potential factor to a named finding from Part A.</li><li>Replace the unsupported training judgment with the documented question about continuity and instructional support.</li><li>Explain the institutional or structural process involved and identify the evidence needed to test the proposed explanation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\" data-current-question=\"2\"><span id=\"b-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.2 / Explaining the impact of the factors</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tHow do these specific contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, impact student learning or well-being for the student group?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how each identified factor could affect this group's learning or well-being. Describe the pathway from the condition to a student experience and its possible consequence. Keep the explanation tied to Part A evidence; naming a factor again or saying it causes the gap does not explain its impact.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><ul><li>Explain how the identified institutional or structural factors relate to the group's equity issue.</li><li>Support proposed effects with the Step 1 findings and relevant research.</li><li>For Level 4, develop detailed explanations of several factors and their contribution to disparities.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Full Rubric 1.4</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The institutional and structural conditions identified in the inquiry may affect both learning and well-being. They offer possible explanations for the approximately 15-percentage-point difference in the rounded chronic absence averages, but their individual effects have not been measured. The first concern is newcomers' access to instruction that helps them develop English while completing the rest of their education. Students who still struggle with the language after two years of ELD may find graduation courses increasingly difficult and may need to recover credits from earlier failures. Difficulty in English therefore extends beyond language-development classes into other subject areas. Repeated academic frustration can reduce confidence and make regular attendance seem less worthwhile.</p>\n<p>The second concern is the newer placement policy and the instructional preparation accompanying it. Teachers had limited time to prepare for classes containing students at very different proficiency levels. Without sufficiently differentiated tasks and language supports, instruction appropriate for some students may leave others either overwhelmed or insufficiently challenged. Students may also miss targeted assistance with the particular reclassification criteria they have not met. Feeling misplaced or overlooked can affect self-esteem and belonging. The student's account connects this experience with disengagement, although it remains an individual account rather than evidence of the policy's effect on every English learner.</p>\n<p>The third concern involves housing and transportation. Uncertainty about where to sleep or whether there will be enough food can make immediate survival more pressing than school assignments. These circumstances may bring substantial stress and interrupt routines that support learning. Unpredictable bus service creates a different daily burden: students may not know whether they will arrive on time or be able to reach campus at all. Repeated travel difficulties can increase anxiety and make staying home seem easier than attempting the journey.</p>\n<p>The combined concerns are therefore academic, social, and emotional. Language barriers can limit success in several courses; inappropriate placement can undermine confidence; and economic instability can prevent attendance regardless of motivation. These conditions may reinforce one another, weakening students' sense that they belong and can succeed at school. Understanding their effects requires attention to what students report, what support they actually receive, and how their attendance and learning develop over time. The evidence establishes an ongoing disparity and reasons to investigate these pathways, rather than a measured causal contribution from each factor.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain one evidence-supported pathway for each factor.</li><li>Remove the assertion that the identified factors produced the whole numerical disparity.</li><li>Distinguish attendance-access barriers from assumptions about students' motivation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\" data-current-question=\"3\"><span id=\"b-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.3 / Using research to examine factors</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tCite research related to your findings regarding contributing factors that may have created or added to the equity gap you have identified for the student group.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use research to examine the contributing factors identified from your findings. For each relevant study, explain what it found, how that finding relates to your factor, and why the connection is appropriate for this group and setting. Embed citations in the response as directed by the template and handbook.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><ul><li>Cite research relevant to the potential contributing factors.</li><li>Explain the connection between the research, local findings, and selected equity gap.</li><li>Support increasingly detailed understanding of the factors rather than adding citations without analysis.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Full Rubric 1.4</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The research considered in this inquiry provides context for the three possible contributing factors. For instructional access, Santibanez and Gandara (2018), drawing on Rueda and Stillman (2012), describe preparing teachers for increasingly diverse classrooms as a longstanding challenge. They emphasize the particular demands of secondary schooling: English learners face complex academic content and language expectations while having relatively little time to acquire the skills needed for graduation. This is relevant to newcomers at Riverview, who may expect to learn English quickly enough to participate fully in their new school. The teacher's account of frequent enrollment and interrupted pacing makes preparation and instructional support worth examining. The research does not establish that this particular teacher lacks competence, but it clarifies why the demands of her assignment matter. When language development and coursework become difficult together, students may become discouraged and their connection to school may weaken.</p>\n<p>A second line of research concerns placement and access to academic content. Callahan and colleagues (2010), discussing earlier work by Harklau, Linquanti, and MacSwan and Rolstad, explain how English learner placement can delay access to subject matter until students are judged sufficiently proficient. Students can then be expected to succeed in content they had fewer opportunities to study while learning English. That problem is relevant to the student interviewed here. His English learner designation continued through private middle school, and he entered high school without having completed reclassification. He described being distressed by an ELD assignment despite speaking English fluently. The extra English period felt restrictive, and he associated the placement with reduced self-confidence, disengagement, and absence. The literature helps explain why placement deserves examination, while his account identifies a local experience rather than demonstrating the district policy's overall effect.</p>\n<p>The third research connection concerns socioeconomic conditions. Santibanez, Gottfried, and Freeman (2024), referencing Attendance Works and Everyone Graduates Center (2017), discuss circumstances associated with absence among English-classified students. These include poverty, food insecurity, health concerns, transportation difficulties, limited adult supervision, and responsibilities such as caring for siblings or other relatives. Older students may be especially likely to miss school because of family obligations. Riverview serves a low-income immigrant community, and the scenario identifies approximately 30% of its overall enrollment as living in poverty. The counselor's and parent's interviews offer local examples of housing and transportation barriers consistent with that broader discussion. The alignment supports further inquiry but does not tell us how many local absences arise from each circumstance.</p>\n<p>The National Center for Homeless Education (2022) also draws attention to students whose homelessness and chronic absence overlap. Its reporting identifies unaccompanied students as approximately 9% of students experiencing homelessness in each year since 2015-2016, and migratory students as approximately 1% since at least 2014-2015. It also describes small annual increases, no greater than one percentage point, in the English learner share of students experiencing homelessness between 2014-2015 and 2017-2018. These figures concern reported populations and should not be treated as estimates for Riverview.</p>\n<p>For this reason, the school needs a receptive approach at enrollment as well as continuing follow-up. A welcoming first contact can begin a relationship through which a student later feels able to describe a difficulty affecting attendance.</p>\n<p>Newcomer enrollment at the school changes in response to social conditions beyond the school's control. The practical implication is to establish a welcoming reception and a reliable connection to assistance as soon as students enroll. Students facing unstable circumstances need to know which adults they can approach and whether school is a dependable place to seek help. Across the three research connections, I see reasons to examine instructional preparation, placement, and access supports together. I would use local evidence to determine how these broader findings apply to particular students and to avoid assuming that a published association proves the cause of the school's attendance gap.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Verify each cited study and identify its relevant finding, population, and setting.</li><li>Explain which factor the finding supports and which Part A evidence supplies the local connection.</li><li>Qualify the application where the research setting or evidence differs from this school.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\" data-current-question=\"4\"><span id=\"b-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.4 / Identifying the educational need</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">4.\tIdentify areas of educational need related to the single equity gap for a group of students (e.g., the school needs additional supports or materials, necessary specialists or counselors, after-school programs for students).</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Identify what the selected group needs in order to experience more equitable learning or well-being. Describe the unmet condition indicated by the inquiry before naming a program. Supports, staffing, materials, or opportunities may be relevant, but explain the need they would address and its relationship to the single equity gap.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.5</h3><ul><li>Draw the educational need from the equity analysis and contributing factors.</li><li>Keep the need responsive to the identified student group.</li><li>Establish a focused basis for the feasible problem statement evaluated by Rubric 1.5.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Full Rubric 1.5</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Addressing the attendance disparity requires school supports that respond to the instructional, institutional, and access conditions identified in the inquiry. I propose four connected areas of need.</p>\n<p>First, Riverview needs a committed group of ELD teachers who want to develop their practice with English learners. This professional learning community would study research-informed differentiation, scaffolding, and culturally and linguistically responsive teaching. Peer mentors, instructional coaches, and the bilingual program coordinator could guide that learning. The intended benefit is more consistent access to meaningful instruction, helping students experience academic success and see value in attending school regularly.</p>\n<p>Second, students need focused reclassification assistance. The English learner coordinator or an instructional coach could use ELPAC domain results to identify the specific skills keeping students from meeting the criteria. A Tier 2 pull-out program would provide small-group support once or twice weekly during the semester before the spring assessment. This would give long-term English learners a clearer path toward reclassification and show that adults are attending to their individual progress.</p>\n<p>Third, the school needs culturally and linguistically relevant extended-learning experiences. Visits to locations around students' new city could provide opportunities to develop English through social interaction outside formal classroom tasks. Such experiences would broaden learning, create enjoyable shared activities, and strengthen students' participation in school life.</p>\n<p>These supports would be coordinated around students rather than offered as disconnected programs. The teacher group addresses everyday learning, the intervention addresses specific language needs, and the extended opportunities and attendance team address connection and access.</p>\n<p>Finally, attendance counselors need support to organize intervention teams. Teachers, academic counselors, school-climate advocates, parent volunteers, and administrators could each take responsibility for particular students with frequent absences. Team members would contact home when a student is absent, welcome the student back, and hold conversations that build trust and reveal barriers. They would also connect students with resources suited to those circumstances. Together, these actions would make attendance support more personal and continuous. Their effectiveness would need to be monitored through both implementation records and student outcomes, with attention to whether the intended assistance actually reaches the students who need it.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>State the unmet educational or access condition in plain language.</li><li>Explain the Part A evidence and contributing factors that support that need.</li><li>Reserve detailed program design for Part C after the problem has been defined.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\" data-current-question=\"5\"><span id=\"b-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / II.5 / Developing a feasible problem statement</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">5.\tProblem Statement: Describe the equity gap that needs to be addressed at the chosen school for the California state indicator and the identified student group’s area of need (achievement and/or well-being).</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe the single equity gap the school needs to address, keeping the indicator, student group, and learning or well-being need explicit. Draw the statement from the inquiry and contributing factors. Explain why the problem is responsive to the group and feasible to address within the school's actual context.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.5</h3><ul><li>Develop a feasible problem statement from the equity analysis and contributing factors.</li><li>Make the statement clearly responsive to the selected group's learning or well-being.</li><li>For Level 4, explain responsiveness and feasibility in the school's culture and context.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Full Rubric 1.5</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>My equity analysis identified a persistent difference on the chronic absenteeism indicator at Riverview High. English learners averaged approximately 50% across the three years, compared with approximately 35% for the overall school population. The rounded difference is about 15 percentage points. Interviews added several concerns to the numerical pattern: the continuity of English language instruction, students' connection to school, and the effects of difficult socioeconomic circumstances. These findings give the attendance disparity a more specific local context, while leaving questions about the extent and contribution of each condition.</p>\n<p>The analysis suggests three areas for action. Newcomers need dependable access to instruction that supports language development and challenging academic work. Long-term English learners need appropriate placement and assistance with reclassification under the revised district policy. Students facing housing or transportation difficulties need practical and relational support that makes attendance possible. The persistence of the gap indicates an unresolved problem; it does not, by itself, establish that every existing school response has failed. School records and student experiences would be needed to determine which services have reached whom.</p>\n<p>English learners need educators who are willing to strengthen their professional knowledge and invest in their achievement. Improved instructional support should help students develop language, participate in other subjects, and experience pride in belonging to a school that takes their learning seriously. They also need adults who will organize targeted reclassification assistance early in high school. The requirements become more demanding in the upper grades, making ninth- and tenth-grade support especially relevant. Earlier progress may help students avoid feeling trapped in a program and give them a clearer reason to continue toward a diploma.</p>\n<p>Students with repeated absences also need a trusted adult who checks on their well-being, communicates with family, and helps set achievable goals. Balfanz and Byrnes' 2013 study on combating chronic absenteeism discusses quality mentoring within a schoolwide effort as a promising attendance and achievement strategy. That research supports considering mentoring here, while local evidence would be necessary to judge its results. The concern is that students' difficulties can remain unnoticed when monitoring, relationships, and intervention responsibilities are fragmented.</p>\n<p>The proposed response therefore has several linked components. An ELD teacher group would pursue professional learning suited to the range of language needs in its classrooms. A Tier 2 pull-out program would provide intensive, individualized assistance with the reclassification skills that students still need to demonstrate. Extended-learning opportunities would create social settings for using English and connecting with school. A designated group of staff mentors would maintain contact with students who are chronically absent and help them obtain appropriate support. These actions respond to the identified needs rather than treating attendance as a matter of reminders alone.</p>\n<p>The proposal fits several existing resources in Riverview's context. A bilingual coordinator could organize professional learning for interested ELD teachers. A part-time intervention coordinator could work with that coordinator to design and operate the Tier 2 program. The school also has three attendance counselors who commonly supervise five or more interns. They could organize mentoring and recruit willing school personnel to participate. This staffing makes the proposal plausible, although schedules, responsibilities, and available time would still need agreement before implementation. The work would require coordination among people whose roles already touch different parts of the problem.</p>\n<p>The available roles are an important part of the feasibility argument. The proposal does not begin by assuming an entirely new department or staff. It asks how existing coordination, counseling, and instructional responsibilities might be aligned more deliberately. That alignment would still require agreement about workload and priorities so that responsibility for English learner support is clear and sustainable.</p>\n<p>My problem statement is that English learners experience a sustained attendance disparity while instructional, placement, and access supports may not consistently match their varied needs. The school needs a coordinated, individualized response that strengthens teaching, advances reclassification, creates meaningful participation, and connects students with trusted adults. These changes align with the school's commitment to a personalized education that prepares each student for the future. The intended outcome is a reduction in the chronic absence difference, with evidence collected to determine whether the proposed supports are implemented, who benefits, and what needs revision. Closing the gap remains a goal to evaluate rather than a result that can be promised in advance.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Reduce the statement to one evidence-supported educational need and a feasible school-level scope.</li><li>Support feasibility with actual responsibilities, time, resources, authority, and constraints.</li><li>Use percentage-point units and describe attendance improvement as an intended outcome to assess.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\" data-current-question=\"1\"><span id=\"strategy-map\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-c\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 1 / Describing proposed strategies</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tFor the student equity gap you identified in Step 1, describe the potential strategies you identified for equitable school improvement and how they are to be applied.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe the strategies you propose and how each would operate at the school. Connect them to the equity gap and explain the activities, responsibilities, and application well enough for the reader to understand the proposal. This is a plan for improvement; distinguish proposed actions from actions already completed.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Connecting the proposal to the evidence</summary><p>Keep the reasoning visible without repeating the full investigation. A proposed strategy responds to a supported finding or a clearly identified condition to investigate; it is not evidence that improvement has already occurred.</p><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Connection</th><th scope=\"col\">What to explain</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Finding and potential factor</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">Identify the evidence and the school-related condition it suggests. Keep an untested explanation provisional.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Proposed action and access</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">Explain what would change for the selected student group and why that change addresses the need.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Feedback and refinement</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">Identify who examined feasibility, what they contributed, and how the proposal changed.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Remaining uncertainty</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">State what would need to be examined before claiming that the response works.</td></tr></tbody></table></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><ul><li>Propose strategies informed by the analysis, factors, and problem statement.</li><li>Explain how the strategies are applied and aligned with school commitments.</li><li>For Level 4, show a strategic and contextually responsive approach to the equity issue.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Full Rubric 1.6</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The attendance analysis shows a rounded three-year average chronic absence rate of approximately 50% for English learners and 35% for all students at Riverview High. I selected two strategies that respond to the resulting disparity of about 15 percentage points and can draw on resources already present at the school. One addresses classroom learning opportunities; the other addresses personal connections and access to assistance.</p>\n<p>The first strategy is an ELD teacher learning group focused on strengthening instruction. It responds to the possible structural factor involving newcomers' access to challenging, well-supported learning. The language-progress figures, including the approximately 48% average advancing a reported level, led me to examine instructional opportunities alongside attendance. That figure does not mean that everyone else made no progress, but it reinforces the need to understand how students are supported. When students struggle in courses and accumulate missing graduation credits, frustration may contribute to disengagement. The proposed group would help teachers examine that concern through their own practice.</p>\n<p>The bilingual coordinator would lead meetings twice each month during the fall and once each month in the spring. Before and between meetings, the coordinator would assemble resources on specially designed academic instruction in English, culturally and linguistically responsive pedagogy, and translanguaging. These materials would guide collaborative planning rather than function only as a reading list. Teachers would establish an instructional goal, select a strategy, and use it with students before the next meeting. They would then bring evidence of engagement and learning to discuss what happened.</p>\n<p>The group would also examine attendance and academic information to consider how classroom conditions respond to different learners. Its inquiry would follow a recurring plan, implementation, study, and adjustment process: decide what to try, use it, evaluate the evidence, and share the findings. I would first present the proposal to the principal for approval and then introduce it at an ELD department meeting to recruit interested teachers. The purpose is to build professional capacity and improve the quality of students' daily experience. More meaningful instruction may strengthen engagement and attendance, but those outcomes would need to be checked rather than assumed.</p>\n<p>The second strategy is a mentoring program with regular well-being check-ins for students who are chronically absent. It responds to the housing and transportation barriers described in the qualitative evidence. A dependable adult relationship could strengthen contact with families, help students feel connected to campus, and make it easier to identify a problem before it repeatedly interrupts attendance. The program would combine relationship-building with access to practical resources.</p>\n<p>An attendance counselor would lead the program and introduce it at the monthly meeting of staff whose assignments are outside the classroom. The counselor would present the attendance evidence, identify students needing individualized intervention, and explain the responsibilities and intended outcomes. Volunteers would each be matched with one student. A one-page conversation guide would help mentors ask about well-being, attendance, and barriers, while the counselor would remain available to coordinate school and community services.</p>\n<p>The two proposals are intended to complement one another. An improved classroom experience cannot by itself remove a housing or transportation barrier, and a supportive mentor cannot replace appropriate language instruction. Their connection is the student experience: one strengthens access to learning during the day, while the other helps identify circumstances that interrupt participation. Coordinating these efforts would keep the problem statement visible across the different adults involved.</p>\n<p>Mentors would meet as a group once a month throughout the school year. They would report progress or continuing difficulty, raise questions, and seek help with concerns beyond their roles. Their conversations with students could reveal housing, transportation, or other needs that are not visible in an attendance percentage. Connecting students and families with suitable assistance could reduce some of the stressors that interfere with participation. Together, the two strategies address the problem statement through stronger instruction and more individualized support. Their implementation would need to be documented so the school can distinguish the intended benefits from changes actually observed.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Correct the quantitative interpretation supporting the instructional proposal.</li><li>Explain why the selected strategies fit the supported need and which concerns they leave unresolved.</li><li>Specify the route from identifying a barrier to obtaining assistance and the responsibilities requiring agreement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\" data-current-question=\"2\"><span id=\"c-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 2 / Explaining how strategies address the problem</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tExplain how your potential strategies address the equity gap described in your problem statement.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why each proposed strategy is expected to address the problem statement. Describe the mechanism: what would change in students' access or experience, and how that change relates to the identified need. Use relevant evidence to justify the expectation without presenting a hoped-for outcome as an established result.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><ul><li>Explain how strategies respond to the identified problem and supporting findings.</li><li>Keep the mechanism of improvement relevant to the selected group and school.</li><li>Distinguish the proposal's rationale from demonstrated implementation results.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Full Rubric 1.6</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The proposed ELD teacher group addresses the problem statement by investing in the educators who work with English learners every day. Under the bilingual coordinator's guidance, teachers would study and practice ways to organize classrooms, build relationships, scaffold tasks, and differentiate instruction. These everyday choices communicate whether students' needs are recognized. When the classroom experience is responsive, students may be more likely to feel that they belong and that their education matters. When it is not, frustration can add to an existing pattern of disengagement.</p>\n<p>Strengthening teacher preparation is a concern beyond Riverview. Santibanez and Gandara (2018) examine the limited availability of teachers fully authorized to teach English learners and discuss teachers' expressed need for coaching, observing colleagues, and collaborative professional learning. That finding supports the proposed combination of shared planning, practice, and review. A teacher group could provide more sustained assistance than isolated presentations because members would examine what happens when they apply a strategy. The intended result is improved instruction, with increased engagement and attendance as outcomes to investigate. The research does not guarantee those outcomes for this school.</p>\n<p>The mentoring and well-being program addresses a different part of the problem. Riverview has coordinators, counselors, itinerant staff, and assistant principals whose work could support a targeted Tier 3 intervention under an attendance counselor's leadership. Matching these adults with individual students would use existing staff relationships and knowledge. Students facing traumatic or unstable circumstances could have someone who notices their absence, listens to their concerns, and encourages them through difficult days.</p>\n<p>Mentors could also identify family needs early enough for the school to arrange timely help. A trusting relationship may reveal a transportation, safety, or housing concern that a formal attendance notice does not uncover. The counselor would coordinate the appropriate resources, while the mentor maintains the personal connection. This combines emotional support with practical assistance rather than expecting encouragement alone to remove barriers.</p>\n<p>The approach depends on coordinated follow-through. The mentor maintains contact, while the counselor helps arrange services that fall outside a mentor's role. This division makes the relationship more useful when the student identifies a practical need.</p>\n<p>Balfanz and Byrnes' New York City study (2013) provides a relevant research connection. In the quarter of participating schools showing the strongest effects, chronically absent students supported by Success Mentors gained more than a month of attendance on average. That result is specific to those schools and should not be presented as the expected gain at Riverview. It nevertheless supports investigating a coordinated mentoring approach. My rationale is that students may be more willing and able to continue attending when adults respond to their circumstances and help them access support. Local attendance records and implementation evidence would show whether the proposed relationships are contributing to that goal.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain the proposed change in student experience for each strategy.</li><li>Verify and contextualize the research, including the restriction to the highest-impact schools.</li><li>State attendance improvement as an outcome to examine and distinguish relational encouragement from material assistance.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\" data-current-question=\"3\"><span id=\"c-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 3 / Connecting strategies to factors and school goals</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tExplain how your potential strategies for improvement address or take into account the potential contributing factors—including institutional and/or structural factors—and how they align with the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Make two connections for each strategy: explain which contributing factor it addresses and how it aligns with a specific school commitment. Show the chain from finding to factor to action to school goal. Explain any important factor the chosen strategies do not address.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><ul><li>Connect strategies to the findings and institutional or structural factors.</li><li>Explain alignment with specific elements of the school's vision, mission, or goals.</li><li>Show why the approach responds to this school's equity issue and context.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Full Rubric 1.6</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The ELD teacher learning group responds to the first proposed contributing factor: inconsistent access to instruction that meets newcomers' language and academic needs. Its immediate goal is to strengthen teaching so students can engage with demanding content and experience progress. A more positive learning experience may support reengagement and attendance. The equity analysis supplied several reasons to consider this approach, including the language-progress pattern and the survey average showing that approximately 49% of English learners regarded classroom learning as enjoyable. These measures raise questions about instruction and experience, but do not establish that students who answered differently are choosing employment or family duties instead of school.</p>\n<p>The interviews add more specific context. The newcomer teacher described the difficulty of sustaining lessons while new students enter throughout the year. The student described an ELD placement that he experienced as inappropriate and discouraging. Both accounts suggest areas for professional learning: planning for changing enrollment, differentiating tasks, and understanding students' perceptions of placement. They do not support attributing the entire attendance disparity to teachers. The proposed group would give teachers a way to examine those conditions together and test responses in their classrooms.</p>\n<p>This strategy aligns with the mission's commitment to developing staff capacity to serve all students. Collaborative study and review of classroom evidence would make that commitment concrete for English learners. The group would also support individualized learning by asking teachers to consider differences in language proficiency and educational experience. The expected attendance benefit follows from an improved daily learning experience, but the school would need evidence to determine whether that benefit occurs.</p>\n<p>The mentoring program responds to the third proposed contributing factor: socioeconomic conditions that interfere with attendance. Its immediate goal is a consistent relationship through which students feel recognized and can obtain help. The counselor's housing account and the parent's transportation account identify barriers that cannot be resolved simply by telling students to attend more often. A mentor could learn about the specific difficulty, communicate with the family, and coordinate assistance through the attendance counselor.</p>\n<p>The extracurricular survey provides another question about connection to school. English learners' participation averaged about 40%, suggesting that many were not engaged in those activities. Students uncertain about where they will sleep or how they will get home may have limited ability to remain for clubs or sports. The survey alone does not identify those circumstances, but the interviews explain why access deserves attention. Mentoring could help students explore available activities while also addressing the conditions that make participation difficult.</p>\n<p>The mission and vision therefore provide more than introductory language. They offer a basis for asking whether each proposed action delivers the kind of support the school says students should receive.</p>\n<p>The program connects with the vision of an individualized education leading toward college and career readiness. Each mentor would attend to one student's academic and personal circumstances rather than provide the same response to every absence. This is a practical expression of the school's stated commitment. Across both strategies, the connection to school goals is therefore specific: develop educators' capacity to improve learning, and provide personal support that helps students participate. The school would assess those commitments through the services delivered and the experiences and outcomes of the students involved.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>For each strategy, identify the finding, factor, proposed action, and specific school commitment.</li><li>Remove unsupported assumptions about students' motives.</li><li>Account for the placement concern and explain how mentors would respond to needs beyond their role or resources.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\" data-current-question=\"4\"><span id=\"c-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 4 / Applying targeted feedback</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">4.\tDescribe the targeted feedback you received on each proposed strategy from your administrator, supervisor, or other key educational leader(s), and explain how you would adjust your proposed strategies to address the needs of the student group and inform equitable school improvement based on that feedback.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Show what feedback you received on each proposed strategy and what you changed, or would change, because of it. Explain the reason for each decision, including a recommendation you did not adopt. Keep the sequence visible so the reader can follow the proposal from feedback through revision.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.7</h3><ul><li>Apply targeted feedback to strengthen the proposed strategies.</li><li>Explain appropriate adjustments and their implications for the group and school.</li><li>For Level 4, seek further feedback on revised strategies and develop a detailed communication plan across diverse educational partners.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Full Rubric 1.7</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>In the first feedback round, I met with Riverview's principal. I sought his perspective because he had worked with the attendance counselors for two years on reducing chronic absence. He responded favorably to both proposals and suggested ways to make each more useful within the school.</p>\n<p>For the ELD teacher group, he recommended using ELPAC domain results to identify the language skills most in need of support. He also wanted the work to influence instruction beyond ELD classrooms. He suggested that participants collect evidence of the strategies they use and the results they observe, then present that work to the entire faculty at the end of the year. Such a presentation could encourage teachers in other content areas to form a similar group or adopt useful practices. His feedback shifted the proposal toward both targeted planning and wider sharing of classroom evidence.</p>\n<p>For mentoring, the principal recommended adding questions about sports and clubs to the check-in guide. Discussing students' interests could connect them with additional trusted adults and create more reasons to participate in campus life. The intention was to expand belonging through relationships and activities rather than limit every conversation to absence.</p>\n<p>I made several changes after this meeting. For the instructional group, I planned for the bilingual coordinator to have the access needed to create a group within the school's learning management system. That shared space would hold resources, lesson plans, and materials developed or used by participants. Other faculty members could be invited to explore the collection, and faculty meetings could draw attention to it. This would make the group's work accessible across departments and support broader use of language-development strategies. The principal's recommendation about domain-level evidence would also inform the group's instructional priorities.</p>\n<p>For mentoring, I planned to prepare a directory of clubs with meeting times and locations, together with a sports list showing seasons and coach contact information. Mentors could use these resources while discussing a student's interests. Having the information available would make it easier to turn a conversation about belonging into a specific connection on campus.</p>\n<p>In the second feedback round, I shared the revised proposals with an attendance counselor. The counselor noted that students facing economic hardship often think about finding work. She recommended that the ELD group regularly connect learning with workplace skills so students can recognize its practical value. In response, I planned to involve the work-based learning coordinator. That coordinator could arrange guest speakers or field trips connected to skills and topics teachers were addressing. This addition would help relate classroom learning to future opportunities without assuming that all students have the same plans.</p>\n<p>The second round built on the first rather than repeat the original proposal unchanged. I presented the adjustments already made so the counselor could respond to a more developed plan. This sequence helped me see feedback as an ongoing process: one colleague identified instructional sharing and belonging, while another added practical concerns about relevance and staffing.</p>\n<p>The counselor also raised a recruitment concern for mentoring. Staff outside classroom assignments may be reluctant to give up work time, particularly when a new program's demands are uncertain. She suggested inviting classroom teachers as well, with training and resources to mentor one of their own students. I therefore added a faculty-wide presentation in which the counselor and I would share the English learner attendance evidence, explain the mentoring responsibilities, and invite teachers to volunteer. This broadened the potential mentor pool and made the support structure clearer. Across the two rounds, the feedback changed instructional priorities, resource sharing, activity connections, work-related learning opportunities, and recruitment. These were concrete revisions to the proposals, while staffing and implementation arrangements would still need to be confirmed.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Trace each substantive recommendation to a decision, proposal change, and reason.</li><li>Explain how ELPAC domains and implementation evidence would guide the instructional work.</li><li>Distinguish completed proposal revisions from staffing, resources, and activities still awaiting agreement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\" data-current-question=\"5\"><span id=\"c-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 5 / Building participation and anticipating implications</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">5.\tDescribe steps you would take to create school-level and community educational partner buy-in to the final adjusted set of proposed strategies. What potential implications for proposed strategies do you anticipate encountering as you address the equity gap at the school?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how different educational partners would become involved in the adjusted proposal and what practical consequences or constraints you anticipate. Specify opportunities for people to influence the plan, not only receive an announcement. Connect likely implementation difficulties to the steps you would take to address them.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.7</h3><ul><li>Provide relevant steps for buy-in and communication.</li><li>Anticipate realistic implications of implementation at this school.</li><li>For Level 4, develop a detailed, workable communication plan involving diverse educational partners, alongside further feedback on revisions.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Full Rubric 1.7</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Successful implementation would require participation from teachers, staff with support assignments, families, and students. I would use the attendance evidence to explain the concern and describe how each proposed strategy responds to it. Communication would need to make both the purpose and the responsibilities understandable so that participation rests on a shared view of the work.</p>\n<p>To recruit ELD teachers, the bilingual coordinator and I would attend an English department meeting. We would present the chronic absence disparity, explain the possible instructional contributing factor, and describe the teacher group's goals and meeting expectations. The invitation would emphasize equitable opportunities for English learners and ask interested teachers to commit to collaborative learning. For mentoring, the attendance counselor and I would present the same attendance concern at a faculty meeting, explain the check-in responsibilities and available support, and invite volunteers from across the school.</p>\n<p>I would also seek the participation of the bilingual coordinator and attendance counselors as program leaders. Offering to share leadership would help distribute planning and follow-up responsibilities. We would agree together on how to organize the work and connect it with the school's commitments to individualized education and staff development. These activities fit aspects of their existing roles, but competing responsibilities and limited time may have prevented similar work from developing. Shared planning could make the proposals more feasible, although the actual time available would need to be established rather than presumed.</p>\n<p>For families, the bilingual coordinator, attendance counselor, and I would arrange an after-school meeting for parents of students in ELD. Invitations would include mailed letters, flyers sent home, and a principal's telephone and email message. A light dinner would be offered. At the meeting, we would explain the attendance figures, discuss the value of consistent participation, and introduce the instructional group and mentoring program. We would ask families to support attendance while also listening to the barriers that make it difficult. That exchange would help distinguish a need for information from a need for practical assistance.</p>\n<p>For students, I would introduce myself during ELD classes and explain that I would return to learn about their classroom experiences. Regular visits would communicate continuing interest in their education. I would make a point of recognizing students with previous attendance difficulties when they are present and encouraging their return. For mentees, I would seek funding for tangible recognition when they reach and sustain agreed attendance goals. Possible incentives include a breakfast treat, a pizza lunch, movie tickets, or school merchandise. Such recognition would accompany personal support rather than replace attention to barriers beyond students' control.</p>\n<p>Several implementation difficulties are foreseeable. Initial enthusiasm may fade without regular follow-up, clear responsibilities, and recognition of participants' efforts. Teachers and mentors may carry out the work unevenly, creating different experiences for students. Staff who already feel overextended may withdraw. The leaders would therefore need to monitor participation and workload, provide support, and discuss inconsistencies before they become established patterns. A collaborative approach should make those concerns visible rather than assume that volunteering guarantees sustained implementation.</p>\n<p>The invitation to participate would need to remain connected with those shared purposes throughout the year. Teachers and support staff should understand how their particular contribution fits the broader response, and families and students should be able to describe their experience of it. That continuing communication can help identify problems early and keep the work from depending only on initial enthusiasm.</p>\n<p>The possible benefits include more consistent attendance, stronger family communication, and better access to school and community resources. Regular examination of evidence could also strengthen decision-making across the school. Recognition events and shared celebrations may help English learners feel that their participation is valued. Coordinating school, home, and community efforts could create a more dependable support network. These benefits remain intended outcomes, and the school would need to review both implementation and student experiences to determine whether they occur. Clear communication, shared responsibility, and attention to the practical demands of participation are central to building lasting support for the two strategies.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Specify when each group can question or change the proposal and how its feedback returns to decisions.</li><li>Provide accessible participation arrangements suited to language and scheduling needs.</li><li>Explain how workload, confidentiality, recruitment, and unequal access would change the implementation plan.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\" data-current-question=\"1\"><span id=\"d-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-d\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / 1 / Explaining the choice and influence of feedback</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tProvide your rationale for the administrator, supervisor, or educational leader feedback you chose to gather in Step 3 and how the feedback impacted your approach to building positive educational partner buy-in.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why you chose these feedback partners and how their feedback changed your approach to gaining participation. Connect the partner's relevant expertise to a specific recommendation, decision, and lesson for leadership. Praise for a leader's qualities does not by itself explain the effect of the feedback.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><ul><li>Explain the rationale for selecting feedback partners and its effect on the approach to buy-in.</li><li>Refer to specific evidence from the cycle.</li><li>For Level 4, show how school context affects the approach to equity leadership.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Full Rubric 1.8</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I chose Riverview's principal as a feedback partner because of his experience with instructional improvement and attendance interventions. He had been at the school for two years, and I had observed him use his broader educational experience when guiding structural changes. I trusted his judgment and wanted to understand how my proposals fit both the school's needs and the expectations of the district and state. His approach also provided a model of leadership that keeps student needs visible while involving colleagues in decisions.</p>\n<p>Our conversations broadened my understanding of how to build support for change. He endorsed the ELD teacher group in part because it allows teachers to exercise professional judgment while developing their capacity. That helped me see the importance of inviting teachers into a shared inquiry. People may be more willing to participate when they can influence the work and understand its purpose. His recommendation to use ELPAC domain evidence made that purpose more concrete: the group could identify a learning need, select an instructional response, and examine a measurable result. Evidence would help explain why the work is worthwhile rather than leave it appearing to be an additional task based on one person's preference.</p>\n<p>His suggestion about clubs and sports also changed my thinking. The extracurricular survey had raised a question about connection to school, and he showed how mentoring conversations could explore that question with individual students. Asking about interests could connect a student with additional adults and activities while helping the mentor understand what participation means to that student. This reminded me that numerical patterns become more useful when they guide conversations, and that keeping students' experiences central can help educational partners see the value of a proposal.</p>\n<p>I selected the attendance counselor because she works directly with students who are chronically absent and with families experiencing economic hardship. I wanted her judgment about feasibility, not only agreement with the general ideas. Across her career she had implemented both collaboratively developed interventions and district-directed programs. That experience gave her insight into the conditions that encourage or limit staff participation.</p>\n<p>Her advice to connect ELD lessons with workplace skills led me to recognize the work-based learning coordinator as an additional resource. Teachers could receive help arranging speakers or experiences that make the practical value of learning more visible. This connection could strengthen participation in the teacher group because members would have another colleague supporting their efforts. It also widened my view of the resources already available at Riverview.</p>\n<p>Both colleagues also helped me connect participation with support. Teachers asked to change practice need useful resources and an opportunity to exercise judgment. Staff asked to mentor need a clear role and someone to consult. Recognizing those conditions changed how I would invite people into the work.</p>\n<p>For mentoring, the counselor anticipated that staff outside classroom assignments might not have time or willingness to participate. Her suggestion to include classroom teachers changed my recruitment plan. The counselor and I would present the attendance evidence and mentoring structure to the entire faculty, explain the support volunteers would receive, and invite broader participation. I learned that building support can involve changing who is invited, not merely improving the explanation given to the original group. Taken together, the two feedback partners helped me connect evidence, professional autonomy, practical resources, and shared responsibility. Their contributions made the proposals more specific and helped me think about participation as something developed through collaboration rather than secured through an announcement.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use a specific recommendation and decision to explain the contribution of each partner.</li><li>Identify the assumption or leadership practice that changed.</li><li>Distinguish intended participation from actual agreement and reconcile the reflection with Part C.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\" data-current-question=\"2\"><span id=\"d-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / 2 / Learning, strengths, and areas for growth</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tReflect on what you have learned, including strengths and areas for growth as an equity-driven leader. Then discuss and refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3 as to how you addressed equity needs and equity gaps for the identified group of students at your school.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what you learned about equity leadership, identify strengths and areas for growth, and show how your work addressed this group's equity needs. Support the reflection with specific evidence from Steps 1, 2, or 3. Describe changes in your understanding and decisions, not only the activities you completed or traits you believe you possess.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><ul><li>Reflect on growth in addressing the selected group's equity needs and gaps.</li><li>Use specific cycle evidence to explain strengths and areas for growth.</li><li>For Level 4, demonstrate how social, economic, or cultural context affects the leadership approach.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Full Rubric 1.8</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>This cycle changed the way I approach improvement work. At the beginning, I tended to move quickly from a problem to an interpretation or a proposed solution. Collecting evidence, reading about improvement, and reflecting at each step helped me slow that process. I began to ask what I still needed to understand about the school's social and cultural setting before deciding what should change. Listening to other people made the problem more complex, but it also made my thinking more responsive to the students and adults involved. I now see that an equity focus requires attention to how a system operates, whose experiences are represented, and whose needs may remain less visible.</p>\n<p>During Step 1, I learned to use several sources to develop an understanding of a student population. The attendance figures established a pattern that needed explanation. They did not supply the experiences behind the pattern. Looking carefully at the numbers helped me decide what to ask in interviews and whose perspectives could contribute. Rather than gather qualitative information without a purpose, I tried to use it to investigate questions the numerical evidence left unresolved. This required planning questions that were relevant to each person's role and sensitive to the circumstances they described. The teacher, student, counselor, and parent helped me see different aspects of the same attendance concern.</p>\n<p>This process also changed how I think about the responsibilities of a principal. Examining equity differences should be a recurring part of leadership, not an activity completed only for one project. A leader needs to understand students' progress and the school systems that support or interrupt it. Conversations with teachers, students, and families can help interpret numerical patterns, while those patterns can suggest useful questions for future conversations. In a principal role, I would therefore pay close attention to the concerns and proposals people bring forward. They may reveal conditions that are not apparent in a report. I would use that information to build collaborative responses and to revisit whether those responses are working.</p>\n<p>Step 2 helped me think more carefully about institutional and structural conditions. I needed to distinguish long-established expectations, beliefs, and practices from the arrangements and resources through which work is organized. Riverview has relatively little staff turnover, and some ways of working have become familiar over many years. I began to consider whether those routines could make certain English learner needs less visible. That possibility should be examined with evidence rather than treated as a conclusion about colleagues' intentions. It also means that improvement may require people to reconsider familiar assumptions together. Understanding the school's structures helped me identify resources that could support a feasible response, while understanding its institutional culture helped me anticipate the work involved in gaining participation.</p>\n<p>The course reading that most directly challenged my habits was Bryk and colleagues' Learning to Improve (2015). Their discussion of moving prematurely to solutions helped me recognize a tendency in my own practice. Experience can make a proposed answer come to mind quickly, but familiarity with a problem does not establish that I understand its current form. I need a disciplined pause between noticing a concern and recommending an intervention. The equity analysis gave me practice in that pause: examine the pattern, ask what is uncertain, seek relevant perspectives, and reconsider the explanation. I want evidence-driven inquiry to become a routine part of how I work rather than an additional step I use only when time permits.</p>\n<p>In Step 3, I used a fishbone diagram to organize possible contributing factors. Seeing the factors together helped me consider the wider system rather than focus on a single explanation. The diagram did not prove that each proposed factor caused the gap; it helped me identify relationships that deserved attention. From that analysis, I selected two strategies suited to the school's context: collaborative learning for ELD teachers and individualized mentoring for students with frequent absences. I considered the available personnel, the practical demands of implementation, and research relevant to each approach. This made the proposals more grounded than an immediate response based only on the attendance percentage.</p>\n<p>Feedback from the principal and attendance counselor showed me that an equity-oriented leader must be willing to revise a proposal. Their contributions changed the instructional focus, the resources available to teachers, the mentoring conversation guide, and the recruitment plan. Sharing an idea transparently and allowing others to influence it can strengthen both the idea and participation in carrying it out. I intend to continue practicing these skills through improvement cycles in my current role. My aim is to keep students' needs central, use evidence carefully, remain open while collecting accounts, and use decision processes that give educational partners a meaningful voice.</p>\n<p>I also learned more about my own strengths and limitations. One important realization was how much I rely on prior knowledge when I encounter a problem. Watching the principal respond to everyday school concerns helped me notice a different approach. I paid attention to his choice of language, his effort to remain neutral, and the way he made decision-making visible to others. Because Riverview is a pilot school, democratic participation is especially important to its leadership. I began considering how I could practice those habits now, rather than wait until I hold an administrative position. Observing a colleague's response gave me a concrete way to examine my own.</p>\n<p>I see analysis, critical thinking, and collaboration as strengths I can build on. The proposals reflect my belief that school improvement depends on people working across classrooms and offices. My experience allows me to contribute ideas and help colleagues develop capacity, while I also need to remain willing to learn from perspectives different from mine. The principal and counselor demonstrated how quickly a proposal can improve when another person notices a resource, a constraint, or a student need I had overlooked. Those exchanges reinforced my commitment to shared work. At the same time, describing myself as a strong analyst is not enough; I need to check my interpretations, especially when separate percentages or individual accounts tempt me to make broader claims.</p>\n<p>After learning this inquiry process, I would like to examine other state indicators for English learners, including graduation, college and career readiness, and SBAC proficiency at Riverview. If those analyses reveal further differences, I would bring the findings to the Governance Council so an appropriate committee could investigate them. I would participate in the committee's work and invite additional educational partners whose perspectives are relevant. The purpose would be shared responsibility for understanding the concern and developing a response, not simply presenting a finished solution for approval. This could help keep English learner needs visible across the school's improvement work.</p>\n<p>Understanding context also requires recognizing the difference between a familiar routine and a well-supported explanation of its effects. Longstanding practices may contain useful knowledge as well as limitations. I need to ask colleagues how those practices developed, what purposes they serve, and what evidence they use to judge them. That kind of inquiry can make a proposed change more informed and can show respect for the people who have sustained the school's work.</p>\n<p>The governance and committee meetings give me a setting to practice this discipline. I can listen to how members frame concerns, notice which perspectives enter the discussion, and consider how decisions are connected with student evidence. These experiences can help me develop the patience and contextual understanding that my tendency to move quickly toward solutions sometimes overlooks. They also allow me to contribute as a collaborator while learning from the leadership already present in the school.</p>\n<p>My growth areas follow directly from these experiences. I need to continue interrupting the habit of deciding too soon, and I need a deeper understanding of existing school systems before recommending replacements or additions. Colleagues may already be doing work that I have not yet seen. Recognizing that work is necessary both for an accurate analysis and for respectful collaboration. I have begun attending Governance Council and School Culture Committee meetings to learn how decisions are made and who is involved. Those meetings give me opportunities to observe, contribute, and develop a more informed leadership approach. The cycle has shown me that understanding school culture and context is not background preparation; it is part of the work of identifying an equity concern and deciding how to address it.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Pair each claimed strength or growth area with an event, decision, or finding from the cycle.</li><li>Explain one initial interpretation that changed after considering another perspective.</li><li>Show how the school's context shaped an action and define the professional practice that needs improvement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\" data-current-question=\"3\"><span id=\"d-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / 3 / Planning professional learning</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tBased on the analysis of your experiences in Cycle 1, identify specific professional learning goals for yourself and describe future steps for your professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Turn the learning from this cycle into specific professional goals and future steps. Identify the skill you need to develop, what you will do to develop it, and how you will judge your progress. A new committee role or another data project is a learning opportunity; explain the leadership growth it is intended to produce.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><ul><li>Identify future professional growth grounded in the cycle experience.</li><li>Explain clear strengths and learning needs using relevant evidence.</li><li>Connect future practice to equity leadership and the school's context.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Full Rubric 1.8</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>One professional learning goal is to apply the attendance equity-analysis process to other student populations at Riverview, including students experiencing homelessness, youth in foster care, Black students, and students with disabilities. I want to understand whether the proposed instructional and mentoring supports fit their circumstances or whether different contributing conditions call for other responses. Comparing these inquiries could deepen my understanding of the school's systems while keeping the mission's commitment to individualized education in view. I would be looking for both shared patterns and meaningful differences, rather than assume that one strategy serves every group in the same way.</p>\n<p>A second goal is to seek a position on the Governance Council. Participating as a voting member would give me closer experience with the institutional issues the school faces and the processes used to address them. I would learn through discussions with colleagues, the consideration of competing perspectives, and the work of reaching agreement. This would help me develop a more democratic leadership approach and a stronger understanding of how support for a decision is built. It would also give me repeated opportunities to listen before proposing an answer, a habit I identified as needing further development during this cycle. These experiences would be directly relevant to a future administrative role.</p>\n<p>My third goal is to lead an improvement cycle within my current coordination responsibilities. I am responsible for a committee implementing a community-school plan and deciding how its budget will support that work. Although I am not an administrator, leading this schoolwide effort requires the critical thinking, collaborative planning, and participation-building skills practiced in Cycle 1. It provides an immediate setting in which to continue developing them.</p>\n<p>I would begin by creating a meeting timeline for the remainder of the year. The schedule would take account of deadlines for reviewing survey information, setting implementation goals, and reflecting on results before the year ends. From that timeline, I would identify monthly objectives and prepare agendas that specify the decisions or measurable products needed at each meeting. I would arrive with the data, materials, and background information members need to make informed choices.</p>\n<p>The committee work will require the same attention to participation that I practiced in this inquiry. Preparing an agenda is only the beginning; I will need to help members interpret information, consider different views, and agree on responsibilities they can carry out. Reviewing our actions after each meeting will help me connect planning with implementation and notice when the process needs adjustment. This makes the current coordination role a practical opportunity to develop leadership before moving into administration.</p>\n<p>Each meeting would close with individual and shared next steps, and the following meeting would include a report on what happened. Afterward, I would review whether our actions still align with the implementation plan and revise the next agenda when necessary. At the end of the year, I would examine whether the committee met its goals and what tangible evidence supports that judgment. I would also seek feedback on my leadership so that completing the plan is not the only measure of learning. These three goals extend the cycle into further equity inquiry, participation in governance, and direct leadership of collaborative implementation.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Name the leadership skill, activity, timeframe, and feedback source for each goal.</li><li>Define evidence of improved practice, such as a corrected interpretation or a decision changed by another perspective.</li><li>Include quantitative interpretation and inquiry discipline among the learning needs supported by this example.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><section id=\"version-alignment\" class=\"major-section\">\n<h2>Read the template and handbook together</h2>\n<p>This comparison uses the current Version 09 Parts A-D templates and Cycle 1 Performance Assessment Guide. The template organizes the response; the handbook supplies the task directions, evidence requirements, and scoring rubrics.</p>\n<div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Current template and handbook comparison\"><table class=\"audit evidence-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Current template</th><th scope=\"col\">Current handbook</th><th scope=\"col\">What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Part A organization</th><td>I: initial quantitative collection and group selection; II: qualitative collection; III: school commitments; IV: equity-gap analysis.</td><td>Uses the same sequence and explains the collection and analysis tasks on pp. 6-9.</td><td>The documents agree on the structure. Complete the collection tasks and use their findings in the five analysis responses.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Quantitative-data cross-reference</th><td>Part A IV.1 refers to quantitative data in \"Section II,\" although Initial Data Collection is Section I.</td><td>Repeats that cross-reference on p. 8; its initial quantitative collection is also Section I.</td><td>This is a shared internal inconsistency, not a disagreement between the documents. Retain the official prompt, address its quantitative-analysis task, and seek program clarification about the reference.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Part B problem statement</th><td>Section II labels the problem-statement prompt as question 5, following four factor/need questions.</td><td>Places the problem statement under Section II on p. 13 without numbering it 5; p. 14 explains its purpose.</td><td>These refer to the same task. Use the template numbering and the handbook's explanation of a focused problem statement.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Part C feedback</th><td>Question 4 asks about feedback on each strategy and the resulting adjustments.</td><td>The introduction on p. 17 explains that feedback should address appropriateness, feasibility, educational partners, and resources/services.</td><td>Use those considerations to prepare for feedback and explain the decisions that follow it.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Part D reflection</th><td>Three prompts address feedback, learning with strengths/growth, and future professional learning. The written template allows up to five pages.</td><td>Uses the same three prompts on p. 20 and permits up to five written pages or five minutes of video explanation.</td><td>Answer all three prompts with specific cycle evidence. Consult the handbook when selecting the written or video format.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"source-note\">Locations are printed handbook page numbers. The cross-reference observation is instructional guidance, not an official CTC correction.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"A-sources\" class=\"major-section evidence-guide\">\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">Part A / Preparing the investigation</p><h2>Find the evidence before drafting</h2>\n<p>Start with the question: which student group is experiencing an unequal outcome, and what school conditions or access to support might help explain it? The CTC guide names four starting sources, five categories of quantitative evidence, and a range of qualitative approaches. Use the sources that answer your question; collecting every report is not an assessment requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>The required foundation:</strong> choose one of the six state indicators, analyze its school data across the three most recent years, identify one student group, collect and analyze at least three different qualitative sources, and connect the findings to school vision, mission, and/or goals. The guide directs candidates to select another indicator if three years of quantitative data are unavailable for the original choice. <a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-note-ctc\">CTC guide, printed pages 6-9</a>.</p>\n<h3>All six permitted state indicators</h3>\n<ul class=\"indicator-list\"><li>Chronic Absenteeism</li><li>Suspension Rate</li><li>English Learner Progress</li><li>Graduation Rate</li><li>Academic Performance</li><li>College/Career Readiness</li></ul>\n<p>The list identifies the choices for the focal indicator. Grades, climate surveys, staffing, and program participation can add relevant context; they do not replace the selected indicator and its three-year analysis.</p>\n\n<h3>All four starting sources named by CTC</h3>\n<div class=\"source-directory\">\n<section id=\"source-dashboard\"><h4>California School Dashboard</h4><p>Locate the selected school's indicator results and available student-group information. Keep the school identity, reporting year, measure, group, counts, and source notes with your working record. Compare actual measures across years and check reporting changes.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> establish the outcome pattern and explain the selected group. <strong>Part B:</strong> retain that same outcome concern while investigating factors; the pattern alone does not identify a cause.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Named in CTC guide, p. 6. Starting address: <code>https://caschooldashboard.org/</code>. School records may be needed to resolve missing information.</p></section>\n<section id=\"source-lcap\"><h4>Local Control and Accountability Plan (LCAP)</h4><p>Obtain the district or charter school's adopted plan and relevant updates. Find the goal, student group, action, resources, and reported progress connected to the concern. The LCAP describes goals, actions, services, and expenditures. <a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-note-lcap\">CDE LCAP guidance</a>.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> compare the intended allocation and outcome with the findings. <strong>Part B:</strong> investigate whether a proposed problem fits existing priorities and resources. A planned action is not proof that support reached students.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Named in CTC guide, p. 6. Obtain the actual local plan from the district/charter website or office; a state template is not your school's evidence.</p></section>\n<section id=\"source-eddata\"><h4>Ed-Data</h4><p>Find the school or district profile and inspect the available measures relevant to your chosen indicator and group. Preserve reporting years and source definitions, and reconcile any apparent difference with the original source.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> add useful comparison or context. <strong>Part B:</strong> use it to test a factual assumption before writing about resources or conditions. The same underlying state figures presented on two websites are not independent confirmation.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Named in CTC guide, p. 6. Starting address: <code>https://www.ed-data.org/</code>.</p></section>\n<section id=\"source-sarc\"><h4>School Accountability Report Card (SARC)</h4><p>Obtain the school's available report cards and inspect the sections relevant to your inquiry. Record each report's publication date and the data year of the particular table; they may differ.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> use relevant school information to give the indicator analysis context. <strong>Part B:</strong> use documented conditions to frame questions about processes and resources, then verify present practice locally.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Named in CTC guide, p. 6. Look on the school/district website or request it from the school office. CDE's SARC starting address: <code>https://www.cde.ca.gov/ta/ac/sa/</code>.</p></section>\n</div>\n\n<section id=\"source-quantitative\"><h3>All five quantitative evidence categories in the guide</h3>\n<p>These are categories of possible supporting evidence from the guide, not five additional required datasets. The examples below preserve the scope of the list on printed page 6.</p>\n<div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Quantitative evidence categories\"><table class=\"audit evidence-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Category and supporting evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Use in Part A</th><th scope=\"col\">Follow-up for Part B</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Student performance: grades or promotion; end-of-course assessments; course completion; career pathway enrollment, completion, and dropout; interim/periodic assessments; graduation and college-going rates.</th><td>Clarify the learning pattern and its relationship to the selected indicator.</td><td>Investigate access to learning, pathways, or support. A result does not establish the quality of instruction by itself.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Student engagement: attendance and program participation, such as AP enrollment.</th><td>Compare relevant participation patterns by group and year.</td><td>Examine schedules, entry criteria, outreach, and access barriers rather than assume motivation.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Teacher qualifications: years teaching, time at the school, and assignment monitoring.</th><td>Describe relevant staffing context using appropriate records.</td><td>Examine support, continuity, or assignment processes. Do not infer an individual's competence from group statistics.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">School characteristics: average class size, demographics, and school budget.</th><td>Describe the population and relevant allocation of resources.</td><td>Check how allocation becomes usable support. A budget entry does not establish implementation or impact.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">School, teacher, and student/family/guardian culture: culture and climate data.</th><td>Investigate experiences of connection, safety, or support relevant to the indicator.</td><td>Compare perspectives and investigate a plausible mechanism without treating a perception measure as proof of cause.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>\n\n<section id=\"source-qualitative\"><h3>The qualitative sources and activities CTC describes</h3>\n<p>The guide names interview responses, observation notes, and document analysis as examples, and describes interviews, observations, and surveys as possible collection strategies. It also offers the four activities below. Select at least three different relevant sources and explain what each contributes; three websites repeating one dataset do not become three qualitative sources. <a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-note-ctc\">CTC guide, p. 7</a>.</p>\n<ol class=\"qualitative-options\">\n<li><strong>Observations and participant perspectives:</strong> observe in and around the school, including classrooms; use focus groups or surveys with students, teachers, families/guardians, and other representative educational partners.</li>\n<li><strong>Audits of processes and practices:</strong> examine teaching practices, academic intervention, course offerings, class schedules, family/guardian participation, professional development, or the school budget, including evidence of effectiveness related to the identified need.</li>\n<li><strong>Further educational-partner input:</strong> use surveys, interviews, or focus groups with leaders, teachers, students, families/guardians, or community members about strengths, interests, and needs, including those of the selected group.</li>\n<li><strong>Student school culture:</strong> examine course taking, extracurricular activities, discipline practices, academic and social environments, and student-engagement improvement efforts for opportunities related to the indicator and group.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The material and the analysis determine the evidence type.</strong> Numerical survey percentages are quantitative. Analyzed open responses, observation notes, and document passages may contribute qualitative evidence. Record actual findings and how you analyzed them, including differing accounts. Use appropriate access, participation, and confidentiality arrangements.</p>\n<p>The guide also calls for the school's vision, mission, and/or goals and describes checking the relationship between resource allocations and outcomes. It identifies additional inquiry such as student shadowing when evidence is insufficient. A local document can contain both numbers and narrative; analyze each for what it can establish. <a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-note-ctc\">CTC guide, p. 8</a>.</p></section>\n\n<h3>Additional sources for this inquiry</h3>\n<div class=\"source-directory\">\n<section id=\"source-wasc\"><h4>WASC materials</h4><p>Where available, request the latest school self-study, visiting committee report, schoolwide action plan, and subsequent progress report. WASC's progress guidance emphasizes examining implementation and evidence of effects on learning and well-being. <a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-note-wasc\">ACS WASC guidance</a>.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> locate relevant commitments and documented findings. <strong>Part B:</strong> examine whether a concern has already been identified, what response was planned, and what progress evidence exists. Read specific passages and their supporting evidence; the existence of a report or accreditation does not resolve the equity question.</p><p class=\"source-note\">An optional local source for this course, not a named requirement in the Cycle 1 guide. A school may not have WASC materials; use other relevant school plans and process evidence where appropriate.</p></section>\n<section id=\"source-chks\"><h4>California Healthy Kids Survey (CHKS)</h4><p>CHKS addresses school climate and safety, student wellness, resiliency, and connectedness. Seek the available school/district report, administration year, participating grades, response counts/rates, and relevant item wording. CDE identifies companion staff and parent surveys within CalSCHLS. <a href=\"#cycle/c1/source-note-chks\">CDE CHKS guidance</a>.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> use relevant findings to frame questions about experience and access. <strong>Part B:</strong> examine those questions with local qualitative evidence. Preserve differences in populations and years; anonymous survey percentages cannot identify the individual students in an attendance table.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Optional supporting evidence. The sample uses a School Experience Survey; it is not identified as CHKS. Do not relabel it or present numerical CHKS results as the required qualitative sources.</p></section>\n<section id=\"source-analyzer\"><h4>Equity Gap Analyzer</h4><p>Use the instructor's site to organize public evidence for the correct school: confirm the school and CDS identifier, examine the available report and timeline, and retain the actual years, values, counts, and source qualifications. Check important figures against their underlying sources and local records.</p><p><strong>Part A:</strong> identify a defensible pattern and questions for local inquiry. <strong>Part B:</strong> return to that pattern while investigating processes and educational need. An app-generated narrative is a starting point for analysis, not evidence that interviews occurred or that a proposed cause has been established.</p><figure class=\"analyzer-example\"><button type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge saved Equity Gap Analyzer view\" title=\"Enlarge saved Analyzer view\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 1: Saved Equity Gap app school-selection screen, showing Jordan High and its CDS identifier\"><img src=\"assets/gap-app-pages/school.png\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1080\" alt=\"Saved Equity Gap app school-selection screen, showing Jordan High and its CDS identifier\" loading=\"lazy\"></button><figcaption>Saved October 6, 2026 public-app view. Jordan High is a separate illustration, not the Riverview High sample. The image is a dated reference, not a live connection.</figcaption></figure><p class=\"source-note\">Site address: <code>https://explorer.chrisboosalis.com</code>. This description uses the locally preserved app report and capture record. Live availability and interface were not verified in this review.</p></section>\n</div>\n\n<h3>A working source record</h3><p>For each source, retain its title, owner, date, exact page/table/item, population, measure, finding, limitation, and the Part A or B question it helps answer. For numerical comparisons, retain the year and denominator. For qualitative material, retain the analyzed passage or observation and the interpretation. Keep source evidence separate from your own inference.</p>\n<section class=\"source-citations\" aria-label=\"Source notes for the evidence guide\">\n<h3>Source notes</h3>\n<p id=\"source-note-ctc\"><strong>CTC:</strong> supplied <cite>CalAPA Leadership Cycle 1 Assessment Guide</cite>, Version 09, copyright 2026, printed pp. 6-9 and 13-14. All four named starting sources, all six indicators, all five quantitative categories, and the qualitative examples above were checked against that local guide. These are the guide's examples, not an exhaustive list of every acceptable source.</p>\n<p id=\"source-note-lcap\"><strong>CDE:</strong> <cite>Local Control and Accountability Plan (LCAP)</cite>, checked October 9, 2026. Source address: <code>https://www.cde.ca.gov/re/lc/</code>.</p>\n<p id=\"source-note-wasc\"><strong>ACS WASC:</strong> <cite>Reflections: Effective Mid-cycle and Other Progress Reports</cite>, Spring 2021, checked October 9, 2026. Source address: <code>https://acswasc.org/writing-effective-school-reports/</code>. The specific suggested uses in Parts A and B are instructional applications.</p>\n<p id=\"source-note-chks\"><strong>CDE:</strong> <cite>California Healthy Kids Survey</cite>, checked October 9, 2026. Source address: <code>https://www.cde.ca.gov/LS/he/at/chks.asp</code>. The comparison and interpretation cautions are instructional guidance.</p>\n</section>\n<h3>Current Part A template</h3><div class=\"template-pages\"><figure><button type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Part A template page 1\" title=\"Enlarge template page 1\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 1: Current Cycle 1 Part A template, page 1\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-A-1.png\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" alt=\"Current Cycle 1 Part A template, page 1\"></button><figcaption>Part A template / page 1</figcaption></figure><figure><button type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Part A template page 2\" title=\"Enlarge template page 2\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Cycle 1: Current Cycle 1 Part A template, page 2\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-A-2.png\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" alt=\"Current Cycle 1 Part A template, page 2\"></button><figcaption>Part A template / page 2</figcaption></figure></div></section><section id=\"limits\" class=\"major-section\"><h2>What you will submit</h2><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Part, Submission, Maximum length\"><table class=\"c1-limits\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Submission</th><th scope=\"col\">Maximum length</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Data tables and equity-gap analysis</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 pages of responses</strong>, excluding quantitative and qualitative data tables</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">B</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Contributing factors and problem statement</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 pages</strong></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">C</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Strategies, feedback, adjustments, and partner involvement</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 pages</strong></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">D</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Reflective narrative</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 written pages OR 5 minutes of video explanation</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>These are limits for each complete part, not for each question. Submit one file per part. Written files may be DOCX, ODT, or PDF. Use the original templates with Arial 11-point type, single spacing, and one-inch margins. The original template text and your responses count toward the applicable limit, with the stated exclusion for Part A's data tables. The guide does not give charts a separate page-count exemption. Excess pages are not read or scored.</p>\n<p>First develop the evidence and reasoning each question needs; then revise for clarity and length. The teaching explanations and annotated examples on this page are longer than a candidate response. They are not a completed, page-fitted submission to copy. The Part D video option addresses the same three reflection prompts; it is not a recording of a meeting.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">CTC Cycle 1 Version 09: <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">preparing evidence, printed pp. 22-23</a>; <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">submission specifications, printed pp. 24-25</a>. Part D permits asf, qt, mov, mpg, mpeg, avi, wmv, mp4, or m4v video files.</p></section><footer id=\"source-record\" class=\"sources\"><h2>Source notes</h2><p>Questions and requirements come from the current CalAPA Cycle 1 Parts A-D templates and Version 09 Performance Assessment Guide. Full rubric descriptors are reproduced below; explanations and critiques are instructional interpretations.</p><p>The examples are full-length rewrites of material supplied for local teaching review. The scenarios, numerical evidence, and sequence inform the newly drafted prose. The qualitative findings table is a teaching reconstruction. School records, cited research studies, and an official assessor score report were not independently obtained for the example.</p><p>Official template and handbook material copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing.</p></footer><section id=\"score-summary\" class=\"major-section\">\n            <h2>Interpolated Example Status</h2>\n            <p>The examples on this page are newly worded instructional parallels. They are not official exemplars, not candidate submissions, and not scored responses.</p>\n            <p>Use them to study the kind of evidence and reasoning each prompt calls for, then replace the details with your own school context, records, meetings, and reflections.</p>\n          </section><section id=\"rubric-1-1\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.1\" data-score=\"3\"><h3>Rubric 1.1: Quantitative inquiry</h3><p class=\"essential\">Based on the chosen California state indicator, how well does the candidate select and analyze quantitative data sources across the three most recent years, identify patterns and/or trends related to equity, and choose an appropriate student group?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not include quantitative data across the three most recent years. OR Patterns and trends are not identified or are irrelevant. OR Candidate does not select a student group to investigate OR selects a student group not appropriate for the identified indicator.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate selects a California state indicator* and minimally analyzes quantitative data, vaguely identifying patterns and/or trends related to school equity. Candidate is not clear about which student group they will investigate or why the student group was selected.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate selects a California state indicator* and analyzes quantitative data across the three most recent years, clearly identifying general patterns and/or trends related to school equity. Candidate clearly identifies a student group to investigate further and provides a rationale for why this student group was selected.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-1\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explores additional data linked to the indicator that clearly informs their understanding of patterns and/or trends to further clarify group differences within the state indicator selected (e.g., demographic data, ethnicity, gender, language).</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites relevant research that informs their understanding (patterns and/or trends related to equity) of the determined equity issue for the chosen student group.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">California state indicator: Chronic Absenteeism, Suspension Rate, English Learner Progress, Graduation Rate, Academic Performance, College/Career Readiness.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 10. No score is assigned to the rewritten example.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-2\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.2\" data-score=\"2\"><h3>Rubric 1.2: Qualitative inquiry</h3><p class=\"essential\">How does the candidate collect and analyze relevant qualitative data from a range of at least three different qualitative* data sources and explain their relationship of the findings from the analysis to the quantitative** data findings and the identified student group equity issue?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not provide or analyze three qualitative* data sources. OR Candidate provides no or irrelevant information about the connection among qualitative and quantitative data** findings and the identified student group equity issue.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate collects and minimally analyzes a range of qualitative* data from at least three sources. Candidate minimally connects the qualitative data and findings to the quantitative data** findings for the identified student group equity issue.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate collects and analyzes a range of relevant qualitative* data from three different sources. Candidate clearly explains the relationship among the findings from the analyses of the qualitative data, the quantitative data,** and the identified student group equity issue.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-2\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate collects additional qualitative data as appropriate to deepen their understanding of the California state indicator chosen and the student group equity issue, and provides a complete analysis of the relationship between their qualitative and quantitative data findings and the identified student group equity issue.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains how their qualitative data collection strategy is responsive to the complex context in which they are working and demonstrates cultural sensitivity and an appreciation for diverse viewpoints.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Qualitative data include contextual evidence collected through methods such as interviews, focus groups, observations, and document analysis. Quantitative data express quantities, amounts, or ranges. See the full definitions on handbook p. 11.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 11. No score is assigned to the rewritten example.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-3\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.3\" data-score=\"2\"><h3>Rubric 1.3: Equity gap analysis</h3><p class=\"essential\">How does the candidate conduct an equity gap analysis based on the chosen California state indicator to inform their understanding of the equity issues for the identified student group? How does this equity gap align with the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate identifies an equity issue with no evidence of quantitative or qualitative data analysis. OR Candidate does not identify any patterns and/or trends in the equity gap analysis provided. OR Candidate does not provide a description of an equity gap for the student group. OR Candidate does not identify the alignment between the equity gap for a student group at the school and the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate identifies an equity issue for an identified student group based on minimal quantitative or qualitative data analysis. Candidate identifies patterns and/or trends that are not clear in the equity gap analysis provided or does not clearly describe the identified patterns and/or trends. Candidate provides a minimal description of an equity gap for the student group. Candidate minimally describes the alignment between the equity gap for a student group at the school and the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate identifies an equity issue for an identified student group clearly based on their quantitative and qualitative data analysis for the chosen state indicator. Candidate clearly describes patterns and/or trends across the quantitative and qualitative data analysis in the equity gap analysis provided. Candidate clearly identifies the alignment between the equity gap for the identified student group and the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-3\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate conducts a thorough equity gap analysis, describing a clear connection from quantitative data findings to supportive qualitative data findings, and provides a sophisticated understanding of the equity disparity identified for the student group.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites and explains how research informs their understanding of the equity gap for the specific student group.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 12. No score is assigned to the rewritten example.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-4\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.4\" data-score=\"2\"><h3>Rubric 1.4: Contributing factors</h3><p class=\"essential\">How does the candidate determine contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, that created or added to the identified equity gap affecting a student group and cite the research supporting their determination?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate identifies contributing factors that are biased, superficial, or irrelevant to the equity gap analysis. OR Candidate does not cite research and/or neglects to draw connections between research and contributing factors.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate identifies potential contributing factors and minimally describes how they relate to the equity gap analysis. Candidate attempts to draw connections between research and contributing factors, but citations are not related to the equity gap.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly uses the equity gap analysis and findings from the quantitative and qualitative data analyses to determine contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, that created or added to an equity gap affecting a student group. Candidate cites relevant research to support potential contributing factors that create or add to the identified equity gap affecting a student group.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-4\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains in detail (with supporting evidence from Step 1) how several contributing factors can create or add to equity differences or disparities for a student group.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate demonstrates a sophisticated, research-based understanding of the systemic, institutional, or structural causes of the identified single equity gap for a group of students at the school.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 15. No score is assigned to the rewritten example.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-5\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.5\" data-score=\"2\"><h3>Rubric 1.5: Problem statement</h3><p class=\"essential\">How does the candidate use the equity gap analysis and identification of potential contributing factors to develop a feasible problem statement related to student achievement and/or well-being?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not use the equity gap analysis or potential contributing factors to develop a problem statement. OR Candidate’s problem statement is not responsive to the needs of the student group.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate attempts to use the equity gap analysis and potential contributing factors to develop a problem statement, but it is not clear how the problem statement supports student achievement and/or well-being of the student group. Candidate’s problem statement is only partially responsive to the needs of the student group.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate develops a feasible problem statement related to student achievement and/or well-being of the student group that clearly draws from the equity gap analysis and potential contributing factors identified. Candidate’s problem statement is clearly responsive to the needs of the student group.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-5\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains why their problem statement is responsive and feasible for the school’s culture and context.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites relevant evidence-based practices or research on how the area of educational need has been addressed in other school settings to improve achievement and/or well-being for similar student groups.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 16. No score is assigned to the rewritten example.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-6\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.6\" data-score=\"3\"><h3>Rubric 1.6: Strategies and alignment</h3><p class=\"essential\">How are the strategy(ies) proposed for equitable school improvement for the student group well informed by the findings of the equity gap analysis, including contributing factors, and responsive to the problem statement? How is the proposed strategy(ies) aligned to the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not propose strategy(ies) for equitable school improvement informed by the findings. OR Proposed strategy(ies) are not aligned with the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate’s proposed strategy(ies) are minimally informed by the findings and provide only general reference to the equity gap analysis, contributing factors, and/or problem statement. Proposed strategy(ies) are partially aligned with the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate’s proposed strategy(ies) for equitable school improvement for the student group are well informed by the findings of the equity gap analysis and the contributing factors are responsive to the problem statement. Proposed strategy(ies) are clearly aligned with the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-6\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides relevant strategy(ies) that strategically focus on equitable student and school improvement and represent a contextually responsive approach to addressing the equity issue or educational need.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides research-based evidence of the relevance of the proposed strategy(ies) and their implementation for improving student achievement and/or well-being for the specific student group and school.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 18. No score is assigned to the rewritten example.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-7\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.7\" data-score=\"4\"><h3>Rubric 1.7: Feedback and feasibility</h3><p class=\"essential\">How does the candidate apply the feedback received from administrators or educational leaders familiar with the school culture and context and describe next steps for creating educational partner buy-in and potential implications for the adjusted set of strategy(ies)?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not apply feedback from an administrator/supervisor/educational leader to adjust or strengthen their proposed strategy(ies). OR Candidate states their plan(s) to communicate the proposed strategy(ies) with little or no explanation of steps for buy-in from educational partner(s). OR Candidate does not identify anticipated implications for proposed strategy(ies).</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate vaguely describes feedback received from an administrator/supervisor/educational leader and makes irrelevant and/or inappropriate adjustments to proposed strategy(ies). Candidate vaguely describes their plan(s) to communicate the proposed strategy(ies) with educational partner(s); it is not clear that educational partner(s) will have the opportunity to develop buy-in. Candidate does not clearly describe anticipated, realistic implications that may be encountered at the school related to implementation of proposed strategy(ies).</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly applies feedback received from an administrator/supervisor/educational leader to adjust or strengthen proposed strategy(ies). Candidate clearly provides relevant and appropriate next steps for creating buy-in and communicating with educational partner(s) to address the equity gap and learning need(s). Candidate clearly describes anticipated, realistic implications that may be encountered at the school related to the implementation of proposed strategy(ies).</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-7\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate seeks additional rounds of feedback from other administrators or educational partners on the revised strategy(ies) and develops a detailed strategic plan to communicate and share the plan with a diverse range of educational partners to ensure they are proposing a workable/feasible approach to addressing the equity gap and learning need.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate plans to coach educational partners to examine and address potential biases that could impact student learning and/or well-being due to identified equity gaps, including those specifically related to sources of education disadvantage or discrimination, and is transparent about the potential underlying contributing factors.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 19. No score is assigned to the rewritten example.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-1-8\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\" data-rubric=\"1.8\" data-score=\"3\"><h3>Rubric 1.8: Leadership learning</h3><p class=\"essential\">How did the leadership feedback shape the candidate’s equity-focused strategy, build educational partner buy- in, and support their growth in addressing equity needs and gaps for the identified student group? How does the candidate reflect on their current strengths and identify future areas for growth in equity leadership?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not discuss or only mentions their selected administrator/supervisor/educational leader and/or the importance of educational partner buy-in is not discussed in the reflection. OR Candidate does not reflect on their growth as an equity-driven leader and/or does not address the equity needs or equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site. OR Candidate does not refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. OR Candidate does not discuss strengths or areas for future professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate provides limited insight into why they selected the administrator/supervisor/educational leader for feedback and how that feedback impacted their approach to build buy-in. Candidate vaguely reflects on their growth as an equity-driven leader and minimally discusses their ability to address the equity needs and/or equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site, referring to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. Candidate does not clearly explain their current strengths and/or vaguely mentions future areas for growth in equity leadership.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly explains their rationale for selecting the administrator/supervisor/educational leader to provide feedback on the selected strategy(ies) and how that feedback impacted their approach to build buy-in from school personnel. Candidate clearly reflects on their growth as an equity-driven leader and discusses their ability to address the equity needs and equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site, referring to specific, relevant evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. Candidate clearly explains their current strengths and identifies future areas for growth in equity leadership.</p></section><section class=\"level\" id=\"level4-8\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate’s reflection demonstrates how the school context— including social, economic, or cultural contexts— impacts their approach to providing equity-driven leadership.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate utilizes experiences from Steps 1, 2, and 3 in confronting equity issues and gaps to describe what leadership skills have been learned and how those skills may be used in the future.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Full level descriptors from the current handbook, printed page 21. No score is assigned to the rewritten example.</p></section></div>","search":[{"title":"Part A / I.1 / Data and student-group selection","url":"#cycle/c1/A-data","html":"<span id=\"a-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-a\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.1 / Data and student-group selection</h2><section class=\"question\"><span id=\"A-group\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tIdentify the one specific student group you have chosen to further investigate.</p><p class=\"task-directions\">Directions: Complete the following table. While this template reflects the categories found on the California School Dashboard, it is neither an exclusive nor a mandatory list of categories, and you may add or delete rows as needed.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Choose one state indicator, compare the school and its student groups across the three most recent years, and identify one group for further investigation. Make the table understandable: the measure, years, groups, and reporting populations should be clear. The short identification answer names the group; Part A IV.1 develops the reason for choosing it.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Reading the data accurately</summary><p>A rate is a count divided by the population eligible for that measure. Keep the numerator, denominator, reporting year, and definition together; enrollment may not be the reporting denominator. Subtract two rates to describe a percentage-point difference.</p><p>Compare both the group's trend and its difference from the comparison group. Schoolwide results include the selected group; overlapping group rows cannot be added. Annual results may involve different students, so they are not automatically a matched-cohort comparison. Missing or suppressed results are not zero.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.1</h3><ul><li>Analyze three years of quantitative data for one state indicator.</li><li>Identify relevant patterns and explain the selected student group.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how additional data clarify group differences within the indicator.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Full Rubric 1.1</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Selected California indicator: Chronic Absenteeism. The attendance figures below are from the LAUSD Whole Child Integrated Data record used in this instructional scenario.</p><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Three-year chronic absence percentages\"><table class=\"source-table\"><caption>Three-year chronic absence percentages</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Student population</th><th scope=\"col\">Latest reporting year 2024-2025</th><th scope=\"col\">Previous reporting year 2023-2024</th><th scope=\"col\">Earlier reporting year 2022-2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Schoolwide</td><td>33.78%</td><td>36.39%</td><td>35.91%</td></tr><tr><td>English learners</td><td>51.09% of 167 (53% NC)</td><td>51.71% of 170 (59% NC)</td><td>47.27% of 162 (56% NC)</td></tr><tr><td>Socioeconomically disadvantaged</td><td>36.53%</td><td>38.23%</td><td>40.03%</td></tr><tr><td>Students with disabilities</td><td>40.61%</td><td>45.56%</td><td>48.08%</td></tr><tr><td>Asian</td><td>20%</td><td>14.29%</td><td>11.54%</td></tr><tr><td>African American</td><td>42.86% (23 students)</td><td>58.62% (24 students)</td><td>53.85% (25 students)</td></tr><tr><td>Filipino</td><td>20%</td><td>24.14%</td><td>25.71%</td></tr><tr><td>Hispanic</td><td>33.66%</td><td>36.79%</td><td>36.78%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"School Experience Survey: enjoyment of classroom learning\"><table class=\"source-table\"><caption>School Experience Survey: enjoyment of classroom learning</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Student population</th><th scope=\"col\">Latest reporting year 2024-2025</th><th scope=\"col\">Previous reporting year 2023-2024</th><th scope=\"col\">Earlier reporting year 2022-2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Schoolwide</td><td>46%</td><td>41%</td><td>37%</td></tr><tr><td>English learners</td><td>50%</td><td>53%</td><td>45%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"School Experience Survey: participation in school extracurricular activities\"><table class=\"source-table\"><caption>School Experience Survey: participation in school extracurricular activities</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Student population</th><th scope=\"col\">Latest reporting year 2024-2025</th><th scope=\"col\">Previous reporting year 2023-2024</th><th scope=\"col\">Earlier reporting year 2022-2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Schoolwide</td><td>49%</td><td>46%</td><td>52%</td></tr><tr><td>English learners</td><td>37%</td><td>35%</td><td>48%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"California Dashboard: summative ELPAC and English Learner Progress Indicator\"><table class=\"source-table\"><caption>California Dashboard: summative ELPAC and English Learner Progress Indicator</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Population</th><th scope=\"col\">Latest year 2024</th><th scope=\"col\">Previous year 2023</th><th scope=\"col\">Earlier year 2022</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All English learners</td><td>51% advanced one ELPI level; 44.4% stayed at their ELPI level; 4.6% moved down one ELPI level.</td><td>46.7% advanced one ELPI level; 46.2% stayed at their ELPI level; 7.1% moved down one ELPI level.</td><td>45.6% advanced one ELPI level; 41.1% stayed at their ELPI level; 12.8% moved down one ELPI level.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>I selected English learners at Riverview High for further investigation of chronic absence. In each of the three reporting years, their rate exceeds the schoolwide rate. This continuing difference makes the group a relevant focus for examining attendance and access to support. This population remains the focus throughout the analysis and the development of the proposed response.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Report the annual differences in percentage points and explain both the persistent disparity and the final-year improvement in the EL rate.</li><li>Identify the year, population, denominator, and source for each measure; reconcile apparent inconsistencies.</li><li>Keep the selected group consistent and explain the contribution of the additional measures in Part A IV.1.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II / Qualitative evidence table","url":"#cycle/c1/A-qualitative","html":"<span id=\"qualitative-analysis\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II / Qualitative evidence table</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">Directions: Identify each of the three qualitative sources you have selected, and record and analyze the qualitative data you have collected from each source.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Record what each qualitative source revealed and explain what it contributes to understanding the selected group and indicator. A source is the person or material; an interview is a method; a finding is what was learned; analysis explains its significance and limits. Listing interview participants completes only the first part of this task.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Turning sources into findings</summary><p>Choose sources that can illuminate the selected population's experiences and the school conditions related to the indicator. Ask about concrete events: what happened, what support was available, whether it was usable, and what happened next. For documents, compare written expectations with available evidence of follow-through.</p><p>Record each source's contribution before comparing accounts. Look for recurring themes, differences, and counterexamples. A theme is an interpretation supported by evidence, not a count of interview labels. Explain whose experience is represented and what remains unknown; one account does not establish how common a condition is.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.2</h3><ul><li>Collect and analyze at least three different, relevant qualitative sources.</li><li>Explain relationships among their findings, the quantitative data, and the equity issue.</li><li>For Level 4, deepen understanding with additional qualitative data as appropriate and provide a complete connected analysis.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Full Rubric 1.2</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Interview evidence\"><table class=\"source-table\"><caption>Interview evidence</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Source</th><th scope=\"col\">Method</th><th scope=\"col\">Findings</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ELD teacher</td><td>Interview</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Chronically absent EL student</td><td>Interview</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>PSA counselor</td><td>Interview</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>ELAC parent</td><td>Interview</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Qualitative findings and their contribution\"><table class=\"audit evidence-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Reported finding</th><th>Contribution to the inquiry</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">ELD teacher</th><td>Frequent enrollment interrupts instructional continuity and requires repeated review.</td><td>Investigate entry routines and instructional support; this is not evidence of teacher incompetence.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Student</th><td>The student reports a difficult placement experience and improved attendance after placement changed.</td><td>Examine the individual experience without treating it as a groupwide effect.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">PSA counselor</th><td>Unstable housing interferes with a student reaching school.</td><td>Examine attendance access and support; the account does not establish how common the barrier is.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">ELAC parent</th><td>Bus-route changes and less frequent service make travel to school more difficult.</td><td>Investigate transportation conditions and who is affected.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Populate each findings cell with the actual evidence already described in the narrative.</li><li>Compare the accounts to identify themes, differences, and questions that remain unresolved.</li><li>Use a focused follow-up, where needed, to examine an unresolved condition rather than collecting another source merely to increase the count.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / III.1 / School vision and mission","url":"#cycle/c1/A-mission","html":"<span id=\"a-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / III.1 / School vision and mission</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tInvestigate the vision, mission, and/or goals at your school and document your findings.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Investigate the school's adopted vision, mission, or goals and document the commitments relevant to your selected population. Explain why those commitments matter for that group. This question establishes the school's intentions; Part A IV.5 develops the comparison between those intentions and the equity-gap evidence.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><ul><li>Document specific school commitments that can inform the equity analysis.</li><li>Connect those commitments to the selected group and indicator in the Part A analysis.</li><li>Rubric 1.3 evaluates the integrated analysis across the relevant responses, including the thorough synthesis required at Level 4.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Riverview High's vision centers on an education shaped around individual learners so that graduates are ready to enter college, begin a career, or pursue both. Its mission identifies five responsibilities: (1) set ambitious expectations and offer demanding, standards-aligned learning connected to situations beyond school; (2) maintain a secure, personal learning setting with the relationships associated with a small school; (3) strengthen the knowledge and abilities of teachers and other staff to meet every student's needs; (4) connect students and families with community services that reduce barriers to learning; and (5) give educational partners meaningful influence and opportunities to participate in improvements to their school and surrounding community. These responsibilities apply across the full student population.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the source and date of the school's adopted vision, mission, or goals.</li><li>Select the commitments most relevant to EL students' instructional experience and attendance access.</li><li>Develop the evidence-based comparison in Part A IV.5, distinguishing intended provision from students' reported experiences.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / IV.1 / Selecting the equity gap","url":"#cycle/c1/A1","html":"<span id=\"a-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.1 / Selecting the equity gap</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tReflecting upon the data collected and analyzed, what supports your decision to select this specific equity gap for your identified group of students? Share your analysis for the specific quantitative data you included in Section II of the Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis template and your rationale for collecting it. How are these data relevant to understanding equity issues at the school for student groups?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why this student group and equity gap deserve investigation. Interpret the three-year quantitative pattern, explain why you collected the additional numerical data, and show what those data contribute to understanding equity at the school. A list of percentages is incomplete without the reasoning that connects them to your choice.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/version-alignment\">Template and handbook cross-reference note</a>.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.1</h3><ul><li>Explain the three-year pattern and the rationale for selecting the group.</li><li>Show how additional quantitative findings clarify the indicator and group differences.</li><li>Distinguish observed patterns from explanations the available data do not establish.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Full Rubric 1.1</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I selected Chronic Absenteeism as the California indicator for this investigation. The opening table compares three reporting years for the entire school and several demographic groups. Across those years, the schoolwide rate at Riverview High averages approximately 35%, providing a reference point for examining differences among groups. The comparable averages are about 50% for English learners, 38% for socioeconomically disadvantaged students, and 45% for students with disabilities. Asian students have the lowest average, approximately 15%. The averages for African American, Filipino, and Hispanic students are approximately 51%, 23%, and 36%, respectively. These figures describe overlapping populations rather than separate portions of the student body.</p>\n<p>Most groups show lower chronic absence rates by 2024-2025 than they did in 2022-2023. The year-to-year movements are not identical, however. English learners, Asian students, and African American students experienced an increase in the middle year, followed by some improvement in the final year. For English learners, the first-to-last-year comparison remains unfavorable even though their final-year rate is slightly lower than the previous year's. Comparing the rounded three-year averages gives a difference of roughly 15 percentage points between English learners and all students. I chose the English learner population for further inquiry because the disparity has continued and because the group represents approximately 166 students, a substantial part of this school. The annual figures also show that the schoolwide improvement has outpaced improvement for this group.</p>\n<p>To investigate what might accompany this attendance pattern, I examined the School Experience Survey and the California Dashboard's English Learner Progress Indicator. These additional measures concern classroom experience, participation in school life, and language development. I selected them to explore possible explanations for the attendance difference, recognizing that a relationship between measures would need further investigation before it could be treated as a cause.</p>\n<p>The Academic Focus item in the survey asks whether students experience their teachers' lessons as enjoyable. I considered this because a positive classroom experience may support a student's willingness to attend. Across the three years, approximately 49% of English learners responded favorably, compared with about 41% of students overall. English learners therefore reported more favorable experiences on this item than the schoolwide population. At the same time, the remaining approximately 51% prompted questions about students who may not experience that connection. The survey alone does not tell me whether those respondents are the students with frequent absences, and the nonpositive responses could include neutral views.</p>\n<p>The school-climate item addresses participation in extracurricular activities. In secondary schools, athletics and clubs can give students additional reasons to attend: athletic eligibility may depend on attendance, while clubs involve responsibilities to peers and projects. I wanted to know whether English learners had comparable opportunities for these connections. Their average participation rate was approximately 40%, compared with 49% schoolwide. That difference raises a question about access to school activities and the sense of belonging those activities can provide. It may be relevant to attendance, but participation and attendance records would need to be examined together to establish who experiences both conditions.</p>\n<p>Finally, I examined language development because missed instruction and academic progress can influence one another. The ELPI table shows that, on average, approximately 48% of English learners moved to a higher reported proficiency level. About half of the school's English learner population consists of newcomers. These students may be developing initial English skills while adjusting to a new culture, housing arrangements, and, in some cases, earlier traumatic experiences. I wondered whether the students not advancing a reported level were predominantly newcomers, although the aggregate figures cannot answer that question. Remaining at a level also does not necessarily mean that no learning occurred.</p>\n<p>The additional tables therefore serve different purposes in the inquiry. The classroom-experience item concerns how students perceive learning, the participation item concerns their involvement beyond lessons, and the language indicator concerns reported progress. I selected each because it might clarify a different dimension of school connection. Their value depends on keeping those distinctions visible as I compare them with attendance.</p>\n<p>One possible explanation is that insufficient language support contributes to academic frustration, weakened engagement, and eventually absence. Another is that social, emotional, or economic circumstances interrupt attendance and thereby reduce opportunities to develop English. Both directions deserve attention. The relationship between attendance and achievement discussed by Liu and colleagues (2021) provides a reason to investigate these connections, but does not identify the explanation at Riverview. My working concern is that newcomers may face several of these challenges at once. I would need student-level attendance, language-development, and support information before concluding how much of the chronic absence pattern belongs to that subgroup.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use annual percentage-point differences and acknowledge the final-year improvement in the EL rate.</li><li>Remove unsupported claims that identify absent students from separate survey or newcomer percentages.</li><li>Explain how each additional finding changes the interpretation of the attendance disparity and identify what remains uncertain.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / IV.2 / Explaining the qualitative sources","url":"#cycle/c1/A2","html":"<span id=\"a-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.2 / Explaining the qualitative sources</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tDescribe the three different qualitative data sources you included in Section II of the Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis template. How did these sources provide more information about this student group for the California state indicator?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>For each qualitative source, explain the perspective it provides, what you learned, and how that finding adds to your understanding of the selected group and indicator. Explain why the source was useful. A biography of each participant or a list of interviews does not show the contribution of the evidence.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.2</h3><ul><li>Describe at least three different qualitative sources and analyze their relevant findings.</li><li>Explain what each source adds to understanding the group and state indicator.</li><li>Use the findings together to deepen the inquiry, with further qualitative evidence as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Full Rubric 1.2</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>To understand English learners' attendance experiences from several positions, I interviewed an English language development teacher, a student with a history of chronic absence, a Pupil Services and Attendance counselor, and a parent who participates in the English Learner Advisory Committee. Each person could describe a different part of the student's relationship with school: instruction, placement, attendance support, or family access.</p>\n<p>The ELD teacher is early in her career and teaches the newcomer course that begins the district's English learner instructional sequence. Newly enrolled students enter her classes throughout the year, sometimes more than two in an almost alternate-week pattern. She wants each arrival to feel included, but repeatedly introducing students and revisiting objectives interrupts the continuity of lessons. She also described irregular attendance among students already enrolled. Some miss class for vaccination appointments; others take part-time jobs when a parent is ill. Several are adjusting to unstable housing or difficult experiences in their countries of origin. Her account helped me see how circumstances outside the classroom can weaken continuity inside it. It also connects with Bryk, Gomez, and colleagues' discussion of the increasing complexity of heterogeneous classrooms (p. 62). The account suggests a systemic instructional challenge, not simply a question of one teacher's effort or competence.</p>\n<p>The student I interviewed had recently been reclassified after previously being chronically absent. He explained that he missed much of the preceding year because he disliked his courses and relationships with teachers. Although he spoke English fluently, his English learner designation from public elementary school had remained in place through private middle school. On entering Riverview, he was assigned to ELD classes. He described feeling discouraged, depressed, and disconnected in that placement. The English learner coordinator subsequently helped him complete reclassification, and he entered mainstream English at the end of that year. He reported attending more consistently and enjoying both his classes and teachers afterward. His experience made timely reclassification and appropriate scheduling important questions for this inquiry. It does not establish that all English learners with frequent absences have the same placement problem.</p>\n<p>The attendance counselor described how a parent's substance use and homelessness had affected one student's ability to reach school. The student and his parents were living in a vehicle. On some mornings he awoke in a different city, far from campus, because the family had moved overnight. After learning of the circumstances, the counselor contacted the child welfare agency, and the student subsequently entered foster care. The counselor used this example to explain how traumatic and unstable living circumstances can interfere with daily attendance and a student's connection to school. I understood it as an account of a serious access barrier, while recognizing that it does not indicate how many English learners experience that barrier.</p>\n<p>The source choices were deliberate. The teacher could describe instructional conditions that an attendance report cannot show, while the student could explain the meaning of a placement experience. The counselor understood the coordination of attendance and family assistance, and the parent understood community travel conditions. Comparing these perspectives helped me avoid relying on only the school office or only a classroom account.</p>\n<p>The ELAC parent added a community transportation perspective. Two bus routes had formerly served the school area at roughly fifteen-minute intervals. Following a service change, families depended on one route with less frequent service and stops farther from some homes. Students sometimes faced long waits or could not board as expected. For families without the resources for private transportation, missing a bus could mean remaining home rather than walking three to five miles. This interview broadened my understanding of the conditions surrounding attendance. Taken together, the accounts suggest that instructional continuity, appropriate placement, housing stability, and transportation all merit examination. They offer distinct explanations to investigate rather than a single explanation that can be assumed to apply to every English learner.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Organize each source around its perspective, finding, contribution to the attendance inquiry, and limitation.</li><li>Qualify the placement conclusion as an individual account and identify the further evidence needed for a broader claim.</li><li>Explain how sources were selected and how collection arrangements supported relevant, accessible participation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / IV.3 / Connecting qualitative and quantitative findings","url":"#cycle/c1/A3","html":"<span id=\"a-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.3 / Connecting qualitative and quantitative findings</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tWhat patterns and/or trends related to the California state indicator and student group equity issues did you find in the qualitative data? How do they relate to the quantitative data patterns and/or trends you identified?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Move from individual accounts to patterns across the qualitative evidence. Identify which sources support each pattern, then explain how it relates to the numerical trend. Include evidence that complicates your explanation. The task is to connect different kinds of evidence, not repeat every interview or assume that all sources confirm the same story.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubrics 1.2 and 1.3</h3><ul><li>Rubric 1.2: analyze relationships among qualitative findings and quantitative patterns.</li><li>Rubric 1.3: integrate the findings into a clear understanding of the group's equity issue.</li><li>A stronger synthesis explains both supporting evidence and the limits of the proposed interpretation.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Full Rubric 1.2</a> / <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Three related concerns emerged when I compared the interviews. The first involved students' opportunities to develop English and progress through their courses. The newcomer teacher described frequent changes in class membership and repeated review that made instruction difficult to sustain. The wider concern was that some students continued to struggle with communication, reclassification, and other content courses after several years of ELD instruction. The ELPI figures add a related question: approximately 48% of English learners advanced a reported proficiency level on average across the three years. The remaining students cannot simply be described as learning nothing, because maintaining a reporting category may include growth within that category. Nevertheless, the combination of reported classroom interruptions and the progression figures makes instructional continuity worth examining.</p>\n<p>A second concern was connection to school. Newcomers may find it difficult to build relationships when they cannot easily communicate with teachers or classmates. The recently reclassified student's account showed a different experience: feeling confined to an inappropriate placement despite speaking English fluently. His frustration and disengagement suggest that belonging can be affected by placement as well as by language access. The approximately 40% average extracurricular participation rate for English learners provides another reason to ask how students become involved in school life. It does not establish that the interviewed student, newcomers, and nonparticipants are the same students.</p>\n<p>The third concern involved material conditions outside school. The attendance counselor described housing instability, while the ELAC parent explained reduced access to public transportation. Both accounts point to difficulties reaching campus, regardless of a student's interest in learning. Students managing these circumstances may have to prioritize immediate family needs and basic stability. The roughly 50% average chronic absence rate among English learners makes these accounts relevant to the inquiry, but it does not quantify how common each barrier is. Together, the three concerns connect language-learning opportunities, belonging, and access to attendance. They also show why an explanation based entirely on student motivation would leave important parts of the evidence unaddressed.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Name each theme and identify the specific accounts supporting or complicating it.</li><li>Correct the interpretation of ELPI categories before connecting them to instructional experience.</li><li>Explain the relationship to the numerical pattern without claiming that separate datasets identify the same students.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / IV.4 / Defining the gap and using research","url":"#cycle/c1/A4","html":"<span id=\"a-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.4 / Defining the gap and using research</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">4.\tDefine the equity gap you identified for a student group through your quantitative and qualitative data analyses. Discuss related research and explain how this research informs and/or supports your equity gap analysis finding for the California state indicator and student group you identified.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>State precisely what the equity gap is for the selected group and indicator. Bring together the numerical disparity and the qualitative findings that help interpret it. Then explain how relevant research informs that interpretation. Research should contribute an idea or finding you can apply and examine, not serve as a quotation added after the conclusion.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><ul><li>Define the group's equity gap through integrated quantitative and qualitative analysis.</li><li>Explain patterns and the relationship between resources, access, and outcomes.</li><li>Discuss the research required by the prompt and explain its relevance to the selected group and indicator.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The equity concern at Riverview High is the sustained difference in chronic absenteeism between English learners and the school as a whole. Over the three reporting years, the rounded averages are approximately 50% and 35%, respectively, a difference of about 15 percentage points. This pattern calls for examining whether the supports available to English learners fit the different circumstances within that population.</p>\n<p>Finn and colleagues (2023) discuss the importance of considering long-term English learners and newcomers separately when studying secondary-school attendance. In addition to common reasons for missing school, such as illness, moving, or reduced academic motivation, attendance may reflect a mismatch between students' needs and the services they receive. The interview accounts make that distinction useful here. Newcomers may need help understanding instruction while also adjusting to a new country, limited family income, or housing insecurity. Language access and connections to social services could both matter to their ability to attend regularly.</p>\n<p>Long-term English learners may face a different set of concerns. The student interview raises questions about reclassification support and course placement. When students remain in language-development courses that they experience as inappropriate, their schedules can also restrict elective choices or participation in athletics. Taking two English periods each year may reduce access to the parts of school they find most engaging. These are potential mechanisms to investigate, rather than effects established for the entire group by one interview.</p>\n<p>Understanding those differences is necessary when considering how support systems can be developed and maintained over time.</p>\n<p>The research discussion of reclassification is relevant because students designated as fluent English proficient generally enter a more mainstream educational program and may encounter fewer forms of tracking, labeling, and exclusion associated with continued English learner designation. Carlson and colleagues (2016) are cited in that discussion of academic outcomes, while the source also acknowledges that findings are not uniformly positive. This literature helps frame questions about services; it does not supply a local estimate of their effect on attendance. The two broad directions for further inquiry are therefore newcomer access to instruction and social support, and long-term English learners' access to appropriate reclassification and programming. Sustained systems would need to respond to both, with local evidence showing which students need which forms of assistance.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Define the disparity with the selected group, indicator, years, and correct numerical units.</li><li>Connect each interpretive claim to an actual quantitative or qualitative finding.</li><li>Explain the research connection and retain uncertainty where local evidence is incomplete.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / IV.5 / Connecting the gap to school commitments","url":"#cycle/c1/A5","html":"<span id=\"a-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / IV.5 / Connecting the gap to school commitments</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">5.\tHow is the equity gap you identified for a student group at your school related to specific components of the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Compare the equity-gap findings with specific school commitments documented in Part A III. Explain what the school promises for this population, what the evidence suggests about students' experiences, and where the two align or appear to differ. Repeating the mission or stating that the gap conflicts with it leaves the comparison unfinished.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><ul><li>Connect the selected group's equity gap to specific school commitments.</li><li>Use quantitative and qualitative findings to explain the relationship.</li><li>For Level 4, provide a thorough synthesis of the disparity and the conditions that help interpret it.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Full Rubric 1.3</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Riverview's vision commits the school to an individualized education that prepares students for college, careers, or both. The English learner experiences described in this inquiry raise questions about how fully that commitment is realized. Under the district's newer programming policy, placement follows grade level rather than language proficiency. That arrangement may make it harder to match ELD instruction to individual needs. Reclassification assistance also needs examination for whether it responds to the particular requirements each student has yet to meet.</p>\n<p>Students who speak English fluently but remain classified as English learners may experience their assigned courses as limiting. The student interview illustrates how that frustration can be associated with disengagement and absence. Newcomers face a different concern: after several years of high school, limited English proficiency or uncertainty about college readiness may lead them to question the value of completing school and to consider other future options. These possibilities connect directly to the mission's emphasis on a secure, personal, small-school learning environment that addresses academic and social-emotional needs.</p>\n<p>The attendance disparity remained across the three years despite existing school systems. That persistence warrants reviewing how individualized support reaches English learners, but it does not by itself demonstrate that every service had no effect. The school needs to examine what was offered, who received it, and whether the support matched different subgroup circumstances. A more coordinated approach to placement, reclassification, and attendance assistance would help the school assess its progress toward the mission. The central comparison is between the promised individualized experience and the instructional and access difficulties reported by students, staff, and families.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Pair each relevant commitment with the particular finding that helps assess it.</li><li>Replace claims of no effect with an evidence-based account of the unresolved access or outcome concern.</li><li>Identify the service or implementation evidence needed to distinguish planned support from support received.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / I.1 / Identifying potential contributing factors","url":"#cycle/c1/B1","html":"<span id=\"b-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-b\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.1 / Identifying potential contributing factors</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tWhat potential contributing factors are suggested by the data you have collected and analyzed that may have created or added to the equity gap you identified for a student group at your school?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Identify plausible conditions that may have created or contributed to the single equity gap. Draw each factor from the Part A findings and explain the school practice, arrangement, or process involved. 'Potential' matters: the evidence should justify investigating the factor without pretending that the inquiry has proved its causal effect.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><ul><li>Use the equity analysis to identify relevant contributing factors, including institutional or structural factors.</li><li>Connect factors to supporting inquiry evidence and relevant research across Part B.</li><li>For Level 4, explain several factors in detail using supporting evidence from Step 1.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Full Rubric 1.4</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The Step 1 analysis identified a continuing attendance disparity for English learners at Riverview High. Their rounded three-year chronic absence rate is approximately 50%, compared with 35% for the school overall. This difference of about 15 percentage points led me to consider three possible contributing conditions, using both the numerical evidence and the interviews. I distinguish conditions suggested by the evidence from causes that would require further verification.</p>\n<p>The first possible factor is structural: newcomers may not have consistent access to demanding instruction that is appropriately supported for their language development. The ELD teacher described limited preparation for the newcomer course and difficulty maintaining a coherent pace while students enter throughout the year. Frequent arrivals require her to revisit content and welcome new learners while continuing instruction for students already present. Her relative inexperience and the demands of the enrollment pattern may affect the learning opportunities available in that classroom. The ELPI figures, with about 48% of English learners advancing a reported level on average, provide a related reason to examine instructional support. They do not measure this teacher's competence or establish that the students maintaining a level made no progress. My concern is that limited access to English can also make other courses difficult, leading students to fall behind in credits. Academic frustration may then weaken their connection to school or make employment seem more immediately worthwhile than continuing their education.</p>\n<p>The second possible factor concerns an institutional decision: the revised district English learner course-placement policy. The former long-term English learner course supplemented secondary English language arts and supported English-fluent students in meeting reclassification requirements. That course was removed, and students are now assigned to ELD courses by grade rather than by proficiency. The resulting classrooms include a wider range of language needs. Some students may interpret work below their perceived proficiency as a remedial placement and feel that the school underestimates their abilities. The recently reclassified student described precisely that reaction. He associated his ELD assignment with feeling unintelligent, reduced self-esteem, disengagement, and frequent absence. The survey's approximately 49% favorable average on enjoyable learning raises a related question about classroom experience, although the other 51% cannot be assumed to be the students who are absent. His account supports examining the placement process and differentiated instruction rather than generalizing his experience to everyone.</p>\n<p>The third possible factor is structural access to school, particularly housing instability and unreliable public transportation. The attendance counselor described newly arrived students whose families face unstable employment and difficult family relationships. In the example she shared, homelessness made reaching campus unpredictable. Such experiences can interrupt attendance and make it difficult to sustain relationships with classmates or continuity in coursework. The ELAC parent described a separate but related barrier: fewer bus routes, less frequent service, and stops farther from some homes. Students unable to arrange another ride may remain home after a missed or delayed bus.</p>\n<p>The distinction among these factors matters for planning. Professional learning could address aspects of instruction, while a placement policy requires attention to institutional decisions. Housing and transportation concerns require coordination with services beyond a classroom. Treating them as interchangeable would obscure the different responsibilities and resources involved in responding to students.</p>\n<p>These access conditions also raise questions about participation beyond the school day. English learners reported extracurricular participation averaging about 40%, compared with 49% for students overall. Students who must leave immediately to secure transportation, or who are absent altogether, may have fewer opportunities to join clubs and sports. However, the survey does not reveal the reason for each student's nonparticipation. Together, the three possible factors concern instructional access, placement and reclassification, and the practical ability to attend and participate. They suggest where the school could investigate its systems, while leaving open how strongly each condition contributes to the overall attendance disparity.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Trace each potential factor to a named finding from Part A.</li><li>Replace the unsupported training judgment with the documented question about continuity and instructional support.</li><li>Explain the institutional or structural process involved and identify the evidence needed to test the proposed explanation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / I.2 / Explaining the impact of the factors","url":"#cycle/c1/B2","html":"<span id=\"b-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.2 / Explaining the impact of the factors</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tHow do these specific contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, impact student learning or well-being for the student group?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how each identified factor could affect this group's learning or well-being. Describe the pathway from the condition to a student experience and its possible consequence. Keep the explanation tied to Part A evidence; naming a factor again or saying it causes the gap does not explain its impact.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><ul><li>Explain how the identified institutional or structural factors relate to the group's equity issue.</li><li>Support proposed effects with the Step 1 findings and relevant research.</li><li>For Level 4, develop detailed explanations of several factors and their contribution to disparities.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Full Rubric 1.4</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The institutional and structural conditions identified in the inquiry may affect both learning and well-being. They offer possible explanations for the approximately 15-percentage-point difference in the rounded chronic absence averages, but their individual effects have not been measured. The first concern is newcomers' access to instruction that helps them develop English while completing the rest of their education. Students who still struggle with the language after two years of ELD may find graduation courses increasingly difficult and may need to recover credits from earlier failures. Difficulty in English therefore extends beyond language-development classes into other subject areas. Repeated academic frustration can reduce confidence and make regular attendance seem less worthwhile.</p>\n<p>The second concern is the newer placement policy and the instructional preparation accompanying it. Teachers had limited time to prepare for classes containing students at very different proficiency levels. Without sufficiently differentiated tasks and language supports, instruction appropriate for some students may leave others either overwhelmed or insufficiently challenged. Students may also miss targeted assistance with the particular reclassification criteria they have not met. Feeling misplaced or overlooked can affect self-esteem and belonging. The student's account connects this experience with disengagement, although it remains an individual account rather than evidence of the policy's effect on every English learner.</p>\n<p>The third concern involves housing and transportation. Uncertainty about where to sleep or whether there will be enough food can make immediate survival more pressing than school assignments. These circumstances may bring substantial stress and interrupt routines that support learning. Unpredictable bus service creates a different daily burden: students may not know whether they will arrive on time or be able to reach campus at all. Repeated travel difficulties can increase anxiety and make staying home seem easier than attempting the journey.</p>\n<p>The combined concerns are therefore academic, social, and emotional. Language barriers can limit success in several courses; inappropriate placement can undermine confidence; and economic instability can prevent attendance regardless of motivation. These conditions may reinforce one another, weakening students' sense that they belong and can succeed at school. Understanding their effects requires attention to what students report, what support they actually receive, and how their attendance and learning develop over time. The evidence establishes an ongoing disparity and reasons to investigate these pathways, rather than a measured causal contribution from each factor.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain one evidence-supported pathway for each factor.</li><li>Remove the assertion that the identified factors produced the whole numerical disparity.</li><li>Distinguish attendance-access barriers from assumptions about students' motivation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / I.3 / Using research to examine factors","url":"#cycle/c1/B3","html":"<span id=\"b-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.3 / Using research to examine factors</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tCite research related to your findings regarding contributing factors that may have created or added to the equity gap you have identified for the student group.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use research to examine the contributing factors identified from your findings. For each relevant study, explain what it found, how that finding relates to your factor, and why the connection is appropriate for this group and setting. Embed citations in the response as directed by the template and handbook.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><ul><li>Cite research relevant to the potential contributing factors.</li><li>Explain the connection between the research, local findings, and selected equity gap.</li><li>Support increasingly detailed understanding of the factors rather than adding citations without analysis.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Full Rubric 1.4</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The research considered in this inquiry provides context for the three possible contributing factors. For instructional access, Santibanez and Gandara (2018), drawing on Rueda and Stillman (2012), describe preparing teachers for increasingly diverse classrooms as a longstanding challenge. They emphasize the particular demands of secondary schooling: English learners face complex academic content and language expectations while having relatively little time to acquire the skills needed for graduation. This is relevant to newcomers at Riverview, who may expect to learn English quickly enough to participate fully in their new school. The teacher's account of frequent enrollment and interrupted pacing makes preparation and instructional support worth examining. The research does not establish that this particular teacher lacks competence, but it clarifies why the demands of her assignment matter. When language development and coursework become difficult together, students may become discouraged and their connection to school may weaken.</p>\n<p>A second line of research concerns placement and access to academic content. Callahan and colleagues (2010), discussing earlier work by Harklau, Linquanti, and MacSwan and Rolstad, explain how English learner placement can delay access to subject matter until students are judged sufficiently proficient. Students can then be expected to succeed in content they had fewer opportunities to study while learning English. That problem is relevant to the student interviewed here. His English learner designation continued through private middle school, and he entered high school without having completed reclassification. He described being distressed by an ELD assignment despite speaking English fluently. The extra English period felt restrictive, and he associated the placement with reduced self-confidence, disengagement, and absence. The literature helps explain why placement deserves examination, while his account identifies a local experience rather than demonstrating the district policy's overall effect.</p>\n<p>The third research connection concerns socioeconomic conditions. Santibanez, Gottfried, and Freeman (2024), referencing Attendance Works and Everyone Graduates Center (2017), discuss circumstances associated with absence among English-classified students. These include poverty, food insecurity, health concerns, transportation difficulties, limited adult supervision, and responsibilities such as caring for siblings or other relatives. Older students may be especially likely to miss school because of family obligations. Riverview serves a low-income immigrant community, and the scenario identifies approximately 30% of its overall enrollment as living in poverty. The counselor's and parent's interviews offer local examples of housing and transportation barriers consistent with that broader discussion. The alignment supports further inquiry but does not tell us how many local absences arise from each circumstance.</p>\n<p>The National Center for Homeless Education (2022) also draws attention to students whose homelessness and chronic absence overlap. Its reporting identifies unaccompanied students as approximately 9% of students experiencing homelessness in each year since 2015-2016, and migratory students as approximately 1% since at least 2014-2015. It also describes small annual increases, no greater than one percentage point, in the English learner share of students experiencing homelessness between 2014-2015 and 2017-2018. These figures concern reported populations and should not be treated as estimates for Riverview.</p>\n<p>For this reason, the school needs a receptive approach at enrollment as well as continuing follow-up. A welcoming first contact can begin a relationship through which a student later feels able to describe a difficulty affecting attendance.</p>\n<p>Newcomer enrollment at the school changes in response to social conditions beyond the school's control. The practical implication is to establish a welcoming reception and a reliable connection to assistance as soon as students enroll. Students facing unstable circumstances need to know which adults they can approach and whether school is a dependable place to seek help. Across the three research connections, I see reasons to examine instructional preparation, placement, and access supports together. I would use local evidence to determine how these broader findings apply to particular students and to avoid assuming that a published association proves the cause of the school's attendance gap.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Verify each cited study and identify its relevant finding, population, and setting.</li><li>Explain which factor the finding supports and which Part A evidence supplies the local connection.</li><li>Qualify the application where the research setting or evidence differs from this school.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / I.4 / Identifying the educational need","url":"#cycle/c1/B4","html":"<span id=\"b-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / I.4 / Identifying the educational need</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">4.\tIdentify areas of educational need related to the single equity gap for a group of students (e.g., the school needs additional supports or materials, necessary specialists or counselors, after-school programs for students).</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Identify what the selected group needs in order to experience more equitable learning or well-being. Describe the unmet condition indicated by the inquiry before naming a program. Supports, staffing, materials, or opportunities may be relevant, but explain the need they would address and its relationship to the single equity gap.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.5</h3><ul><li>Draw the educational need from the equity analysis and contributing factors.</li><li>Keep the need responsive to the identified student group.</li><li>Establish a focused basis for the feasible problem statement evaluated by Rubric 1.5.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Full Rubric 1.5</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Addressing the attendance disparity requires school supports that respond to the instructional, institutional, and access conditions identified in the inquiry. I propose four connected areas of need.</p>\n<p>First, Riverview needs a committed group of ELD teachers who want to develop their practice with English learners. This professional learning community would study research-informed differentiation, scaffolding, and culturally and linguistically responsive teaching. Peer mentors, instructional coaches, and the bilingual program coordinator could guide that learning. The intended benefit is more consistent access to meaningful instruction, helping students experience academic success and see value in attending school regularly.</p>\n<p>Second, students need focused reclassification assistance. The English learner coordinator or an instructional coach could use ELPAC domain results to identify the specific skills keeping students from meeting the criteria. A Tier 2 pull-out program would provide small-group support once or twice weekly during the semester before the spring assessment. This would give long-term English learners a clearer path toward reclassification and show that adults are attending to their individual progress.</p>\n<p>Third, the school needs culturally and linguistically relevant extended-learning experiences. Visits to locations around students' new city could provide opportunities to develop English through social interaction outside formal classroom tasks. Such experiences would broaden learning, create enjoyable shared activities, and strengthen students' participation in school life.</p>\n<p>These supports would be coordinated around students rather than offered as disconnected programs. The teacher group addresses everyday learning, the intervention addresses specific language needs, and the extended opportunities and attendance team address connection and access.</p>\n<p>Finally, attendance counselors need support to organize intervention teams. Teachers, academic counselors, school-climate advocates, parent volunteers, and administrators could each take responsibility for particular students with frequent absences. Team members would contact home when a student is absent, welcome the student back, and hold conversations that build trust and reveal barriers. They would also connect students with resources suited to those circumstances. Together, these actions would make attendance support more personal and continuous. Their effectiveness would need to be monitored through both implementation records and student outcomes, with attention to whether the intended assistance actually reaches the students who need it.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>State the unmet educational or access condition in plain language.</li><li>Explain the Part A evidence and contributing factors that support that need.</li><li>Reserve detailed program design for Part C after the problem has been defined.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / II.5 / Developing a feasible problem statement","url":"#cycle/c1/B5","html":"<span id=\"b-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / II.5 / Developing a feasible problem statement</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">5.\tProblem Statement: Describe the equity gap that needs to be addressed at the chosen school for the California state indicator and the identified student group’s area of need (achievement and/or well-being).</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe the single equity gap the school needs to address, keeping the indicator, student group, and learning or well-being need explicit. Draw the statement from the inquiry and contributing factors. Explain why the problem is responsive to the group and feasible to address within the school's actual context.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.5</h3><ul><li>Develop a feasible problem statement from the equity analysis and contributing factors.</li><li>Make the statement clearly responsive to the selected group's learning or well-being.</li><li>For Level 4, explain responsiveness and feasibility in the school's culture and context.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Full Rubric 1.5</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>My equity analysis identified a persistent difference on the chronic absenteeism indicator at Riverview High. English learners averaged approximately 50% across the three years, compared with approximately 35% for the overall school population. The rounded difference is about 15 percentage points. Interviews added several concerns to the numerical pattern: the continuity of English language instruction, students' connection to school, and the effects of difficult socioeconomic circumstances. These findings give the attendance disparity a more specific local context, while leaving questions about the extent and contribution of each condition.</p>\n<p>The analysis suggests three areas for action. Newcomers need dependable access to instruction that supports language development and challenging academic work. Long-term English learners need appropriate placement and assistance with reclassification under the revised district policy. Students facing housing or transportation difficulties need practical and relational support that makes attendance possible. The persistence of the gap indicates an unresolved problem; it does not, by itself, establish that every existing school response has failed. School records and student experiences would be needed to determine which services have reached whom.</p>\n<p>English learners need educators who are willing to strengthen their professional knowledge and invest in their achievement. Improved instructional support should help students develop language, participate in other subjects, and experience pride in belonging to a school that takes their learning seriously. They also need adults who will organize targeted reclassification assistance early in high school. The requirements become more demanding in the upper grades, making ninth- and tenth-grade support especially relevant. Earlier progress may help students avoid feeling trapped in a program and give them a clearer reason to continue toward a diploma.</p>\n<p>Students with repeated absences also need a trusted adult who checks on their well-being, communicates with family, and helps set achievable goals. Balfanz and Byrnes' 2013 study on combating chronic absenteeism discusses quality mentoring within a schoolwide effort as a promising attendance and achievement strategy. That research supports considering mentoring here, while local evidence would be necessary to judge its results. The concern is that students' difficulties can remain unnoticed when monitoring, relationships, and intervention responsibilities are fragmented.</p>\n<p>The proposed response therefore has several linked components. An ELD teacher group would pursue professional learning suited to the range of language needs in its classrooms. A Tier 2 pull-out program would provide intensive, individualized assistance with the reclassification skills that students still need to demonstrate. Extended-learning opportunities would create social settings for using English and connecting with school. A designated group of staff mentors would maintain contact with students who are chronically absent and help them obtain appropriate support. These actions respond to the identified needs rather than treating attendance as a matter of reminders alone.</p>\n<p>The proposal fits several existing resources in Riverview's context. A bilingual coordinator could organize professional learning for interested ELD teachers. A part-time intervention coordinator could work with that coordinator to design and operate the Tier 2 program. The school also has three attendance counselors who commonly supervise five or more interns. They could organize mentoring and recruit willing school personnel to participate. This staffing makes the proposal plausible, although schedules, responsibilities, and available time would still need agreement before implementation. The work would require coordination among people whose roles already touch different parts of the problem.</p>\n<p>The available roles are an important part of the feasibility argument. The proposal does not begin by assuming an entirely new department or staff. It asks how existing coordination, counseling, and instructional responsibilities might be aligned more deliberately. That alignment would still require agreement about workload and priorities so that responsibility for English learner support is clear and sustainable.</p>\n<p>My problem statement is that English learners experience a sustained attendance disparity while instructional, placement, and access supports may not consistently match their varied needs. The school needs a coordinated, individualized response that strengthens teaching, advances reclassification, creates meaningful participation, and connects students with trusted adults. These changes align with the school's commitment to a personalized education that prepares each student for the future. The intended outcome is a reduction in the chronic absence difference, with evidence collected to determine whether the proposed supports are implemented, who benefits, and what needs revision. Closing the gap remains a goal to evaluate rather than a result that can be promised in advance.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Reduce the statement to one evidence-supported educational need and a feasible school-level scope.</li><li>Support feasibility with actual responsibilities, time, resources, authority, and constraints.</li><li>Use percentage-point units and describe attendance improvement as an intended outcome to assess.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / 1 / Describing proposed strategies","url":"#cycle/c1/C1","html":"<span id=\"strategy-map\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-c\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 1 / Describing proposed strategies</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tFor the student equity gap you identified in Step 1, describe the potential strategies you identified for equitable school improvement and how they are to be applied.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe the strategies you propose and how each would operate at the school. Connect them to the equity gap and explain the activities, responsibilities, and application well enough for the reader to understand the proposal. This is a plan for improvement; distinguish proposed actions from actions already completed.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Connecting the proposal to the evidence</summary><p>Keep the reasoning visible without repeating the full investigation. A proposed strategy responds to a supported finding or a clearly identified condition to investigate; it is not evidence that improvement has already occurred.</p><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Connection</th><th scope=\"col\">What to explain</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Finding and potential factor</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">Identify the evidence and the school-related condition it suggests. Keep an untested explanation provisional.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Proposed action and access</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">Explain what would change for the selected student group and why that change addresses the need.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Feedback and refinement</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">Identify who examined feasibility, what they contributed, and how the proposal changed.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Connection\">Remaining uncertainty</td><td data-label=\"What to explain\">State what would need to be examined before claiming that the response works.</td></tr></tbody></table></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><ul><li>Propose strategies informed by the analysis, factors, and problem statement.</li><li>Explain how the strategies are applied and aligned with school commitments.</li><li>For Level 4, show a strategic and contextually responsive approach to the equity issue.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Full Rubric 1.6</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The attendance analysis shows a rounded three-year average chronic absence rate of approximately 50% for English learners and 35% for all students at Riverview High. I selected two strategies that respond to the resulting disparity of about 15 percentage points and can draw on resources already present at the school. One addresses classroom learning opportunities; the other addresses personal connections and access to assistance.</p>\n<p>The first strategy is an ELD teacher learning group focused on strengthening instruction. It responds to the possible structural factor involving newcomers' access to challenging, well-supported learning. The language-progress figures, including the approximately 48% average advancing a reported level, led me to examine instructional opportunities alongside attendance. That figure does not mean that everyone else made no progress, but it reinforces the need to understand how students are supported. When students struggle in courses and accumulate missing graduation credits, frustration may contribute to disengagement. The proposed group would help teachers examine that concern through their own practice.</p>\n<p>The bilingual coordinator would lead meetings twice each month during the fall and once each month in the spring. Before and between meetings, the coordinator would assemble resources on specially designed academic instruction in English, culturally and linguistically responsive pedagogy, and translanguaging. These materials would guide collaborative planning rather than function only as a reading list. Teachers would establish an instructional goal, select a strategy, and use it with students before the next meeting. They would then bring evidence of engagement and learning to discuss what happened.</p>\n<p>The group would also examine attendance and academic information to consider how classroom conditions respond to different learners. Its inquiry would follow a recurring plan, implementation, study, and adjustment process: decide what to try, use it, evaluate the evidence, and share the findings. I would first present the proposal to the principal for approval and then introduce it at an ELD department meeting to recruit interested teachers. The purpose is to build professional capacity and improve the quality of students' daily experience. More meaningful instruction may strengthen engagement and attendance, but those outcomes would need to be checked rather than assumed.</p>\n<p>The second strategy is a mentoring program with regular well-being check-ins for students who are chronically absent. It responds to the housing and transportation barriers described in the qualitative evidence. A dependable adult relationship could strengthen contact with families, help students feel connected to campus, and make it easier to identify a problem before it repeatedly interrupts attendance. The program would combine relationship-building with access to practical resources.</p>\n<p>An attendance counselor would lead the program and introduce it at the monthly meeting of staff whose assignments are outside the classroom. The counselor would present the attendance evidence, identify students needing individualized intervention, and explain the responsibilities and intended outcomes. Volunteers would each be matched with one student. A one-page conversation guide would help mentors ask about well-being, attendance, and barriers, while the counselor would remain available to coordinate school and community services.</p>\n<p>The two proposals are intended to complement one another. An improved classroom experience cannot by itself remove a housing or transportation barrier, and a supportive mentor cannot replace appropriate language instruction. Their connection is the student experience: one strengthens access to learning during the day, while the other helps identify circumstances that interrupt participation. Coordinating these efforts would keep the problem statement visible across the different adults involved.</p>\n<p>Mentors would meet as a group once a month throughout the school year. They would report progress or continuing difficulty, raise questions, and seek help with concerns beyond their roles. Their conversations with students could reveal housing, transportation, or other needs that are not visible in an attendance percentage. Connecting students and families with suitable assistance could reduce some of the stressors that interfere with participation. Together, the two strategies address the problem statement through stronger instruction and more individualized support. Their implementation would need to be documented so the school can distinguish the intended benefits from changes actually observed.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Correct the quantitative interpretation supporting the instructional proposal.</li><li>Explain why the selected strategies fit the supported need and which concerns they leave unresolved.</li><li>Specify the route from identifying a barrier to obtaining assistance and the responsibilities requiring agreement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / 2 / Explaining how strategies address the problem","url":"#cycle/c1/C2","html":"<span id=\"c-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 2 / Explaining how strategies address the problem</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tExplain how your potential strategies address the equity gap described in your problem statement.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why each proposed strategy is expected to address the problem statement. Describe the mechanism: what would change in students' access or experience, and how that change relates to the identified need. Use relevant evidence to justify the expectation without presenting a hoped-for outcome as an established result.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><ul><li>Explain how strategies respond to the identified problem and supporting findings.</li><li>Keep the mechanism of improvement relevant to the selected group and school.</li><li>Distinguish the proposal's rationale from demonstrated implementation results.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Full Rubric 1.6</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The proposed ELD teacher group addresses the problem statement by investing in the educators who work with English learners every day. Under the bilingual coordinator's guidance, teachers would study and practice ways to organize classrooms, build relationships, scaffold tasks, and differentiate instruction. These everyday choices communicate whether students' needs are recognized. When the classroom experience is responsive, students may be more likely to feel that they belong and that their education matters. When it is not, frustration can add to an existing pattern of disengagement.</p>\n<p>Strengthening teacher preparation is a concern beyond Riverview. Santibanez and Gandara (2018) examine the limited availability of teachers fully authorized to teach English learners and discuss teachers' expressed need for coaching, observing colleagues, and collaborative professional learning. That finding supports the proposed combination of shared planning, practice, and review. A teacher group could provide more sustained assistance than isolated presentations because members would examine what happens when they apply a strategy. The intended result is improved instruction, with increased engagement and attendance as outcomes to investigate. The research does not guarantee those outcomes for this school.</p>\n<p>The mentoring and well-being program addresses a different part of the problem. Riverview has coordinators, counselors, itinerant staff, and assistant principals whose work could support a targeted Tier 3 intervention under an attendance counselor's leadership. Matching these adults with individual students would use existing staff relationships and knowledge. Students facing traumatic or unstable circumstances could have someone who notices their absence, listens to their concerns, and encourages them through difficult days.</p>\n<p>Mentors could also identify family needs early enough for the school to arrange timely help. A trusting relationship may reveal a transportation, safety, or housing concern that a formal attendance notice does not uncover. The counselor would coordinate the appropriate resources, while the mentor maintains the personal connection. This combines emotional support with practical assistance rather than expecting encouragement alone to remove barriers.</p>\n<p>The approach depends on coordinated follow-through. The mentor maintains contact, while the counselor helps arrange services that fall outside a mentor's role. This division makes the relationship more useful when the student identifies a practical need.</p>\n<p>Balfanz and Byrnes' New York City study (2013) provides a relevant research connection. In the quarter of participating schools showing the strongest effects, chronically absent students supported by Success Mentors gained more than a month of attendance on average. That result is specific to those schools and should not be presented as the expected gain at Riverview. It nevertheless supports investigating a coordinated mentoring approach. My rationale is that students may be more willing and able to continue attending when adults respond to their circumstances and help them access support. Local attendance records and implementation evidence would show whether the proposed relationships are contributing to that goal.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain the proposed change in student experience for each strategy.</li><li>Verify and contextualize the research, including the restriction to the highest-impact schools.</li><li>State attendance improvement as an outcome to examine and distinguish relational encouragement from material assistance.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / 3 / Connecting strategies to factors and school goals","url":"#cycle/c1/C3","html":"<span id=\"c-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 3 / Connecting strategies to factors and school goals</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tExplain how your potential strategies for improvement address or take into account the potential contributing factors—including institutional and/or structural factors—and how they align with the school’s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Make two connections for each strategy: explain which contributing factor it addresses and how it aligns with a specific school commitment. Show the chain from finding to factor to action to school goal. Explain any important factor the chosen strategies do not address.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><ul><li>Connect strategies to the findings and institutional or structural factors.</li><li>Explain alignment with specific elements of the school's vision, mission, or goals.</li><li>Show why the approach responds to this school's equity issue and context.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Full Rubric 1.6</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The ELD teacher learning group responds to the first proposed contributing factor: inconsistent access to instruction that meets newcomers' language and academic needs. Its immediate goal is to strengthen teaching so students can engage with demanding content and experience progress. A more positive learning experience may support reengagement and attendance. The equity analysis supplied several reasons to consider this approach, including the language-progress pattern and the survey average showing that approximately 49% of English learners regarded classroom learning as enjoyable. These measures raise questions about instruction and experience, but do not establish that students who answered differently are choosing employment or family duties instead of school.</p>\n<p>The interviews add more specific context. The newcomer teacher described the difficulty of sustaining lessons while new students enter throughout the year. The student described an ELD placement that he experienced as inappropriate and discouraging. Both accounts suggest areas for professional learning: planning for changing enrollment, differentiating tasks, and understanding students' perceptions of placement. They do not support attributing the entire attendance disparity to teachers. The proposed group would give teachers a way to examine those conditions together and test responses in their classrooms.</p>\n<p>This strategy aligns with the mission's commitment to developing staff capacity to serve all students. Collaborative study and review of classroom evidence would make that commitment concrete for English learners. The group would also support individualized learning by asking teachers to consider differences in language proficiency and educational experience. The expected attendance benefit follows from an improved daily learning experience, but the school would need evidence to determine whether that benefit occurs.</p>\n<p>The mentoring program responds to the third proposed contributing factor: socioeconomic conditions that interfere with attendance. Its immediate goal is a consistent relationship through which students feel recognized and can obtain help. The counselor's housing account and the parent's transportation account identify barriers that cannot be resolved simply by telling students to attend more often. A mentor could learn about the specific difficulty, communicate with the family, and coordinate assistance through the attendance counselor.</p>\n<p>The extracurricular survey provides another question about connection to school. English learners' participation averaged about 40%, suggesting that many were not engaged in those activities. Students uncertain about where they will sleep or how they will get home may have limited ability to remain for clubs or sports. The survey alone does not identify those circumstances, but the interviews explain why access deserves attention. Mentoring could help students explore available activities while also addressing the conditions that make participation difficult.</p>\n<p>The mission and vision therefore provide more than introductory language. They offer a basis for asking whether each proposed action delivers the kind of support the school says students should receive.</p>\n<p>The program connects with the vision of an individualized education leading toward college and career readiness. Each mentor would attend to one student's academic and personal circumstances rather than provide the same response to every absence. This is a practical expression of the school's stated commitment. Across both strategies, the connection to school goals is therefore specific: develop educators' capacity to improve learning, and provide personal support that helps students participate. The school would assess those commitments through the services delivered and the experiences and outcomes of the students involved.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>For each strategy, identify the finding, factor, proposed action, and specific school commitment.</li><li>Remove unsupported assumptions about students' motives.</li><li>Account for the placement concern and explain how mentors would respond to needs beyond their role or resources.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / 4 / Applying targeted feedback","url":"#cycle/c1/C4","html":"<span id=\"c-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 4 / Applying targeted feedback</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">4.\tDescribe the targeted feedback you received on each proposed strategy from your administrator, supervisor, or other key educational leader(s), and explain how you would adjust your proposed strategies to address the needs of the student group and inform equitable school improvement based on that feedback.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Show what feedback you received on each proposed strategy and what you changed, or would change, because of it. Explain the reason for each decision, including a recommendation you did not adopt. Keep the sequence visible so the reader can follow the proposal from feedback through revision.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.7</h3><ul><li>Apply targeted feedback to strengthen the proposed strategies.</li><li>Explain appropriate adjustments and their implications for the group and school.</li><li>For Level 4, seek further feedback on revised strategies and develop a detailed communication plan across diverse educational partners.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Full Rubric 1.7</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>In the first feedback round, I met with Riverview's principal. I sought his perspective because he had worked with the attendance counselors for two years on reducing chronic absence. He responded favorably to both proposals and suggested ways to make each more useful within the school.</p>\n<p>For the ELD teacher group, he recommended using ELPAC domain results to identify the language skills most in need of support. He also wanted the work to influence instruction beyond ELD classrooms. He suggested that participants collect evidence of the strategies they use and the results they observe, then present that work to the entire faculty at the end of the year. Such a presentation could encourage teachers in other content areas to form a similar group or adopt useful practices. His feedback shifted the proposal toward both targeted planning and wider sharing of classroom evidence.</p>\n<p>For mentoring, the principal recommended adding questions about sports and clubs to the check-in guide. Discussing students' interests could connect them with additional trusted adults and create more reasons to participate in campus life. The intention was to expand belonging through relationships and activities rather than limit every conversation to absence.</p>\n<p>I made several changes after this meeting. For the instructional group, I planned for the bilingual coordinator to have the access needed to create a group within the school's learning management system. That shared space would hold resources, lesson plans, and materials developed or used by participants. Other faculty members could be invited to explore the collection, and faculty meetings could draw attention to it. This would make the group's work accessible across departments and support broader use of language-development strategies. The principal's recommendation about domain-level evidence would also inform the group's instructional priorities.</p>\n<p>For mentoring, I planned to prepare a directory of clubs with meeting times and locations, together with a sports list showing seasons and coach contact information. Mentors could use these resources while discussing a student's interests. Having the information available would make it easier to turn a conversation about belonging into a specific connection on campus.</p>\n<p>In the second feedback round, I shared the revised proposals with an attendance counselor. The counselor noted that students facing economic hardship often think about finding work. She recommended that the ELD group regularly connect learning with workplace skills so students can recognize its practical value. In response, I planned to involve the work-based learning coordinator. That coordinator could arrange guest speakers or field trips connected to skills and topics teachers were addressing. This addition would help relate classroom learning to future opportunities without assuming that all students have the same plans.</p>\n<p>The second round built on the first rather than repeat the original proposal unchanged. I presented the adjustments already made so the counselor could respond to a more developed plan. This sequence helped me see feedback as an ongoing process: one colleague identified instructional sharing and belonging, while another added practical concerns about relevance and staffing.</p>\n<p>The counselor also raised a recruitment concern for mentoring. Staff outside classroom assignments may be reluctant to give up work time, particularly when a new program's demands are uncertain. She suggested inviting classroom teachers as well, with training and resources to mentor one of their own students. I therefore added a faculty-wide presentation in which the counselor and I would share the English learner attendance evidence, explain the mentoring responsibilities, and invite teachers to volunteer. This broadened the potential mentor pool and made the support structure clearer. Across the two rounds, the feedback changed instructional priorities, resource sharing, activity connections, work-related learning opportunities, and recruitment. These were concrete revisions to the proposals, while staffing and implementation arrangements would still need to be confirmed.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Trace each substantive recommendation to a decision, proposal change, and reason.</li><li>Explain how ELPAC domains and implementation evidence would guide the instructional work.</li><li>Distinguish completed proposal revisions from staffing, resources, and activities still awaiting agreement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / 5 / Building participation and anticipating implications","url":"#cycle/c1/C5","html":"<span id=\"c-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / 5 / Building participation and anticipating implications</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">5.\tDescribe steps you would take to create school-level and community educational partner buy-in to the final adjusted set of proposed strategies. What potential implications for proposed strategies do you anticipate encountering as you address the equity gap at the school?</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how different educational partners would become involved in the adjusted proposal and what practical consequences or constraints you anticipate. Specify opportunities for people to influence the plan, not only receive an announcement. Connect likely implementation difficulties to the steps you would take to address them.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.7</h3><ul><li>Provide relevant steps for buy-in and communication.</li><li>Anticipate realistic implications of implementation at this school.</li><li>For Level 4, develop a detailed, workable communication plan involving diverse educational partners, alongside further feedback on revisions.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Full Rubric 1.7</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Successful implementation would require participation from teachers, staff with support assignments, families, and students. I would use the attendance evidence to explain the concern and describe how each proposed strategy responds to it. Communication would need to make both the purpose and the responsibilities understandable so that participation rests on a shared view of the work.</p>\n<p>To recruit ELD teachers, the bilingual coordinator and I would attend an English department meeting. We would present the chronic absence disparity, explain the possible instructional contributing factor, and describe the teacher group's goals and meeting expectations. The invitation would emphasize equitable opportunities for English learners and ask interested teachers to commit to collaborative learning. For mentoring, the attendance counselor and I would present the same attendance concern at a faculty meeting, explain the check-in responsibilities and available support, and invite volunteers from across the school.</p>\n<p>I would also seek the participation of the bilingual coordinator and attendance counselors as program leaders. Offering to share leadership would help distribute planning and follow-up responsibilities. We would agree together on how to organize the work and connect it with the school's commitments to individualized education and staff development. These activities fit aspects of their existing roles, but competing responsibilities and limited time may have prevented similar work from developing. Shared planning could make the proposals more feasible, although the actual time available would need to be established rather than presumed.</p>\n<p>For families, the bilingual coordinator, attendance counselor, and I would arrange an after-school meeting for parents of students in ELD. Invitations would include mailed letters, flyers sent home, and a principal's telephone and email message. A light dinner would be offered. At the meeting, we would explain the attendance figures, discuss the value of consistent participation, and introduce the instructional group and mentoring program. We would ask families to support attendance while also listening to the barriers that make it difficult. That exchange would help distinguish a need for information from a need for practical assistance.</p>\n<p>For students, I would introduce myself during ELD classes and explain that I would return to learn about their classroom experiences. Regular visits would communicate continuing interest in their education. I would make a point of recognizing students with previous attendance difficulties when they are present and encouraging their return. For mentees, I would seek funding for tangible recognition when they reach and sustain agreed attendance goals. Possible incentives include a breakfast treat, a pizza lunch, movie tickets, or school merchandise. Such recognition would accompany personal support rather than replace attention to barriers beyond students' control.</p>\n<p>Several implementation difficulties are foreseeable. Initial enthusiasm may fade without regular follow-up, clear responsibilities, and recognition of participants' efforts. Teachers and mentors may carry out the work unevenly, creating different experiences for students. Staff who already feel overextended may withdraw. The leaders would therefore need to monitor participation and workload, provide support, and discuss inconsistencies before they become established patterns. A collaborative approach should make those concerns visible rather than assume that volunteering guarantees sustained implementation.</p>\n<p>The invitation to participate would need to remain connected with those shared purposes throughout the year. Teachers and support staff should understand how their particular contribution fits the broader response, and families and students should be able to describe their experience of it. That continuing communication can help identify problems early and keep the work from depending only on initial enthusiasm.</p>\n<p>The possible benefits include more consistent attendance, stronger family communication, and better access to school and community resources. Regular examination of evidence could also strengthen decision-making across the school. Recognition events and shared celebrations may help English learners feel that their participation is valued. Coordinating school, home, and community efforts could create a more dependable support network. These benefits remain intended outcomes, and the school would need to review both implementation and student experiences to determine whether they occur. Clear communication, shared responsibility, and attention to the practical demands of participation are central to building lasting support for the two strategies.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Specify when each group can question or change the proposal and how its feedback returns to decisions.</li><li>Provide accessible participation arrangements suited to language and scheduling needs.</li><li>Explain how workload, confidentiality, recruitment, and unequal access would change the implementation plan.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / 1 / Explaining the choice and influence of feedback","url":"#cycle/c1/D1","html":"<span id=\"d-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-d\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / 1 / Explaining the choice and influence of feedback</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">1.\tProvide your rationale for the administrator, supervisor, or educational leader feedback you chose to gather in Step 3 and how the feedback impacted your approach to building positive educational partner buy-in.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why you chose these feedback partners and how their feedback changed your approach to gaining participation. Connect the partner's relevant expertise to a specific recommendation, decision, and lesson for leadership. Praise for a leader's qualities does not by itself explain the effect of the feedback.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><ul><li>Explain the rationale for selecting feedback partners and its effect on the approach to buy-in.</li><li>Refer to specific evidence from the cycle.</li><li>For Level 4, show how school context affects the approach to equity leadership.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Full Rubric 1.8</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I chose Riverview's principal as a feedback partner because of his experience with instructional improvement and attendance interventions. He had been at the school for two years, and I had observed him use his broader educational experience when guiding structural changes. I trusted his judgment and wanted to understand how my proposals fit both the school's needs and the expectations of the district and state. His approach also provided a model of leadership that keeps student needs visible while involving colleagues in decisions.</p>\n<p>Our conversations broadened my understanding of how to build support for change. He endorsed the ELD teacher group in part because it allows teachers to exercise professional judgment while developing their capacity. That helped me see the importance of inviting teachers into a shared inquiry. People may be more willing to participate when they can influence the work and understand its purpose. His recommendation to use ELPAC domain evidence made that purpose more concrete: the group could identify a learning need, select an instructional response, and examine a measurable result. Evidence would help explain why the work is worthwhile rather than leave it appearing to be an additional task based on one person's preference.</p>\n<p>His suggestion about clubs and sports also changed my thinking. The extracurricular survey had raised a question about connection to school, and he showed how mentoring conversations could explore that question with individual students. Asking about interests could connect a student with additional adults and activities while helping the mentor understand what participation means to that student. This reminded me that numerical patterns become more useful when they guide conversations, and that keeping students' experiences central can help educational partners see the value of a proposal.</p>\n<p>I selected the attendance counselor because she works directly with students who are chronically absent and with families experiencing economic hardship. I wanted her judgment about feasibility, not only agreement with the general ideas. Across her career she had implemented both collaboratively developed interventions and district-directed programs. That experience gave her insight into the conditions that encourage or limit staff participation.</p>\n<p>Her advice to connect ELD lessons with workplace skills led me to recognize the work-based learning coordinator as an additional resource. Teachers could receive help arranging speakers or experiences that make the practical value of learning more visible. This connection could strengthen participation in the teacher group because members would have another colleague supporting their efforts. It also widened my view of the resources already available at Riverview.</p>\n<p>Both colleagues also helped me connect participation with support. Teachers asked to change practice need useful resources and an opportunity to exercise judgment. Staff asked to mentor need a clear role and someone to consult. Recognizing those conditions changed how I would invite people into the work.</p>\n<p>For mentoring, the counselor anticipated that staff outside classroom assignments might not have time or willingness to participate. Her suggestion to include classroom teachers changed my recruitment plan. The counselor and I would present the attendance evidence and mentoring structure to the entire faculty, explain the support volunteers would receive, and invite broader participation. I learned that building support can involve changing who is invited, not merely improving the explanation given to the original group. Taken together, the two feedback partners helped me connect evidence, professional autonomy, practical resources, and shared responsibility. Their contributions made the proposals more specific and helped me think about participation as something developed through collaboration rather than secured through an announcement.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use a specific recommendation and decision to explain the contribution of each partner.</li><li>Identify the assumption or leadership practice that changed.</li><li>Distinguish intended participation from actual agreement and reconcile the reflection with Part C.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / 2 / Learning, strengths, and areas for growth","url":"#cycle/c1/D2","html":"<span id=\"d-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / 2 / Learning, strengths, and areas for growth</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">2.\tReflect on what you have learned, including strengths and areas for growth as an equity-driven leader. Then discuss and refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3 as to how you addressed equity needs and equity gaps for the identified group of students at your school.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what you learned about equity leadership, identify strengths and areas for growth, and show how your work addressed this group's equity needs. Support the reflection with specific evidence from Steps 1, 2, or 3. Describe changes in your understanding and decisions, not only the activities you completed or traits you believe you possess.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><ul><li>Reflect on growth in addressing the selected group's equity needs and gaps.</li><li>Use specific cycle evidence to explain strengths and areas for growth.</li><li>For Level 4, demonstrate how social, economic, or cultural context affects the leadership approach.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Full Rubric 1.8</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>This cycle changed the way I approach improvement work. At the beginning, I tended to move quickly from a problem to an interpretation or a proposed solution. Collecting evidence, reading about improvement, and reflecting at each step helped me slow that process. I began to ask what I still needed to understand about the school's social and cultural setting before deciding what should change. Listening to other people made the problem more complex, but it also made my thinking more responsive to the students and adults involved. I now see that an equity focus requires attention to how a system operates, whose experiences are represented, and whose needs may remain less visible.</p>\n<p>During Step 1, I learned to use several sources to develop an understanding of a student population. The attendance figures established a pattern that needed explanation. They did not supply the experiences behind the pattern. Looking carefully at the numbers helped me decide what to ask in interviews and whose perspectives could contribute. Rather than gather qualitative information without a purpose, I tried to use it to investigate questions the numerical evidence left unresolved. This required planning questions that were relevant to each person's role and sensitive to the circumstances they described. The teacher, student, counselor, and parent helped me see different aspects of the same attendance concern.</p>\n<p>This process also changed how I think about the responsibilities of a principal. Examining equity differences should be a recurring part of leadership, not an activity completed only for one project. A leader needs to understand students' progress and the school systems that support or interrupt it. Conversations with teachers, students, and families can help interpret numerical patterns, while those patterns can suggest useful questions for future conversations. In a principal role, I would therefore pay close attention to the concerns and proposals people bring forward. They may reveal conditions that are not apparent in a report. I would use that information to build collaborative responses and to revisit whether those responses are working.</p>\n<p>Step 2 helped me think more carefully about institutional and structural conditions. I needed to distinguish long-established expectations, beliefs, and practices from the arrangements and resources through which work is organized. Riverview has relatively little staff turnover, and some ways of working have become familiar over many years. I began to consider whether those routines could make certain English learner needs less visible. That possibility should be examined with evidence rather than treated as a conclusion about colleagues' intentions. It also means that improvement may require people to reconsider familiar assumptions together. Understanding the school's structures helped me identify resources that could support a feasible response, while understanding its institutional culture helped me anticipate the work involved in gaining participation.</p>\n<p>The course reading that most directly challenged my habits was Bryk and colleagues' Learning to Improve (2015). Their discussion of moving prematurely to solutions helped me recognize a tendency in my own practice. Experience can make a proposed answer come to mind quickly, but familiarity with a problem does not establish that I understand its current form. I need a disciplined pause between noticing a concern and recommending an intervention. The equity analysis gave me practice in that pause: examine the pattern, ask what is uncertain, seek relevant perspectives, and reconsider the explanation. I want evidence-driven inquiry to become a routine part of how I work rather than an additional step I use only when time permits.</p>\n<p>In Step 3, I used a fishbone diagram to organize possible contributing factors. Seeing the factors together helped me consider the wider system rather than focus on a single explanation. The diagram did not prove that each proposed factor caused the gap; it helped me identify relationships that deserved attention. From that analysis, I selected two strategies suited to the school's context: collaborative learning for ELD teachers and individualized mentoring for students with frequent absences. I considered the available personnel, the practical demands of implementation, and research relevant to each approach. This made the proposals more grounded than an immediate response based only on the attendance percentage.</p>\n<p>Feedback from the principal and attendance counselor showed me that an equity-oriented leader must be willing to revise a proposal. Their contributions changed the instructional focus, the resources available to teachers, the mentoring conversation guide, and the recruitment plan. Sharing an idea transparently and allowing others to influence it can strengthen both the idea and participation in carrying it out. I intend to continue practicing these skills through improvement cycles in my current role. My aim is to keep students' needs central, use evidence carefully, remain open while collecting accounts, and use decision processes that give educational partners a meaningful voice.</p>\n<p>I also learned more about my own strengths and limitations. One important realization was how much I rely on prior knowledge when I encounter a problem. Watching the principal respond to everyday school concerns helped me notice a different approach. I paid attention to his choice of language, his effort to remain neutral, and the way he made decision-making visible to others. Because Riverview is a pilot school, democratic participation is especially important to its leadership. I began considering how I could practice those habits now, rather than wait until I hold an administrative position. Observing a colleague's response gave me a concrete way to examine my own.</p>\n<p>I see analysis, critical thinking, and collaboration as strengths I can build on. The proposals reflect my belief that school improvement depends on people working across classrooms and offices. My experience allows me to contribute ideas and help colleagues develop capacity, while I also need to remain willing to learn from perspectives different from mine. The principal and counselor demonstrated how quickly a proposal can improve when another person notices a resource, a constraint, or a student need I had overlooked. Those exchanges reinforced my commitment to shared work. At the same time, describing myself as a strong analyst is not enough; I need to check my interpretations, especially when separate percentages or individual accounts tempt me to make broader claims.</p>\n<p>After learning this inquiry process, I would like to examine other state indicators for English learners, including graduation, college and career readiness, and SBAC proficiency at Riverview. If those analyses reveal further differences, I would bring the findings to the Governance Council so an appropriate committee could investigate them. I would participate in the committee's work and invite additional educational partners whose perspectives are relevant. The purpose would be shared responsibility for understanding the concern and developing a response, not simply presenting a finished solution for approval. This could help keep English learner needs visible across the school's improvement work.</p>\n<p>Understanding context also requires recognizing the difference between a familiar routine and a well-supported explanation of its effects. Longstanding practices may contain useful knowledge as well as limitations. I need to ask colleagues how those practices developed, what purposes they serve, and what evidence they use to judge them. That kind of inquiry can make a proposed change more informed and can show respect for the people who have sustained the school's work.</p>\n<p>The governance and committee meetings give me a setting to practice this discipline. I can listen to how members frame concerns, notice which perspectives enter the discussion, and consider how decisions are connected with student evidence. These experiences can help me develop the patience and contextual understanding that my tendency to move quickly toward solutions sometimes overlooks. They also allow me to contribute as a collaborator while learning from the leadership already present in the school.</p>\n<p>My growth areas follow directly from these experiences. I need to continue interrupting the habit of deciding too soon, and I need a deeper understanding of existing school systems before recommending replacements or additions. Colleagues may already be doing work that I have not yet seen. Recognizing that work is necessary both for an accurate analysis and for respectful collaboration. I have begun attending Governance Council and School Culture Committee meetings to learn how decisions are made and who is involved. Those meetings give me opportunities to observe, contribute, and develop a more informed leadership approach. The cycle has shown me that understanding school culture and context is not background preparation; it is part of the work of identifying an equity concern and deciding how to address it.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Pair each claimed strength or growth area with an event, decision, or finding from the cycle.</li><li>Explain one initial interpretation that changed after considering another perspective.</li><li>Show how the school's context shaped an action and define the professional practice that needs improvement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / 3 / Planning professional learning","url":"#cycle/c1/D3","html":"<span id=\"d-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / 3 / Planning professional learning</h2><section class=\"question\"><p class=\"prompt\">3.\tBased on the analysis of your experiences in Cycle 1, identify specific professional learning goals for yourself and describe future steps for your professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Turn the learning from this cycle into specific professional goals and future steps. Identify the skill you need to develop, what you will do to develop it, and how you will judge your progress. A new committee role or another data project is a learning opportunity; explain the leadership growth it is intended to produce.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><ul><li>Identify future professional growth grounded in the cycle experience.</li><li>Explain clear strengths and learning needs using relevant evidence.</li><li>Connect future practice to equity leadership and the school's context.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Full Rubric 1.8</a></p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>One professional learning goal is to apply the attendance equity-analysis process to other student populations at Riverview, including students experiencing homelessness, youth in foster care, Black students, and students with disabilities. I want to understand whether the proposed instructional and mentoring supports fit their circumstances or whether different contributing conditions call for other responses. Comparing these inquiries could deepen my understanding of the school's systems while keeping the mission's commitment to individualized education in view. I would be looking for both shared patterns and meaningful differences, rather than assume that one strategy serves every group in the same way.</p>\n<p>A second goal is to seek a position on the Governance Council. Participating as a voting member would give me closer experience with the institutional issues the school faces and the processes used to address them. I would learn through discussions with colleagues, the consideration of competing perspectives, and the work of reaching agreement. This would help me develop a more democratic leadership approach and a stronger understanding of how support for a decision is built. It would also give me repeated opportunities to listen before proposing an answer, a habit I identified as needing further development during this cycle. These experiences would be directly relevant to a future administrative role.</p>\n<p>My third goal is to lead an improvement cycle within my current coordination responsibilities. I am responsible for a committee implementing a community-school plan and deciding how its budget will support that work. Although I am not an administrator, leading this schoolwide effort requires the critical thinking, collaborative planning, and participation-building skills practiced in Cycle 1. It provides an immediate setting in which to continue developing them.</p>\n<p>I would begin by creating a meeting timeline for the remainder of the year. The schedule would take account of deadlines for reviewing survey information, setting implementation goals, and reflecting on results before the year ends. From that timeline, I would identify monthly objectives and prepare agendas that specify the decisions or measurable products needed at each meeting. I would arrive with the data, materials, and background information members need to make informed choices.</p>\n<p>The committee work will require the same attention to participation that I practiced in this inquiry. Preparing an agenda is only the beginning; I will need to help members interpret information, consider different views, and agree on responsibilities they can carry out. Reviewing our actions after each meeting will help me connect planning with implementation and notice when the process needs adjustment. This makes the current coordination role a practical opportunity to develop leadership before moving into administration.</p>\n<p>Each meeting would close with individual and shared next steps, and the following meeting would include a report on what happened. Afterward, I would review whether our actions still align with the implementation plan and revise the next agenda when necessary. At the end of the year, I would examine whether the committee met its goals and what tangible evidence supports that judgment. I would also seek feedback on my leadership so that completing the plan is not the only measure of learning. These three goals extend the cycle into further equity inquiry, participation in governance, and direct leadership of collaborative implementation.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Name the leadership skill, activity, timeframe, and feedback source for each goal.</li><li>Define evidence of improved practice, such as a corrected interpretation or a decision changed by another perspective.</li><li>Include quantitative interpretation and inquiry discipline among the learning needs supported by this example.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"}],"anchors":["top","preparation","foundation","A-data","a-q1","part-a","A-group","A-qualitative","qualitative-analysis","A-mission","a-q2","A1","a-q3","A2","a-q4","A3","a-q5","A4","a-q6","A5","a-q7","B1","b-q1","part-b","B2","b-q2","B3","b-q3","B4","b-q4","B5","b-q5","C1","strategy-map","c-q1","part-c","C2","c-q2","C3","c-q3","C4","c-q4","C5","c-q5","D1","d-q1","part-d","D2","d-q2","D3","d-q3","version-alignment","A-sources","source-dashboard","source-lcap","source-eddata","source-sarc","source-quantitative","source-qualitative","source-wasc","source-chks","source-analyzer","source-note-ctc","source-note-lcap","source-note-wasc","source-note-chks","limits","source-record","score-summary","rubric-1-1","level4-1","rubric-1-2","level4-2","rubric-1-3","level4-3","rubric-1-4","level4-4","rubric-1-5","level4-5","rubric-1-6","level4-6","rubric-1-7","level4-7","rubric-1-8","level4-8"],"aliases":{"part-a":"A-data","a-q1":"A-data","a-q2":"A-mission","a-q3":"A1","a-q4":"A2","a-q5":"A3","a-q6":"A4","a-q7":"A5","part-b":"B1","b-q1":"B1","b-q2":"B2","b-q3":"B3","b-q4":"B4","b-q5":"B5","part-c":"C1","c-q1":"C1","c-q2":"C2","c-q3":"C3","c-q4":"C4","c-q5":"C5","part-d":"D1","d-q1":"D1","d-q2":"D2","d-q3":"D3","foundation":"preparation","rubric-map":"score-summary","final-check":"score-summary","strategy-map":"C1","qualitative-analysis":"A-qualitative"}},"c2":{"title":"Cycle 2: Understanding the Questions","html":"<div class=\"revised-cycle\"><header class=\"intro guide-heading\" id=\"top\"><h1>Cycle 2: Understanding the Questions</h1><p class=\"lead\">Cycle 2: Facilitate collaborative professional learning</p><p>Current questions, plain-language explanations, rubric components, and a critique of the interpolated example. Requirements come from the supplied Version 09 templates and handbook.</p></header><details class=\"contents-index\"><summary>Questions and reference</summary><nav aria-label=\"Cycle 2 questions\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/top\" aria-current=\"location\">Introduction</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/preparation\">Before you begin</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A1\">I.1 / Investigating professional learning</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A2\">I.2 / Implications for facilitation</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A3\">II.1 / Selecting the educational focus</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A4\">II.2 / Choosing community members</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A5\">II.3 / Understanding representation</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A6\">II.4 / Explaining positionality</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/A7\">II.5 / Expected benefits for students</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/B\">Planning meeting agenda</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/C\">Planning meeting minutes</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D1\">II.1 / Moving from data to a problem</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D2\">II.2 / Analyzing differentiated data</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D3\">II.3 / Identifying a change in practice</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D4\">II.4 / Connecting goals, standards, and differences</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D5\">III.1 / Selecting one strategy together</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D6\">III.2 / Learning and implementing the strategy</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D7\">III.3 / Anticipated learning benefits</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/D8\">III.4 / Monitoring implementation</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/E1\">Implementation agenda 1</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/E2\">Implementation agenda 2</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/E3\">Implementation agenda 3</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/F1\">Implementation minutes 1</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/F2\">Implementation minutes 2</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/F3\">Implementation minutes 3</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/G\">Collaborative work products</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/H\">Implementation meeting video clips</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/I1\">1 / Establishing meeting purpose</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/I2\">2 / Evaluating implementation data</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/I3\">3 / Reaching shared understanding</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/I4\">4 / Supporting equitable participation</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/I5\">5 / Determining next steps together</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/J1\">1 / Results, feedback, and leadership growth</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/J2\">2 / Professionalism, integrity, and equity</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/J3\">3 / Responding to school context</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/J4\">4 / Professional learning goals</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/alignment\">Template and handbook</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/limits\">Submission requirements</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/source-record\">Source notes</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/scores\">Example status</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-1\">Rubric 2.1: Collaborative professional learning</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</a></nav></details><section id=\"preparation\" class=\"preparation major-section\"><span id=\"foundation\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Before you begin</h2><p>Read the whole cycle before convening the group: investigate the educational focus and membership; plan a shared problem and strategy; implement the strategy and examine initial results in at least three implementation meetings; reflect on your facilitation. Plan evidence collection and recording permissions before the meetings occur.</p><p>Agendas show intended work, minutes show what happened, work products show what the group used or produced, and commentary explains your leadership in the clips. These are connected forms of evidence, not interchangeable assignments.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Scores are instructional estimates of the complete rubric, not separate scores for individual questions. Video-dependent estimates are provisional; recordings were not reviewed. The interpolated examples are teaching parallels to examine, not models to copy.</p></section><article id=\"A1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-a\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.1 / Investigating professional learning</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe and analyze the role of collaborative professional learning at your school site/educational setting as it relates to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>a.\tProvide information about any formal training offered to professional educators (e.g., teachers, specialists, aides, support staff) at your school site/educational setting within the past three years related to collaborative professional learning, communities of practice, or group learning process.</p><p>b.\tDescribe and analyze the ways in which professional educators (e.g., teachers, specialists, aides, support staff) at your school site/educational setting currently engage with one another in professional learning. Describe the impact and/or lack of impact of this professional learning in relation to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>c.\tDescribe and analyze the ways in which professional educators engage with one another in communities of practice. Describe the impact and/or lack of impact of the community of practice approach in relation to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>d.\tDescribe how collaborative professional learning reflects evidence-based adult learning processes and cite relevant research to support your analysis.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Answer all four subparts: training offered within three years; current professional learning and its impact; communities of practice and their impact; and the adult-learning principles that explain those practices. A community of practice involves sustained shared inquiry, not simply attendance at a meeting.</p><p>Separate what you observed or found in records from what you expect collaboration might accomplish. When impact evidence is unavailable, explain that limitation rather than presenting a benefit as an established result.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.1: Collaborative professional learning</h3><ul><li>Describe current collaborative professional learning in detail and analyze its relationship to student learning or well-being.</li><li>For Level 4, explain relationships among different forms of professional learning and their impact, supported by observations, records, surveys, work, or consultations.</li><li>For Level 5, use adult-learning evidence or research to critique those practices and explain implications for facilitating the community.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>During the preceding three years, the school's formal preparation for collaborative professional learning included an introduction to an improvement cycle organized around planning, trying a change, studying evidence, and deciding what to do next. Administrators introduced this process last year as part of a schoolwide effort to improve reading comprehension in English language arts. In a professional development session, they explained how a common inquiry process could help teams examine learning and coordinate instruction. The initial preparation was brief, but it gave grade levels a shared way to organize their work.</p>\n<p>The school created one slide presentation containing templates that every grade level used. Teams recorded a problem of practice, selected a strategy, identified evidence, and analyzed student learning in that shared space. Because the presentation was available schoolwide, teachers could see other teams' questions, instructional choices, and findings. Grade-level members completed the process together rather than submit separate individual plans. At several points during the year, teams presented results to colleagues. This helped establish common language for discussing reading comprehension and made the relationship between collaborative inquiry and the schoolwide goal more visible.</p>\n<p>Current collaboration takes place through several established arrangements. Schoolwide professional development occurs two or three times a month and brings together teachers, specialists, coordinators, and administrators. Sessions may involve reviewing school priorities, examining iReady and DIBELS results, preparing for state assessments, or clarifying curriculum expectations. These activities can support a shared understanding of what students should learn and more consistent access to instruction. Their value depends partly on how much time remains for examining teaching and learning after other business is addressed.</p>\n<p>Teachers also meet with their grade-level colleagues every other week during the instructional day. They discuss recent data, student work, and pacing. Those conversations can lead to prompt reteaching or changes in instruction when a need becomes apparent. The school offers up to two additional hours of optional after-school collaboration each month. Participation in that time varies because teachers' schedules differ. Consequently, some teams benefit from more shared planning than others, and the effects of that additional time are uneven across the school.</p>\n<p>More structured data conversations are beginning to develop, but their use is not yet consistent. Collaboration could help staff recognize academic needs early and coordinate support for English learners and students receiving intervention. Logistical requirements can also limit that potential. Teams are expected to meet when all members are available, which means some groups have difficulty meeting at all while others engage in sustained inquiry. Paraprofessionals are not regularly included in these arrangements. Their absence from planning can weaken the connection between classroom instruction and the additional support students receive.</p>\n<p>The school's history of professional learning has nevertheless established an expectation that teachers work together. Regular professional development and grade-level meetings make discussion of data, instruction, and pacing part of normal professional practice. These routines have helped coordinate classrooms and given teachers recurring opportunities to learn from one another. They provide a foundation for a community of practice because collaboration is familiar rather than entirely new.</p>\n<p>The quality of that collaboration varies. Sessions centered on logistics leave less time for exploring an instructional problem. Optional meeting time and the requirement for full attendance can make participation uneven. The common inquiry process introduced last year strengthened the work by giving teams a specific sequence and a shared place to document evidence. Viewing one another's results increased transparency and supported alignment. Professional learning therefore helps collaboration when it gives educators time and structure to investigate instruction, while arrangements emphasizing compliance or restricting participation can limit its usefulness.</p>\n<p>Several adult-learning principles are visible in these practices. Teachers work with current assessment information and instructional questions that arise in their classrooms. This reflects Knowles' account of adult learning as relevant to professional responsibilities and organized around problems adults need to address (1984). The immediate connection to students gives the work a practical purpose and allows teachers to draw on their existing experience.</p>\n<p>The shared presentation also allowed grade levels to learn about approaches outside their own team. Seeing another group's problem, strategy, and evidence could broaden the conversation about a common reading goal. This transparency was a practical feature of the process, alongside the more formal expectation that teams present their results.</p>\n<p>The meetings also illustrate learning through social participation. In the traditions associated with Vygotsky and with Lave and Wenger, understanding develops through interaction and shared activity. Teachers compare interpretations, solve problems together, and build collective expertise. Finally, the inquiry cycle includes collaboration, attention to student outcomes, and continuing reflection, features discussed by Darling-Hammond and colleagues in their work on effective professional learning. Not every session reflects these principles equally. The strongest alignment appears when educators examine evidence together, make an instructional decision, try it, and return to consider what students' responses suggest.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the dates, participants, and purposes of relevant training across the three-year period, including years with no such training.</li><li>Compare two forms of professional learning using a concrete record of a decision, instructional follow-through, and student evidence.</li><li>Use the adult-learning ideas to examine both strengths and weaknesses of the local practices; distinguish established effects from plausible benefits.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.2 / Implications for facilitation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tAs a result of your investigation and given the educational setting as well as available data related to student learning and/or well-being, describe the most important issues you will need to consider in preparing to facilitate and support a community of practice to engage in collaborative professional learning.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what the investigation requires you to do as facilitator. Connect each priority to the school context, available student evidence, and this group's likely participation. Do not repeat the entire description from question 1.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.1: Collaborative professional learning</h3><ul><li>Describe current collaborative professional learning in detail and analyze its relationship to student learning or well-being.</li><li>For Level 4, explain relationships among different forms of professional learning and their impact, supported by observations, records, surveys, work, or consultations.</li><li>For Level 5, use adult-learning evidence or research to critique those practices and explain implications for facilitating the community.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Two conditions need attention as I prepare to facilitate this team. The first is reliable participation. Even within one grade level, attendance changes with the meeting arrangement and time of day. When members are missing, the group loses perspectives needed for a shared interpretation of student learning. Decisions may then feel less collective, and the team may need to repeat discussions before moving forward. I will need to establish arrangements that make sustained participation feasible.</p>\n<p>The second condition is a clear inquiry routine. Existing meetings sometimes rely on informal discussion and move from instruction into scheduling or other logistics. I will need protocols that bring the team back to evidence, help members examine student responses, and support reflection on the strategies they try. Consistent structures can make the difference between meeting regularly and learning together. My facilitation should help the group use its time to reach shared, evidence-informed decisions about instruction and subsequent adjustments.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Specify an agreed meeting schedule and the school support needed to make participation possible.</li><li>Name the data-discussion routine and explain how it will separate observations, interpretations, and decisions.</li><li>Connect each facilitation choice to a documented concern from the investigation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.1 / Selecting the educational focus</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe the agreed-upon area of educational focus for improving student learning and/or well-being (obtained with guidance from one or more school administrators and/or teacher leaders) selected for the group’s professional learning. Be sure to include a discussion of the relevant data that supports the selection of this focus and the connection between the educational focus and the vision, mission, and/or goals in your school site/educational setting.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Name the educational focus agreed with an administrator or teacher leader, explain the student data supporting it, and connect it to an actual school commitment. The broad focus is established here; the community jointly defines the more specific problem of practice later.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Three different decisions</summary><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Decision</th><th scope=\"col\">Purpose</th><th scope=\"col\">Who shapes it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Educational focus</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">The broad area of student learning or well-being that warrants inquiry.</td><td data-label=\"Who shapes it\">Established with administrator or teacher-leader guidance.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Problem of practice</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">A specific, changeable aspect of adult practice identified through evidence.</td><td data-label=\"Who shapes it\">Defined jointly by the community during planning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Selected strategy</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">One evidence-based response that fits the problem and setting.</td><td data-label=\"Who shapes it\">Chosen jointly after considering research, evidence, and feasibility.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Do not arrive with every decision already made. Facilitation creates opportunities for colleagues to challenge an interpretation and influence the response.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The educational focus is mathematics, particularly progress toward proficiency on the Smarter Balanced Assessment. Administration and I selected this focus after examining school SBA trends and district priorities. The district expects students in grades three through five to move an average of 40 points nearer proficiency by 2026, with a 50-point gain expected for targeted groups. Our school has shown continuing growth and currently scores above the proficiency threshold in mathematics. However, its latest Distance from Standard value of 43.7 remains below the district's projected 2025-2026 target, so further acceleration is needed. Mathematics therefore provides a relevant focus even in a school with positive overall results. The work also connects with the mission of equitable access to rigorous teaching and with the district's longer-term intention that students be prepared for more advanced mathematics, including Algebra. The focus therefore connects immediate school improvement with later learning opportunities.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the year, population, source, and comparison target for the Distance from Standard value.</li><li>Explain why the third-grade evidence warrants this focus while keeping schoolwide and grade-level results distinct.</li><li>State the relevant school commitment and the administrator or teacher-leader role in agreeing to the focus.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.2 / Choosing community members</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tProvide a list of the job titles for each member of the community of practice group, detailing the specific reason(s) for each member’s inclusion and referencing the area of educational focus.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>List each partner's job title and explain the expertise or instructional responsibility that makes that person useful for the mathematics focus. The community requires 3-5 partners in addition to the candidate. Shared availability alone is not a membership rationale.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The community includes three third-grade teachers. One teaches an SAS class with a broad range of learner needs. Her perspective can help the group choose mathematics approaches that remain useful across different levels of readiness while supporting the SBA focus.</p>\n<p>A second SAS teacher contributes experience with enrichment and accelerated instruction. As the grade-level chair, she can also help coordinate the team's work and maintain an appropriate level of challenge across classrooms.</p>\n<p>The general education teacher represents another range of instructional levels within third grade. His knowledge of scaffolds and additional support will help the group consider how students can gain access to the mathematics proficiency goal.</p>\n<p>Third grade is the first year students take the mathematics SBA. Working with this team offers an opportunity to strengthen that transition and, if the collaboration proves useful, extend its approach to the upper grades as the school pursues the district numeracy targets.</p>\n<p>Their agreement established a shared starting commitment for the planned inquiry.</p>\n<p>I invited the teachers during a grade-level meeting by explaining the community's purpose and its relationship to the schoolwide mathematics goal. Each agreed to participate. They saw the work as an opportunity to improve mathematics teaching, support students approaching their first SBA, and develop greater instructional consistency across the grade level.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Differentiate each teacher's specific contribution to identifying and addressing the mathematics need.</li><li>Explain how the group will obtain relevant perspectives not represented in its membership.</li><li>Keep the facilitator distinct from the three partners and retain the actual commitment process.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.3 / Understanding representation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tProvide a description of each group member’s demographics and how this reflects or is different from your school site/educational setting’s demographics.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe each member's appropriately provided demographic information and compare it with the school community. Explain representation without assuming that shared identity guarantees shared perspectives or that a person speaks for an entire group.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The two SAS teachers and the general education teacher bring different backgrounds to the group. One SAS teacher identifies as Hispanic, connecting with the approximately 59% of students identified as Latino, the school's largest demographic group. The other SAS teacher identifies as White, a group comprising about 16% of enrollment. The general education teacher is Armenian. Armenian identity is not listed separately in the school's demographic chart, although many families in the community share that background. His perspective therefore relates to a substantial community presence that is less visible in the chart's reporting categories. These descriptions concern representation within this particular school community.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the source, year, and category definitions for the school demographic comparison.</li><li>Use member-provided information and explain any noncomparable categories.</li><li>Describe how differing perspectives will be sought and respected in the mathematics inquiry.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A6\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.4 / Explaining positionality</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tProvide a brief description of your relationship or positionality (e.g., coach, mentor, teacher colleague, administrator) to each member of the group.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain your working relationship and authority in relation to each partner. Consider how grade-level relationships, status, experience, or prior collaboration may shape participation even when you are not an evaluator.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I teach second grade at the same school and participate as a colleague of the third-grade team. My students enter their grade next, so I bring knowledge of the foundational skills and instructional experiences that precede third-grade mathematics. That position allows me to contribute to discussion of the transition between grades and the preparation students receive. I do not supervise or evaluate these teachers. My role is that of a peer and facilitator, working alongside them to examine evidence and improve mathematics learning rather than directing their performance from an administrative position. That colleague relationship shapes how I invite participation and discuss evidence.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Describe any relevant differences in your relationship with each partner.</li><li>Explain how the grade-level chair's role or your feeder-grade position could shape discussion.</li><li>Identify one facilitation choice that protects candid participation without assuming all members feel equally comfortable.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A7\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.5 / Expected benefits for students</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>5.\tExplain how the work of this group is likely to advance conditions for students’ learning and/or well-being in relation to the area of educational focus.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain the pathway from the community's adult learning to improved conditions for students. Identify what teachers may learn or change, what students will experience differently, and why that could help with the mathematics focus. These are expected effects, not completed outcomes.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The community's intended benefit is more consistent and responsive mathematics instruction across third grade. By examining student evidence together, teachers can identify numeracy needs and select approaches that strengthen the foundational skills required for SBA success. Joint planning and reflection should help establish coherent routines, shared expectations, and suitable supports for learners with different starting points. Students may then experience clearer instruction and greater confidence in mathematics, contributing to learning and well-being.</p>\n<p>The community will revisit those expectations as evidence becomes available.</p>\n<p>The group also creates a continuing process for revisiting those decisions. Teachers can bring new evidence, compare how students respond, and modify instruction when needs change. Shared analysis can strengthen their understanding of effective practice and distribute responsibility for student progress across the team. Over time, that process may support more equitable learning opportunities by making instructional choices deliberate and responsive. These are expected benefits to investigate through implementation and student evidence, rather than outcomes already demonstrated before the community begins its work.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use prospective language for effects that have not yet been observed.</li><li>Identify the particular opportunities to learn that coordinated planning should improve.</li><li>Name the student evidence the group will later examine to see whether those opportunities and benefits occurred.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\"><span id=\"b-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-b\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / Planning meeting agenda</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for the planning meeting. You may use either this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nAfter creating the agenda, use it to facilitate meetings with group members’ active involvement to review and analyze data—including student demographics, learning, academic performance, and/or well-being—and other relevant student information related to the selected area of educational focus. Record minutes during the planning meeting, describing what was discussed, agreements reached, and the next steps the group will take.</p><p>1.\tReview the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/professional context:</p><p>2.\tReview and analyze data:</p><p>a.\tData source(s):</p><p>b.\tDiscussion of data:</p><p>3.\tIdentify a problem of practice:</p><p>4.\tAddressing the problem of practice:</p><p>5.\tImplementation plan:</p><p>6.\tNext steps and action items:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part B template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Prepare the meeting before it occurs. Include who will attend, the purpose, norms, data, discussion of the problem, strategy selection, implementation planning, and next actions. The prompts below are the current optional format; the agenda itself is required.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Meeting date: October 13, 2025. Meeting time: 1:30-2:30 p.m.</p>\n<p>Working agreements: Give the discussion full attention; arrive prepared to contribute; consider perspectives different from one's own; and keep the conversation focused on the shared purpose. These agreements will guide both participation and collaborative decision-making throughout the meeting.</p>\n<p>Participants: Ms. W., third-grade SAS teacher and grade-level chair; Ms. A., third-grade SAS teacher; Mr. A., third-grade general education teacher; and Ms. K., second-grade teacher serving as facilitator.</p>\n<p>Student focus: Third-grade learners, with particular attention to foundational numeracy needs identified in beginning-of-year iReady results.</p>\n<p>Meeting purpose: Examine available assessment information, including SBA and iReady, establish a shared instructional problem, and select one evidence-informed approach to support third-grade number sense.</p>\n<p>School and district connection: Revisit the district goal for grades three through five to move an average of 40 points toward SBA proficiency by 2026. Connect this target with the school's commitment to rigorous instruction that is accessible to all learners. Use iReady growth expectations as ongoing information about progress toward the larger mathematics goals.</p>\n<p>Evidence for review: Third-grade iReady beginning-of-year overall placement; iReady domain results for Number and Operations, Algebraic Thinking, and Measurement and Data; and mathematics SBA Distance from Standard trends.</p>\n<p>Data discussion: Compare overall placement with domain strengths and areas needing support. Identify patterns that may explain difficulty accessing grade-level standards, especially foundational skills needed for later concepts. Invite teachers to connect the assessment information with what they observe in classrooms. Use the discussion to move toward a problem the whole team recognizes.</p>\n<p>Problem of practice: Define an instructional concern that is specific enough to guide action, supported by the evidence, and within teachers' influence. Focus on how instruction can improve access to grade-level mathematics rather than describe students only in terms of low scores. Clarify which foundational needs the first cycle should address.</p>\n<p>Strategy discussion: Consider more than one research-informed approach. Compare each option with the identified need, the range of student readiness, and the realities of classroom implementation. Reach a shared decision on one primary strategy after members have had an opportunity to discuss its fit.</p>\n<p>Implementation planning: Outline the routines, group arrangements, common materials, and monitoring tools needed to use the selected strategy across classrooms. Agree on how the team will coordinate the work while remaining responsive to individual students. Establish an initial timeline for reviewing evidence and discussing progress.</p>\n<p>Decision record: Capture the problem statement, the reason for the selected approach, and any implementation questions still requiring evidence. Make these notes available to all participants so the next meeting can continue from a common account of the discussion.</p>\n<p>Closing decisions: Record responsibilities, resources to prepare, evidence to collect, and the next collaboration date. Confirm what each person will bring back so the following meeting can build directly on the decisions made today.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Allocate realistic discussion time to data interpretation, alternatives, and implementation decisions.</li><li>Specify which dated data or student work members will examine and what questions will guide that examination.</li><li>Close with a plan to record owners, deadlines, needed support, and the next meeting.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\"><span id=\"c-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-c\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / Planning meeting minutes</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: While facilitating your planning meeting with your group members, record minutes of the discussion. Be sure to describe what was discussed, agreements reached, and the next steps the group will take. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tReview the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/professional context:</p><p>2.\tReview and analyze data:</p><p>3.\tIdentify a problem of practice:</p><p>4.\tAddressing the problem of practice:</p><p>5.\tImplementation plan:</p><p>6.\tNext steps and action items:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part C template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Record the actual discussion, differing interpretations, agreements, and next steps. Show how the group moved from evidence to a changeable problem and a selected strategy. Minutes are a record of the group's reasoning, not a second agenda or a transcript of everything said.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The meeting opened by connecting the inquiry with the school's commitment to rigorous, accessible instruction. Teachers discussed how strong early mathematics understanding prepares students for more advanced work later. We also revisited the district expectation that students in grades three through five move an average of 40 points closer to SBA proficiency by 2026. Alongside this longer-term target, members considered the importance of meeting iReady typical and stretch growth expectations during the year. The group agreed that strengthening number sense was a meaningful contribution to both the school mission and district priorities.</p>\n<p>I then guided the team through the third-grade beginning-of-year iReady results. We began with overall placement, where relatively few students were at or above grade level and most were one or more grades below. Moving to the domain results, we identified notable needs in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. I asked members to consider how these areas support addition and subtraction with regrouping, a major third-grade focus and a foundation for later mathematics. This sequence helped the group move from a broad performance pattern toward a more specific instructional concern.</p>\n<p>To connect assessment reports with classroom experience, I invited teachers to share early work samples and observations. Several described difficulty with place value and regrouping. Others noted students who already showed strong conceptual understanding and needed additional challenge. I summarized this range so that our planning would address both groups. The discussion made clear that a single uniform task would not necessarily provide appropriate access for everyone. Students needing foundational instruction and students ready for enrichment were present within the same grade level and, often, within the same classroom.</p>\n<p>The team considered what this range meant for its practice. I prompted members to think about how more deliberate planning and greater consistency across classrooms could support different needs. We agreed that instruction would need to be focused enough to accelerate progress while allowing teachers to respond to students' readiness. I closed the data discussion by restating the shared concern: we needed a coordinated approach that could provide differentiated opportunities without losing coherence across the grade. This understanding became the basis for our problem of practice and strategy discussion.</p>\n<p>The group framed its problem as a need for more consistent, targeted early numeracy instruction because gaps in foundational number sense were limiting students' access to grade-level mathematics. The intended improvement was progress toward iReady growth expectations and SBA proficiency. This wording connected student evidence with an instructional responsibility the team could address. It also made the problem more actionable than a general statement that mathematics scores needed to rise.</p>\n<p>In developing that statement, I returned the group to beginning-of-year placement, domain results, and classroom work. Teachers identified number and operations concepts that underpin addition and subtraction with regrouping. I asked how difficulty with those concepts affects participation in grade-level work and may contribute to continuing performance differences. Members also emphasized that the problem could not be addressed by planning only for students below grade level. Students performing at or above expectations needed purposeful opportunities to extend their reasoning. We therefore identified grouping and targeted practice as aspects of instruction that required reconsideration.</p>\n<p>The team agreed that this problem was sufficiently focused, relevant to school and district goals, and within our instructional influence. Addressing it would require intentional changes in how teachers organize learning, reinforce foundational concepts, and provide enrichment. We wanted students to receive appropriately demanding mathematics rather than equate differentiation with easier work for some and additional work for others. The problem statement gave us a shared basis for comparing possible strategies.</p>\n<p>I introduced two research-informed options for discussion. The first was flexible grouping based on current skill needs, using the NCSM resource Supporting All Students Through Flexible Grouping Practices. I highlighted temporary placement, movement as students develop, and targeted teaching without permanent tracking. I asked how that approach could serve students needing foundational support as well as students ready for enrichment. Teachers recognized that their range of readiness made a flexible structure relevant. They also discussed the importance of using current evidence when assigning or changing groups.</p>\n<p>The second option was a daily mathematics reasoning routine, drawing on Heinemann's Create Confident Problem Solvers with Number Talks. We considered how Number Talks could develop conceptual understanding, number sense, and mathematical discussion. Structured opportunities to explain reasoning may be particularly helpful for English learners and students who need support expressing their thinking. Teachers saw how these routines could complement the core program by making students' strategies and language more visible. This comparison gave the group more than one plausible response to the identified problem.</p>\n<p>After discussing the evidence and classroom conditions, the team selected flexible, skill-based grouping as the primary strategy. Members felt it offered the most direct way to provide different levels of support and challenge across the grade. We used the local name Math Crew for the approach, while identifying the underlying strategy as the flexible grouping model discussed by NCSM. The name was a convenient way to refer to the work, not a separate evidence base. Teachers wanted groups that could address place value and regrouping for some students while providing more advanced problem-solving opportunities for others.</p>\n<p>I next facilitated an initial implementation plan. We narrowed the first cycle to addition and subtraction with regrouping so that the work would be manageable and coordinated. Both the assessment information and classroom observations supported this choice. During the meeting, teachers developed a short preassessment of place value and regrouping concepts. They agreed to administer it before the next meeting and use the results to make grouping decisions. This meant that group membership would follow evidence about the selected skills rather than broad impressions of student ability.</p>\n<p>We discussed what teaching within the groups might involve. Some students would work on composing and decomposing numbers, relationships within place value, and concrete or visual representations of regrouping. Others would engage in extension and more complex problem solving. I emphasized that the groups should remain temporary and change as students' understanding develops. The purpose was to provide an appropriate next learning opportunity, not assign a lasting label to a student. Members agreed that the preassessment and subsequent work would help determine where each student should begin and when a change was appropriate.</p>\n<p>For consistency, teachers agreed to protect a common instructional period for Math Crew and use shared language, routines, and visual models. We also planned to exchange resources and collaborate on lessons. This would support alignment while preserving teachers' ability to respond to the learners in front of them. The duration of the first cycle would be decided after reviewing the preassessment. A postassessment would then provide information about progress and help the team determine subsequent instruction. These arrangements linked the strategy with a process for studying its implementation rather than treating selection as the end of planning.</p>\n<p>The comparison of strategies also helped distinguish a teaching routine from a grouping structure. Number Talks could provide regular opportunities to reason and explain, while flexible grouping could organize more targeted instruction around different skill needs. The team did not need to regard one as universally better. It needed to decide which would serve as the primary response to this particular problem in the first cycle.</p>\n<p>The shared preassessment was an important part of that decision. Broad iReady placement information had identified a concern, but the regrouping tasks would give teachers more immediate evidence about the concepts they intended to teach. Discussing the assessment together also helped members establish a common interpretation of what students would be asked to demonstrate. That preparation would support later discussion of group placement and progress.</p>\n<p>The implementation plan retained both consistency and teacher judgment. Shared time, language, and models would make the arrangement coherent for students moving among classrooms. Within that structure, teachers could select scaffolds and extensions appropriate to the group. The team would revisit those choices after seeing student work, so the initial plan was a starting point for inquiry rather than a permanent assignment of students or instructional tasks.</p>\n<p>At the close, we confirmed responsibilities and the next meeting's purpose. Teachers would administer and score the regrouping preassessment, then bring results, observations, and questions about grouping or instruction. Together we would finalize groups, determine the length of the first cycle, and identify or adapt resources for each group. We would also plan the postassessment used to examine progress. My continuing role would be to help members notice patterns, compare interpretations, coordinate classroom expectations, and make shared adjustments. The next steps kept the inquiry connected to evidence and to the practical decisions needed to improve access to grade-level mathematics.</p>\n<p>The recorded decisions provide a shared reference for checking what was implemented and what the next discussion needs to resolve.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Record specific member contributions, differing views, and how agreement was established.</li><li>Attach the actual group-level data or work used to define the need.</li><li>List each agreed action with its owner, due date, and evidence to bring back.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-d\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.1 / Moving from data to a problem</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe how you: (a) worked collaboratively with the group to analyze student data sets on the educational focus; (b) selected a problem of practice based on the area of educational focus; and (c) addressed how practitioners could potentially improve student learning and/or well-being by improving the current practices at your school site/educational setting.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain the collaborative reasoning: which data the group examined, what members noticed, which problem they selected, and how changing current instruction could help. Keep the educational focus, problem of practice, and selected strategy distinct.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>At the planning meeting, I organized the review around several sources related to third-grade mathematics. We began with beginning-of-year iReady results and the SBA numeracy trend so everyone had the same starting information. I asked teachers to examine overall placement, including the share of students below grade level, before moving to individual domains. Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking showed particular areas of need. I invited members to compare these results across classrooms and describe what they were seeing during instruction, giving the assessment reports a more specific classroom context.</p>\n<p>To develop the analysis, I asked the team to identify patterns that appeared across the grade and consider which prerequisite skills were making grade-level mathematics difficult. These prompts helped connect scores with observable learning. Teachers described students' difficulty composing and decomposing numbers, understanding place value, and using regrouping. They explained how those difficulties affected participation in current tasks rather than simply repeat the assessment labels.</p>\n<p>The focus remained on changes the teachers could make together.</p>\n<p>I also made space for evidence about students who were already meeting or exceeding expectations. Members recognized that these learners needed enrichment while classmates needed foundational support. Bringing both needs into the discussion helped us avoid defining the problem only around remediation. The group developed a shared problem of practice centered on more deliberate, consistent instruction in foundational number sense. We concluded that this would require changes in the organization of mathematics instruction, particularly grouping and the reinforcement of prerequisite skills. Those changes would need to work across classrooms and support progress toward both iReady growth expectations and the SBA goal.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Show one data pattern and the member discussion that shaped the problem.</li><li>State what is inconsistent or insufficient in current instruction and what the group can change.</li><li>Connect the selected problem to the shared evidence without treating the strategy as already decided.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.2 / Analyzing differentiated data</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tExplain how you worked collaboratively with the group to review your collected data on student demographics, student learning and academic performance or well-being, and other relevant data differentiated by student groups.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group compared data across student groups. Identify the measures, populations, and findings, then explain what members made of the differences. Mentioning several group names does not demonstrate a differentiated analysis.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I helped the team extend its review to demographic and performance information, including English learners, students with disabilities, and students without identified support needs. The beginning-of-year iReady results showed many students below grade level in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. Teachers described similar concerns in several classrooms and across groups, suggesting a shared instructional issue rather than a difficulty confined to one class.</p>\n<p>We then compared those patterns with student work and daily observations. Members described limited understanding of place value and regrouping, explaining how these gaps made grade-level tasks less accessible. They also identified students with stronger understanding who needed opportunities for extension. This comparison made the range of readiness within each classroom clearer. It supported planning for both foundational instruction and enrichment, while keeping the discussion connected to actual student responses rather than relying on demographic categories alone.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Provide the actual results for at least two relevant groups, with dates and denominators or clearly described work evidence.</li><li>Explain how overlapping group memberships and different measures affect comparison.</li><li>Show how the group used the differences to refine the problem rather than simply reporting them.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.3 / Identifying a change in practice</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tDescribe the ways you facilitated the meeting to acknowledge how the problem of practice might be addressed with a change in current practices in your professional context.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how you helped the group recognize that something adults currently do can change. Name the instructional practice under discussion and show how members connected that change to the evidence. This is separate from merely identifying low performance.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I asked the team to examine whether its current arrangements allowed teachers to respond to the range of readiness we had identified. We considered which teaching practices could address differing starting points more deliberately and how changes in grouping might bring instruction closer to students' particular skill needs. These questions shifted the conversation from describing performance to considering what adults could change. Teachers recognized that the problem called for adjustments in the way mathematics instruction was organized and delivered, especially the support provided within groups.</p>\n<p>We agreed to frame the problem as a need for more consistent and targeted early numeracy teaching because gaps in foundational number sense were limiting access to grade-level mathematics. The intended direction was improved progress toward iReady growth expectations and SBA proficiency. This statement connected the student need with an instructional response within the team's influence and gave members a common purpose for the next stage of planning.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the current practice the team agreed was limiting access to targeted instruction.</li><li>Record what members said that showed their recognition of the needed change.</li><li>Explain why the proposed change follows from the evidence, keeping alternative explanations visible.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.4 / Connecting goals, standards, and differences</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tHow does the identified problem of practice: (a) correspond to the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/educational setting; (b) reflect California state standards and/or curriculum frameworks or equivalent, or other applicable standards; and (c) address existing differences in student group performance or well-being?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all three connections: school commitments, relevant standards or frameworks, and actual differences in student learning or well-being. Explain how each connection supports the selected problem, not just that the problem aligns with them.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The problem of practice connects with the school's commitment to rigorous learning opportunities that are accessible to every student and prepare them for advanced mathematics. Strengthening number sense also supports the district's longer-term numeracy priorities. It is consistent with the California Mathematics Framework's attention to conceptual understanding, numerical fluency, and flexible approaches to solving problems.</p>\n<p>The focus responds to performance differences visible in third grade. Foundational needs appeared across several student groups, and students below grade level would need accelerated progress to meet annual growth expectations. Addressing those needs can improve access to current instruction. At the same time, the team must provide continued challenge for students already on track so that the strategy advances learning across the full range of readiness.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the relevant grade-level learning expectation and the work that reveals difficulty with it.</li><li>Connect the school goal to the instructional opportunity the group can change.</li><li>Use actual group differences to explain why the problem matters for equitable access.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.1 / Selecting one strategy together</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tExplain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting in selecting one relevant evidence-based strategy that addresses the problem of practice, potentially leading to improved student learning and/or well-being across student groups. Be sure to include how you facilitated the group to help make their selection.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group considered and selected one relevant evidence-based strategy. Include the alternatives, criteria, member contributions, and your facilitation. The group's decision is the object of analysis, not just your preferred strategy.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>After the team established its instructional problem, I introduced two research-informed possibilities. One was temporary grouping by skill, supported by NCSM's Supporting All Students Through Flexible Grouping Practices. The other was a daily reasoning routine such as Number Talks, using Heinemann's Create Confident Problem Solvers with Number Talks. We discussed what each would look like in practice and compared its fit with students' needs, implementation demands, and opportunities to differentiate across readiness levels.</p>\n<p>I supported the decision by asking members to consider which option most directly addressed the addition and subtraction needs in the data, which could serve the range of learners, and which we could use consistently across classrooms. These questions gave the comparison shared criteria rather than leave the choice to individual preference. Teachers noted that flexible grouping would allow them to reorganize students around current skills, teach particular concepts more directly, and offer enrichment to students who already demonstrated proficiency.</p>\n<p>The group reached agreement on flexible grouping, which we called Math Crew locally. We selected it because it appeared to offer the best fit for foundational numeracy needs while supporting differentiated teaching for all learners. The local name refers to our implementation of the strategy; the research basis remains the flexible grouping approach discussed in the resource.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain the specific instructional features the selected strategy will include.</li><li>Show how members evaluated the evidence and considered an alternative.</li><li>Document how shared agreement was reached, including concerns or conditions attached to the choice.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D6\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.2 / Learning and implementing the strategy</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tDescribe the group’s rationale for selecting this evidence-based strategy. How will the group learn this strategy and how to implement it? What challenges do you anticipate the group might encounter and how could they be addressed?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why the group chose the strategy, how teachers will learn to use it, and how anticipated challenges will be addressed. Prior exposure to grouping is not the same as a plan to learn a particular instructional approach.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Teachers chose flexible grouping because their classes include students well below, at, and above grade-level expectations. Temporary groups organized around current skills seemed a practical way to provide more precisely matched instruction. Members also knew that kindergarten through second grade had used similar arrangements in English language arts. That experience offered a local model for responding to varied learning needs. Because I had experience with that work, I could help the team, and members could also consult K-2 colleagues who had used flexible groups extensively.</p>\n<p>During the meeting, we created a short preassessment to guide Math Crew placement. It addresses addition and subtraction with regrouping and the underlying place-value understanding those operations require. Teachers agreed to administer and score it before the next community meeting. We would then use the results to determine groups and instructional priorities rather than assign students solely from a broad assessment level. Members also discussed resources, manipulatives, routines, and possible teaching approaches. They expressed interest in observing one another during implementation to learn how the groups functioned in different classrooms.</p>\n<p>We anticipated several practical challenges. The team would need a dependable instructional period, a process for moving students as they progressed, and enough alignment that group experiences remained coherent across classrooms. Teachers agreed to use common routines and language and to check in regularly during subsequent meetings. Those arrangements would help preserve flexibility without making the work unmanageable. They would also provide opportunities to notice when a group or lesson needed adjustment. The intention was a responsive system in which current evidence informs placement and instruction, supported by collaboration rather than fixed group assignments for the duration of the year.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Specify a rehearsal, observation, or collaborative planning activity with a date and responsible person.</li><li>Define the instructional routine, evidence for changing groups, and safeguards against permanent low-expectation placement.</li><li>Match anticipated challenges for specific learners with feasible supports.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D7\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.3 / Anticipated learning benefits</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tWhat improvements for student learning and/or well-being related to the problem of practice do the group expect the selected evidence-based strategy will yield?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe what different students are expected to learn or experience because of the selected strategy. Explain the instructional mechanism: what teachers will do differently and why that should address the identified need. Keep predictions distinct from outcomes.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The team expects more closely matched instruction to strengthen students' number sense, accuracy, and confidence with multidigit addition and subtraction. Students who find regrouping difficult would receive targeted support with the concepts underlying the procedure. Those already meeting or exceeding expectations would work in enrichment groups that extend reasoning and problem solving. In both cases, the goal is instruction suited to current readiness rather than the same task for everyone.</p>\n<p>Teachers also anticipate greater engagement and less frustration during core mathematics lessons as students become more secure with prerequisite skills. More appropriate support and challenge may improve access to grade-level content and students' sense of competence. These are intended academic and well-being benefits. The group will need to examine student work and assessment results to determine which benefits appear and where further changes are needed.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each group's observed need to a specific instructional action and expected benefit.</li><li>Identify how conceptual reasoning, procedural accuracy, and enrichment will each be examined.</li><li>Use an appropriate source for any well-being or engagement claim.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D8\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q8\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.4 / Monitoring implementation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tHow will you facilitate the group in determining a monitoring process to understand the impact of the evidence-based strategy? Describe how you and the group plan to monitor and analyze initial implementation.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group will check both whether the strategy was implemented and what students initially learned. Specify evidence, timing, interpretation, and decision rules. The monitoring process must be manageable and jointly understood.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I will help the group establish a monitoring process that is clear enough to guide decisions and manageable enough to sustain. The shared preassessment will provide the starting evidence for Math Crew grouping. Teachers will administer and score it before our next meeting, when we will compare results and confirm placements together. This gives the team a common reference for discussing students' initial understanding.</p>\n<p>During implementation, members will collect work samples, exit responses, observation notes, and informal checks focused on place value and regrouping. I will prompt the group to look for recurring patterns, identify students whose placement may need to change, and distinguish early progress from continuing difficulty. Each implementation meeting will begin with a brief review of this evidence. The purpose is to decide whether instruction or group arrangements need adjustment while the work is underway.</p>\n<p>This process links each meeting with the instruction occurring between meetings.</p>\n<p>We also agreed to define an instructional period before administering a postassessment. Comparing the initial and later evidence will help us evaluate early changes in understanding. It will not by itself prove that grouping caused every change, but it will support a more informed discussion than impressions alone. Through shared analysis, regular check-ins, and reflection on classroom evidence, the team will monitor both the use of the strategy and students' responses to it.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Agree on dates, participants, scoring criteria, and the relationship between pre- and post-assessments.</li><li>Record how often and how the strategy was used, alongside student results.</li><li>Set a decision rule for adapting instruction and specify who will bring evidence to each meeting.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-e\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / Implementation agenda 1</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for each of the three required implementation meetings. Within each agenda, you will want to create opportunities for group members to review norms, review and analyze data, and discuss and determine the next steps. You may either use this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tEarly returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>2.\tSummary of the meeting:</p><p>3.\tNext steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Plan discussion of evidence from the strategy's initial use, not only its preparation. Include the required meeting information, norms, evidence review, summary, and next steps. A summary item on an agenda means planning time to summarize; it does not mean writing the conclusions in advance.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Connecting the implementation meetings</summary><p>Each meeting should carry the inquiry forward: revisit the previous commitment, examine evidence from actual implementation, compare interpretations, decide what to retain or adjust, and establish responsibilities and support. The next meeting examines what followed. These are implementation meetings, not repeated planning sessions.</p><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Record</th><th scope=\"col\">What it should make visible</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Record\">Evidence returning to the group</td><td data-label=\"What it should make visible\">What was tried, what students demonstrated, and what is still uncertain. Include difficulties and counterexamples.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Record\">Shared interpretation and decision</td><td data-label=\"What it should make visible\">How members used the evidence, where they disagreed, and why the group retained or adjusted the approach.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Record\">Support and follow-through</td><td data-label=\"What it should make visible\">Who will do what, what help is needed, and what evidence the group will revisit. Later minutes record what actually happened.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>An agenda anticipates this work. Minutes document it; products preserve the tools or analysis the group actually used. They should agree without copying one another.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Implementation meeting 1: Third grade, November 5, 2025, 2:30-3:30 p.m.</p>\n<p>Participation agreements: Attend to the discussion, arrive ready to contribute, consider alternative perspectives, and keep the conversation connected to the meeting purpose. The group will use these agreements while examining evidence and making decisions.</p>\n<p>Participants: Ms. W., third-grade SAS teacher and grade-level chair; Ms. A., third-grade SAS teacher; Mr. A., third-grade general education teacher; and Ms. K., second-grade teacher and facilitator.</p>\n<p>Purpose: Review the initial use of Math Crew and the information emerging from the preassessment and classroom observations. Discuss how the strategy appears to support students with different levels of readiness in addition and subtraction with regrouping. Because implementation has only recently begun, the meeting will focus on questions and emerging patterns rather than final claims about effectiveness.</p>\n<p>Initial evidence: Confirm whether teachers have completed the preassessment and invite their first observations about student responses. Compare patterns across classrooms and describe the variation in readiness within each class. Discuss early experiences organizing the groups, including student movement, routines, and expectations. Identify any questions about launching the strategy that need a shared response.</p>\n<p>Discussion summary: Name the common patterns in students' understanding of regrouping. Identify promising features and difficulties in the initial rollout, including engagement, transitions, and coordination among teachers. Record questions that remain open or need more evidence. The facilitator will draw together the observations and ask members to confirm whether the summary represents their experience.</p>\n<p>Actions before the next meeting: Continue temporary groups focused on addition and subtraction with regrouping. Refine expectations and procedures for moving between classrooms as needed. Gather further student work and observations during instruction so that the next discussion includes evidence of both learning and implementation. Identify practical supports or changes that would make the work more consistent.</p>\n<p>The record should distinguish a procedure that is operating smoothly from evidence that students have mastered the mathematics. Both kinds of information are useful at this early stage.</p>\n<p>Closing agreement: Confirm the focus of the next meeting and the information each teacher will bring. Clarify responsibilities for collecting evidence and following up on any shared concerns. The aim is to keep the first implementation cycle coordinated while allowing the team to respond to what it learns about students and the group structure.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify what instruction will have occurred before the meeting and what new evidence members will bring.</li><li>Reserve time to distinguish launch logistics from students' mathematical understanding.</li><li>Plan to record agreed actions, support, owners, and the next review date.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / Implementation agenda 2</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for each of the three required implementation meetings. Within each agenda, you will want to create opportunities for group members to review norms, review and analyze data, and discuss and determine the next steps. You may either use this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tEarly returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>2.\tSummary of the meeting:</p><p>3.\tNext steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use the second agenda to revisit the first meeting's decisions and examine new evidence. Ask what was tried, what students did, and what may need adjustment. Repeating the same agenda headings is appropriate; repeating a generic discussion without follow-through is not.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Implementation meeting 2: Third grade, November 19, 2025, 2:30-3:30 p.m.</p>\n<p>Participation agreements: Give the meeting full attention, prepare to collaborate, remain receptive to different interpretations, and stay focused on the inquiry. These agreements support a discussion in which members can compare experiences and make shared instructional decisions.</p>\n<p>Participants: Ms. W., third-grade SAS teacher and grade-level chair; Ms. A., third-grade SAS teacher; Mr. A., third-grade general education teacher; and Ms. K., second-grade teacher facilitating the community.</p>\n<p>Purpose: Reflect on the early Math Crew sessions, examine how students are responding within their current groups, and consider whether changes in teaching or placement would better meet their needs. The team will treat the available information as early implementation evidence rather than a final evaluation.</p>\n<p>Evidence and experiences: Invite each teacher to describe recent sessions and students' responses. Look for indications of engagement, developing understanding, and persistent difficulty. Discuss whether particular students may need a different group and what evidence supports that possibility. Compare the instructional strategies being used at different levels and explain how lessons are being planned and adapted for each group.</p>\n<p>Shared interpretation: Identify patterns that occur in more than one classroom. Note strengths in engagement and instruction, along with changes under consideration. Revisit whether the current focus and flexible grouping approach still fit the student needs identified in the inquiry. The facilitator will summarize the themes, connect related observations, and check whether members agree with the interpretation.</p>\n<p>Next actions: Decide which proposed instructional changes or student moves should be tried. Continue with planned lessons for each group while monitoring progress and participation. Consider whether the current cycle needs additional time before the postassessment. Identify the work samples, assessment information, or observations that will be most useful for the following meeting.</p>\n<p>Members will explain the evidence behind a proposed move so that group changes remain connected with learning needs and are understood across the team.</p>\n<p>Closing agreement: Confirm what each member will bring next time and identify any materials, coordination, or other support needed. Record the decisions so that teachers leave with the same understanding of the immediate plan. The meeting should result in specific actions that respond to students' current learning, while preserving the flexibility of the grouping arrangement as new evidence becomes available.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Bring forward specific actions agreed in meeting 1 and identify their owners.</li><li>Specify the work or assessment evidence needed to evaluate each proposed grouping change.</li><li>Plan discussion of different student experiences, including students not benefiting yet.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / Implementation agenda 3</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for each of the three required implementation meetings. Within each agenda, you will want to create opportunities for group members to review norms, review and analyze data, and discuss and determine the next steps. You may either use this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tEarly returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>2.\tSummary of the meeting:</p><p>3.\tNext steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Plan a shared examination of initial results and a decision about what comes next. Compare meaningful evidence across the implementation period and retain uncertainty about causes or measures. The final scheduled meeting should lead to specific action, not only celebration.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Implementation meeting 3: Third grade, December 10, 2025, 2:30-3:30 p.m.</p>\n<p>Participation agreements: Be attentive, prepare to contribute, consider other viewpoints, and maintain the instructional focus. Use these agreements to support shared interpretation of the evidence.</p>\n<p>Participants: Ms. W., third-grade SAS teacher and chair; Ms. A., third-grade SAS teacher; Mr. A., third-grade general education teacher; and Ms. K., second-grade teacher and facilitator.</p>\n<p>Purpose: Examine the Math Crew postassessment, identify progress and continuing needs, and agree on subsequent mathematics instruction across groups. The discussion will draw on results and observations collected over several weeks of implementation.</p>\n<p>Evidence for discussion: Share each classroom's postassessment results and describe patterns of improvement, maintained performance, or unresolved difficulty. Examine regrouping, word problems, and prerequisite skills. Compare students' responses to the group structure and teaching, including differences among levels rather than relying only on an overall impression.</p>\n<p>Synthesis: Identify patterns common to several classrooms and consider what they suggest about the current approach. Discuss features of implementation that appear useful, questions that remain unanswered, and areas needing refinement. The facilitator will connect observations across members and check that the group has a shared understanding of both progress and continuing instructional concerns.</p>\n<p>Decisions and next steps: Consider changes in instruction suggested by the postassessment. Decide whether to extend the present cycle or revise its focus, and identify possible priorities for the next period of learning. Discuss how a future preassessment could target the relevant standards more precisely. Agree on the evidence or reflections members should bring to the next meeting.</p>\n<p>Keep the interpretation connected with the skills actually assessed and the instruction students received during the cycle.</p>\n<p>The closing record will distinguish decisions already reached from questions requiring additional information. This will allow the team to continue the inquiry with a clear plan and to use the assessment as a basis for instructional adjustment rather than treat it only as an endpoint.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Prepare separate counts for improved, unchanged, and lower scores, with the matched student denominator.</li><li>Bring the assessment tasks and scoring criteria so rigor and alignment can be examined.</li><li>Plan to decide which instruction continues, changes, or ends and how the next decision will be monitored.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"F1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"F\"><span id=\"f-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-f\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / Implementation minutes 1</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Document the group’s learning progress by maintaining detailed agendas and minutes that you will submit for each implementation meeting. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nWithin the minutes, document the range of agreed-upon support you provide to the group and/or to individual members.\nBased on your implementation meeting discussion and analysis of findings, track progress, early impact, and/or initial results of the group’s implementation of the strategy (e.g., Is the strategy making a difference in what or how students are learning and/or experiencing at school?).\nDocument agreed-upon next steps the group will take between implementation meetings.</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a.\tWhat is going well?</p><p>b.\tWhat needs adjustment?</p><p>c.\tPLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part F template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Document what the first implementation meeting actually established: evidence from instruction, what is working, needed changes, member reflections, and agreed support. Include enough specificity for the next meeting to check follow-through.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Teachers reported a generally smooth beginning to Math Crew. Every participant had administered the addition and subtraction preassessment. The results showed a broad range of readiness across classrooms, reinforcing the reason for temporary skill groups. Several teachers described stronger performance in addition than in subtraction requiring regrouping. Word problems were particularly difficult, and English learners needed additional help with the academic language used in those tasks. These observations gave the team a more precise starting point than the broader iReady and SBA patterns alone.</p>\n<p>Members also described practical successes in getting the groups underway. Students were moving between classrooms, teachers had explained expectations, and procedures such as name tags and lining up were helping transitions. Practicing the movement routines appeared to make students more comfortable with rotations. I drew attention to these examples because a predictable structure can support access to instruction. They were evidence that the arrangements were becoming workable, even though they did not yet demonstrate an academic effect.</p>\n<p>Some procedures still required attention. Name tags were not used consistently, and expectations needed reinforcement across classrooms. Teachers wanted to continue developing language support for students who struggled with mathematics word problems. They also discussed the need to share information when a student required additional behavioral or safety support, including a risk of leaving the assigned area. Knowing these needs before a transition would help colleagues respond consistently. I asked how clearer communication and common expectations could address the concerns and recorded the areas requiring follow-up.</p>\n<p>To encourage reflection, I invited teachers to compare the preassessment with their expectations and describe their first experiences teaching the groups. They said the results largely confirmed needs they had suspected from the earlier assessment information. Members also reported that planning together and agreeing on expectations increased their confidence. Sharing responsibility made implementation feel more coordinated than arranging groups independently. Several noted that students seemed to enjoy working with another teacher, which appeared to support engagement during the new routine.</p>\n<p>The team had connected the Math Crew name with the school's existing character and growth-mindset framework. I acknowledged how that connection could help students understand and accept the new arrangement. I emphasized common language so that students would hear a consistent explanation of the groups' purpose and encounter familiar expectations in each room. Teachers felt that this shared approach was helping students manage transitions and begin taking responsibility for the routines. The discussion linked student participation with deliberate adult coordination rather than attribute a smooth start only to student enthusiasm.</p>\n<p>I also encouraged proactive communication about academic, behavioral, and transition needs. Teachers described how exchanging this information helped them support students safely in another classroom. I affirmed the value of those practices and asked members to continue sharing relevant information as group membership changed. Flexible placement requires continuing communication, because a receiving teacher may need to know more than the student's assessment score. The group regarded these conversations as part of implementation rather than an unrelated administrative task.</p>\n<p>In summarizing the meeting, I connected the preassessment patterns with the initial classroom observations. The team continued to see addition and subtraction with regrouping as an appropriate focus. Members identified engagement, clearer expectations, and improving transitions as early strengths, while language scaffolds and consistent procedures needed further attention. We agreed to continue Math Crew and refine the arrangements as more evidence became available.</p>\n<p>The discussion also reinforced why logistical details belong in an instructional meeting during a new implementation. Students need to know where to go and what to expect before they can use the learning time well. Consistent procedures allow teachers to focus more of that time on the different academic needs identified by the assessment.</p>\n<p>Before the next meeting, teachers would collect student work, observations, and notes about responses to instruction. They would continue using the initial assessment to inform groups while watching for evidence that a placement or lesson needed to change. I would organize shared observations, support check-ins, and facilitate the next reflection. Our next discussion would examine early progress, possible changes in grouping or teaching, and the supports needed by students requiring additional scaffolding or enrichment.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Record one concrete early learning pattern alongside the launch logistics.</li><li>Distinguish member reports, documents examined, and your own observations.</li><li>Assign the agreed procedural or instructional changes to named roles and set a review date.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"F2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"F\"><span id=\"f-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / Implementation minutes 2</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Document the group’s learning progress by maintaining detailed agendas and minutes that you will submit for each implementation meeting. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nWithin the minutes, document the range of agreed-upon support you provide to the group and/or to individual members.\nBased on your implementation meeting discussion and analysis of findings, track progress, early impact, and/or initial results of the group’s implementation of the strategy (e.g., Is the strategy making a difference in what or how students are learning and/or experiencing at school?).\nDocument agreed-upon next steps the group will take between implementation meetings.</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a.\tWhat is going well?</p><p>b.\tWhat needs adjustment?</p><p>c.\tPLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part F template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Show what the group learned after trying the strategy and carrying out prior decisions. Record the evidence behind adjustments, including student movement between groups, instructional supports, and the support you agreed to provide.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I opened the discussion by asking teachers what they were noticing as Math Crew continued. They reported that students generally responded positively to the rotation structure and seemed motivated by working with different teachers. Several described students looking forward to the group period. Some learners were already demonstrating strong addition-with-regrouping skills and appeared ready for more demanding work. Other teachers described success using visual models, gestures, and clearly sequenced steps to help students interpret and solve word problems. These examples gave the group concrete teaching practices to discuss rather than rely only on a general impression that students enjoyed the groups.</p>\n<p>I connected these accounts to the purpose of intentional grouping and planning. The observations suggested that teachers were creating more accessible entry points while also identifying students who needed additional challenge. Members working with advanced groups said students were eager to attempt more complex problems. I acknowledged this as an indication that matching challenge to readiness was relevant across the range of learners. I summarized the shared pattern: students were responding at different levels, but teachers were finding ways to engage them with work appropriate to those levels. These remained early indicators from classroom experience, requiring continued review alongside learning evidence.</p>\n<p>We then examined what needed refinement. Word problems remained difficult for some students, particularly when they involved comparison language or unfamiliar academic vocabulary. Teachers also described uncertainty about ones, tens, and hundreds during regrouping. I asked members to explain how they were responding. They discussed adding visual representations, modeling their reasoning, using gestures, and breaking a procedure into an explicit sequence. This allowed colleagues to connect a particular difficulty with a particular instructional adjustment and consider whether that adjustment might be useful in another group.</p>\n<p>Placement was another area for review. Some students were progressing more quickly than expected, while others revealed needs that had not been obvious at the outset. Teachers discussed moving students between groups. I emphasized that such movement is central to a flexible arrangement: a change in placement can show that teachers are attending to current evidence rather than indicate that the initial plan was unsuccessful. We also considered pace. Students ready to advance needed continued challenge, while others required more time with foundational concepts. Toward the end of this discussion, the team agreed that the current cycle might need to run longer before we moved to a new focus.</p>\n<p>I helped the group identify three connected areas for adjustment: the instructional supports within each group, the evidence used for placement changes, and the time available for learning. Framing these as normal parts of implementation made room for teachers to discuss difficulty openly. The purpose was to improve the fit between instruction and students rather than defend the original plan. Members could then consider revisions without treating every change as a departure from the agreed strategy.</p>\n<p>During reflection, teachers again noted students' interest in rotations and the opportunity to learn with different adults. They described greater participation and a growing sense of responsibility for learning. They also identified explicit demonstrations, visuals, and hands-on materials as useful supports for regrouping and word problems. I acknowledged these observations while drawing attention to the team's role in making the supports available. Members were comparing experiences, modifying tasks, moving students when appropriate, and planning different levels of challenge. Their collaboration was becoming visible in instructional decisions rather than only in meeting attendance.</p>\n<p>I summarized that teachers were beginning to plan deliberately for each group's needs. This included selecting tasks, deciding how much support to provide, and identifying opportunities for extension. Sharing those decisions helped the grade level maintain coherence while avoiding identical instruction for students with different starting points. I connected that work with shared responsibility for student learning and more equitable access to suitable instruction. The team would still need to study outcomes over time before making a broad judgment about effectiveness.</p>\n<p>The extension of the cycle was therefore a response to evidence, not simply a change of calendar. Teachers wanted enough time for students to develop understanding before drawing conclusions from the later assessment. At the same time, students ready for greater challenge would continue receiving differentiated tasks rather than wait for every group to move together.</p>\n<p>At the close, we agreed to extend the current cycle and continue monitoring progress. Teachers would keep planning targeted lessons using foundational practice, visual modeling, and enrichment tasks. They would reconsider placement as new evidence emerged and document observations or student work for the next meeting. I clarified what members should bring and restated my role in supporting coordination and reflection. The next discussion would review the effects of continued instruction, decide whether further refinements were needed, and consider readiness for a new content focus. These actions linked the meeting's observations with an explicit plan for the next stage of implementation.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the evidence supporting at least one group movement or instructional adjustment.</li><li>Report completion and results of actions agreed in the previous meeting.</li><li>Record who will provide the agreed support and what evidence will return in meeting 3.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"F3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"F\"><span id=\"f-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / Implementation minutes 3</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Document the group’s learning progress by maintaining detailed agendas and minutes that you will submit for each implementation meeting. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nWithin the minutes, document the range of agreed-upon support you provide to the group and/or to individual members.\nBased on your implementation meeting discussion and analysis of findings, track progress, early impact, and/or initial results of the group’s implementation of the strategy (e.g., Is the strategy making a difference in what or how students are learning and/or experiencing at school?).\nDocument agreed-upon next steps the group will take between implementation meetings.</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a.\tWhat is going well?</p><p>b.\tWhat needs adjustment?</p><p>c.\tPLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part F template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Record the group's interpretation of initial results, including successes, continuing needs, limitations, and next actions. Show how members reached a shared conclusion rather than simply declaring that the strategy worked.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I facilitated a review of the postassessment after several weeks of Math Crew instruction. Teachers reported that most students had maintained or improved their performance. One account identified growth for 14 of 17 students, while another described 17 of 20 as maintaining or improving. Many students gained one or two performance points. The distinction between growth and maintaining a score needs to remain visible when interpreting those reports. The results nevertheless gave the team a concrete basis for examining progress rather than relying exclusively on students' enthusiasm for the groups.</p>\n<p>We compared patterns across classrooms. Teachers described how grouping had allowed them to match instruction more closely to readiness. Students prepared for greater challenge worked on more complex tasks, while others received focused teaching in place value and regrouping. I highlighted the relationship between these instructional decisions and the progress teachers described. The evidence suggested promising changes in students' access and engagement, although a before-and-after comparison alone cannot isolate the effect of grouping from other instruction during the same period. The discussion therefore considered both the results and the way the strategy had been used.</p>\n<p>We also examined students who showed less progress. Teachers identified continuing difficulties with foundational place value and a need for additional targeted support. Members raised concerns about the postassessment itself, including whether some items matched the instruction students had received and whether their complexity was appropriate for all groups. These concerns mattered because an assessment can be difficult for reasons beyond the particular skill the team intended to measure. I asked members to consider pacing and assessment design together rather than interpret every low result as the same learning problem.</p>\n<p>The group agreed that future assessments should connect more precisely with the selected standards and skill progression. Teachers also wanted to check placement more often so students could move sooner when they needed different support or challenge. I emphasized that identifying these changes was part of responsive implementation. The evidence was useful because it helped the team reconsider its practice, not because it produced an uncomplicated success story. This discussion gave us specific improvements to carry into the next cycle.</p>\n<p>In reflecting on the strategy, teachers said that a narrower range of readiness within each group made focused teaching more manageable. They could respond to misconceptions more quickly and select tasks suited to the learners present. Reviewing results with colleagues also increased their confidence in decisions about grouping, lesson planning, and task modification. I acknowledged how this shared analysis supported a collective responsibility for outcomes. The teachers were using information from several classrooms to guide their decisions rather than treating each classroom as a separate inquiry.</p>\n<p>My summary brought together the positive performance patterns, continuing place-value needs, and assessment-alignment concerns. The group saw value in maintaining a flexible structure while improving how it assessed and responded to learning. We discussed moving most students toward multiplication and division, with continued small-group instruction in addition and subtraction for those who needed it. That direction allowed the team to advance the curriculum without assuming that every student was ready for the same next step.</p>\n<p>The meeting also clarified that progress should be examined within groups as well as across the grade. A generally positive total can coexist with students who still need substantial help. Teachers wanted the next plan to preserve that distinction. The assessment discussion was similarly specific: greater rigor is useful when it reveals the intended learning, but a task that does not align with the taught skill can make interpretation harder. These considerations informed both the proposed new content and the continued foundational support.</p>\n<p>For the next cycle, teachers agreed to revise the preassessment around specific standards and consider released or standards-based items. They would bring updated instructional plans and assessment tools to the next meeting. Continued monitoring would help determine whether students needed more foundational practice or were ready for greater challenge. I clarified these expectations and connected them with the concerns raised during the meeting. Our next discussion would examine how the revised assessment process supports grouping and instruction. The outcome was a plan to retain useful features of the strategy while addressing the limitations the evidence had revealed.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Link each reported result to the exact assessment, scoring method, and group of students.</li><li>Record the differing interpretations and how the group resolved or retained uncertainty.</li><li>Specify the next instructional response for students needing reinforcement and those ready to extend learning.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"G\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"G\"><span id=\"g-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-g\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part G / Collaborative work products</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Collect and submit key collaborative work products (up to 10 pages) used during the implementation meetings (e.g., lesson plans, student work products, instructional materials, assessment tools, data or evidence collected).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 19; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit actual products used or created in the community's work, within the handbook's page limit. Explain their purpose, when the group used them, and which decisions they support. A product can document planning, implementation, or a result, but those functions should not be confused.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Work product 1: Two-page regrouping planning record. The team developed this document together to define its addition and subtraction focus. It connects lesson planning, task choices, and the interpretation of student understanding so that teachers can coordinate their work. The two pages serve as a common reference for the instructional cycle.</p>\n<p>Work product 2: Initial planning minutes. This record describes the team's development of a preassessment and its decisions about the practical arrangements for small-group mathematics. It shows how assessment preparation and classroom logistics were considered together before implementation.</p>\n<p>Work product 3: Scoring and grouping minutes. These notes record the discussion of initial assessment results, the formation of temporary instructional groups, and the allocation of teaching responsibilities. They connect student evidence with the organization of targeted instruction.</p>\n<p>Work product 4: Two-page common assessment. The blank instrument represents the tasks used before and after instruction to examine students' understanding of regrouping. Its role is to support comparisons over the cycle and inform subsequent teaching, grouping, and reflection.</p>\n<p>Work product 5: Class assessment summary. This record presents initial and later scores for 17 students without identifying them. The commentary associated with the third clip refers to it during discussion of student outcomes and the level of assessment demand.</p>\n<p>Each record has a distinct place in the sequence from initial planning to the review of results.</p>\n<p>Work product 6: Additional postassessment discussed with clip 3. This blank assessment is referenced in the discussion of whether the tasks matched the instructional focus and expected rigor. Together, the products document planning, implementation, and reflection; their descriptions do not substitute for independently checking the underlying evidence.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Label each product with its date, purpose, group use, and relationship to a specific decision.</li><li>Distinguish blank tools, completed work, aggregated results, and the assessment that raised alignment concerns.</li><li>Reconcile the visible score pairs, the 14-of-17 heading, and the broader 72-of-85 report; distinguish growth, maintenance, and decline.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"H\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"H\"><span id=\"h-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-h\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part H / Implementation meeting video clips</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: From the videos of the implementation meetings where you and the individuals participating are seen and heard, select and submit 3 video clips, each up to 5 minutes (assessors will only watch and score the first 5 minutes of each video clip submitted), that include the following:</p><p>Video Clip 1: Setting the Purpose and Data Review (up to 5 minutes) o Select/create a video clip that demonstrates how you set the purpose for the meeting and how you facilitate the community of practice in analyzing the data from the implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy. • Video Clip 2: Professional Collaboration (up to 5 minutes) o Select/create a video clip that demonstrates your facilitation of the community of practice and document their agreements, their decisions, and how they arrive at a consensual understanding of the initial implementation results. Also include how you facilitate the community of practice to (a) respect diverse viewpoints, (b) respond to individual and collective feedback, (c) maintain the group’s focus and energy, and (d) monitor progress toward goals of the meetings.</p><p>Video Clip 3: Results and Next Steps (up to 5 minutes) • Select/create a video clip that demonstrates how you facilitate the community of practice in reporting and discussing the results of their implementation of the evidenced-based strategy and jointly plan for next steps/between meetings.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 20 and following page; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit the three required clips from authentic implementation meetings: setting purpose and examining data; professional collaboration; and results with jointly planned next steps. The handbook specifies up to five minutes for each clip. The written commentary must refer to what is actually visible and audible.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The commentary describes several recordings that were unavailable for inspection. This section therefore contains neither a replacement video nor a fabricated account of recorded dialogue.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Check the actual recordings against the three required functions and the time limits.</li><li>Verify that the candidate and required participants can be seen and heard and that permissions are in place.</li><li>Ensure each commentary claim aligns to its cited clip and timestamp; do not stage or script missing evidence.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-i\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 1 / Establishing meeting purpose</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe how you set the purpose for the meetings/reviewed the problem of practice with the group.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use a specific clip moment to explain how you established the meeting's purpose and connected it to the problem of practice. Include what you did, why, and the response or change in the discussion that supports your interpretation of its impact.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The first clip is described as opening with a welcome and a statement that the team would review the beginning of Math Crew implementation (0:00-0:28). I then revisit the working agreements: attentive participation, preparation, openness to other perspectives, and maintaining the focus. I ask members to confirm those agreements before proceeding (0:29-0:53). This opening is intended to establish both a respectful way of working and a shared reason for the meeting. The emphasis is on collective responsibility for the inquiry rather than separate reports from individual classrooms.</p>\n<p>I next connect the discussion with earlier evidence and decisions. The commentary references our SBA and iReady review and the selection of addition and subtraction with regrouping as a prerequisite for grade-level mathematics (0:55-1:26). I restate the instructional problem as a need for more targeted, differentiated teaching in foundational numeracy so students can progress toward grade-level expectations (1:26-1:40). I also revisit flexible grouping through Math Crew and ask whether the team remains ready to continue with the approach (1:40-2:16). Returning to these decisions keeps the relationship between adult practice and student learning visible across meetings.</p>\n<p>The opening also provides an opportunity to renew agreement rather than assume that a previous decision still represents everyone's view. Confirming the strategy before analysis makes later discussion of adjustments easier to locate within the shared plan. It gives the team a common reference for distinguishing a change in implementation from a change in the overall purpose.</p>\n<p>The third clip is described as beginning with another explicit statement of purpose. After several weeks of implementation, the team would examine postassessment evidence and decide what should happen next (0:00-0:18). I frame the conversation as learning about the strategy's results and using that learning to guide subsequent action. This repeated opening links the meetings as stages in an ongoing inquiry rather than unrelated conversations. Across the two examples, purpose-setting involves identifying the current stage of the work while retaining the same instructional concern. The timestamps are references in the written commentary; the recordings themselves were not available to verify the described interactions.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Select a precise moment that includes both the purpose-setting action and the group's response.</li><li>Explain why that framing was needed at that point in implementation.</li><li>Describe its observable effect without assuming that a welcome or nod demonstrates shared understanding.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 2 / Evaluating implementation data</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tDescribe how you and the community of practice evaluated data from the implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group interpreted evidence from using the strategy. Distinguish baseline information, implementation observations, and post-assessment findings. Show your role in helping members examine what the evidence does and does not establish.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Across the three described clips, the discussion moves from initial assessment information to classroom implementation evidence and then to postassessment results. My intention is to help teachers interpret what the evidence suggests about instruction, not simply report scores. That progression gives the data a role in revising practice and deciding what support students need next.</p>\n<p>In clip 1, I invite members to describe what they noticed on the assessment used for grouping (2:17-2:39). Teachers identify subtraction with regrouping and word problems as particular difficulties (2:44-4:12). I draw together observations across classrooms and identify the language demands of word problems as an equity concern for English learners (4:13-4:41). This synthesis helps turn individual accounts into a shared view of the instructional needs the team should monitor.</p>\n<p>Clip 2 shifts to what teachers are seeing while the groups operate. I begin by asking whether those observations suggest any adjustments (0:00-0:09). Members describe students ready for additional challenge or a different group (0:12-0:31), and students struggling with comparison language in word problems (0:34-0:47). They explain their use of visuals, gestures, and sequenced problem-solving steps (0:49-1:20). Other contributions concern students' motivation when tasks provide an appropriate level of challenge (1:27-1:58).</p>\n<p>I connect these examples with the purpose of flexible grouping. Different responses help teachers identify readiness and adapt instruction. Later in the clip, I explain that movement among groups is expected as students develop or reveal further needs (4:27-4:45). This frames adjustment as part of the agreed strategy rather than evidence that implementation is inconsistent.</p>\n<p>The three stages use different kinds of information for different decisions. Initial results help identify where to begin, classroom observations help teachers adjust while instruction is underway, and later results help them consider what to retain or change. Keeping that sequence visible supports a continuing inquiry instead of treating one assessment result as a complete evaluation of the approach.</p>\n<p>In clip 3, teachers discuss postassessment figures, including 14 of 17 and 17 of 20 students described as maintaining or improving (0:19-1:21). They report one- or two-point increases for many students and identify place-value needs among those with less progress (0:41-1:21). I summarize the broadly positive pattern while acknowledging continuing support needs (2:48-3:01). The discussion considers both promising results and limitations, including the meaning of maintaining a score. These descriptions show the intended movement from evidence to interpretation, but the unavailable recordings prevent independent confirmation of the interactions and the precise language used.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify which evidence at each timestamp is baseline, implementation, or outcome evidence.</li><li>Report the actual comparison, including students with no gain or a lower score.</li><li>Explain the question or facilitation move that helped the group test an interpretation rather than merely share results.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 3 / Reaching shared understanding</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tDescribe any agreements or decisions made by the group and how you facilitated the community of practice to arrive at a consensual understanding of any initial implementation results.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe the agreement and how members arrived at it. Identify the evidence, alternatives or concerns, and your action in checking understanding. Agreement is not established solely because the facilitator summarized a conclusion.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>In the first clip, I begin by confirming the team's common understanding of the problem and selected approach before turning to assessment analysis (0:29-2:16). After teachers describe initial results, I connect their accounts by identifying shared needs in subtraction with regrouping and the language of word problems (4:13-4:41). This summary is intended to help members agree on what the information suggests and what deserves attention during implementation. Agreement is grounded in the observations members have contributed.</p>\n<p>The second clip concerns decisions about teaching and group placement. Teachers discuss moving students when their readiness changes (0:12-0:31 and 2:27-3:27). I reinforce that changing groups is appropriate when current evidence indicates a different need (4:27-4:45). Presenting movement as an expected feature of the approach supports continued use of the strategy while leaving room for revision. It helps the team avoid treating the original grouping plan as fixed.</p>\n<p>In the third clip, consensus develops around the interpretation of results and the next instructional direction. Teachers report both progress and continuing needs. I ask what the results suggest we should do next (3:37-3:41). A member explains that a narrower readiness range makes focused support easier (3:43-4:10), then suggests making the next preassessment more specific (4:14-4:26). I summarize a direction in which many students move to new skills while others continue receiving small-group or push-in assistance (4:28-4:46).</p>\n<p>The summaries also make the basis for the next decision visible. Members can hear how their comments have been combined and can correct an interpretation before it becomes the team's plan. That checking process is particularly relevant when students need different next steps.</p>\n<p>Before the clip ends, I restate the next steps and relate them to the assessment discussion and teacher reflections (4:46-4:59). The intention is to confirm that the plan represents the group's contributions and addresses different readiness levels. Across the meetings, synthesis and confirmation help connect individual observations with collective action. A written description of consensus still has limits: the recordings were not available to examine whether each member explicitly agreed or how disagreement was handled. The commentary should therefore be read as an account of the facilitation, with its evidence status kept visible.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>State one precise decision and the evidence used to make it.</li><li>Describe a differing view or uncertainty and how it influenced the decision.</li><li>Identify the clip moment where members confirmed the shared interpretation or retained an unresolved question.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 4 / Supporting equitable participation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tDescribe how you</p><p>valued and respected diverse viewpoints,</p><p>responded to individual and collective feedback,</p><p>maintained the group’s focus and energy, and</p><p>monitored progress toward the goals of the meetings.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all four components: diverse viewpoints, individual and collective feedback, group focus and energy, and progress toward the meeting goals. Analyze the effect of your actions on participation and reasoning, not simply your use of polite language.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Across the three described clips, I use questions, acknowledgement, and summaries to support teacher participation while keeping the instructional purpose visible. Different interpretations can contribute to a fuller understanding of students rather than simply slow agreement.</p>\n<p>In clip 1, I revisit agreements about respect and openness to other perspectives (0:29-0:52). I invite each teacher to contribute observations (2:27-2:40). When members identify different concerns, including subtraction difficulty and language access for English learners, I connect them in the summary rather than select only one as relevant (4:13-4:41). This gives several kinds of classroom evidence a place in the shared interpretation.</p>\n<p>Clip 2 opens with a focused question about changes suggested by current observations (0:00-0:09). I use follow-up questions to clarify contributions. For example, when a teacher describes a group as especially difficult, I clarify whether the reference concerns the level of challenge and the support required (1:50-1:57). Later, I summarize the different supports groups need and the expected movement between them (4:27-5:00). These moves are intended to maintain a useful instructional focus without dismissing teachers' experiences.</p>\n<p>Maintaining focus therefore involves both inviting contributions and connecting them with the purpose. Acknowledging a concern is followed by asking what it means for instruction, assessment, or the next decision the team needs to make.</p>\n<p>In clip 3, I identify the purpose as reviewing results and deciding next steps (0:00-0:18). I listen to concerns about assessment demand and students with limited progress (1:32-2:46), acknowledge those concerns, and also note the broadly positive pattern reported by teachers (2:48-3:01). I then ask the group to consider subsequent action (3:37-3:41). The account illustrates an effort to balance recognition of strengths with attention to unresolved needs. Because the clips were unavailable, the written commentary cannot by itself verify how equitably speaking time was distributed or how participants experienced these facilitation choices.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify who contributed at the cited moments and how you invited perspectives not yet heard.</li><li>Show how feedback changed the discussion or decision, rather than only noting that it was acknowledged.</li><li>Connect a specific focus or pacing adjustment to progress on the meeting's goal.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 5 / Determining next steps together</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>5.\tDescribe how you collaborated with the group to determine any next steps for implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Show how evidence led the group to agree on its next actions. Explain what the candidate and members will do, how the plan responds to different needs, and how later evidence will reveal whether the response was useful.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Across the meetings, I connect evidence with action by posing questions, summarizing proposals, and checking the group's direction. The purpose is to make subsequent steps emerge from shared discussion rather than assign them independently after the meeting.</p>\n<p>In clip 1, after the preassessment discussion, I ask whether groups have started and how frequently they are meeting (4:44-4:58). This links the initial data with the practical use of the strategy and establishes that implementation itself needs monitoring.</p>\n<p>In clip 2, members propose changes in placement, additional visuals and gestures, and renewed attention to place-value concepts (0:12-1:21 and 2:27-3:52). I acknowledge these suggestions and explain that changes in grouping and teaching belong within the flexible approach (4:27-4:45). This supports the team's willingness to adapt the plan as students respond.</p>\n<p>Clip 3 focuses on the postassessment and the next content priorities. I ask how we should respond both to students needing reinforcement and to those ready to advance (3:34-3:41). Teachers support continued small-group addition and subtraction practice for some learners while most begin multiplication and division (4:27-4:44). They also identify a need for assessments that more precisely match the intended standards and reveal students' understanding (4:15-4:25).</p>\n<p>The action sequence includes implementation frequency, instructional supports, placement decisions, new content, and assessment design. Each is connected with evidence discussed by the team rather than introduced as an unrelated task.</p>\n<p>I close by summarizing these decisions so members leave with a shared account of responsibilities and direction. The described next steps connect teacher dialogue with student evidence and the goal of access to grade-level mathematics. The written commentary supplies the sequence and timestamps; it does not replace direct review of the recordings, which were unavailable for inspection.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each next step to a stated finding and the students it is intended to support.</li><li>Record the responsible person, needed support, timeline, and evidence for the next review.</li><li>Trace at least one action from agreement to implementation to the following meeting's interpretation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-j\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 1 / Results, feedback, and leadership growth</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tRefer to specific evidence (including agendas, minutes, group work products, implementation data, videos, and/or participant feedback) to analyze how your leadership actions influenced the community of practice’s progress toward its goal. Reflect on initial implementation results and feedback from the group to evaluate your strengths and areas for growth as a facilitator. Make explicit connections between the evidence, your leadership skills, equitable&nbsp;facilitation practices, and your next steps for professional growth.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Bring the evidence, your leadership actions, initial results, member feedback, strengths, growth needs, equitable facilitation, and professional next steps into one analysis. The question asks what the evidence teaches you about your leadership, not only whether the group completed its work.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>My leadership in Cycle 2 centered on establishing the conditions for a community of practice to move from planning into instruction and reflection. I prepared structures and shared resources that helped members focus on the problem of students' understanding of addition and subtraction with regrouping. The aim was to make targeted small-group teaching a coordinated inquiry rather than a set of unrelated classroom activities. Preparation mattered because members needed both an instructional purpose and workable arrangements before they could study students' responses.</p>\n<p>After the initial planning session, I facilitated another meeting devoted to the common preassessment and implementation logistics. The team developed the assessment together and clarified a regular weekly period for small-group instruction, as recorded in work product 2. Establishing the tool, schedule, and content focus in advance reduced the number of practical questions teachers had to resolve separately. It allowed the subsequent discussion to concentrate on students' needs and the teaching those needs required.</p>\n<p>I did not attend the following data-analysis meeting. The minutes nevertheless show that members continued the work: they scored the initial assessment together, compared patterns, and formed groups around the identified skills, as documented in work product 3. This is relevant evidence of continuity and shared responsibility. The team could act without waiting for me to direct each step. It suggests that the earlier planning supported distributed leadership, although it would be an overstatement to attribute every independent decision solely to my facilitation.</p>\n<p>The written account of clip 1 describes teachers referring to common instructional practices during early implementation. The shared assessment, work product 4, supplied initial and later measures of understanding. In the account of clip 3, teachers use the class score summary in work product 5 to discuss outcomes and question assessment demand and alignment. These connections among tools, meetings, and instruction show how the community intended to monitor early results and revise practice. The recordings were unavailable for independent review, so the written descriptions remain the evidence available for those interactions.</p>\n<p>Advance preparation and clear structure were strengths in my facilitation. Planning for both instruction and logistics helped members continue the inquiry, including at a meeting I could not attend. The minutes and commentary describe teachers analyzing results, grouping students, and discussing instruction in relation to the same goal. The aggregate report indicates that 72 of 85 students, approximately 85%, maintained or improved their scores. For students who gained one or two points on an eight-point assessment, those gains represent 12.5 or 25 percentage points of the available scale. Maintaining and improving should be distinguished, and these changes should not be described as proof that the strategy alone caused growth. They do, however, provide evidence for the team's reflection.</p>\n<p>A second strength was supporting an atmosphere in which teachers could discuss difficulties honestly. The account of clip 3 includes concern that a postassessment demanded more than some students had encountered in instruction. We treated that concern as a question about alignment rather than an occasion to blame a teacher or student. Members' willingness to raise the issue suggests a degree of trust and shared ownership. It also gave the group useful information for revising future assessments and deciding what students still needed.</p>\n<p>When I asked for feedback, members described the meetings as organized, collaborative, and supportive of candid reflection. Several expressed interest in having me facilitate future discussions. I value that feedback, while recognizing that positive comments should be considered alongside observed practice and specific examples. It encouraged me to retain the structures that were useful and to improve the moments where my facilitation could be clearer. Creating conditions for professional learning includes making it possible to examine misalignment without defensiveness and turn that examination into an instructional decision.</p>\n<p>My clearest growth area was synthesizing contributions while a discussion was still unfolding. At times, I found it difficult to retain the details from several teachers and immediately articulate the pattern connecting them. Reviewing the written record and my account of the recordings helped me identify places where a pause, a paraphrase, or visible notes could have improved accuracy. I want to slow the conversation deliberately when needed so that a summary represents the contributions rather than merely the comments I remember most readily.</p>\n<p>The group's continued work during my absence also challenged the idea that facilitation requires constant control. Shared tools and a clear purpose can allow members to lead, while the facilitator helps maintain continuity across meetings. I want to build on that experience by making opportunities for member leadership more deliberate.</p>\n<p>This is also an equity concern. Listening carefully, checking interpretations, and making ideas visible can prevent the most confident voices from determining the group's conclusion too early. Documentation has a related role: the minutes preserved decisions and allowed the inquiry to continue between meetings. In future facilitation, I would use shared notes during the conversation to capture patterns, questions, and next steps as they emerge. These changes would build on my preparation strengths while improving real-time synthesis, representation of different perspectives, and clarity about collective decisions.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Separate improved, unchanged, and lower scores; state the score scale and correct the units for gains.</li><li>Use a specific member comment and a meeting event to explain a strength and a growth need.</li><li>Connect the proposed changes in listening, synthesis, and documentation to equitable participation and a way to evaluate your growth.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 2 / Professionalism, integrity, and equity</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tRefer to specific evidence (including the agendas and minutes, group work products and initial implementation results, videos and commentary, and/or group feedback on the process) of how effectively you maintained a high standard of professionalism, integrity, and equity (e.g., respect for diverse viewpoints) as you worked with members of the community of practice.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Analyze how you acted professionally and equitably throughout the inquiry. Use decisions or difficult moments to show how you treated disagreement, represented evidence accurately, and shared influence. Listing norms is not the same as demonstrating their use.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I sought to demonstrate professionalism and integrity by keeping the discussion respectful, centered on student evidence, and open to shared decisions. At the additional planning meeting, I organized the agenda so members could contribute to assessment design and instructional arrangements, as shown in work product 2. We used the common student information to guide decisions rather than treat an individual's preference as sufficient justification. This supported a professional learning environment in which colleagues could explain their reasoning and influence the plan.</p>\n<p>The working agreements also supported equity in participation. Openness to different perspectives, focused discussion, and respectful interaction were identified in the planning agenda and revisited in later agendas. These agreements were intended to give different instructional experiences a place in the conversation while maintaining the common goal. Their value depends on how they are enacted, including whether members' ideas are heard and accurately represented.</p>\n<p>During implementation discussions, I encouraged candid examination of practice and acknowledged teachers' professional knowledge. The account of clip 3 describes concern that a postassessment, identified in work product 6, exceeded the demand of the instruction. I guided the discussion toward the relationship between teaching and assessment and the changes needed next. Focusing on that relationship allowed the team to consider a difficulty without assigning blame. It illustrates the kind of trust and professional responsibility I wanted to support: use a concern as evidence for learning and improvement. The written account describes this intention and action, while the unavailable recording limits independent verification.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Analyze a specific difficult exchange or disagreement and its effect on participation.</li><li>Show how members' contributions changed a decision across more than one stage of the cycle.</li><li>Explain how assessment limitations changed what you were willing to claim about learning.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 3 / Responding to school context</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tWhat aspects of school culture and context influenced the facilitation of a community of practice at your school site/educational setting? What actions did you take to respond to these contextually based challenges or supports?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain which features of school culture or scheduling supported or constrained the work, what you did in response, and what resulted. Distinguish the group's independent work from meetings you personally facilitated.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>School conditions shaped both the implementation plan and my facilitation. Limited instructional time made it necessary to use existing arrangements carefully. The team established a dependable weekly period for small groups that fit the school schedule and mathematics priorities. This helped make the strategy feasible without displacing the core instruction it was intended to support.</p>\n<p>Grade-level collaboration time was another important condition. Teams have scheduled opportunities to meet twice a month while students attend physical education. Because I teach a different grade, I could not attend the second community meeting held during that period. I supported the teachers' decision to use the time rather than ask them to wait for me. They wanted to score the preassessment and form groups, and their initiative indicated growing ownership of the inquiry.</p>\n<p>The school's emphasis on collaboration and shared leadership helped make that possible. Members continued analysis and planning independently. My response was to ensure that the earlier structures were clear and then support coordination and reflection in subsequent meetings. I did not need to reclaim control simply because the team had progressed without my presence. Their ability to continue was relevant to the community's development.</p>\n<p>Using existing time also respected teachers' responsibilities and allowed their motivation to carry the work forward within the school's established schedule.</p>\n<p>Teachers also brought different levels of experience with regrouping instruction. I kept the discussion connected to common student evidence so those differences could become resources for problem-solving rather than reasons to label colleagues or students negatively. This supported a shared responsibility for learners across classrooms. Together, the scheduling arrangements, collaborative culture, and varied instructional experience required facilitation that combined preparation with flexibility. The work had to fit the setting while still allowing members to shape and sustain the inquiry themselves.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Provide a dated chronology separating additional planning sessions from the required implementation meetings.</li><li>Identify which meetings you attended and what you learned from records of others.</li><li>Explain how support before and after the independent session affected the work without claiming actions you did not perform.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 4 / Professional learning goals</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tBased on the analysis of your experiences in Cycle 2, identify specific professional learning goals and describe your next steps for growth.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Set specific professional learning goals derived from the cycle's evidence. Explain what you will practice or study, where you will apply it, what feedback you will seek, and how you will judge progress.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>My next professional learning goals follow from the strengths and difficulties identified in the meeting records and reflection. First, I want to improve how I synthesize contributions during a discussion. Preparation helped the community work productively, but I need to pause more consistently, paraphrase ideas, and record them where everyone can see them. I plan to use structured shared notes and deliberate pauses so patterns can be identified accurately and different voices remain represented in the summary.</p>\n<p>Second, I want to develop shared leadership more intentionally. The team already demonstrated that it could continue the inquiry without me. I can build on that by inviting members to lead parts of meetings, guide a data discussion, or summarize the group's thinking. I will ask about their interests and prepare agendas that distribute these responsibilities. This should deepen participation and ownership while giving members opportunities to practice facilitation themselves.</p>\n<p>Member feedback will help me judge the usefulness of these changes.</p>\n<p>I also plan to use documentation more actively during meetings, capturing decisions and next steps as the conversation develops rather than reconstruct them afterward. Brief opportunities for participant feedback will help me learn whether those changes improve clarity and inclusion. These goals connect directly to the cycle: retain useful planning structures, strengthen listening and synthesis, and create more opportunities for the community to lead its own work.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Turn each goal into a specific practice to try in an identified future meeting or inquiry cycle.</li><li>Specify the feedback and meeting evidence that will show whether the practice helped.</li><li>Set a review point to examine progress and revise the professional learning plan.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><section id=\"alignment\" class=\"major-section\"><h2>Read the template and handbook together</h2><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\"><table class=\"audit\"><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Current template</th><th>Current handbook</th><th>Meaning for the student</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part A inquiry</td><td>Question I.1 separates formal training, professional learning, communities of practice, and adult-learning research.</td><td>Investigation directions and Rubric 2.1 connect practices to student learning or well-being (pp. 6-8, 11).</td><td>Answer each subpart, then explain relationships. Meeting frequency is not evidence of impact.</td></tr><tr><td>Part D numbering</td><td>The two sections are labeled II and III.</td><td>Planning directions label the corresponding sections I and II (pp. 14-15).</td><td>Match by task and section title. Preserve the supplied template labels; seek program clarification rather than renumbering official text.</td></tr><tr><td>Agendas and minutes</td><td>Parts B, C, E, and F offer optional meeting-record formats.</td><td>Planning records and at least three implementation agendas and minutes are required (pp. 13-15, 18-19).</td><td>Optional format does not mean optional evidence. Actual implementation must occur while the implementation meetings are being conducted.</td></tr><tr><td>Video commentary</td><td>Part I provides five prompts and clip, timestamp, action, reason, and impact fields.</td><td>All five prompts must be addressed at least once across three clips, with aligned commentary (pp. 21-22).</td><td>Organize the evidence by the actual clip moments. This review groups passages by question for teaching; it does not replace the submission fields.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflection</td><td>Part J has four questions; question 1 includes results, feedback, strengths, growth, equity, and next steps.</td><td>Reflection directions and Rubric 2.7 assess the connections among evidence and leadership learning (pp. 26-27).</td><td>Bring the related reflection passages together. A separate account of success does not replace analysis of feedback or growth.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note\">Page references are printed handbook pages. These are instructional observations, not official CTC corrections.</p></section><section id=\"limits\" class=\"major-section\"><h2>Submission requirements</h2><table class=\"audit\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>A</th><td>Context, educational focus, and community of practice</td><td>Up to 5 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>B</th><td>Planning meeting agenda</td><td>1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>C</th><td>Planning meeting minutes</td><td>1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>D</th><td>Problem of practice and selected strategy</td><td>Up to 4 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>E</th><td>Implementation meeting agendas</td><td>At least 3 meetings; all agendas in 1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>F</th><td>Implementation meeting minutes</td><td>At least 3 meetings; all minutes in 1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>G</th><td>Collaborative group work products</td><td>Up to 10 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>H</th><td>Three implementation meeting video clips</td><td>Exactly 3 video files; up to 5 minutes each</td></tr><tr><th>I</th><td>Commentary on implementation meeting clips</td><td>Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>J</th><td>Reflective narrative</td><td>Up to 4 written pages OR 5 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Meeting-record templates marked optional may be replaced by an appropriate local format; the required evidence is not optional. Consult the current handbook for recording permissions, technical specifications, and submission requirements.</p></section><section id=\"source-record\" class=\"sources\"><h2>Source notes</h2><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf\">Current Cycle 2 handbook</a>. Official material copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Interpolated example wording is newly drafted for instruction; original source wording is not retained in these app examples. Example references to research are preserved, but those studies and the school records were not independently verified. No assessor score report or video recording was available.</p><ul class=\"template-links\"><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S1_A_ContextEdFocusCommunityPrac.docx\">Part A template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_B_PlanningMeetingAgenda_OptionalTemplate.docx\">Part B template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_C_PlanningMeetingMinutes_OptionalTemplate.docx\">Part C template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_D_ProbPracDescStrategy.docx\">Part D template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_E_AgendasImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\">Part E template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_F_MinutesImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\">Part F template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_I_CommentaryImplementationMeetings.docx\">Part I template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S4_J_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\">Part J template</a></li></ul><p>Companion reviews: <a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Cycle 1</a> | <a href=\"#cycle/c3\">Cycle 3</a>.</p></section><section id=\"scores\" class=\"major-section\">\n            <h2>Interpolated Example Status</h2>\n            <p>The examples on this page are newly worded instructional parallels. They are not official exemplars, not candidate submissions, and not scored responses.</p>\n            <p>Use them to study the kind of evidence and reasoning each prompt calls for, then replace the details with your own school context, records, meetings, and reflections.</p>\n          </section><section id=\"rubric-2-1\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.1: Collaborative professional learning</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate describe and analyze the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting and how do these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not describe the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. OR Candidate does not analyze how these practices relate to student learning and/or well- being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate minimally describes the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. Candidate provides a vague analysis of how these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate provides a clear detailed description of the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. Candidate provides a clear analysis of how these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains the relationship between different forms of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting and how these practices have impacted student learning and/or well-being, providing evidence from observations, documentation, surveys, student work, and/or staff consultations.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate critiques collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting, referring to evidence-based adult learning processes and/or research that supports or refutes the practices and outlines implications for facilitating a community of practice at their school site/educational setting.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Source of Evidence: • Part A: Written Narrative: Context, Area of Educational Focus, Community of Practice (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 11.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-2-2\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate select an area of educational focus based on student data that corresponds to the vision, mission, and/or goals of their school site/educational setting? How does the candidate choose a group of educators/educational partners to participate in a community of practice in relation to the selected area of educational focus?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not select or selects an area of educational focus that is not based on student data or the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate’s school site/educational setting. OR Candidate provides little detail about demographic characteristics and/or does not provide reasons for member inclusion in the community of practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate selects and minimally describes an area of educational focus loosely based on student data and/or the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate’s school site/educational setting. Candidate lists group members and provides unclear or irrelevant details of their demographics and characteristics; rationale for member inclusion in the community of practice is not clear.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate selects an area of educational focus, clearly referring to data on student learning and/or well-being, and explains the connection to the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate’s school site/educational setting. Candidate clearly describes community of practice group members by demographic characteristics, current job titles, assignments, and positional relationships and provides clear reasons for each member’s inclusion in the group in relation to the selected area of educational focus.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate articulates how the work of the group is likely to advance conditions for student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides a rationale for including the specific members in their community of practice to ensure diverse viewpoints are represented and respected, leading to supports for all students to learn and to experience a positive sense of well-being.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Source of Evidence: • Part A: Written Narrative: Context, Area of Educational Focus, Community of Practice (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1C CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 12.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-2-3\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><p>Essential Question: Using the agreed-upon area of educational focus as a starting point, how does the candidate work collaboratively with the group to review data sets and select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being? How does the candidate work with the group members to acknowledge that the problem of practice might be addressed with a change in current instructional practice at their school site/educational setting?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not involve the group in analyzing data sets to select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. OR Candidate provides no evidence that they worked collaboratively with the group to select the problem of practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate minimally involves the group in the analysis of data sets and/or the selection of the problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. Candidate minimally involves the group in the selection of the problem of practice and/or minimally describes how the problem of practice might be mitigated by a change of current practices at their school site/educational setting.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly explains how they worked collaboratively with the group to analyze student data sets on the selected educational focus and select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. Candidate clearly describes how they worked collaboratively with group members to acknowledge that the problem of practice might be mitigated by a change of current practices at their school site/educational setting.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate differentiates the academic performance for more than one student group and/or issue of well-being, providing supporting student data/evidence and explains how they facilitated the group to collaboratively address these differences in defining the problem of practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains in detail both how they facilitated the collaborative process with the group members to analyze student data/evidence and why specific facilitation skills were used.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part B: Planning Meeting Agenda (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part C: Planning Meeting Minutes (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part D: Written Narrative: Identifying a Problem of Practice and a Strategy to Address It (up to 4 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1C CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5B, 5C</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 16.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-2-4\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate explain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence-based strategy to address the selected problem of practice? How does the candidate describe the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not explain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence- based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate does not describe the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate vaguely explains the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence- based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate minimally discusses the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly explains the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence- based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate clearly describes the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate describes in detail how the collaboratively determined evidence-based strategy will have a positive impact on student learning and/or well-being across specific student groups.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate thoroughly explains potential implementation implications and/or challenges across multiple student groups and suggests feasible next steps for each group, as appropriate.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part B: Planning Meeting Agenda (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part C: Planning Meeting Minutes (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part D: Written Narrative: Identifying a Problem of Practice and a Strategy to Address It (up to 4 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 17.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-2-5\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze a variety of their facilitation skills of group learning— including documenting group decisions, supporting diverse viewpoints, and/or maintaining group focus and energy— while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus, feedback, and progress during the implementation meetings?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not demonstrate facilitation skills during the implementation meetings. OR Candidate’s commentary does not provide an analysis of their facilitation of group learning or is a summary of what was observable in the video.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate demonstrates minimal facilitation skills that are vaguely supportive of group learning during the implementation meetings while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus documenting group decisions, respecting diverse viewpoints, and/or maintaining group focus and energy. Candidate’s commentary does not clearly provide an analysis (what they did and why) of their facilitation of group learning.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly demonstrates facilitation skills that support group learning during the implementation meetings while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus, including documenting group decisions, respecting diverse viewpoints, and maintaining group focus and energy. Candidate’s commentary provides a clear analysis (what they did and why) of their facilitation of group learning.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate purposefully seeks diverse viewpoints and clearly encourages all members of the community to speak and share ideas during meetings.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate uses their commentary to critique their facilitation skills used during the implementation meetings based on group learning processes and/or research that supports facilitation practices.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part E: Agendas for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part F: Minutes for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part G: Key Collaborative Work Products (up to 10 pages) • Part H: 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (up to 5 minutes each) • Part I: Commentary for the 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 23.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-2-6\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate facilitate a discussion that leads to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results? How does the candidate collaboratively lead the group in jointly determining next steps?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not facilitate a discussion about implementation results. OR Next steps are not determined. OR Candidate’s commentary does not provide an analysis of their facilitation of group discussion or is a summary of what was observable in the video.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate facilitates a discussion that does not clearly lead to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results. It is not clear that the candidate works collaboratively with the group to jointly determine the next steps. Candidate’s commentary provides a vague analysis of their facilitation of group discussions.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate facilitates a discussion that clearly leads to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results. Candidate clearly works collaboratively with the group to jointly determine next steps. Candidate’s commentary provides a clear analysis of their facilitation of the group discussions.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate consistently addresses initial results and next steps to inform the collaborative implementation process with group members both within and between meetings.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate collaborates with the group to adjust the implementation strategy as needed in response to its challenges or successes.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part E: Agendas for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part F: Minutes for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) • Part G: Key Collaborative Work Products (up to 10 pages) • Part H: 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (up to 5 minutes each) • Part I: Commentary for the 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1B CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2C</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 24.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-2-7\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate reflect upon initial implementation results and feedback from the group to analyze their leadership skills and practices to make specific connections to their strengths and areas for growth, and identify next steps, referring to specific evidence from any of the four steps? How does the candidate demonstrate a variety of leadership skills used to equitably facilitate a community of practice?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate provides no connection to initial implementation results and/or feedback from the group to make specific connections to their strengths or areas for growth, or next steps related to leadership practices. OR Candidate does not include specific evidence from any of the cycle’s four steps to support their reflections and inform their analysis. OR Candidate does not describe their leadership practice using an equitable leadership approach to facilitate a community of practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate vaguely or inaccurately reflects upon initial implementation results and/or feedback from the group to make connections to their strengths, areas for growth, and/or next steps. Candidate vaguely refers to evidence from any of the cycle’s four steps to support their reflections and/or inform their analysis. Candidate vaguely demonstrates their capacity to facilitate a community of practice. It is not clear that the candidate has an equitable approach to leadership practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly reflects upon initial implementation results and feedback from the group to analyze their leadership skills, making specific connections to their strengths, areas for growth, and next steps. Candidate clearly refers to specific, appropriate evidence from any of the four steps to support their reflections and inform their analysis. Candidate clearly demonstrates how their leadership practice equitably facilitated a community of practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides a deep analysis that illustrates how they maintained professionalism and integrity as well as employed an equitable leadership approach consistently throughout the community of practice inquiry process.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate consults evidence- based practices and/or research related to equitable leadership development, adult learning, and/or group facilitation, then uses the information to analyze their own leadership practices and set professional goals during this cycle.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Source of Evidence: • Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 5 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5B</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 27.</p></section></div>","search":[{"title":"Part A / I.1 / Investigating professional learning","url":"#cycle/c2/A1","html":"<span id=\"a-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-a\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.1 / Investigating professional learning</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe and analyze the role of collaborative professional learning at your school site/educational setting as it relates to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>a.\tProvide information about any formal training offered to professional educators (e.g., teachers, specialists, aides, support staff) at your school site/educational setting within the past three years related to collaborative professional learning, communities of practice, or group learning process.</p><p>b.\tDescribe and analyze the ways in which professional educators (e.g., teachers, specialists, aides, support staff) at your school site/educational setting currently engage with one another in professional learning. Describe the impact and/or lack of impact of this professional learning in relation to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>c.\tDescribe and analyze the ways in which professional educators engage with one another in communities of practice. Describe the impact and/or lack of impact of the community of practice approach in relation to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>d.\tDescribe how collaborative professional learning reflects evidence-based adult learning processes and cite relevant research to support your analysis.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Answer all four subparts: training offered within three years; current professional learning and its impact; communities of practice and their impact; and the adult-learning principles that explain those practices. A community of practice involves sustained shared inquiry, not simply attendance at a meeting.</p><p>Separate what you observed or found in records from what you expect collaboration might accomplish. When impact evidence is unavailable, explain that limitation rather than presenting a benefit as an established result.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.1: Collaborative professional learning</h3><ul><li>Describe current collaborative professional learning in detail and analyze its relationship to student learning or well-being.</li><li>For Level 4, explain relationships among different forms of professional learning and their impact, supported by observations, records, surveys, work, or consultations.</li><li>For Level 5, use adult-learning evidence or research to critique those practices and explain implications for facilitating the community.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>During the preceding three years, the school's formal preparation for collaborative professional learning included an introduction to an improvement cycle organized around planning, trying a change, studying evidence, and deciding what to do next. Administrators introduced this process last year as part of a schoolwide effort to improve reading comprehension in English language arts. In a professional development session, they explained how a common inquiry process could help teams examine learning and coordinate instruction. The initial preparation was brief, but it gave grade levels a shared way to organize their work.</p>\n<p>The school created one slide presentation containing templates that every grade level used. Teams recorded a problem of practice, selected a strategy, identified evidence, and analyzed student learning in that shared space. Because the presentation was available schoolwide, teachers could see other teams' questions, instructional choices, and findings. Grade-level members completed the process together rather than submit separate individual plans. At several points during the year, teams presented results to colleagues. This helped establish common language for discussing reading comprehension and made the relationship between collaborative inquiry and the schoolwide goal more visible.</p>\n<p>Current collaboration takes place through several established arrangements. Schoolwide professional development occurs two or three times a month and brings together teachers, specialists, coordinators, and administrators. Sessions may involve reviewing school priorities, examining iReady and DIBELS results, preparing for state assessments, or clarifying curriculum expectations. These activities can support a shared understanding of what students should learn and more consistent access to instruction. Their value depends partly on how much time remains for examining teaching and learning after other business is addressed.</p>\n<p>Teachers also meet with their grade-level colleagues every other week during the instructional day. They discuss recent data, student work, and pacing. Those conversations can lead to prompt reteaching or changes in instruction when a need becomes apparent. The school offers up to two additional hours of optional after-school collaboration each month. Participation in that time varies because teachers' schedules differ. Consequently, some teams benefit from more shared planning than others, and the effects of that additional time are uneven across the school.</p>\n<p>More structured data conversations are beginning to develop, but their use is not yet consistent. Collaboration could help staff recognize academic needs early and coordinate support for English learners and students receiving intervention. Logistical requirements can also limit that potential. Teams are expected to meet when all members are available, which means some groups have difficulty meeting at all while others engage in sustained inquiry. Paraprofessionals are not regularly included in these arrangements. Their absence from planning can weaken the connection between classroom instruction and the additional support students receive.</p>\n<p>The school's history of professional learning has nevertheless established an expectation that teachers work together. Regular professional development and grade-level meetings make discussion of data, instruction, and pacing part of normal professional practice. These routines have helped coordinate classrooms and given teachers recurring opportunities to learn from one another. They provide a foundation for a community of practice because collaboration is familiar rather than entirely new.</p>\n<p>The quality of that collaboration varies. Sessions centered on logistics leave less time for exploring an instructional problem. Optional meeting time and the requirement for full attendance can make participation uneven. The common inquiry process introduced last year strengthened the work by giving teams a specific sequence and a shared place to document evidence. Viewing one another's results increased transparency and supported alignment. Professional learning therefore helps collaboration when it gives educators time and structure to investigate instruction, while arrangements emphasizing compliance or restricting participation can limit its usefulness.</p>\n<p>Several adult-learning principles are visible in these practices. Teachers work with current assessment information and instructional questions that arise in their classrooms. This reflects Knowles' account of adult learning as relevant to professional responsibilities and organized around problems adults need to address (1984). The immediate connection to students gives the work a practical purpose and allows teachers to draw on their existing experience.</p>\n<p>The shared presentation also allowed grade levels to learn about approaches outside their own team. Seeing another group's problem, strategy, and evidence could broaden the conversation about a common reading goal. This transparency was a practical feature of the process, alongside the more formal expectation that teams present their results.</p>\n<p>The meetings also illustrate learning through social participation. In the traditions associated with Vygotsky and with Lave and Wenger, understanding develops through interaction and shared activity. Teachers compare interpretations, solve problems together, and build collective expertise. Finally, the inquiry cycle includes collaboration, attention to student outcomes, and continuing reflection, features discussed by Darling-Hammond and colleagues in their work on effective professional learning. Not every session reflects these principles equally. The strongest alignment appears when educators examine evidence together, make an instructional decision, try it, and return to consider what students' responses suggest.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the dates, participants, and purposes of relevant training across the three-year period, including years with no such training.</li><li>Compare two forms of professional learning using a concrete record of a decision, instructional follow-through, and student evidence.</li><li>Use the adult-learning ideas to examine both strengths and weaknesses of the local practices; distinguish established effects from plausible benefits.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / I.2 / Implications for facilitation","url":"#cycle/c2/A2","html":"<span id=\"a-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.2 / Implications for facilitation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tAs a result of your investigation and given the educational setting as well as available data related to student learning and/or well-being, describe the most important issues you will need to consider in preparing to facilitate and support a community of practice to engage in collaborative professional learning.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what the investigation requires you to do as facilitator. Connect each priority to the school context, available student evidence, and this group's likely participation. Do not repeat the entire description from question 1.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.1: Collaborative professional learning</h3><ul><li>Describe current collaborative professional learning in detail and analyze its relationship to student learning or well-being.</li><li>For Level 4, explain relationships among different forms of professional learning and their impact, supported by observations, records, surveys, work, or consultations.</li><li>For Level 5, use adult-learning evidence or research to critique those practices and explain implications for facilitating the community.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Two conditions need attention as I prepare to facilitate this team. The first is reliable participation. Even within one grade level, attendance changes with the meeting arrangement and time of day. When members are missing, the group loses perspectives needed for a shared interpretation of student learning. Decisions may then feel less collective, and the team may need to repeat discussions before moving forward. I will need to establish arrangements that make sustained participation feasible.</p>\n<p>The second condition is a clear inquiry routine. Existing meetings sometimes rely on informal discussion and move from instruction into scheduling or other logistics. I will need protocols that bring the team back to evidence, help members examine student responses, and support reflection on the strategies they try. Consistent structures can make the difference between meeting regularly and learning together. My facilitation should help the group use its time to reach shared, evidence-informed decisions about instruction and subsequent adjustments.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Specify an agreed meeting schedule and the school support needed to make participation possible.</li><li>Name the data-discussion routine and explain how it will separate observations, interpretations, and decisions.</li><li>Connect each facilitation choice to a documented concern from the investigation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.1 / Selecting the educational focus","url":"#cycle/c2/A3","html":"<span id=\"a-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.1 / Selecting the educational focus</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe the agreed-upon area of educational focus for improving student learning and/or well-being (obtained with guidance from one or more school administrators and/or teacher leaders) selected for the group’s professional learning. Be sure to include a discussion of the relevant data that supports the selection of this focus and the connection between the educational focus and the vision, mission, and/or goals in your school site/educational setting.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Name the educational focus agreed with an administrator or teacher leader, explain the student data supporting it, and connect it to an actual school commitment. The broad focus is established here; the community jointly defines the more specific problem of practice later.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Three different decisions</summary><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Decision</th><th scope=\"col\">Purpose</th><th scope=\"col\">Who shapes it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Educational focus</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">The broad area of student learning or well-being that warrants inquiry.</td><td data-label=\"Who shapes it\">Established with administrator or teacher-leader guidance.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Problem of practice</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">A specific, changeable aspect of adult practice identified through evidence.</td><td data-label=\"Who shapes it\">Defined jointly by the community during planning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Selected strategy</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">One evidence-based response that fits the problem and setting.</td><td data-label=\"Who shapes it\">Chosen jointly after considering research, evidence, and feasibility.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Do not arrive with every decision already made. Facilitation creates opportunities for colleagues to challenge an interpretation and influence the response.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The educational focus is mathematics, particularly progress toward proficiency on the Smarter Balanced Assessment. Administration and I selected this focus after examining school SBA trends and district priorities. The district expects students in grades three through five to move an average of 40 points nearer proficiency by 2026, with a 50-point gain expected for targeted groups. Our school has shown continuing growth and currently scores above the proficiency threshold in mathematics. However, its latest Distance from Standard value of 43.7 remains below the district's projected 2025-2026 target, so further acceleration is needed. Mathematics therefore provides a relevant focus even in a school with positive overall results. The work also connects with the mission of equitable access to rigorous teaching and with the district's longer-term intention that students be prepared for more advanced mathematics, including Algebra. The focus therefore connects immediate school improvement with later learning opportunities.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the year, population, source, and comparison target for the Distance from Standard value.</li><li>Explain why the third-grade evidence warrants this focus while keeping schoolwide and grade-level results distinct.</li><li>State the relevant school commitment and the administrator or teacher-leader role in agreeing to the focus.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.2 / Choosing community members","url":"#cycle/c2/A4","html":"<span id=\"a-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.2 / Choosing community members</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tProvide a list of the job titles for each member of the community of practice group, detailing the specific reason(s) for each member’s inclusion and referencing the area of educational focus.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>List each partner's job title and explain the expertise or instructional responsibility that makes that person useful for the mathematics focus. The community requires 3-5 partners in addition to the candidate. Shared availability alone is not a membership rationale.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The community includes three third-grade teachers. One teaches an SAS class with a broad range of learner needs. Her perspective can help the group choose mathematics approaches that remain useful across different levels of readiness while supporting the SBA focus.</p>\n<p>A second SAS teacher contributes experience with enrichment and accelerated instruction. As the grade-level chair, she can also help coordinate the team's work and maintain an appropriate level of challenge across classrooms.</p>\n<p>The general education teacher represents another range of instructional levels within third grade. His knowledge of scaffolds and additional support will help the group consider how students can gain access to the mathematics proficiency goal.</p>\n<p>Third grade is the first year students take the mathematics SBA. Working with this team offers an opportunity to strengthen that transition and, if the collaboration proves useful, extend its approach to the upper grades as the school pursues the district numeracy targets.</p>\n<p>Their agreement established a shared starting commitment for the planned inquiry.</p>\n<p>I invited the teachers during a grade-level meeting by explaining the community's purpose and its relationship to the schoolwide mathematics goal. Each agreed to participate. They saw the work as an opportunity to improve mathematics teaching, support students approaching their first SBA, and develop greater instructional consistency across the grade level.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Differentiate each teacher's specific contribution to identifying and addressing the mathematics need.</li><li>Explain how the group will obtain relevant perspectives not represented in its membership.</li><li>Keep the facilitator distinct from the three partners and retain the actual commitment process.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.3 / Understanding representation","url":"#cycle/c2/A5","html":"<span id=\"a-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.3 / Understanding representation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tProvide a description of each group member’s demographics and how this reflects or is different from your school site/educational setting’s demographics.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe each member's appropriately provided demographic information and compare it with the school community. Explain representation without assuming that shared identity guarantees shared perspectives or that a person speaks for an entire group.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The two SAS teachers and the general education teacher bring different backgrounds to the group. One SAS teacher identifies as Hispanic, connecting with the approximately 59% of students identified as Latino, the school's largest demographic group. The other SAS teacher identifies as White, a group comprising about 16% of enrollment. The general education teacher is Armenian. Armenian identity is not listed separately in the school's demographic chart, although many families in the community share that background. His perspective therefore relates to a substantial community presence that is less visible in the chart's reporting categories. These descriptions concern representation within this particular school community.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the source, year, and category definitions for the school demographic comparison.</li><li>Use member-provided information and explain any noncomparable categories.</li><li>Describe how differing perspectives will be sought and respected in the mathematics inquiry.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.4 / Explaining positionality","url":"#cycle/c2/A6","html":"<span id=\"a-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.4 / Explaining positionality</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tProvide a brief description of your relationship or positionality (e.g., coach, mentor, teacher colleague, administrator) to each member of the group.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain your working relationship and authority in relation to each partner. Consider how grade-level relationships, status, experience, or prior collaboration may shape participation even when you are not an evaluator.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I teach second grade at the same school and participate as a colleague of the third-grade team. My students enter their grade next, so I bring knowledge of the foundational skills and instructional experiences that precede third-grade mathematics. That position allows me to contribute to discussion of the transition between grades and the preparation students receive. I do not supervise or evaluate these teachers. My role is that of a peer and facilitator, working alongside them to examine evidence and improve mathematics learning rather than directing their performance from an administrative position. That colleague relationship shapes how I invite participation and discuss evidence.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Describe any relevant differences in your relationship with each partner.</li><li>Explain how the grade-level chair's role or your feeder-grade position could shape discussion.</li><li>Identify one facilitation choice that protects candid participation without assuming all members feel equally comfortable.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.5 / Expected benefits for students","url":"#cycle/c2/A7","html":"<span id=\"a-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.5 / Expected benefits for students</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>5.\tExplain how the work of this group is likely to advance conditions for students’ learning and/or well-being in relation to the area of educational focus.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain the pathway from the community's adult learning to improved conditions for students. Identify what teachers may learn or change, what students will experience differently, and why that could help with the mathematics focus. These are expected effects, not completed outcomes.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Educational focus and group selection</h3><ul><li>Connect the educational focus to student data and school commitments.</li><li>Explain members' demographics, assignments, positional relationships, and reasons for inclusion.</li><li>For Level 4, explain how the group's work is likely to improve learning conditions; Level 5 adds a rationale for ensuring diverse, respected viewpoints.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The community's intended benefit is more consistent and responsive mathematics instruction across third grade. By examining student evidence together, teachers can identify numeracy needs and select approaches that strengthen the foundational skills required for SBA success. Joint planning and reflection should help establish coherent routines, shared expectations, and suitable supports for learners with different starting points. Students may then experience clearer instruction and greater confidence in mathematics, contributing to learning and well-being.</p>\n<p>The community will revisit those expectations as evidence becomes available.</p>\n<p>The group also creates a continuing process for revisiting those decisions. Teachers can bring new evidence, compare how students respond, and modify instruction when needs change. Shared analysis can strengthen their understanding of effective practice and distribute responsibility for student progress across the team. Over time, that process may support more equitable learning opportunities by making instructional choices deliberate and responsive. These are expected benefits to investigate through implementation and student evidence, rather than outcomes already demonstrated before the community begins its work.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use prospective language for effects that have not yet been observed.</li><li>Identify the particular opportunities to learn that coordinated planning should improve.</li><li>Name the student evidence the group will later examine to see whether those opportunities and benefits occurred.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / Planning meeting agenda","url":"#cycle/c2/B","html":"<span id=\"b-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"b-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-b\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / Planning meeting agenda</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for the planning meeting. You may use either this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nAfter creating the agenda, use it to facilitate meetings with group members’ active involvement to review and analyze data—including student demographics, learning, academic performance, and/or well-being—and other relevant student information related to the selected area of educational focus. Record minutes during the planning meeting, describing what was discussed, agreements reached, and the next steps the group will take.</p><p>1.\tReview the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/professional context:</p><p>2.\tReview and analyze data:</p><p>a.\tData source(s):</p><p>b.\tDiscussion of data:</p><p>3.\tIdentify a problem of practice:</p><p>4.\tAddressing the problem of practice:</p><p>5.\tImplementation plan:</p><p>6.\tNext steps and action items:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part B template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Prepare the meeting before it occurs. Include who will attend, the purpose, norms, data, discussion of the problem, strategy selection, implementation planning, and next actions. The prompts below are the current optional format; the agenda itself is required.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Meeting date: October 13, 2025. Meeting time: 1:30-2:30 p.m.</p>\n<p>Working agreements: Give the discussion full attention; arrive prepared to contribute; consider perspectives different from one's own; and keep the conversation focused on the shared purpose. These agreements will guide both participation and collaborative decision-making throughout the meeting.</p>\n<p>Participants: Ms. W., third-grade SAS teacher and grade-level chair; Ms. A., third-grade SAS teacher; Mr. A., third-grade general education teacher; and Ms. K., second-grade teacher serving as facilitator.</p>\n<p>Student focus: Third-grade learners, with particular attention to foundational numeracy needs identified in beginning-of-year iReady results.</p>\n<p>Meeting purpose: Examine available assessment information, including SBA and iReady, establish a shared instructional problem, and select one evidence-informed approach to support third-grade number sense.</p>\n<p>School and district connection: Revisit the district goal for grades three through five to move an average of 40 points toward SBA proficiency by 2026. Connect this target with the school's commitment to rigorous instruction that is accessible to all learners. Use iReady growth expectations as ongoing information about progress toward the larger mathematics goals.</p>\n<p>Evidence for review: Third-grade iReady beginning-of-year overall placement; iReady domain results for Number and Operations, Algebraic Thinking, and Measurement and Data; and mathematics SBA Distance from Standard trends.</p>\n<p>Data discussion: Compare overall placement with domain strengths and areas needing support. Identify patterns that may explain difficulty accessing grade-level standards, especially foundational skills needed for later concepts. Invite teachers to connect the assessment information with what they observe in classrooms. Use the discussion to move toward a problem the whole team recognizes.</p>\n<p>Problem of practice: Define an instructional concern that is specific enough to guide action, supported by the evidence, and within teachers' influence. Focus on how instruction can improve access to grade-level mathematics rather than describe students only in terms of low scores. Clarify which foundational needs the first cycle should address.</p>\n<p>Strategy discussion: Consider more than one research-informed approach. Compare each option with the identified need, the range of student readiness, and the realities of classroom implementation. Reach a shared decision on one primary strategy after members have had an opportunity to discuss its fit.</p>\n<p>Implementation planning: Outline the routines, group arrangements, common materials, and monitoring tools needed to use the selected strategy across classrooms. Agree on how the team will coordinate the work while remaining responsive to individual students. Establish an initial timeline for reviewing evidence and discussing progress.</p>\n<p>Decision record: Capture the problem statement, the reason for the selected approach, and any implementation questions still requiring evidence. Make these notes available to all participants so the next meeting can continue from a common account of the discussion.</p>\n<p>Closing decisions: Record responsibilities, resources to prepare, evidence to collect, and the next collaboration date. Confirm what each person will bring back so the following meeting can build directly on the decisions made today.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Allocate realistic discussion time to data interpretation, alternatives, and implementation decisions.</li><li>Specify which dated data or student work members will examine and what questions will guide that examination.</li><li>Close with a plan to record owners, deadlines, needed support, and the next meeting.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / Planning meeting minutes","url":"#cycle/c2/C","html":"<span id=\"c-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"c-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-c\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / Planning meeting minutes</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: While facilitating your planning meeting with your group members, record minutes of the discussion. Be sure to describe what was discussed, agreements reached, and the next steps the group will take. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tReview the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/professional context:</p><p>2.\tReview and analyze data:</p><p>3.\tIdentify a problem of practice:</p><p>4.\tAddressing the problem of practice:</p><p>5.\tImplementation plan:</p><p>6.\tNext steps and action items:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part C template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Record the actual discussion, differing interpretations, agreements, and next steps. Show how the group moved from evidence to a changeable problem and a selected strategy. Minutes are a record of the group's reasoning, not a second agenda or a transcript of everything said.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The meeting opened by connecting the inquiry with the school's commitment to rigorous, accessible instruction. Teachers discussed how strong early mathematics understanding prepares students for more advanced work later. We also revisited the district expectation that students in grades three through five move an average of 40 points closer to SBA proficiency by 2026. Alongside this longer-term target, members considered the importance of meeting iReady typical and stretch growth expectations during the year. The group agreed that strengthening number sense was a meaningful contribution to both the school mission and district priorities.</p>\n<p>I then guided the team through the third-grade beginning-of-year iReady results. We began with overall placement, where relatively few students were at or above grade level and most were one or more grades below. Moving to the domain results, we identified notable needs in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. I asked members to consider how these areas support addition and subtraction with regrouping, a major third-grade focus and a foundation for later mathematics. This sequence helped the group move from a broad performance pattern toward a more specific instructional concern.</p>\n<p>To connect assessment reports with classroom experience, I invited teachers to share early work samples and observations. Several described difficulty with place value and regrouping. Others noted students who already showed strong conceptual understanding and needed additional challenge. I summarized this range so that our planning would address both groups. The discussion made clear that a single uniform task would not necessarily provide appropriate access for everyone. Students needing foundational instruction and students ready for enrichment were present within the same grade level and, often, within the same classroom.</p>\n<p>The team considered what this range meant for its practice. I prompted members to think about how more deliberate planning and greater consistency across classrooms could support different needs. We agreed that instruction would need to be focused enough to accelerate progress while allowing teachers to respond to students' readiness. I closed the data discussion by restating the shared concern: we needed a coordinated approach that could provide differentiated opportunities without losing coherence across the grade. This understanding became the basis for our problem of practice and strategy discussion.</p>\n<p>The group framed its problem as a need for more consistent, targeted early numeracy instruction because gaps in foundational number sense were limiting students' access to grade-level mathematics. The intended improvement was progress toward iReady growth expectations and SBA proficiency. This wording connected student evidence with an instructional responsibility the team could address. It also made the problem more actionable than a general statement that mathematics scores needed to rise.</p>\n<p>In developing that statement, I returned the group to beginning-of-year placement, domain results, and classroom work. Teachers identified number and operations concepts that underpin addition and subtraction with regrouping. I asked how difficulty with those concepts affects participation in grade-level work and may contribute to continuing performance differences. Members also emphasized that the problem could not be addressed by planning only for students below grade level. Students performing at or above expectations needed purposeful opportunities to extend their reasoning. We therefore identified grouping and targeted practice as aspects of instruction that required reconsideration.</p>\n<p>The team agreed that this problem was sufficiently focused, relevant to school and district goals, and within our instructional influence. Addressing it would require intentional changes in how teachers organize learning, reinforce foundational concepts, and provide enrichment. We wanted students to receive appropriately demanding mathematics rather than equate differentiation with easier work for some and additional work for others. The problem statement gave us a shared basis for comparing possible strategies.</p>\n<p>I introduced two research-informed options for discussion. The first was flexible grouping based on current skill needs, using the NCSM resource Supporting All Students Through Flexible Grouping Practices. I highlighted temporary placement, movement as students develop, and targeted teaching without permanent tracking. I asked how that approach could serve students needing foundational support as well as students ready for enrichment. Teachers recognized that their range of readiness made a flexible structure relevant. They also discussed the importance of using current evidence when assigning or changing groups.</p>\n<p>The second option was a daily mathematics reasoning routine, drawing on Heinemann's Create Confident Problem Solvers with Number Talks. We considered how Number Talks could develop conceptual understanding, number sense, and mathematical discussion. Structured opportunities to explain reasoning may be particularly helpful for English learners and students who need support expressing their thinking. Teachers saw how these routines could complement the core program by making students' strategies and language more visible. This comparison gave the group more than one plausible response to the identified problem.</p>\n<p>After discussing the evidence and classroom conditions, the team selected flexible, skill-based grouping as the primary strategy. Members felt it offered the most direct way to provide different levels of support and challenge across the grade. We used the local name Math Crew for the approach, while identifying the underlying strategy as the flexible grouping model discussed by NCSM. The name was a convenient way to refer to the work, not a separate evidence base. Teachers wanted groups that could address place value and regrouping for some students while providing more advanced problem-solving opportunities for others.</p>\n<p>I next facilitated an initial implementation plan. We narrowed the first cycle to addition and subtraction with regrouping so that the work would be manageable and coordinated. Both the assessment information and classroom observations supported this choice. During the meeting, teachers developed a short preassessment of place value and regrouping concepts. They agreed to administer it before the next meeting and use the results to make grouping decisions. This meant that group membership would follow evidence about the selected skills rather than broad impressions of student ability.</p>\n<p>We discussed what teaching within the groups might involve. Some students would work on composing and decomposing numbers, relationships within place value, and concrete or visual representations of regrouping. Others would engage in extension and more complex problem solving. I emphasized that the groups should remain temporary and change as students' understanding develops. The purpose was to provide an appropriate next learning opportunity, not assign a lasting label to a student. Members agreed that the preassessment and subsequent work would help determine where each student should begin and when a change was appropriate.</p>\n<p>For consistency, teachers agreed to protect a common instructional period for Math Crew and use shared language, routines, and visual models. We also planned to exchange resources and collaborate on lessons. This would support alignment while preserving teachers' ability to respond to the learners in front of them. The duration of the first cycle would be decided after reviewing the preassessment. A postassessment would then provide information about progress and help the team determine subsequent instruction. These arrangements linked the strategy with a process for studying its implementation rather than treating selection as the end of planning.</p>\n<p>The comparison of strategies also helped distinguish a teaching routine from a grouping structure. Number Talks could provide regular opportunities to reason and explain, while flexible grouping could organize more targeted instruction around different skill needs. The team did not need to regard one as universally better. It needed to decide which would serve as the primary response to this particular problem in the first cycle.</p>\n<p>The shared preassessment was an important part of that decision. Broad iReady placement information had identified a concern, but the regrouping tasks would give teachers more immediate evidence about the concepts they intended to teach. Discussing the assessment together also helped members establish a common interpretation of what students would be asked to demonstrate. That preparation would support later discussion of group placement and progress.</p>\n<p>The implementation plan retained both consistency and teacher judgment. Shared time, language, and models would make the arrangement coherent for students moving among classrooms. Within that structure, teachers could select scaffolds and extensions appropriate to the group. The team would revisit those choices after seeing student work, so the initial plan was a starting point for inquiry rather than a permanent assignment of students or instructional tasks.</p>\n<p>At the close, we confirmed responsibilities and the next meeting's purpose. Teachers would administer and score the regrouping preassessment, then bring results, observations, and questions about grouping or instruction. Together we would finalize groups, determine the length of the first cycle, and identify or adapt resources for each group. We would also plan the postassessment used to examine progress. My continuing role would be to help members notice patterns, compare interpretations, coordinate classroom expectations, and make shared adjustments. The next steps kept the inquiry connected to evidence and to the practical decisions needed to improve access to grade-level mathematics.</p>\n<p>The recorded decisions provide a shared reference for checking what was implemented and what the next discussion needs to resolve.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Record specific member contributions, differing views, and how agreement was established.</li><li>Attach the actual group-level data or work used to define the need.</li><li>List each agreed action with its owner, due date, and evidence to bring back.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / II.1 / Moving from data to a problem","url":"#cycle/c2/D1","html":"<span id=\"d-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-d\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.1 / Moving from data to a problem</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe how you: (a) worked collaboratively with the group to analyze student data sets on the educational focus; (b) selected a problem of practice based on the area of educational focus; and (c) addressed how practitioners could potentially improve student learning and/or well-being by improving the current practices at your school site/educational setting.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain the collaborative reasoning: which data the group examined, what members noticed, which problem they selected, and how changing current instruction could help. Keep the educational focus, problem of practice, and selected strategy distinct.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>At the planning meeting, I organized the review around several sources related to third-grade mathematics. We began with beginning-of-year iReady results and the SBA numeracy trend so everyone had the same starting information. I asked teachers to examine overall placement, including the share of students below grade level, before moving to individual domains. Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking showed particular areas of need. I invited members to compare these results across classrooms and describe what they were seeing during instruction, giving the assessment reports a more specific classroom context.</p>\n<p>To develop the analysis, I asked the team to identify patterns that appeared across the grade and consider which prerequisite skills were making grade-level mathematics difficult. These prompts helped connect scores with observable learning. Teachers described students' difficulty composing and decomposing numbers, understanding place value, and using regrouping. They explained how those difficulties affected participation in current tasks rather than simply repeat the assessment labels.</p>\n<p>The focus remained on changes the teachers could make together.</p>\n<p>I also made space for evidence about students who were already meeting or exceeding expectations. Members recognized that these learners needed enrichment while classmates needed foundational support. Bringing both needs into the discussion helped us avoid defining the problem only around remediation. The group developed a shared problem of practice centered on more deliberate, consistent instruction in foundational number sense. We concluded that this would require changes in the organization of mathematics instruction, particularly grouping and the reinforcement of prerequisite skills. Those changes would need to work across classrooms and support progress toward both iReady growth expectations and the SBA goal.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Show one data pattern and the member discussion that shaped the problem.</li><li>State what is inconsistent or insufficient in current instruction and what the group can change.</li><li>Connect the selected problem to the shared evidence without treating the strategy as already decided.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / II.2 / Analyzing differentiated data","url":"#cycle/c2/D2","html":"<span id=\"d-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.2 / Analyzing differentiated data</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tExplain how you worked collaboratively with the group to review your collected data on student demographics, student learning and academic performance or well-being, and other relevant data differentiated by student groups.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group compared data across student groups. Identify the measures, populations, and findings, then explain what members made of the differences. Mentioning several group names does not demonstrate a differentiated analysis.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I helped the team extend its review to demographic and performance information, including English learners, students with disabilities, and students without identified support needs. The beginning-of-year iReady results showed many students below grade level in Number and Operations and Algebraic Thinking. Teachers described similar concerns in several classrooms and across groups, suggesting a shared instructional issue rather than a difficulty confined to one class.</p>\n<p>We then compared those patterns with student work and daily observations. Members described limited understanding of place value and regrouping, explaining how these gaps made grade-level tasks less accessible. They also identified students with stronger understanding who needed opportunities for extension. This comparison made the range of readiness within each classroom clearer. It supported planning for both foundational instruction and enrichment, while keeping the discussion connected to actual student responses rather than relying on demographic categories alone.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Provide the actual results for at least two relevant groups, with dates and denominators or clearly described work evidence.</li><li>Explain how overlapping group memberships and different measures affect comparison.</li><li>Show how the group used the differences to refine the problem rather than simply reporting them.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / II.3 / Identifying a change in practice","url":"#cycle/c2/D3","html":"<span id=\"d-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.3 / Identifying a change in practice</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tDescribe the ways you facilitated the meeting to acknowledge how the problem of practice might be addressed with a change in current practices in your professional context.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how you helped the group recognize that something adults currently do can change. Name the instructional practice under discussion and show how members connected that change to the evidence. This is separate from merely identifying low performance.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I asked the team to examine whether its current arrangements allowed teachers to respond to the range of readiness we had identified. We considered which teaching practices could address differing starting points more deliberately and how changes in grouping might bring instruction closer to students' particular skill needs. These questions shifted the conversation from describing performance to considering what adults could change. Teachers recognized that the problem called for adjustments in the way mathematics instruction was organized and delivered, especially the support provided within groups.</p>\n<p>We agreed to frame the problem as a need for more consistent and targeted early numeracy teaching because gaps in foundational number sense were limiting access to grade-level mathematics. The intended direction was improved progress toward iReady growth expectations and SBA proficiency. This statement connected the student need with an instructional response within the team's influence and gave members a common purpose for the next stage of planning.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the current practice the team agreed was limiting access to targeted instruction.</li><li>Record what members said that showed their recognition of the needed change.</li><li>Explain why the proposed change follows from the evidence, keeping alternative explanations visible.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / II.4 / Connecting goals, standards, and differences","url":"#cycle/c2/D4","html":"<span id=\"d-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / II.4 / Connecting goals, standards, and differences</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tHow does the identified problem of practice: (a) correspond to the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/educational setting; (b) reflect California state standards and/or curriculum frameworks or equivalent, or other applicable standards; and (c) address existing differences in student group performance or well-being?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all three connections: school commitments, relevant standards or frameworks, and actual differences in student learning or well-being. Explain how each connection supports the selected problem, not just that the problem aligns with them.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><ul><li>Explain how the group jointly analyzed student data and selected the problem of practice.</li><li>Show how the group recognized a change in current practice that could address the problem.</li><li>For Level 4, use evidence to differentiate more than one group's performance or well-being and explain how those differences shaped the problem.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The problem of practice connects with the school's commitment to rigorous learning opportunities that are accessible to every student and prepare them for advanced mathematics. Strengthening number sense also supports the district's longer-term numeracy priorities. It is consistent with the California Mathematics Framework's attention to conceptual understanding, numerical fluency, and flexible approaches to solving problems.</p>\n<p>The focus responds to performance differences visible in third grade. Foundational needs appeared across several student groups, and students below grade level would need accelerated progress to meet annual growth expectations. Addressing those needs can improve access to current instruction. At the same time, the team must provide continued challenge for students already on track so that the strategy advances learning across the full range of readiness.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the relevant grade-level learning expectation and the work that reveals difficulty with it.</li><li>Connect the school goal to the instructional opportunity the group can change.</li><li>Use actual group differences to explain why the problem matters for equitable access.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / III.1 / Selecting one strategy together","url":"#cycle/c2/D5","html":"<span id=\"d-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.1 / Selecting one strategy together</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tExplain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting in selecting one relevant evidence-based strategy that addresses the problem of practice, potentially leading to improved student learning and/or well-being across student groups. Be sure to include how you facilitated the group to help make their selection.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group considered and selected one relevant evidence-based strategy. Include the alternatives, criteria, member contributions, and your facilitation. The group's decision is the object of analysis, not just your preferred strategy.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>After the team established its instructional problem, I introduced two research-informed possibilities. One was temporary grouping by skill, supported by NCSM's Supporting All Students Through Flexible Grouping Practices. The other was a daily reasoning routine such as Number Talks, using Heinemann's Create Confident Problem Solvers with Number Talks. We discussed what each would look like in practice and compared its fit with students' needs, implementation demands, and opportunities to differentiate across readiness levels.</p>\n<p>I supported the decision by asking members to consider which option most directly addressed the addition and subtraction needs in the data, which could serve the range of learners, and which we could use consistently across classrooms. These questions gave the comparison shared criteria rather than leave the choice to individual preference. Teachers noted that flexible grouping would allow them to reorganize students around current skills, teach particular concepts more directly, and offer enrichment to students who already demonstrated proficiency.</p>\n<p>The group reached agreement on flexible grouping, which we called Math Crew locally. We selected it because it appeared to offer the best fit for foundational numeracy needs while supporting differentiated teaching for all learners. The local name refers to our implementation of the strategy; the research basis remains the flexible grouping approach discussed in the resource.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain the specific instructional features the selected strategy will include.</li><li>Show how members evaluated the evidence and considered an alternative.</li><li>Document how shared agreement was reached, including concerns or conditions attached to the choice.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / III.2 / Learning and implementing the strategy","url":"#cycle/c2/D6","html":"<span id=\"d-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.2 / Learning and implementing the strategy</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tDescribe the group’s rationale for selecting this evidence-based strategy. How will the group learn this strategy and how to implement it? What challenges do you anticipate the group might encounter and how could they be addressed?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why the group chose the strategy, how teachers will learn to use it, and how anticipated challenges will be addressed. Prior exposure to grouping is not the same as a plan to learn a particular instructional approach.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Teachers chose flexible grouping because their classes include students well below, at, and above grade-level expectations. Temporary groups organized around current skills seemed a practical way to provide more precisely matched instruction. Members also knew that kindergarten through second grade had used similar arrangements in English language arts. That experience offered a local model for responding to varied learning needs. Because I had experience with that work, I could help the team, and members could also consult K-2 colleagues who had used flexible groups extensively.</p>\n<p>During the meeting, we created a short preassessment to guide Math Crew placement. It addresses addition and subtraction with regrouping and the underlying place-value understanding those operations require. Teachers agreed to administer and score it before the next community meeting. We would then use the results to determine groups and instructional priorities rather than assign students solely from a broad assessment level. Members also discussed resources, manipulatives, routines, and possible teaching approaches. They expressed interest in observing one another during implementation to learn how the groups functioned in different classrooms.</p>\n<p>We anticipated several practical challenges. The team would need a dependable instructional period, a process for moving students as they progressed, and enough alignment that group experiences remained coherent across classrooms. Teachers agreed to use common routines and language and to check in regularly during subsequent meetings. Those arrangements would help preserve flexibility without making the work unmanageable. They would also provide opportunities to notice when a group or lesson needed adjustment. The intention was a responsive system in which current evidence informs placement and instruction, supported by collaboration rather than fixed group assignments for the duration of the year.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Specify a rehearsal, observation, or collaborative planning activity with a date and responsible person.</li><li>Define the instructional routine, evidence for changing groups, and safeguards against permanent low-expectation placement.</li><li>Match anticipated challenges for specific learners with feasible supports.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / III.3 / Anticipated learning benefits","url":"#cycle/c2/D7","html":"<span id=\"d-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.3 / Anticipated learning benefits</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tWhat improvements for student learning and/or well-being related to the problem of practice do the group expect the selected evidence-based strategy will yield?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe what different students are expected to learn or experience because of the selected strategy. Explain the instructional mechanism: what teachers will do differently and why that should address the identified need. Keep predictions distinct from outcomes.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The team expects more closely matched instruction to strengthen students' number sense, accuracy, and confidence with multidigit addition and subtraction. Students who find regrouping difficult would receive targeted support with the concepts underlying the procedure. Those already meeting or exceeding expectations would work in enrichment groups that extend reasoning and problem solving. In both cases, the goal is instruction suited to current readiness rather than the same task for everyone.</p>\n<p>Teachers also anticipate greater engagement and less frustration during core mathematics lessons as students become more secure with prerequisite skills. More appropriate support and challenge may improve access to grade-level content and students' sense of competence. These are intended academic and well-being benefits. The group will need to examine student work and assessment results to determine which benefits appear and where further changes are needed.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each group's observed need to a specific instructional action and expected benefit.</li><li>Identify how conceptual reasoning, procedural accuracy, and enrichment will each be examined.</li><li>Use an appropriate source for any well-being or engagement claim.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / III.4 / Monitoring implementation","url":"#cycle/c2/D8","html":"<span id=\"d-q8\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / III.4 / Monitoring implementation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tHow will you facilitate the group in determining a monitoring process to understand the impact of the evidence-based strategy? Describe how you and the group plan to monitor and analyze initial implementation.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part D template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group will check both whether the strategy was implemented and what students initially learned. Specify evidence, timing, interpretation, and decision rules. The monitoring process must be manageable and jointly understood.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><ul><li>Explain collaborative selection of one relevant evidence-based strategy and its potential impact.</li><li>For Level 4, detail the expected impact across specific student groups.</li><li>Level 5 adds implementation challenges across multiple groups and feasible next steps for each, as appropriate.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I will help the group establish a monitoring process that is clear enough to guide decisions and manageable enough to sustain. The shared preassessment will provide the starting evidence for Math Crew grouping. Teachers will administer and score it before our next meeting, when we will compare results and confirm placements together. This gives the team a common reference for discussing students' initial understanding.</p>\n<p>During implementation, members will collect work samples, exit responses, observation notes, and informal checks focused on place value and regrouping. I will prompt the group to look for recurring patterns, identify students whose placement may need to change, and distinguish early progress from continuing difficulty. Each implementation meeting will begin with a brief review of this evidence. The purpose is to decide whether instruction or group arrangements need adjustment while the work is underway.</p>\n<p>This process links each meeting with the instruction occurring between meetings.</p>\n<p>We also agreed to define an instructional period before administering a postassessment. Comparing the initial and later evidence will help us evaluate early changes in understanding. It will not by itself prove that grouping caused every change, but it will support a more informed discussion than impressions alone. Through shared analysis, regular check-ins, and reflection on classroom evidence, the team will monitor both the use of the strategy and students' responses to it.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Agree on dates, participants, scoring criteria, and the relationship between pre- and post-assessments.</li><li>Record how often and how the strategy was used, alongside student results.</li><li>Set a decision rule for adapting instruction and specify who will bring evidence to each meeting.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / Implementation agenda 1","url":"#cycle/c2/E1","html":"<span id=\"e-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-e\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / Implementation agenda 1</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for each of the three required implementation meetings. Within each agenda, you will want to create opportunities for group members to review norms, review and analyze data, and discuss and determine the next steps. You may either use this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tEarly returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>2.\tSummary of the meeting:</p><p>3.\tNext steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Plan discussion of evidence from the strategy's initial use, not only its preparation. Include the required meeting information, norms, evidence review, summary, and next steps. A summary item on an agenda means planning time to summarize; it does not mean writing the conclusions in advance.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Connecting the implementation meetings</summary><p>Each meeting should carry the inquiry forward: revisit the previous commitment, examine evidence from actual implementation, compare interpretations, decide what to retain or adjust, and establish responsibilities and support. The next meeting examines what followed. These are implementation meetings, not repeated planning sessions.</p><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Record</th><th scope=\"col\">What it should make visible</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Record\">Evidence returning to the group</td><td data-label=\"What it should make visible\">What was tried, what students demonstrated, and what is still uncertain. Include difficulties and counterexamples.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Record\">Shared interpretation and decision</td><td data-label=\"What it should make visible\">How members used the evidence, where they disagreed, and why the group retained or adjusted the approach.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Record\">Support and follow-through</td><td data-label=\"What it should make visible\">Who will do what, what help is needed, and what evidence the group will revisit. Later minutes record what actually happened.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>An agenda anticipates this work. Minutes document it; products preserve the tools or analysis the group actually used. They should agree without copying one another.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Implementation meeting 1: Third grade, November 5, 2025, 2:30-3:30 p.m.</p>\n<p>Participation agreements: Attend to the discussion, arrive ready to contribute, consider alternative perspectives, and keep the conversation connected to the meeting purpose. The group will use these agreements while examining evidence and making decisions.</p>\n<p>Participants: Ms. W., third-grade SAS teacher and grade-level chair; Ms. A., third-grade SAS teacher; Mr. A., third-grade general education teacher; and Ms. K., second-grade teacher and facilitator.</p>\n<p>Purpose: Review the initial use of Math Crew and the information emerging from the preassessment and classroom observations. Discuss how the strategy appears to support students with different levels of readiness in addition and subtraction with regrouping. Because implementation has only recently begun, the meeting will focus on questions and emerging patterns rather than final claims about effectiveness.</p>\n<p>Initial evidence: Confirm whether teachers have completed the preassessment and invite their first observations about student responses. Compare patterns across classrooms and describe the variation in readiness within each class. Discuss early experiences organizing the groups, including student movement, routines, and expectations. Identify any questions about launching the strategy that need a shared response.</p>\n<p>Discussion summary: Name the common patterns in students' understanding of regrouping. Identify promising features and difficulties in the initial rollout, including engagement, transitions, and coordination among teachers. Record questions that remain open or need more evidence. The facilitator will draw together the observations and ask members to confirm whether the summary represents their experience.</p>\n<p>Actions before the next meeting: Continue temporary groups focused on addition and subtraction with regrouping. Refine expectations and procedures for moving between classrooms as needed. Gather further student work and observations during instruction so that the next discussion includes evidence of both learning and implementation. Identify practical supports or changes that would make the work more consistent.</p>\n<p>The record should distinguish a procedure that is operating smoothly from evidence that students have mastered the mathematics. Both kinds of information are useful at this early stage.</p>\n<p>Closing agreement: Confirm the focus of the next meeting and the information each teacher will bring. Clarify responsibilities for collecting evidence and following up on any shared concerns. The aim is to keep the first implementation cycle coordinated while allowing the team to respond to what it learns about students and the group structure.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify what instruction will have occurred before the meeting and what new evidence members will bring.</li><li>Reserve time to distinguish launch logistics from students' mathematical understanding.</li><li>Plan to record agreed actions, support, owners, and the next review date.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / Implementation agenda 2","url":"#cycle/c2/E2","html":"<span id=\"e-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / Implementation agenda 2</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for each of the three required implementation meetings. Within each agenda, you will want to create opportunities for group members to review norms, review and analyze data, and discuss and determine the next steps. You may either use this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tEarly returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>2.\tSummary of the meeting:</p><p>3.\tNext steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use the second agenda to revisit the first meeting's decisions and examine new evidence. Ask what was tried, what students did, and what may need adjustment. Repeating the same agenda headings is appropriate; repeating a generic discussion without follow-through is not.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Implementation meeting 2: Third grade, November 19, 2025, 2:30-3:30 p.m.</p>\n<p>Participation agreements: Give the meeting full attention, prepare to collaborate, remain receptive to different interpretations, and stay focused on the inquiry. These agreements support a discussion in which members can compare experiences and make shared instructional decisions.</p>\n<p>Participants: Ms. W., third-grade SAS teacher and grade-level chair; Ms. A., third-grade SAS teacher; Mr. A., third-grade general education teacher; and Ms. K., second-grade teacher facilitating the community.</p>\n<p>Purpose: Reflect on the early Math Crew sessions, examine how students are responding within their current groups, and consider whether changes in teaching or placement would better meet their needs. The team will treat the available information as early implementation evidence rather than a final evaluation.</p>\n<p>Evidence and experiences: Invite each teacher to describe recent sessions and students' responses. Look for indications of engagement, developing understanding, and persistent difficulty. Discuss whether particular students may need a different group and what evidence supports that possibility. Compare the instructional strategies being used at different levels and explain how lessons are being planned and adapted for each group.</p>\n<p>Shared interpretation: Identify patterns that occur in more than one classroom. Note strengths in engagement and instruction, along with changes under consideration. Revisit whether the current focus and flexible grouping approach still fit the student needs identified in the inquiry. The facilitator will summarize the themes, connect related observations, and check whether members agree with the interpretation.</p>\n<p>Next actions: Decide which proposed instructional changes or student moves should be tried. Continue with planned lessons for each group while monitoring progress and participation. Consider whether the current cycle needs additional time before the postassessment. Identify the work samples, assessment information, or observations that will be most useful for the following meeting.</p>\n<p>Members will explain the evidence behind a proposed move so that group changes remain connected with learning needs and are understood across the team.</p>\n<p>Closing agreement: Confirm what each member will bring next time and identify any materials, coordination, or other support needed. Record the decisions so that teachers leave with the same understanding of the immediate plan. The meeting should result in specific actions that respond to students' current learning, while preserving the flexibility of the grouping arrangement as new evidence becomes available.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Bring forward specific actions agreed in meeting 1 and identify their owners.</li><li>Specify the work or assessment evidence needed to evaluate each proposed grouping change.</li><li>Plan discussion of different student experiences, including students not benefiting yet.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / Implementation agenda 3","url":"#cycle/c2/E3","html":"<span id=\"e-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / Implementation agenda 3</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Create an agenda for each of the three required implementation meetings. Within each agenda, you will want to create opportunities for group members to review norms, review and analyze data, and discuss and determine the next steps. You may either use this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.</p><p>1.\tEarly returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>2.\tSummary of the meeting:</p><p>3.\tNext steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Plan a shared examination of initial results and a decision about what comes next. Compare meaningful evidence across the implementation period and retain uncertainty about causes or measures. The final scheduled meeting should lead to specific action, not only celebration.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Implementation meeting 3: Third grade, December 10, 2025, 2:30-3:30 p.m.</p>\n<p>Participation agreements: Be attentive, prepare to contribute, consider other viewpoints, and maintain the instructional focus. Use these agreements to support shared interpretation of the evidence.</p>\n<p>Participants: Ms. W., third-grade SAS teacher and chair; Ms. A., third-grade SAS teacher; Mr. A., third-grade general education teacher; and Ms. K., second-grade teacher and facilitator.</p>\n<p>Purpose: Examine the Math Crew postassessment, identify progress and continuing needs, and agree on subsequent mathematics instruction across groups. The discussion will draw on results and observations collected over several weeks of implementation.</p>\n<p>Evidence for discussion: Share each classroom's postassessment results and describe patterns of improvement, maintained performance, or unresolved difficulty. Examine regrouping, word problems, and prerequisite skills. Compare students' responses to the group structure and teaching, including differences among levels rather than relying only on an overall impression.</p>\n<p>Synthesis: Identify patterns common to several classrooms and consider what they suggest about the current approach. Discuss features of implementation that appear useful, questions that remain unanswered, and areas needing refinement. The facilitator will connect observations across members and check that the group has a shared understanding of both progress and continuing instructional concerns.</p>\n<p>Decisions and next steps: Consider changes in instruction suggested by the postassessment. Decide whether to extend the present cycle or revise its focus, and identify possible priorities for the next period of learning. Discuss how a future preassessment could target the relevant standards more precisely. Agree on the evidence or reflections members should bring to the next meeting.</p>\n<p>Keep the interpretation connected with the skills actually assessed and the instruction students received during the cycle.</p>\n<p>The closing record will distinguish decisions already reached from questions requiring additional information. This will allow the team to continue the inquiry with a clear plan and to use the assessment as a basis for instructional adjustment rather than treat it only as an endpoint.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Prepare separate counts for improved, unchanged, and lower scores, with the matched student denominator.</li><li>Bring the assessment tasks and scoring criteria so rigor and alignment can be examined.</li><li>Plan to decide which instruction continues, changes, or ends and how the next decision will be monitored.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part F / Implementation minutes 1","url":"#cycle/c2/F1","html":"<span id=\"f-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-f\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / Implementation minutes 1</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Document the group’s learning progress by maintaining detailed agendas and minutes that you will submit for each implementation meeting. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nWithin the minutes, document the range of agreed-upon support you provide to the group and/or to individual members.\nBased on your implementation meeting discussion and analysis of findings, track progress, early impact, and/or initial results of the group’s implementation of the strategy (e.g., Is the strategy making a difference in what or how students are learning and/or experiencing at school?).\nDocument agreed-upon next steps the group will take between implementation meetings.</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a.\tWhat is going well?</p><p>b.\tWhat needs adjustment?</p><p>c.\tPLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part F template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Document what the first implementation meeting actually established: evidence from instruction, what is working, needed changes, member reflections, and agreed support. Include enough specificity for the next meeting to check follow-through.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Teachers reported a generally smooth beginning to Math Crew. Every participant had administered the addition and subtraction preassessment. The results showed a broad range of readiness across classrooms, reinforcing the reason for temporary skill groups. Several teachers described stronger performance in addition than in subtraction requiring regrouping. Word problems were particularly difficult, and English learners needed additional help with the academic language used in those tasks. These observations gave the team a more precise starting point than the broader iReady and SBA patterns alone.</p>\n<p>Members also described practical successes in getting the groups underway. Students were moving between classrooms, teachers had explained expectations, and procedures such as name tags and lining up were helping transitions. Practicing the movement routines appeared to make students more comfortable with rotations. I drew attention to these examples because a predictable structure can support access to instruction. They were evidence that the arrangements were becoming workable, even though they did not yet demonstrate an academic effect.</p>\n<p>Some procedures still required attention. Name tags were not used consistently, and expectations needed reinforcement across classrooms. Teachers wanted to continue developing language support for students who struggled with mathematics word problems. They also discussed the need to share information when a student required additional behavioral or safety support, including a risk of leaving the assigned area. Knowing these needs before a transition would help colleagues respond consistently. I asked how clearer communication and common expectations could address the concerns and recorded the areas requiring follow-up.</p>\n<p>To encourage reflection, I invited teachers to compare the preassessment with their expectations and describe their first experiences teaching the groups. They said the results largely confirmed needs they had suspected from the earlier assessment information. Members also reported that planning together and agreeing on expectations increased their confidence. Sharing responsibility made implementation feel more coordinated than arranging groups independently. Several noted that students seemed to enjoy working with another teacher, which appeared to support engagement during the new routine.</p>\n<p>The team had connected the Math Crew name with the school's existing character and growth-mindset framework. I acknowledged how that connection could help students understand and accept the new arrangement. I emphasized common language so that students would hear a consistent explanation of the groups' purpose and encounter familiar expectations in each room. Teachers felt that this shared approach was helping students manage transitions and begin taking responsibility for the routines. The discussion linked student participation with deliberate adult coordination rather than attribute a smooth start only to student enthusiasm.</p>\n<p>I also encouraged proactive communication about academic, behavioral, and transition needs. Teachers described how exchanging this information helped them support students safely in another classroom. I affirmed the value of those practices and asked members to continue sharing relevant information as group membership changed. Flexible placement requires continuing communication, because a receiving teacher may need to know more than the student's assessment score. The group regarded these conversations as part of implementation rather than an unrelated administrative task.</p>\n<p>In summarizing the meeting, I connected the preassessment patterns with the initial classroom observations. The team continued to see addition and subtraction with regrouping as an appropriate focus. Members identified engagement, clearer expectations, and improving transitions as early strengths, while language scaffolds and consistent procedures needed further attention. We agreed to continue Math Crew and refine the arrangements as more evidence became available.</p>\n<p>The discussion also reinforced why logistical details belong in an instructional meeting during a new implementation. Students need to know where to go and what to expect before they can use the learning time well. Consistent procedures allow teachers to focus more of that time on the different academic needs identified by the assessment.</p>\n<p>Before the next meeting, teachers would collect student work, observations, and notes about responses to instruction. They would continue using the initial assessment to inform groups while watching for evidence that a placement or lesson needed to change. I would organize shared observations, support check-ins, and facilitate the next reflection. Our next discussion would examine early progress, possible changes in grouping or teaching, and the supports needed by students requiring additional scaffolding or enrichment.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Record one concrete early learning pattern alongside the launch logistics.</li><li>Distinguish member reports, documents examined, and your own observations.</li><li>Assign the agreed procedural or instructional changes to named roles and set a review date.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part F / Implementation minutes 2","url":"#cycle/c2/F2","html":"<span id=\"f-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / Implementation minutes 2</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Document the group’s learning progress by maintaining detailed agendas and minutes that you will submit for each implementation meeting. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nWithin the minutes, document the range of agreed-upon support you provide to the group and/or to individual members.\nBased on your implementation meeting discussion and analysis of findings, track progress, early impact, and/or initial results of the group’s implementation of the strategy (e.g., Is the strategy making a difference in what or how students are learning and/or experiencing at school?).\nDocument agreed-upon next steps the group will take between implementation meetings.</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a.\tWhat is going well?</p><p>b.\tWhat needs adjustment?</p><p>c.\tPLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part F template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Show what the group learned after trying the strategy and carrying out prior decisions. Record the evidence behind adjustments, including student movement between groups, instructional supports, and the support you agreed to provide.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I opened the discussion by asking teachers what they were noticing as Math Crew continued. They reported that students generally responded positively to the rotation structure and seemed motivated by working with different teachers. Several described students looking forward to the group period. Some learners were already demonstrating strong addition-with-regrouping skills and appeared ready for more demanding work. Other teachers described success using visual models, gestures, and clearly sequenced steps to help students interpret and solve word problems. These examples gave the group concrete teaching practices to discuss rather than rely only on a general impression that students enjoyed the groups.</p>\n<p>I connected these accounts to the purpose of intentional grouping and planning. The observations suggested that teachers were creating more accessible entry points while also identifying students who needed additional challenge. Members working with advanced groups said students were eager to attempt more complex problems. I acknowledged this as an indication that matching challenge to readiness was relevant across the range of learners. I summarized the shared pattern: students were responding at different levels, but teachers were finding ways to engage them with work appropriate to those levels. These remained early indicators from classroom experience, requiring continued review alongside learning evidence.</p>\n<p>We then examined what needed refinement. Word problems remained difficult for some students, particularly when they involved comparison language or unfamiliar academic vocabulary. Teachers also described uncertainty about ones, tens, and hundreds during regrouping. I asked members to explain how they were responding. They discussed adding visual representations, modeling their reasoning, using gestures, and breaking a procedure into an explicit sequence. This allowed colleagues to connect a particular difficulty with a particular instructional adjustment and consider whether that adjustment might be useful in another group.</p>\n<p>Placement was another area for review. Some students were progressing more quickly than expected, while others revealed needs that had not been obvious at the outset. Teachers discussed moving students between groups. I emphasized that such movement is central to a flexible arrangement: a change in placement can show that teachers are attending to current evidence rather than indicate that the initial plan was unsuccessful. We also considered pace. Students ready to advance needed continued challenge, while others required more time with foundational concepts. Toward the end of this discussion, the team agreed that the current cycle might need to run longer before we moved to a new focus.</p>\n<p>I helped the group identify three connected areas for adjustment: the instructional supports within each group, the evidence used for placement changes, and the time available for learning. Framing these as normal parts of implementation made room for teachers to discuss difficulty openly. The purpose was to improve the fit between instruction and students rather than defend the original plan. Members could then consider revisions without treating every change as a departure from the agreed strategy.</p>\n<p>During reflection, teachers again noted students' interest in rotations and the opportunity to learn with different adults. They described greater participation and a growing sense of responsibility for learning. They also identified explicit demonstrations, visuals, and hands-on materials as useful supports for regrouping and word problems. I acknowledged these observations while drawing attention to the team's role in making the supports available. Members were comparing experiences, modifying tasks, moving students when appropriate, and planning different levels of challenge. Their collaboration was becoming visible in instructional decisions rather than only in meeting attendance.</p>\n<p>I summarized that teachers were beginning to plan deliberately for each group's needs. This included selecting tasks, deciding how much support to provide, and identifying opportunities for extension. Sharing those decisions helped the grade level maintain coherence while avoiding identical instruction for students with different starting points. I connected that work with shared responsibility for student learning and more equitable access to suitable instruction. The team would still need to study outcomes over time before making a broad judgment about effectiveness.</p>\n<p>The extension of the cycle was therefore a response to evidence, not simply a change of calendar. Teachers wanted enough time for students to develop understanding before drawing conclusions from the later assessment. At the same time, students ready for greater challenge would continue receiving differentiated tasks rather than wait for every group to move together.</p>\n<p>At the close, we agreed to extend the current cycle and continue monitoring progress. Teachers would keep planning targeted lessons using foundational practice, visual modeling, and enrichment tasks. They would reconsider placement as new evidence emerged and document observations or student work for the next meeting. I clarified what members should bring and restated my role in supporting coordination and reflection. The next discussion would review the effects of continued instruction, decide whether further refinements were needed, and consider readiness for a new content focus. These actions linked the meeting's observations with an explicit plan for the next stage of implementation.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the evidence supporting at least one group movement or instructional adjustment.</li><li>Report completion and results of actions agreed in the previous meeting.</li><li>Record who will provide the agreed support and what evidence will return in meeting 3.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part F / Implementation minutes 3","url":"#cycle/c2/F3","html":"<span id=\"f-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / Implementation minutes 3</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Document the group’s learning progress by maintaining detailed agendas and minutes that you will submit for each implementation meeting. You may either record your minutes using this sample template or one you prepare with the support of your program. If using this sample template, type your responses within the brackets following each prompt.\nWithin the minutes, document the range of agreed-upon support you provide to the group and/or to individual members.\nBased on your implementation meeting discussion and analysis of findings, track progress, early impact, and/or initial results of the group’s implementation of the strategy (e.g., Is the strategy making a difference in what or how students are learning and/or experiencing at school?).\nDocument agreed-upon next steps the group will take between implementation meetings.</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a.\tWhat is going well?</p><p>b.\tWhat needs adjustment?</p><p>c.\tPLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part F template; meeting-record format is optional</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Record the group's interpretation of initial results, including successes, continuing needs, limitations, and next actions. Show how members reached a shared conclusion rather than simply declaring that the strategy worked.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I facilitated a review of the postassessment after several weeks of Math Crew instruction. Teachers reported that most students had maintained or improved their performance. One account identified growth for 14 of 17 students, while another described 17 of 20 as maintaining or improving. Many students gained one or two performance points. The distinction between growth and maintaining a score needs to remain visible when interpreting those reports. The results nevertheless gave the team a concrete basis for examining progress rather than relying exclusively on students' enthusiasm for the groups.</p>\n<p>We compared patterns across classrooms. Teachers described how grouping had allowed them to match instruction more closely to readiness. Students prepared for greater challenge worked on more complex tasks, while others received focused teaching in place value and regrouping. I highlighted the relationship between these instructional decisions and the progress teachers described. The evidence suggested promising changes in students' access and engagement, although a before-and-after comparison alone cannot isolate the effect of grouping from other instruction during the same period. The discussion therefore considered both the results and the way the strategy had been used.</p>\n<p>We also examined students who showed less progress. Teachers identified continuing difficulties with foundational place value and a need for additional targeted support. Members raised concerns about the postassessment itself, including whether some items matched the instruction students had received and whether their complexity was appropriate for all groups. These concerns mattered because an assessment can be difficult for reasons beyond the particular skill the team intended to measure. I asked members to consider pacing and assessment design together rather than interpret every low result as the same learning problem.</p>\n<p>The group agreed that future assessments should connect more precisely with the selected standards and skill progression. Teachers also wanted to check placement more often so students could move sooner when they needed different support or challenge. I emphasized that identifying these changes was part of responsive implementation. The evidence was useful because it helped the team reconsider its practice, not because it produced an uncomplicated success story. This discussion gave us specific improvements to carry into the next cycle.</p>\n<p>In reflecting on the strategy, teachers said that a narrower range of readiness within each group made focused teaching more manageable. They could respond to misconceptions more quickly and select tasks suited to the learners present. Reviewing results with colleagues also increased their confidence in decisions about grouping, lesson planning, and task modification. I acknowledged how this shared analysis supported a collective responsibility for outcomes. The teachers were using information from several classrooms to guide their decisions rather than treating each classroom as a separate inquiry.</p>\n<p>My summary brought together the positive performance patterns, continuing place-value needs, and assessment-alignment concerns. The group saw value in maintaining a flexible structure while improving how it assessed and responded to learning. We discussed moving most students toward multiplication and division, with continued small-group instruction in addition and subtraction for those who needed it. That direction allowed the team to advance the curriculum without assuming that every student was ready for the same next step.</p>\n<p>The meeting also clarified that progress should be examined within groups as well as across the grade. A generally positive total can coexist with students who still need substantial help. Teachers wanted the next plan to preserve that distinction. The assessment discussion was similarly specific: greater rigor is useful when it reveals the intended learning, but a task that does not align with the taught skill can make interpretation harder. These considerations informed both the proposed new content and the continued foundational support.</p>\n<p>For the next cycle, teachers agreed to revise the preassessment around specific standards and consider released or standards-based items. They would bring updated instructional plans and assessment tools to the next meeting. Continued monitoring would help determine whether students needed more foundational practice or were ready for greater challenge. I clarified these expectations and connected them with the concerns raised during the meeting. Our next discussion would examine how the revised assessment process supports grouping and instruction. The outcome was a plan to retain useful features of the strategy while addressing the limitations the evidence had revealed.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Link each reported result to the exact assessment, scoring method, and group of students.</li><li>Record the differing interpretations and how the group resolved or retained uncertainty.</li><li>Specify the next instructional response for students needing reinforcement and those ready to extend learning.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part G / Collaborative work products","url":"#cycle/c2/G","html":"<span id=\"g-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-g\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part G / Collaborative work products</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Collect and submit key collaborative work products (up to 10 pages) used during the implementation meetings (e.g., lesson plans, student work products, instructional materials, assessment tools, data or evidence collected).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 19; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit actual products used or created in the community's work, within the handbook's page limit. Explain their purpose, when the group used them, and which decisions they support. A product can document planning, implementation, or a result, but those functions should not be confused.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Work product 1: Two-page regrouping planning record. The team developed this document together to define its addition and subtraction focus. It connects lesson planning, task choices, and the interpretation of student understanding so that teachers can coordinate their work. The two pages serve as a common reference for the instructional cycle.</p>\n<p>Work product 2: Initial planning minutes. This record describes the team's development of a preassessment and its decisions about the practical arrangements for small-group mathematics. It shows how assessment preparation and classroom logistics were considered together before implementation.</p>\n<p>Work product 3: Scoring and grouping minutes. These notes record the discussion of initial assessment results, the formation of temporary instructional groups, and the allocation of teaching responsibilities. They connect student evidence with the organization of targeted instruction.</p>\n<p>Work product 4: Two-page common assessment. The blank instrument represents the tasks used before and after instruction to examine students' understanding of regrouping. Its role is to support comparisons over the cycle and inform subsequent teaching, grouping, and reflection.</p>\n<p>Work product 5: Class assessment summary. This record presents initial and later scores for 17 students without identifying them. The commentary associated with the third clip refers to it during discussion of student outcomes and the level of assessment demand.</p>\n<p>Each record has a distinct place in the sequence from initial planning to the review of results.</p>\n<p>Work product 6: Additional postassessment discussed with clip 3. This blank assessment is referenced in the discussion of whether the tasks matched the instructional focus and expected rigor. Together, the products document planning, implementation, and reflection; their descriptions do not substitute for independently checking the underlying evidence.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Label each product with its date, purpose, group use, and relationship to a specific decision.</li><li>Distinguish blank tools, completed work, aggregated results, and the assessment that raised alignment concerns.</li><li>Reconcile the visible score pairs, the 14-of-17 heading, and the broader 72-of-85 report; distinguish growth, maintenance, and decline.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part H / Implementation meeting video clips","url":"#cycle/c2/H","html":"<span id=\"h-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-h\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part H / Implementation meeting video clips</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: From the videos of the implementation meetings where you and the individuals participating are seen and heard, select and submit 3 video clips, each up to 5 minutes (assessors will only watch and score the first 5 minutes of each video clip submitted), that include the following:</p><p>Video Clip 1: Setting the Purpose and Data Review (up to 5 minutes) o Select/create a video clip that demonstrates how you set the purpose for the meeting and how you facilitate the community of practice in analyzing the data from the implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy. • Video Clip 2: Professional Collaboration (up to 5 minutes) o Select/create a video clip that demonstrates your facilitation of the community of practice and document their agreements, their decisions, and how they arrive at a consensual understanding of the initial implementation results. Also include how you facilitate the community of practice to (a) respect diverse viewpoints, (b) respond to individual and collective feedback, (c) maintain the group’s focus and energy, and (d) monitor progress toward goals of the meetings.</p><p>Video Clip 3: Results and Next Steps (up to 5 minutes) • Select/create a video clip that demonstrates how you facilitate the community of practice in reporting and discussing the results of their implementation of the evidenced-based strategy and jointly plan for next steps/between meetings.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 20 and following page; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit the three required clips from authentic implementation meetings: setting purpose and examining data; professional collaboration; and results with jointly planned next steps. The handbook specifies up to five minutes for each clip. The written commentary must refer to what is actually visible and audible.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The commentary describes several recordings that were unavailable for inspection. This section therefore contains neither a replacement video nor a fabricated account of recorded dialogue.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Check the actual recordings against the three required functions and the time limits.</li><li>Verify that the candidate and required participants can be seen and heard and that permissions are in place.</li><li>Ensure each commentary claim aligns to its cited clip and timestamp; do not stage or script missing evidence.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 1 / Establishing meeting purpose","url":"#cycle/c2/I1","html":"<span id=\"i-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-i\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 1 / Establishing meeting purpose</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tDescribe how you set the purpose for the meetings/reviewed the problem of practice with the group.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use a specific clip moment to explain how you established the meeting's purpose and connected it to the problem of practice. Include what you did, why, and the response or change in the discussion that supports your interpretation of its impact.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The first clip is described as opening with a welcome and a statement that the team would review the beginning of Math Crew implementation (0:00-0:28). I then revisit the working agreements: attentive participation, preparation, openness to other perspectives, and maintaining the focus. I ask members to confirm those agreements before proceeding (0:29-0:53). This opening is intended to establish both a respectful way of working and a shared reason for the meeting. The emphasis is on collective responsibility for the inquiry rather than separate reports from individual classrooms.</p>\n<p>I next connect the discussion with earlier evidence and decisions. The commentary references our SBA and iReady review and the selection of addition and subtraction with regrouping as a prerequisite for grade-level mathematics (0:55-1:26). I restate the instructional problem as a need for more targeted, differentiated teaching in foundational numeracy so students can progress toward grade-level expectations (1:26-1:40). I also revisit flexible grouping through Math Crew and ask whether the team remains ready to continue with the approach (1:40-2:16). Returning to these decisions keeps the relationship between adult practice and student learning visible across meetings.</p>\n<p>The opening also provides an opportunity to renew agreement rather than assume that a previous decision still represents everyone's view. Confirming the strategy before analysis makes later discussion of adjustments easier to locate within the shared plan. It gives the team a common reference for distinguishing a change in implementation from a change in the overall purpose.</p>\n<p>The third clip is described as beginning with another explicit statement of purpose. After several weeks of implementation, the team would examine postassessment evidence and decide what should happen next (0:00-0:18). I frame the conversation as learning about the strategy's results and using that learning to guide subsequent action. This repeated opening links the meetings as stages in an ongoing inquiry rather than unrelated conversations. Across the two examples, purpose-setting involves identifying the current stage of the work while retaining the same instructional concern. The timestamps are references in the written commentary; the recordings themselves were not available to verify the described interactions.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Select a precise moment that includes both the purpose-setting action and the group's response.</li><li>Explain why that framing was needed at that point in implementation.</li><li>Describe its observable effect without assuming that a welcome or nod demonstrates shared understanding.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 2 / Evaluating implementation data","url":"#cycle/c2/I2","html":"<span id=\"i-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 2 / Evaluating implementation data</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tDescribe how you and the community of practice evaluated data from the implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the group interpreted evidence from using the strategy. Distinguish baseline information, implementation observations, and post-assessment findings. Show your role in helping members examine what the evidence does and does not establish.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Across the three described clips, the discussion moves from initial assessment information to classroom implementation evidence and then to postassessment results. My intention is to help teachers interpret what the evidence suggests about instruction, not simply report scores. That progression gives the data a role in revising practice and deciding what support students need next.</p>\n<p>In clip 1, I invite members to describe what they noticed on the assessment used for grouping (2:17-2:39). Teachers identify subtraction with regrouping and word problems as particular difficulties (2:44-4:12). I draw together observations across classrooms and identify the language demands of word problems as an equity concern for English learners (4:13-4:41). This synthesis helps turn individual accounts into a shared view of the instructional needs the team should monitor.</p>\n<p>Clip 2 shifts to what teachers are seeing while the groups operate. I begin by asking whether those observations suggest any adjustments (0:00-0:09). Members describe students ready for additional challenge or a different group (0:12-0:31), and students struggling with comparison language in word problems (0:34-0:47). They explain their use of visuals, gestures, and sequenced problem-solving steps (0:49-1:20). Other contributions concern students' motivation when tasks provide an appropriate level of challenge (1:27-1:58).</p>\n<p>I connect these examples with the purpose of flexible grouping. Different responses help teachers identify readiness and adapt instruction. Later in the clip, I explain that movement among groups is expected as students develop or reveal further needs (4:27-4:45). This frames adjustment as part of the agreed strategy rather than evidence that implementation is inconsistent.</p>\n<p>The three stages use different kinds of information for different decisions. Initial results help identify where to begin, classroom observations help teachers adjust while instruction is underway, and later results help them consider what to retain or change. Keeping that sequence visible supports a continuing inquiry instead of treating one assessment result as a complete evaluation of the approach.</p>\n<p>In clip 3, teachers discuss postassessment figures, including 14 of 17 and 17 of 20 students described as maintaining or improving (0:19-1:21). They report one- or two-point increases for many students and identify place-value needs among those with less progress (0:41-1:21). I summarize the broadly positive pattern while acknowledging continuing support needs (2:48-3:01). The discussion considers both promising results and limitations, including the meaning of maintaining a score. These descriptions show the intended movement from evidence to interpretation, but the unavailable recordings prevent independent confirmation of the interactions and the precise language used.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify which evidence at each timestamp is baseline, implementation, or outcome evidence.</li><li>Report the actual comparison, including students with no gain or a lower score.</li><li>Explain the question or facilitation move that helped the group test an interpretation rather than merely share results.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 3 / Reaching shared understanding","url":"#cycle/c2/I3","html":"<span id=\"i-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 3 / Reaching shared understanding</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tDescribe any agreements or decisions made by the group and how you facilitated the community of practice to arrive at a consensual understanding of any initial implementation results.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Describe the agreement and how members arrived at it. Identify the evidence, alternatives or concerns, and your action in checking understanding. Agreement is not established solely because the facilitator summarized a conclusion.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>In the first clip, I begin by confirming the team's common understanding of the problem and selected approach before turning to assessment analysis (0:29-2:16). After teachers describe initial results, I connect their accounts by identifying shared needs in subtraction with regrouping and the language of word problems (4:13-4:41). This summary is intended to help members agree on what the information suggests and what deserves attention during implementation. Agreement is grounded in the observations members have contributed.</p>\n<p>The second clip concerns decisions about teaching and group placement. Teachers discuss moving students when their readiness changes (0:12-0:31 and 2:27-3:27). I reinforce that changing groups is appropriate when current evidence indicates a different need (4:27-4:45). Presenting movement as an expected feature of the approach supports continued use of the strategy while leaving room for revision. It helps the team avoid treating the original grouping plan as fixed.</p>\n<p>In the third clip, consensus develops around the interpretation of results and the next instructional direction. Teachers report both progress and continuing needs. I ask what the results suggest we should do next (3:37-3:41). A member explains that a narrower readiness range makes focused support easier (3:43-4:10), then suggests making the next preassessment more specific (4:14-4:26). I summarize a direction in which many students move to new skills while others continue receiving small-group or push-in assistance (4:28-4:46).</p>\n<p>The summaries also make the basis for the next decision visible. Members can hear how their comments have been combined and can correct an interpretation before it becomes the team's plan. That checking process is particularly relevant when students need different next steps.</p>\n<p>Before the clip ends, I restate the next steps and relate them to the assessment discussion and teacher reflections (4:46-4:59). The intention is to confirm that the plan represents the group's contributions and addresses different readiness levels. Across the meetings, synthesis and confirmation help connect individual observations with collective action. A written description of consensus still has limits: the recordings were not available to examine whether each member explicitly agreed or how disagreement was handled. The commentary should therefore be read as an account of the facilitation, with its evidence status kept visible.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>State one precise decision and the evidence used to make it.</li><li>Describe a differing view or uncertainty and how it influenced the decision.</li><li>Identify the clip moment where members confirmed the shared interpretation or retained an unresolved question.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 4 / Supporting equitable participation","url":"#cycle/c2/I4","html":"<span id=\"i-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 4 / Supporting equitable participation</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tDescribe how you</p><p>valued and respected diverse viewpoints,</p><p>responded to individual and collective feedback,</p><p>maintained the group’s focus and energy, and</p><p>monitored progress toward the goals of the meetings.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all four components: diverse viewpoints, individual and collective feedback, group focus and energy, and progress toward the meeting goals. Analyze the effect of your actions on participation and reasoning, not simply your use of polite language.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><ul><li>Demonstrate facilitation that supports consensus, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus and energy.</li><li>Analyze what you did and why, using moments visible in the clips.</li><li>For Level 4, purposefully seek diverse viewpoints and clearly encourage every member to contribute.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Across the three described clips, I use questions, acknowledgement, and summaries to support teacher participation while keeping the instructional purpose visible. Different interpretations can contribute to a fuller understanding of students rather than simply slow agreement.</p>\n<p>In clip 1, I revisit agreements about respect and openness to other perspectives (0:29-0:52). I invite each teacher to contribute observations (2:27-2:40). When members identify different concerns, including subtraction difficulty and language access for English learners, I connect them in the summary rather than select only one as relevant (4:13-4:41). This gives several kinds of classroom evidence a place in the shared interpretation.</p>\n<p>Clip 2 opens with a focused question about changes suggested by current observations (0:00-0:09). I use follow-up questions to clarify contributions. For example, when a teacher describes a group as especially difficult, I clarify whether the reference concerns the level of challenge and the support required (1:50-1:57). Later, I summarize the different supports groups need and the expected movement between them (4:27-5:00). These moves are intended to maintain a useful instructional focus without dismissing teachers' experiences.</p>\n<p>Maintaining focus therefore involves both inviting contributions and connecting them with the purpose. Acknowledging a concern is followed by asking what it means for instruction, assessment, or the next decision the team needs to make.</p>\n<p>In clip 3, I identify the purpose as reviewing results and deciding next steps (0:00-0:18). I listen to concerns about assessment demand and students with limited progress (1:32-2:46), acknowledge those concerns, and also note the broadly positive pattern reported by teachers (2:48-3:01). I then ask the group to consider subsequent action (3:37-3:41). The account illustrates an effort to balance recognition of strengths with attention to unresolved needs. Because the clips were unavailable, the written commentary cannot by itself verify how equitably speaking time was distributed or how participants experienced these facilitation choices.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify who contributed at the cited moments and how you invited perspectives not yet heard.</li><li>Show how feedback changed the discussion or decision, rather than only noting that it was acknowledged.</li><li>Connect a specific focus or pacing adjustment to progress on the meeting's goal.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 5 / Determining next steps together","url":"#cycle/c2/I5","html":"<span id=\"i-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 5 / Determining next steps together</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>5.\tDescribe how you collaborated with the group to determine any next steps for implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Show how evidence led the group to agree on its next actions. Explain what the candidate and members will do, how the plan responds to different needs, and how later evidence will reveal whether the response was useful.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.6: Implementation results and next steps</h3><ul><li>Facilitate shared understanding of initial results and jointly determine next steps.</li><li>Analyze how the discussion supported those decisions.</li><li>For Level 4, consistently connect results and next steps within and between meetings; Level 5 adds joint adjustment to successes or challenges.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Across the meetings, I connect evidence with action by posing questions, summarizing proposals, and checking the group's direction. The purpose is to make subsequent steps emerge from shared discussion rather than assign them independently after the meeting.</p>\n<p>In clip 1, after the preassessment discussion, I ask whether groups have started and how frequently they are meeting (4:44-4:58). This links the initial data with the practical use of the strategy and establishes that implementation itself needs monitoring.</p>\n<p>In clip 2, members propose changes in placement, additional visuals and gestures, and renewed attention to place-value concepts (0:12-1:21 and 2:27-3:52). I acknowledge these suggestions and explain that changes in grouping and teaching belong within the flexible approach (4:27-4:45). This supports the team's willingness to adapt the plan as students respond.</p>\n<p>Clip 3 focuses on the postassessment and the next content priorities. I ask how we should respond both to students needing reinforcement and to those ready to advance (3:34-3:41). Teachers support continued small-group addition and subtraction practice for some learners while most begin multiplication and division (4:27-4:44). They also identify a need for assessments that more precisely match the intended standards and reveal students' understanding (4:15-4:25).</p>\n<p>The action sequence includes implementation frequency, instructional supports, placement decisions, new content, and assessment design. Each is connected with evidence discussed by the team rather than introduced as an unrelated task.</p>\n<p>I close by summarizing these decisions so members leave with a shared account of responsibilities and direction. The described next steps connect teacher dialogue with student evidence and the goal of access to grade-level mathematics. The written commentary supplies the sequence and timestamps; it does not replace direct review of the recordings, which were unavailable for inspection.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each next step to a stated finding and the students it is intended to support.</li><li>Record the responsible person, needed support, timeline, and evidence for the next review.</li><li>Trace at least one action from agreement to implementation to the following meeting's interpretation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 1 / Results, feedback, and leadership growth","url":"#cycle/c2/J1","html":"<span id=\"j-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-j\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 1 / Results, feedback, and leadership growth</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tRefer to specific evidence (including agendas, minutes, group work products, implementation data, videos, and/or participant feedback) to analyze how your leadership actions influenced the community of practice’s progress toward its goal. Reflect on initial implementation results and feedback from the group to evaluate your strengths and areas for growth as a facilitator. Make explicit connections between the evidence, your leadership skills, equitable&nbsp;facilitation practices, and your next steps for professional growth.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Bring the evidence, your leadership actions, initial results, member feedback, strengths, growth needs, equitable facilitation, and professional next steps into one analysis. The question asks what the evidence teaches you about your leadership, not only whether the group completed its work.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>My leadership in Cycle 2 centered on establishing the conditions for a community of practice to move from planning into instruction and reflection. I prepared structures and shared resources that helped members focus on the problem of students' understanding of addition and subtraction with regrouping. The aim was to make targeted small-group teaching a coordinated inquiry rather than a set of unrelated classroom activities. Preparation mattered because members needed both an instructional purpose and workable arrangements before they could study students' responses.</p>\n<p>After the initial planning session, I facilitated another meeting devoted to the common preassessment and implementation logistics. The team developed the assessment together and clarified a regular weekly period for small-group instruction, as recorded in work product 2. Establishing the tool, schedule, and content focus in advance reduced the number of practical questions teachers had to resolve separately. It allowed the subsequent discussion to concentrate on students' needs and the teaching those needs required.</p>\n<p>I did not attend the following data-analysis meeting. The minutes nevertheless show that members continued the work: they scored the initial assessment together, compared patterns, and formed groups around the identified skills, as documented in work product 3. This is relevant evidence of continuity and shared responsibility. The team could act without waiting for me to direct each step. It suggests that the earlier planning supported distributed leadership, although it would be an overstatement to attribute every independent decision solely to my facilitation.</p>\n<p>The written account of clip 1 describes teachers referring to common instructional practices during early implementation. The shared assessment, work product 4, supplied initial and later measures of understanding. In the account of clip 3, teachers use the class score summary in work product 5 to discuss outcomes and question assessment demand and alignment. These connections among tools, meetings, and instruction show how the community intended to monitor early results and revise practice. The recordings were unavailable for independent review, so the written descriptions remain the evidence available for those interactions.</p>\n<p>Advance preparation and clear structure were strengths in my facilitation. Planning for both instruction and logistics helped members continue the inquiry, including at a meeting I could not attend. The minutes and commentary describe teachers analyzing results, grouping students, and discussing instruction in relation to the same goal. The aggregate report indicates that 72 of 85 students, approximately 85%, maintained or improved their scores. For students who gained one or two points on an eight-point assessment, those gains represent 12.5 or 25 percentage points of the available scale. Maintaining and improving should be distinguished, and these changes should not be described as proof that the strategy alone caused growth. They do, however, provide evidence for the team's reflection.</p>\n<p>A second strength was supporting an atmosphere in which teachers could discuss difficulties honestly. The account of clip 3 includes concern that a postassessment demanded more than some students had encountered in instruction. We treated that concern as a question about alignment rather than an occasion to blame a teacher or student. Members' willingness to raise the issue suggests a degree of trust and shared ownership. It also gave the group useful information for revising future assessments and deciding what students still needed.</p>\n<p>When I asked for feedback, members described the meetings as organized, collaborative, and supportive of candid reflection. Several expressed interest in having me facilitate future discussions. I value that feedback, while recognizing that positive comments should be considered alongside observed practice and specific examples. It encouraged me to retain the structures that were useful and to improve the moments where my facilitation could be clearer. Creating conditions for professional learning includes making it possible to examine misalignment without defensiveness and turn that examination into an instructional decision.</p>\n<p>My clearest growth area was synthesizing contributions while a discussion was still unfolding. At times, I found it difficult to retain the details from several teachers and immediately articulate the pattern connecting them. Reviewing the written record and my account of the recordings helped me identify places where a pause, a paraphrase, or visible notes could have improved accuracy. I want to slow the conversation deliberately when needed so that a summary represents the contributions rather than merely the comments I remember most readily.</p>\n<p>The group's continued work during my absence also challenged the idea that facilitation requires constant control. Shared tools and a clear purpose can allow members to lead, while the facilitator helps maintain continuity across meetings. I want to build on that experience by making opportunities for member leadership more deliberate.</p>\n<p>This is also an equity concern. Listening carefully, checking interpretations, and making ideas visible can prevent the most confident voices from determining the group's conclusion too early. Documentation has a related role: the minutes preserved decisions and allowed the inquiry to continue between meetings. In future facilitation, I would use shared notes during the conversation to capture patterns, questions, and next steps as they emerge. These changes would build on my preparation strengths while improving real-time synthesis, representation of different perspectives, and clarity about collective decisions.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Separate improved, unchanged, and lower scores; state the score scale and correct the units for gains.</li><li>Use a specific member comment and a meeting event to explain a strength and a growth need.</li><li>Connect the proposed changes in listening, synthesis, and documentation to equitable participation and a way to evaluate your growth.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 2 / Professionalism, integrity, and equity","url":"#cycle/c2/J2","html":"<span id=\"j-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 2 / Professionalism, integrity, and equity</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tRefer to specific evidence (including the agendas and minutes, group work products and initial implementation results, videos and commentary, and/or group feedback on the process) of how effectively you maintained a high standard of professionalism, integrity, and equity (e.g., respect for diverse viewpoints) as you worked with members of the community of practice.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Analyze how you acted professionally and equitably throughout the inquiry. Use decisions or difficult moments to show how you treated disagreement, represented evidence accurately, and shared influence. Listing norms is not the same as demonstrating their use.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I sought to demonstrate professionalism and integrity by keeping the discussion respectful, centered on student evidence, and open to shared decisions. At the additional planning meeting, I organized the agenda so members could contribute to assessment design and instructional arrangements, as shown in work product 2. We used the common student information to guide decisions rather than treat an individual's preference as sufficient justification. This supported a professional learning environment in which colleagues could explain their reasoning and influence the plan.</p>\n<p>The working agreements also supported equity in participation. Openness to different perspectives, focused discussion, and respectful interaction were identified in the planning agenda and revisited in later agendas. These agreements were intended to give different instructional experiences a place in the conversation while maintaining the common goal. Their value depends on how they are enacted, including whether members' ideas are heard and accurately represented.</p>\n<p>During implementation discussions, I encouraged candid examination of practice and acknowledged teachers' professional knowledge. The account of clip 3 describes concern that a postassessment, identified in work product 6, exceeded the demand of the instruction. I guided the discussion toward the relationship between teaching and assessment and the changes needed next. Focusing on that relationship allowed the team to consider a difficulty without assigning blame. It illustrates the kind of trust and professional responsibility I wanted to support: use a concern as evidence for learning and improvement. The written account describes this intention and action, while the unavailable recording limits independent verification.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Analyze a specific difficult exchange or disagreement and its effect on participation.</li><li>Show how members' contributions changed a decision across more than one stage of the cycle.</li><li>Explain how assessment limitations changed what you were willing to claim about learning.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 3 / Responding to school context","url":"#cycle/c2/J3","html":"<span id=\"j-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 3 / Responding to school context</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tWhat aspects of school culture and context influenced the facilitation of a community of practice at your school site/educational setting? What actions did you take to respond to these contextually based challenges or supports?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain which features of school culture or scheduling supported or constrained the work, what you did in response, and what resulted. Distinguish the group's independent work from meetings you personally facilitated.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>School conditions shaped both the implementation plan and my facilitation. Limited instructional time made it necessary to use existing arrangements carefully. The team established a dependable weekly period for small groups that fit the school schedule and mathematics priorities. This helped make the strategy feasible without displacing the core instruction it was intended to support.</p>\n<p>Grade-level collaboration time was another important condition. Teams have scheduled opportunities to meet twice a month while students attend physical education. Because I teach a different grade, I could not attend the second community meeting held during that period. I supported the teachers' decision to use the time rather than ask them to wait for me. They wanted to score the preassessment and form groups, and their initiative indicated growing ownership of the inquiry.</p>\n<p>The school's emphasis on collaboration and shared leadership helped make that possible. Members continued analysis and planning independently. My response was to ensure that the earlier structures were clear and then support coordination and reflection in subsequent meetings. I did not need to reclaim control simply because the team had progressed without my presence. Their ability to continue was relevant to the community's development.</p>\n<p>Using existing time also respected teachers' responsibilities and allowed their motivation to carry the work forward within the school's established schedule.</p>\n<p>Teachers also brought different levels of experience with regrouping instruction. I kept the discussion connected to common student evidence so those differences could become resources for problem-solving rather than reasons to label colleagues or students negatively. This supported a shared responsibility for learners across classrooms. Together, the scheduling arrangements, collaborative culture, and varied instructional experience required facilitation that combined preparation with flexibility. The work had to fit the setting while still allowing members to shape and sustain the inquiry themselves.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Provide a dated chronology separating additional planning sessions from the required implementation meetings.</li><li>Identify which meetings you attended and what you learned from records of others.</li><li>Explain how support before and after the independent session affected the work without claiming actions you did not perform.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 4 / Professional learning goals","url":"#cycle/c2/J4","html":"<span id=\"j-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 4 / Professional learning goals</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tBased on the analysis of your experiences in Cycle 2, identify specific professional learning goals and describe your next steps for growth.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Set specific professional learning goals derived from the cycle's evidence. Explain what you will practice or study, where you will apply it, what feedback you will seek, and how you will judge progress.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on equitable facilitation</h3><ul><li>Use initial results and member feedback to analyze strengths, growth, and next professional steps.</li><li>Support the reflection with specific cycle evidence and explain equitable facilitation.</li><li>For Level 4, deeply analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity consistently across the inquiry; Level 5 uses research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>My next professional learning goals follow from the strengths and difficulties identified in the meeting records and reflection. First, I want to improve how I synthesize contributions during a discussion. Preparation helped the community work productively, but I need to pause more consistently, paraphrase ideas, and record them where everyone can see them. I plan to use structured shared notes and deliberate pauses so patterns can be identified accurately and different voices remain represented in the summary.</p>\n<p>Second, I want to develop shared leadership more intentionally. The team already demonstrated that it could continue the inquiry without me. I can build on that by inviting members to lead parts of meetings, guide a data discussion, or summarize the group's thinking. I will ask about their interests and prepare agendas that distribute these responsibilities. This should deepen participation and ownership while giving members opportunities to practice facilitation themselves.</p>\n<p>Member feedback will help me judge the usefulness of these changes.</p>\n<p>I also plan to use documentation more actively during meetings, capturing decisions and next steps as the conversation develops rather than reconstruct them afterward. Brief opportunities for participant feedback will help me learn whether those changes improve clarity and inclusion. These goals connect directly to the cycle: retain useful planning structures, strengthen listening and synthesis, and create more opportunities for the community to lead its own work.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Turn each goal into a specific practice to try in an identified future meeting or inquiry cycle.</li><li>Specify the feedback and meeting evidence that will show whether the practice helped.</li><li>Set a review point to examine progress and revise the professional learning plan.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"}],"anchors":["top","preparation","foundation","A1","a-q1","part-a","A2","a-q2","A3","a-q3","A4","a-q4","A5","a-q5","A6","a-q6","A7","a-q7","B","b-q6","b-q5","b-q4","b-q3","b-q2","b-q1","part-b","C","c-q6","c-q5","c-q4","c-q3","c-q2","c-q1","part-c","D1","d-q1","part-d","D2","d-q2","D3","d-q3","D4","d-q4","D5","d-q5","D6","d-q6","D7","d-q7","D8","d-q8","E1","e-q1","part-e","E2","e-q2","E3","e-q3","F1","f-q1","part-f","F2","f-q2","F3","f-q3","G","g-q1","part-g","H","h-q1","part-h","I1","i-q1","part-i","I2","i-q2","I3","i-q3","I4","i-q4","I5","i-q5","J1","j-q1","part-j","J2","j-q2","J3","j-q3","J4","j-q4","alignment","limits","source-record","scores","rubric-2-1","rubric-2-2","rubric-2-3","rubric-2-4","rubric-2-5","rubric-2-6","rubric-2-7"],"aliases":{"part-a":"A1","a-q1":"A1","a-q2":"A2","a-q3":"A3","a-q4":"A4","a-q5":"A5","a-q6":"A6","a-q7":"A7","part-b":"B","b-q1":"B","b-q2":"B","b-q3":"B","b-q4":"B","b-q5":"B","b-q6":"B","part-c":"C","c-q1":"C","c-q2":"C","c-q3":"C","c-q4":"C","c-q5":"C","c-q6":"C","part-d":"D1","d-q1":"D1","d-q2":"D2","d-q3":"D3","d-q4":"D4","d-q5":"D5","d-q6":"D6","d-q7":"D7","d-q8":"D8","part-e":"E1","e-q1":"E1","e-q2":"E2","e-q3":"E3","part-f":"F1","f-q1":"F1","f-q2":"F2","f-q3":"F3","part-g":"G","g-q1":"G","part-h":"H","h-q1":"H","part-i":"I1","i-q1":"I1","i-q2":"I2","i-q3":"I3","i-q4":"I4","i-q5":"I5","part-j":"J1","j-q1":"J1","j-q2":"J2","j-q3":"J3","j-q4":"J4","foundation":"preparation","rubric-map":"scores","final-check":"scores"}},"c3":{"title":"Cycle 3: Understanding the Questions","html":"<div class=\"revised-cycle\"><header class=\"intro guide-heading\" id=\"top\"><h1>Cycle 3: Understanding the Questions</h1><p class=\"lead\">Cycle 3: Support teacher growth through coaching</p><p>Current questions, plain-language explanations, rubric components, and a critique of the interpolated example. Requirements come from the supplied Version 09 templates and handbook.</p></header><details class=\"contents-index\"><summary>Questions and reference</summary><nav aria-label=\"Cycle 3 questions\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/top\" aria-current=\"location\">Introduction</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/preparation\">Before you begin</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A1\">I.1 / Analyzing school coaching practices</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A2\">I.2 / Explaining processes and documents</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A3\">I.3 / Teacher input and ownership</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A4\">I.4 / Implications for this coaching cycle</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A5\">I.5 / CSTPs and teacher development</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A6\">II.1 / Understanding the volunteer teacher</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A7\">II.2 / Planning the coaching cycle</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/A8\">II.3 / Anticipating challenges</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/B\">1 / Classroom context and learning goals</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/C\">Teacher lesson plan and student-work description</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/D\">Pre-observation meeting video clips</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/E1\">1 / Teacher self-assessment</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/E2\">2 / Reviewing the lesson together</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/E3\">3 / Jointly selecting the CSTP focus</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/E4\">4 / Agreeing on observation evidence</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/F\">CSTP-focused observation notes</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/G\">Student work from the observed lesson</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/H\">Post-observation meeting video clips</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/I1\">1 / Jointly assessing student learning</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/I2\">2 / Teaching effectiveness and next steps</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/I3\">3 / Learning from teacher feedback</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/J1\">1 / Coaching strengths and areas for growth</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/J2\">2 / Sustaining a two-way partnership</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/J3\">3 / Equity and continuous improvement</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/alignment\">Template and handbook</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/limits\">Submission requirements</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/source-record\">Source notes</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/scores\">Example status</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\">Rubric 3.5: Joint next steps for teacher growth</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-7\">Rubric 3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</a></nav></details><section id=\"preparation\" class=\"preparation major-section\"><span id=\"foundation\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Before you begin</h2><p>Read the whole cycle before meeting the volunteer teacher: investigate school practices and teacher experience; jointly plan a CSTP-focused observation; observe instruction and collect student work; jointly examine the evidence and next steps; reflect on your coaching.</p><p>Plan to observe the lesson live, collecting evidence while instruction occurs, and then use the recording for joint review. The handbook calls for an observation of at least 20 minutes and recording its full duration. Plan permissions, the two pre-observation clips, post-observation clips, and student-work collection beforehand. Coaching decisions should remain jointly owned by you and the teacher.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Scores are instructional estimates of the complete rubric, not separate scores for individual questions. Video-dependent estimates are provisional; recordings were not reviewed. The interpolated examples are teaching parallels to examine, not models to copy.</p></section><article id=\"A1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-a\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.1 / Analyzing school coaching practices</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tOutline how often during the year teachers are coached, observed, or provided instructional feedback and what the intended outcomes are for the school, teachers, and students. After reviewing current practices at this school, provide an analysis of the effectiveness of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain frequency, procedures, and intended outcomes for the school, teachers, and students, then evaluate effectiveness using available evidence. Distinguish evaluation, walkthroughs, and voluntary instructional coaching; they may serve different purposes and create different relationships.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Certificated teachers at this school participate in an annual evaluation with a site administrator. The process begins with a pre-observation conference, includes two formal observations, and concludes each observation with a follow-up conference. At the opening meeting, the teacher consults district materials and selects one or two elements of the California Standards for the Teaching Profession as a focus. Those elements guide both the teacher's preparation and the administrator's collection of evidence. One observation occurs in the fall and another in the spring, with a post-observation conference held within five days of each visit.</p>\n<p>During the follow-up conferences, the administrator discusses instructional practice in relation to the selected standards. The stated purposes are to maintain a strong certificated staff capable of advancing student achievement and to help teachers understand and develop their practice. Administrators also conduct several informal walkthroughs during the year. These shorter visits do not appear to have a consistent preconference or postconference attached to them. The formal process therefore provides a clearer sequence for preparation and feedback than the informal visits.</p>\n<p>Recent achievement results raise questions about the school's improvement work. Across grades three through five, the proportion meeting ELA proficiency declined from 34% in 2023 to 18% in 2025. Mathematics proficiency declined from 24% to 17% over the same period. These trends do not isolate the effect of evaluation or coaching, but they indicate that the school cannot assume its existing practices have produced the intended achievement gains. Additional evidence would be needed to understand the relationship between teacher support, instructional changes, and student outcomes.</p>\n<p>This year is the district's first year of implementing instructional coaching. The elementary school is one of four sites assigned a full-time coach. Teachers may volunteer for a coaching cycle, and the process depends on establishing a trusting, collaborative relationship. Rather than begin with an administrator's evaluation requirements, the coach and teacher identify a focus connected to student strengths and needs. They use the CSTPs to help establish goals, plan instruction together, and decide what evidence of student learning to collect.</p>\n<p>The partners also decide how the lesson will be taught and observed. The teacher may teach while the coach observes; the coach may model while the teacher observes; or they may teach together and bring their observations to the later discussion. These options allow the arrangement to respond to the teacher's learning needs. Afterward, they examine student work and observation information, reflect on instruction, make adjustments, acknowledge progress, and identify subsequent steps. The intended emphasis is learning through shared analysis rather than simply receiving a judgment about performance.</p>\n<p>Because the coaching role is new at the site, its effectiveness cannot yet be established. A later review would need to include next year's SBAC ELA and mathematics results together with other quantitative and qualitative evidence. Student outcomes should be considered alongside information about participation, the coaching process, and changes in instruction. This would provide a more useful account than attributing a future score change to coaching alone. The present task is to implement the process thoughtfully and collect evidence that can inform that later analysis.</p>\n<p>Both administrator evaluation and instructional coaching contain practices related to adult learning. Aguilar (2024) emphasizes trust, attention to adults' previous experience, agency in learning, and opportunities to apply knowledge and receive relevant feedback. In the preconference, teachers exercise agency by selecting a standards focus and a content area. They draw on what they know about their students when designing lessons and identifying strengths and needs. An agreed observation focus helps make the evidence relevant to the learning they intend to pursue.</p>\n<p>The contrast between evaluation and voluntary coaching is relevant to how teachers may experience feedback. Both use standards and observation, but the coach must establish a relationship in which the teacher can discuss uncertainty and request help. Jointly deciding whether to observe, model, or co-teach gives the partners a way to match the process to the teacher's learning. The follow-up discussion then provides an opportunity to examine the resulting evidence together and choose an appropriate continuation of that work.</p>\n<p>The postconference provides a setting for timely feedback and reflection. In the administrator process, feedback about observed practice may help a teacher retain new learning and transfer it into everyday instruction. In coaching, discussion centers on student evidence and the reasons a particular instructional problem matters. This fits an adult-learning orientation toward practical problems and meaningful application. The processes are not identical, especially in their evaluative roles, but each can support learning when teachers understand the purpose, contribute to the focus, and have an opportunity to use the feedback. Trust and a clear connection to student needs are especially important as the new coaching model develops.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Separate established evaluation practices from the new coaching model and identify their timelines.</li><li>Describe what the achievement trend establishes and what it cannot establish about coaching effectiveness.</li><li>Use available teacher feedback, observation records, and changes in instruction to evaluate the process more directly.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.2 / Explaining processes and documents</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tDescribe the processes, forms, and/or documents that are used to guide pre-observation meetings, collect observation data, and focus post-observation meetings (e.g., teacher response forms, observation tools, student work products, next steps for professional growth). If the school does not conduct teacher observations and uses a different approach to provide instructional feedback to teachers, provide a detailed description of the process(es) and forms used.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how documents are used before, during, and after observation. Identify what each tool records, how it connects to the CSTP focus, and how teachers use the resulting evidence. Naming a form is not a description of the process.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>For annual evaluation, the teacher and administrator complete a district digital form during the preconference. It records the selected CSTP elements, the observation arrangements, and relevant lesson context. The administrator uses the same district tool to document evidence during the visit. That record then becomes part of the postconference discussion, where the partners review what was observed and the administrator provides feedback on instruction.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Describe the relevant fields and who completes or reviews them at each stage.</li><li>Explain how observational evidence is separated from interpretation or evaluation.</li><li>Show how the tool connects CSTP evidence, student learning, feedback, and next professional steps.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.3 / Teacher input and ownership</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tExplain the level of input teachers have on the focus or purpose of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain which decisions teachers influence, how they exercise that choice, and any limits on it. Distinguish selecting from options offered by an administrator from genuinely shaping the purpose, evidence, and goals of coaching.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>In the formal evaluation process, teachers choose one or two CSTP elements to concentrate the feedback they receive after observation. They then select a subject area, plan and teach a lesson, and identify the student products to be collected. These choices give the observation a defined instructional focus. In a voluntary coaching cycle, the teacher and instructional coach establish the goals together, collaborate on lesson planning, and agree on the focus of feedback. The CSTPs provide a common reference for that discussion, while the partners connect the selected element with the teacher's learning priorities and students' needs. This shared selection helps make the feedback relevant to the planned lesson.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify a concrete example of teacher input changing the observation or feedback focus.</li><li>Explain how confidentiality, role clarity, and teacher choice operate in the local process.</li><li>Connect these practices to trust and teacher-specific needs using available evidence.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.4 / Implications for this coaching cycle</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tExplain the implications for this cycle based on the analysis of the current role of coaching practices (at the school) and the background and experience of the volunteer teacher.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what you will retain, adapt, or clarify because of the school's practices and this teacher's experience. The task asks for implications, not merely a statement that the teacher already knows the process.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The volunteer teacher is familiar with a sequence of planning, classroom observation, and follow-up reflection from earlier coaching experiences. She has also selected CSTP elements to focus evidence collection and feedback. This cycle can build on that familiarity while involving her again in choosing and applying the standards that will guide the observation and subsequent discussion of instruction.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify which familiar routines can be used and which features of this cycle need clarification.</li><li>Connect a known teacher experience or need to a specific coaching decision.</li><li>Explain how you will check that the teacher understands and agrees to the purpose and evidence collection.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A5\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.5 / CSTPs and teacher development</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>5.\tHow do the CSTPs guide or play a role in teacher growth and development at the school? (If the school uses another set of standards, provide a crosswalk to the CSTPs for the element[s] you have identified.)</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the standards guide ongoing growth: identifying a need, focusing evidence, interpreting practice, and deciding next steps. If a different local framework is used, provide the required crosswalk for the selected elements.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Teachers use selected CSTP elements to guide professional growth in both annual administrator evaluation and voluntary instructional coaching. The standards provide a shared reference for identifying a focus and discussing practice. They focus feedback.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain how the local process uses an element to focus observation evidence and feedback.</li><li>Describe how the same focus informs a professional learning decision.</li><li>Provide a crosswalk only if a different framework is actually used; do not invent one.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A6\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.1 / Understanding the volunteer teacher</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tProvide information about the volunteer teacher’s:</p><p>a.\tprofessional experience as a teacher (including years of service, school site[s], grades and content taught, languages)</p><p>b.\tcurrent assignment (grade level[s], content area, course titles)</p><p>c.\tpast experiences with coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback at the school site, in other previous teaching positions, or during teacher preparation</p><p>d.\tknowledge and use of the CSTP to guide their growth and development as a teacher at the school site, in other previous teaching positions, or during teacher preparation</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all four subparts: professional experience and languages, current assignment, previous coaching experiences, and knowledge and use of the CSTPs. Explain the background accurately enough to support the individualized plan in the next question.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The volunteer has completed two years as a full-time certificated teacher and is entering her third year in the classroom. She currently works at a TK-5 elementary school. In her first year, she taught a combined fourth- and fifth-grade class in another district in an anonymized area. She moved to the present school for her second year and taught fifth grade. She is fluent in English and also speaks and understands Cantonese.</p>\n<p>Her current assignment is a fifth-grade multiple-subject classroom. This means the coaching will take place within a setting she knows from the preceding year, although the students and their learning needs are those of her current class.</p>\n<p>She has participated in several forms of coaching. During student teaching, she met weekly with three other student teachers and an experienced mentor. The group planned lessons and prepared for observations together. Members took turns observing one another with the mentor, followed by discussions of the lesson and instructional feedback. This gave her experience with both observing and being observed in a collaborative setting.</p>\n<p>She also completed two years of beginning-teacher induction, the first in her previous district and the second in the current district. In addition, she participated with her grade-level team in a district science coaching program. That experience included planning and pre-observation meetings, classroom observations, and reflection afterward.</p>\n<p>Across these settings, she has encountered the full sequence of preparation, observation, and reflection. That background means the cycle can connect with established experience while still attending to the particular classroom-management focus she identifies for this year.</p>\n<p>The CSTPs have been part of her development throughout these experiences. She used them during student teaching and during her first two certificated years at both school sites. She will continue using them this year in the annual evaluation process with the administrator. Her previous experience therefore provides a foundation for selecting a standards focus and discussing evidence, while the present cycle offers another opportunity to connect that process with the needs of her fifth-grade students.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Clarify total teaching experience separately from years at the current school.</li><li>Include the teacher's account of what prior coaching helped with and what remained a need.</li><li>Use the background to inform the next answer rather than assuming experience eliminates the need for orientation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A7\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.2 / Planning the coaching cycle</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tExplain how the information you gathered about the school context and the volunteer teacher’s experiences informs your plans for conducting a coaching cycle, including how you will:</p><p>a.\torient the volunteer teacher to participate in your coaching process</p><p>b.\tparticipate in a pre-observation meeting</p><p>c.\tprepare for the observation and collection of evidence (including student work)</p><p>d.\tjointly conduct (with the volunteer teacher) the post-observation meeting</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the school and teacher information shapes four connected steps: orientation, pre-observation meeting, observation and evidence collection, and the joint post-observation meeting. State the purpose of each step and the decisions you will make together.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I will introduce the coaching process during our first conversation about participation and work with the volunteer teacher to arrange the pre-observation meeting. I will explain the three connected stages: planning before the lesson, observing the lesson, and discussing evidence afterward. Because she already has experience with coaching, I will ask questions that help us connect what she has found useful in earlier cycles with this one. The orientation will also cover the practical commitments. For example, I will confirm that the preconference will take approximately thirty minutes and explain the planned recording of that conversation so she understands and agrees to the arrangements.</p>\n<p>At the preconference, we will meet at a mutually workable time to discuss the lesson and the students who will participate. I will invite the teacher to describe student strengths, assets, and needs, then use clarifying and facilitative prompts to help us identify one or two CSTP elements. We will also agree on the student work products that can show learning during the lesson. These decisions will guide what I collect and what we later examine together. Before closing, I will summarize the selected standards and evidence so that we have the same understanding of the observation focus.</p>\n<p>We will review permissions and recording arrangements for the classroom lesson, confirm the observation's duration, and schedule the postconference. I will explain how I will provide a secure link in a shared digital folder so the teacher can view the lesson recording before that meeting. Giving her access in advance will allow her to reflect on the lesson and identify moments she wants to discuss. The recording is one source of evidence alongside observation notes and student work, not a replacement for either.</p>\n<p>I will also explain the intended postconference conversation. We will consider the teacher's self-assessment of instruction and assessment, examine the effectiveness of particular teaching practices, and agree on possible next steps. Those steps may involve resources, professional learning, or further coaching. I want the teacher to know from the beginning that the conversation will involve her interpretation and choices. I will also ask for feedback on the coaching process so I can identify what helped her and what I should improve.</p>\n<p>Before the observation, we will reconfirm the date, time, and length of the live fifth-grade lesson. We will ensure the necessary permissions and protocols are in place for everyone included in the recording. I will prepare a tool for collecting evidence tied to the agreed CSTP focus. Having that tool ready will help me attend to the selected practices and student responses without trying to record every classroom event equally. We will be clear about the student products to retain for analysis.</p>\n<p>During the post-observation meeting, we will review the recording and discuss instructional strengths and needs using the standards-specific notes, lesson events, and student work. I will use clarifying, facilitative, and reflective questions to support a productive conversation. The teacher's perspective will help us understand her intentions, while the evidence will help us examine what students actually did. We will compare those sources when considering teaching effectiveness rather than rely only on a general impression of the lesson.</p>\n<p>The orientation will also help us distinguish the purpose of each stage. The preconference establishes intentions and evidence, the observation records what happens during the live lesson, and the postconference supports interpretation and professional learning. Reviewing those purposes in advance can help the teacher prepare and make the later conversation more collaborative. It also gives us a shared basis for deciding whether the evidence we collect answers the questions we intended to examine.</p>\n<p>Together, we will identify a manageable next step and the resources or additional support that could help the teacher pursue it. We will also examine the assessment evidence to consider what students understood and what requires further instruction. At the end, I will ask the teacher to explain which aspects of my coaching were useful and which were less helpful. Her feedback will contribute to my own reflection on strengths and growth needs. Across the cycle, my role is to organize a focused inquiry with the teacher, maintain a clear connection among goals and evidence, and support professional learning through shared analysis.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each major coaching choice to the teacher's background or a stated need.</li><li>Jointly define what evidence will address both the CSTP focus and lesson learning goals, including student work.</li><li>Confirm live observation, duration, recording permissions, secure handling, and shared review of the evidence.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"A8\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"A\"><span id=\"a-q8\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.3 / Anticipating challenges</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tDescribe any challenges you may face in conducting a coaching cycle with the volunteer teacher and provide strategies for addressing these challenges.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Identify plausible challenges for this teacher and context, explain why each may occur, and give a practical response. Tie the strategy to the challenge rather than listing generic good coaching practices.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>One likely challenge in the preconference is turning a broad concern into a sufficiently clear observation goal. I will prepare questions that help us identify measurable learning intentions, students' strengths and assets, and the work that could demonstrate understanding. We will connect those decisions with the selected CSTP elements so the observation has a manageable focus.</p>\n<p>During the lesson, collecting precise evidence while many events occur at once may be difficult. I will use a prepared observation tool organized around the one or two elements we agreed to examine. This should help me record relevant teacher actions and student responses, along with the work products selected in advance.</p>\n<p>In the postconference, the challenge may be bringing several forms of evidence into one useful learning conversation. I will prepare questions and notes that link particular observation moments, video segments, and student work with the standards focus. Those references can support discussion of both strengths and areas for development. The aim is to make the evidence accessible enough for joint interpretation and a practical next step, while leaving space for the teacher's own reflections.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Ground each anticipated challenge in the school context or teacher's reported experience.</li><li>Specify an observable goal and the evidence fields that will keep the observation focused.</li><li>Plan how to invite disagreement and revise interpretations jointly without taking ownership away from the teacher.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"B\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"B\"><span id=\"b-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-b\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / 1 / Classroom context and learning goals</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tClassroom context, including:</p><p>a.\ttopic of lesson, learning and instructional goals, students’ prior knowledge, and what was taught in the prior lesson</p><p>b.\tclassroom setting (face-to-face, online, or hybrid) and organization for learning (whole class, small groups, individual practice, hybrid)</p><p>c.\tdescription of students served, including their assets and learning needs (e.g., grade level, demographics, student assets [cultural and/or linguistic] and interests, learning supports and adaptations)</p><p>d.\tmaterials and/or resources used during the lesson by the teacher or students to enhance or extend learning</p><p>e.\tother relevant classroom context information (e.g., time of day for the lesson, time of year, support staff that are in the room during the lesson, materials used, safety issues, bell schedule, parent support)</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part B template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all five components: the lesson and prior learning; the setting and organization; students' assets and learning needs; materials; and other relevant context. Explain information that affects instruction and observation, not just demographic totals.</p><p>Distinguish class enrollment from attendance in the observed lesson and distinguish student engagement from achievement of the mathematical goal.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>What each form of evidence contributes</summary><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">What it establishes</th><th scope=\"col\">Its limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Lesson plan</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">Intended learning, instruction, supports, and student work.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">It does not establish what happened during instruction.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Observation notes and lesson recording</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">Specific teaching actions and student responses.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">Events require interpretation; participation alone does not establish learning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Student work</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">What particular learners demonstrated on the task.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">A few examples do not establish a whole-class pattern or the cause of a result.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Meeting clips and commentary</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">The coaching interaction and an explanation of leadership decisions.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">Commentary cannot establish an interaction absent from the submitted evidence.</td></tr></tbody></table></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The observed lesson is Lesson 11 in the adopted fifth-grade mathematics program's second unit, which addresses fractions as quotients and fraction multiplication. Its focus is finding the area of rectangles when a side is a fraction greater than one. Students are expected to describe, orally and in writing, how equivalent multiplication expressions represent the area of such a rectangle. They are also expected to explain their area-finding strategies orally and represent the area with a multiplication expression. These goals combine mathematical reasoning with opportunities to communicate that reasoning in more than one form.</p>\n<p>Earlier lessons involved multiplying fractions by whole numbers with visual models and finding rectangular areas when a fractional side was less than one. Students had practiced using diagrams to explain their thinking both aloud and in writing. For instance, a rectangle model could show the equivalence of two-thirds multiplied by four and the product of two times four multiplied by one-third. The new lesson extends that knowledge to a side greater than one. Students use area diagrams, prior reasoning, and familiar strategies to investigate the new situation within the Number and Operations-Fractions standards.</p>\n<p>The lesson took place in person in a self-contained classroom with the whole class working together. Seventeen students were present. Desks were arranged in pairs and faced a digital display used for lesson materials and images from the document camera. This arrangement supported both partner interaction and attention to shared representations during discussion.</p>\n<p>The class enrollment is 22 students, identified in the scenario as 13 boys and 9 girls. The teacher described them as friendly, interested in participating, and enthusiastic about classroom and school activities. Seven students have individualized education programs and receive push-in or pull-out assistance from specialist staff. Fourteen are identified as multilingual learners or English learners. The class includes 19 Hispanic or Latine students, one multiracial student, one Pacific Islander student, and one Filipino student. Their cultural and linguistic backgrounds are varied, and the teacher identified willingness to contribute ideas as an important class asset.</p>\n<p>For presentation, the teacher used a large television connected to a computer and document camera. Each student also had an individual consumable workbook containing the lesson's materials. Students could therefore refer to their own diagrams and tasks while viewing the corresponding whole-class display.</p>\n<p>The difference between total enrollment and the number present matters when describing this lesson. The class context includes all 22 students, while the observation records the participation of the 17 attending that morning. Similarly, the two paraprofessionals were supporting particular accommodations; their presence should not be interpreted as an identical level of assistance for every learner in the room.</p>\n<p>The observation occurred in early October during the fall semester, from 9:30 to 10:35 in the morning. Two paraprofessionals were present to support three students with accommodations and other assistance specified in their IEPs. The day followed a shortened schedule for parent-teacher conferences, but there were no major schedule changes, significant disruptions, or safety incidents during the lesson. These conditions provide context for interpreting participation and the classroom routines recorded in the observation.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify specific assets and learning needs relevant to explaining fractional area, using teacher or student evidence.</li><li>Explain the planned language supports, adaptations, and adult roles without inferring needs from demographic categories.</li><li>Maintain the distinction between 22 enrolled, 17 present, and the number of work samples actually analyzed.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"C\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"C\"><span id=\"c-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-c\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / Teacher lesson plan and student-work description</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Collect and submit a copy of the volunteer teacher’s lesson plan, including the student work product description.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 11; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Provide the volunteer teacher's actual plan and a description of the student work that will show learning. Explain how the work relates to the lesson goals. An adopted curriculum plan establishes intended instruction, not proof of what occurred.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The teacher used the district-adopted fifth-grade plan for Unit 2, Lesson 11, on rectangular areas with fractional sides greater than one. The source packet contains the lesson materials across eight reproduced pages following the description. Those pages locate the lesson within the unit, identify its mathematical purpose, and provide the activities the teacher used during the live observation.</p>\n<p>The selected student product is the end-of-lesson cool-down, a brief formative assessment associated with California standard 5.NF.B.4.b. It asks students to use a shaded area representation in two ways: first, express the area as a multiplication problem; second, determine the value of that area. The work is intended to show whether students can connect the visual model with multiplication and an area calculation after the lesson.</p>\n<p>The lesson sequence moves from a warm-up into diagram-based area activities and then to the independent closing response. That sequence provides the context for discussing where the teacher modeled, adjusted a task, or checked understanding during the observation.</p>\n<p>The materials include the opening lesson information, warm-up, instructional activities, and the closing assessment. The same cool-down appears on the final page of the supplied lesson packet. Its role in the coaching cycle is distinct from the observation notes: it provides a student response to the mathematics task, while the notes describe teaching and participation during the lesson. Together, the plan and work-product description establish what was intended, what evidence would be collected, and how that evidence connects with the lesson goals.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the actual lesson adaptations agreed with the teacher and distinguish them from the published plan.</li><li>Specify what successful mathematical reasoning would look or sound like.</li><li>Connect the planned work and observation evidence to both the learning goals and the selected CSTP element.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"D\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"D\"><span id=\"d-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-d\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / Pre-observation meeting video clips</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Select and submit 2 video clips, each up to 6 minutes, that include the following:</p><p>Video Clip 1: Teacher Needs, Lesson Plan Review, and CSTP Selection (up to 6 minutes) Select a video clip that demonstrates: • Your coaching, including asking prompting and clarifying questions, that leads to the volunteer teacher’s self-assessment of instructional needs and challenges based on the lesson plan they intend to teach and the student work product that will be the outcome of the lesson; student assets and needs; and classroom context</p><p>Supporting the teacher to then identify one or two elements of one of the six CSTP domains for which they would like observation data collected to guide the post-observation meeting • Video Clip 2: Observation Process and CSTP Data (up to 6 minutes) Select a video clip that demonstrates:  Your review of the coaching and observation process with the teacher • Your review of the CSTP–element-focused data you will collect during the observation to share and discuss in the post-observation meeting, in addition to the student work products and other relevant data shared by the teacher</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 12 and following page; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit two clips, up to six minutes each, showing the required pre-observation discussion. The first concerns teacher needs, the lesson, and CSTP selection; the second concerns the observation process and evidence to collect. Joint decisions must be visible in the actual exchange.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The written account refers to preconference recordings that were unavailable for inspection. No replacement recording or invented transcript is presented as evidence of those coaching conversations.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Verify the two clips against the distinct functions and six-minute limits in the current handbook.</li><li>Ensure the teacher's input and the jointly agreed focus and evidence are visible and audible.</li><li>Check every commentary timestamp against the submitted clips and keep the interaction authentic.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-e\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 1 / Teacher self-assessment</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tVolunteer Teacher’s Self-Assessment: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher determined their instructional needs and challenges based on the CSTP element(s).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the teacher identified instructional needs and challenges and how your listening and questioning helped connect them to the CSTP focus. Analyze the teacher's thinking and the effect of your coaching, not only the questions you planned to ask.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I wanted the preconference to help the teacher identify an instructional focus connected to a CSTP element. I began with questions about the lesson's intended learning so that the standards discussion would grow from the work students would do. In the account of clip 1, from 0:00 to 0:42, I ask the teacher to describe the main goals and then paraphrase her explanation to check my understanding. She identifies mathematical learning that includes speaking, listening, and writing. I note that these different forms of participation may matter when we later discuss students' needs and the observation focus.</p>\n<p>This opening supports a two-way conversation because the teacher supplies the goals and I clarify their meaning rather than select a focus in advance. As she explains the lesson, I listen for connections among the intended mathematics, the ways students will communicate, and the classroom conditions needed for participation. Those connections can help us decide what evidence would be useful. The initial questions therefore serve more than an introductory purpose; they establish a foundation for later choices about observation and feedback.</p>\n<p>I next invite the teacher to describe students' strengths and needs. Around 3:39-4:20, the commentary describes her identifying enthusiasm for participation as a class asset and sustained engagement with challenging fraction work as a need. She sees students as willing to contribute but also recognizes that the content can be demanding. This distinction matters because the coaching should build on their readiness to participate while examining the supports that help participation continue. It gives us a reason to consider teaching practices that maintain engagement when students encounter difficulty.</p>\n<p>I connect that concern with the standards element we select later. Supporting students through challenging mathematics involves more than explaining the content; it also involves routines that help them listen, contribute, and learn from classmates. Active listening during the early part of the conversation allows me to notice that relationship. Looking back, I see that the questions about goals, assets, needs, and context helped the teacher develop the rationale for the focus rather than simply agree with a suggestion from me.</p>\n<p>The classroom context provides another connection. In clip 1 at approximately 4:21-4:28, I ask about the learning environment and classroom culture. The teacher then describes her work on students raising hands and listening while one person speaks. These are specific behaviors she wants to strengthen, not a general statement that the class needs better management. Identifying them gives the observation something concrete to attend to and helps link the teacher's professional interest with students' opportunities to hear and respond to mathematical ideas.</p>\n<p>At approximately 4:38, I invite her to choose a CSTP element on which she would like feedback. We look at the district guide together. At 5:04, I ask which element matches the aspect of teaching she wants to examine in this lesson. At 5:12, she identifies element 2.6, explaining that as a newer teacher she wants to continue developing classroom management and support positive behavior, including one speaker at a time. Her selection follows directly from the needs described earlier in the meeting.</p>\n<p>My role is to help make those connections explicit. I use questions, paraphrases, and confirmation to relate the standards language to the routines and behaviors the teacher has named. This keeps her self-assessment central while also ensuring the focus is observable. Rather than treat the CSTP as a label added after planning, we use it to organize what we will look for and why it matters for students' access to the lesson.</p>\n<p>The teacher's interest in classroom management is therefore connected with access to mathematics rather than treated as a separate concern about order. Students need to hear an explanation, ask a question, and contribute their own reasoning when working with challenging fraction concepts. The routines she identifies can support those opportunities. My responsibility is to help us identify evidence of that relationship without assuming that a quiet room alone shows learning or that enthusiasm alone guarantees understanding. This distinction keeps the observation connected with both the selected standard and the lesson goals.</p>\n<p>In reflection, I see this sequence as a strength of the coaching conversation. The teacher identified learning intentions, student assets, engagement challenges, and classroom routines before choosing the element. I listened for relationships among those topics and helped turn them into an observation plan. The written account also requires care: its references to clips are not independently verified because the recordings were unavailable. What it offers is a described example of how facilitative questions can support a teacher's choice of focus and connect that choice with student participation in demanding content.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Correct clip references by checking the recordings, rather than inferring the intended timestamp.</li><li>Identify the teacher's specific self-assessed need and show how it influenced the jointly selected focus.</li><li>Explain the impact of a facilitative question using the teacher's resulting reasoning, not merely agreement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 2 / Reviewing the lesson together</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tLesson Plan Review: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher reviewed the lesson plan. Think about:</p><p>a.\tstudent work product,</p><p>b.\tclassroom context, and</p><p>c.\tstudents’ assets and learning needs.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Connect the student work, classroom context, and student assets and needs to the lesson review. Show how your questions helped the teacher clarify what students should learn and how the planned evidence will reveal it.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The choice of a student work product began with clarifying the lesson goals. In the first clip's written account, I ask at 0:00-0:10 what the teacher intends students to learn and what they should be able to demonstrate by the end. She describes two related goals: explain orally and in writing how equivalent multiplication expressions represent the area of a rectangle with fractional sides greater than one, and describe area-finding strategies orally while representing the result as a multiplication expression. This establishes both the mathematics and the forms of communication that evidence should address.</p>\n<p>At approximately 0:37, I paraphrase the goals as including speaking and writing, and the teacher confirms that interpretation at 0:42. I then ask how Lesson 11 connects with earlier learning (0:48). She explains that students have already multiplied fractions by whole numbers and found rectangle areas with one fractional side less than one (0:58). This connection helps us see what students can draw on and what is new in the observed lesson. It also makes the intended evidence more specific: we are looking for students to extend familiar area reasoning to a different kind of side length.</p>\n<p>At 1:12, I ask how she will recognize that students have met the goals and which products could make that learning visible. At 1:23, she describes listening to students use sentence frames in conversation and examining their written multiplication expressions for rectangle diagrams. I recognize the alignment with oral and written goals, but I want to clarify the particular work we can retain and analyze after the observation. Discussion during the lesson can reveal reasoning, while a collected product gives us something to examine together in the postconference.</p>\n<p>This is a moment where active listening matters. I am trying to distinguish an intended student behavior from a specific piece of evidence. The teacher has described what she hopes students will do, and my follow-up helps identify where that response will appear in the lesson materials. I do not want to assume that any written page will address the goals equally well. The selected work should allow us to consider the connection among the diagram, multiplication expression, and area calculation.</p>\n<p>At 1:43, I ask whether the lesson includes an exit response or cool-down that can show the intended outcomes. She confirms that it does at 1:52, and I invite her to explain it at 1:53. At 1:55, she describes the closing task: students write a multiplication expression for the area of a shaded region and determine the area. This gives us an identified formative assessment tied to the lesson plan. It also clarifies that the written product primarily captures mathematical representation and calculation, while oral explanation will require attention to the classroom discussion as well.</p>\n<p>The classroom setting is another part of the evidence plan. At approximately 4:21, I invite the teacher to describe this year's classroom culture and learning environment. She responds at 4:30 that students are practicing raising hands when they wish to contribute and listening to one speaker at a time. These are positive participation routines still under development. I see a connection between those routines and the oral learning goal: students need opportunities to speak, but also to hear and respond to the mathematical thinking of others. This context helps us identify what to observe rather than consider the work product in isolation.</p>\n<p>The discussion of assets and needs adds further context. At 3:39, I ask about students' cultural and linguistic strengths and whether any students require substantial scaffolding. At 3:53, the teacher describes a class with many ideas and a strong desire to participate. At 4:05, she identifies maintaining engagement during challenging fraction work as an area needing support. Her response provides a useful starting point, although it is broader than a detailed account of specific cultural or linguistic resources. It identifies willingness to contribute as an asset and sustained engagement as a need.</p>\n<p>I connect these observations with our later choice of a standard concerning an effective learning environment. The teacher's interest in participation routines, the challenge of the mathematics, and the lesson's communication goals all point toward examining how students are supported to listen and contribute. This gives the CSTP focus a practical relationship to the lesson rather than making it an abstract selection from a guide.</p>\n<p>The selected assessment also helps define the limits of what we can infer. A written expression and area answer can show aspects of mathematical understanding, but they do not capture every speaking and listening goal described at the beginning. That is why the observation of discussion remains relevant. The teacher and I need to consider the evidence sources together when we return to the lesson goals in the postconference. Our planning conversation begins that alignment by identifying the product, the expected response, and the classroom conditions under which students will complete it.</p>\n<p>Looking back, the conversation helped us identify several complementary sources of evidence. The cool-down would provide a written response to the area task. Observation notes would describe teaching routines and student participation. The recording, when available to the coaching partners, could help revisit particular interactions. Together these sources could support a richer analysis than any one alone. The written commentary preserves the sequence and its timestamps, while the absence of the recordings in the review means the interactions themselves cannot be independently confirmed.</p>\n<p>The evidence plan keeps the oral and written components visible throughout the cycle.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain the success criteria for the work and the evidence needed for the oral as well as written goals.</li><li>Draw out specific relevant student assets and supports instead of treating demographic labels or enthusiasm as sufficient.</li><li>Show how a contextual detail changed the lesson discussion or observation plan.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 3 / Jointly selecting the CSTP focus</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tCSTP Selection: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher selected one or two CSTP elements based on the volunteer teacher’s self-assessment and students’ assets and learning needs.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why the selected element fits the teacher's self-assessment and students' needs, and how the selection was genuinely joint. The current handbook specifies one or two elements within the same CSTP domain.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>We used the district's detailed CSTP guide to connect the teacher's self-assessment with an observation focus. I invited her to identify one or two elements relevant to her interests and the students' strengths and learning needs. In the written account of clip 1, at approximately 4:38, I explain that we are moving from discussion of the lesson into choosing the teaching practices she would like to explore or receive feedback on. This gives her a clear role in setting the direction.</p>\n<p>At around 5:00, we examine the printed guide together. I ask which element best matches the feedback she wants from this particular lesson. At 5:12, she points to element 2.6 and explains that, as a newer teacher, she wants to keep improving classroom management. She connects that interest with supporting positive behavior, especially listening while one person speaks. The choice therefore draws on both her professional learning priority and a concrete student participation need described earlier.</p>\n<p>In reflection, I see how the order of the conversation supported this selection. We first discussed learning goals, student assets and needs, and the classroom environment. Those topics gave us a basis for interpreting the standards language. Inviting the teacher to choose an element afterward allowed her to explain why it fit rather than simply select a number from the guide.</p>\n<p>The guide is useful here because it gives both partners access to the same language. Pointing to the element and discussing its relationship to the lesson makes the selection visible and open to clarification rather than leaving the focus implicit.</p>\n<p>The resulting focus connects the teacher's instructional strategies with the environment students need to access learning. It also identifies a basis for collecting evidence and providing feedback during the observation. My facilitation helped organize that connection, while the teacher's own account established its relevance. The written commentary describes this collaborative sequence; the unavailable recording limits confirmation of the precise interaction.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Link the selected element to the specific lesson goals and student needs discussed earlier.</li><li>Identify observable teaching practices and student responses that will provide relevant evidence.</li><li>Preserve the teacher's rationale and agency while jointly testing whether the focus is sufficiently precise.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"E4\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"E\"><span id=\"e-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 4 / Agreeing on observation evidence</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tCoaching and Observation Process: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher collaboratively reviewed the coaching and observation process, and the CSTP–element-focused data to be collected during the lesson.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain the jointly agreed process, duration, focus, and evidence collection. Include teacher practices, student responses, and student work. Analyze how the conversation produced shared understanding rather than presenting logistics as a checklist.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>At the end of the preconference, I wanted to confirm the observation arrangements and the specific evidence we had agreed to collect. In the written account of clip 2, from 0:00 to 0:18, I explain that we will review the coaching sequence before closing and ask how long the lesson is expected to take. At 0:19, the teacher consults her plan and estimates approximately an hour to an hour and ten minutes. I record that information and acknowledge it at 0:27. Clarifying the duration helps us plan the observation and recording realistically.</p>\n<p>I then summarize the discussion so far. At approximately 0:31, I refer to the lesson's instructional components, the students' goals, assets, and needs, and our agreement about the focus of data collection. The teacher is described as confirming these points as I review them. My purpose is to check that we have a common understanding and that no important detail has been overlooked while moving from the instructional conversation into logistics. A summary gives the teacher an opportunity to correct or add to my interpretation before we proceed.</p>\n<p>At 0:48, I point to element 2.6 in the district guide and ask whether it is the element we selected. The teacher confirms. At 0:53, I review its focus on routines, procedures, shared expectations, and support for positive behavior that allow all students to learn. Referring to the guide keeps the formal focus visible while we connect it with the classroom behaviors she has already described. We are not adding a new concern at this point; we are clarifying the relationship between the selected element and the planned observation.</p>\n<p>Next, I ask what observable evidence would represent that focus. At approximately 1:02, I paraphrase her earlier emphasis on students demonstrating positive listening behaviors. The teacher confirms at about 1:18. I then ask her to restate the particular behaviors she wants us to notice. This follow-up is important because a broad phrase such as positive behavior could otherwise lead us to attend to different things. Naming the behaviors in advance helps make the observation more focused and the later discussion more useful.</p>\n<p>At 1:23, the teacher identifies raising hands, clarifying classmates' ideas, and adding to a contribution rather than simply repeating it. She also explains that when students repeat an idea, they can acknowledge the classmate whose thinking they are restating. These details connect listening with participation in a shared discussion. They suggest evidence beyond silence or compliance: students can show that they have attended to another person's reasoning by accurately representing or extending it.</p>\n<p>I listen and acknowledge the response while continuing to record the agreed focus. At 1:38, the teacher mentions using a random selection tool to broaden participation and help ensure that everyone has a voice. This adds a teacher action to the student behaviors we have identified. It gives us a way to examine how the teacher creates opportunities for contribution, while also observing how students respond. The distinction matters because the selected standard concerns the teacher's use of routines and supports, not simply whether students behave as expected.</p>\n<p>At approximately 1:44, I summarize that I will attend to both sides of that relationship: students' listening and participation, and the instructional strategies used to encourage those behaviors. The teacher is described as agreeing. I connect this again with the effective learning environment represented by element 2.6. At around 2:00, we both refer to the district guide while I identify the element. Her confirmation at 2:04 and my acknowledgement at 2:05 close this part of the review. These checks help ensure that the observation plan reflects what we developed together.</p>\n<p>I then explain what will happen after the classroom observation. At approximately 2:07, I describe reviewing the lesson video during the postconference along with student products and other evidence related to the selected standard. The teacher confirms the plan at 2:27. This connects collection with its later use: we are gathering information to interpret together, not merely completing an observation form. It also clarifies that the postconference will involve several sources and that the teacher will participate in the analysis.</p>\n<p>The earlier clip contains another part of the same planning process. At around 5:30 in clip 1, I ask which observation evidence would best represent the selected element while pointing to it in the guide. At 5:40, the teacher identifies hand-raising, avoiding simultaneous talk, and repeating classmates' contributions as ways to examine listening. These initial indicators are then refined and confirmed in the closing discussion described above. The two moments together show how an observation focus can develop through questions, clarification, and a final shared summary.</p>\n<p>The review also helps separate an observation indicator from a conclusion. Hand-raising, a request for clarification, or a student's paraphrase can be recorded as an event. Whether those events show that the routine supports learning is something we will discuss with the other evidence afterward. Agreeing on indicators gives us a more reliable starting point for that conversation.</p>\n<p>The teacher's description of acknowledging a classmate when repeating an idea adds a relational dimension to listening. It asks students to recognize another person's contribution rather than present the same idea as their own. Together with the use of a selection tool, this provides specific classroom practices we can examine when considering opportunities for different students to have a voice.</p>\n<p>Looking back, my coaching decisions aimed to make the process predictable and the evidence specific. Confirming duration addressed the practical arrangement; reviewing the standard clarified the professional focus; naming behaviors established what we would observe; and explaining the postconference showed how the evidence would support learning. The teacher's confirmations are described in the commentary, but the recordings were unavailable for independent review. The example therefore illustrates the planned relationship among goals, observation, and feedback without presenting paraphrased dialogue as a verified transcript.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>State the agreed evidence categories and how the observation tool will capture them.</li><li>Identify a teacher contribution that changed or refined the plan.</li><li>Confirm the live observation and subsequent joint video/work review, and explain how the plan addresses the mathematical goals.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"F\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"F\"><span id=\"f-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-f\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / CSTP-focused observation notes</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: During the observation, use an observation tool to collect evidence related to the one or two CSTP elements agreed upon during the pre-observation meeting with the volunteer teacher. In addition to the lesson, collect evidence including student work (process, product, and/or performance) that was the result of the lesson. Submit your notes from the observation and/or forms you used to document the observation as well as the student work product as evidence for Step 3.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 16; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Collect specific evidence during the live observation and connect it to the agreed CSTP element and lesson goals. Separate observed actions and student responses from your interpretation or proposed alternative. Useful notes allow the teacher to examine a claim, not merely accept your judgment.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>From observation to a coaching question</summary><p>Keep three moves distinct: describe an event, offer a cautious interpretation tied to the agreed focus, and invite the teacher to examine it. A question should allow a genuine alternative interpretation.</p><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Observed event</th><th scope=\"col\">Cautious interpretation</th><th scope=\"col\">Question for joint examination</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Observed event\">Illustrative only: a student reports finishing, but the collected response states an answer without explaining the reasoning.</td><td data-label=\"Cautious interpretation\">Completion did not establish understanding in this instance. The record does not establish the student's effort or the teacher's intention.</td><td data-label=\"Question for joint examination\">What does this response show about understanding, and what further evidence would help us decide?</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching demonstration, not an event from the interpolated example or a substitute for observation notes. Connect your actual evidence to the CSTP element and lesson goal selected with the teacher.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Observation focus: CSTP 2, the creation and maintenance of effective learning environments, with attention to element 2.6. The selected element concerns classroom routines, procedures, expectations, and positive-behavior supports that make learning possible for all students. The teacher's self-selected professional focus is classroom management. Agreed indicators include one speaker at a time, students raising hands, listening while classmates speak, and showing understanding by clarifying or restating another student's contribution. The observation will attend to both student responses and the teacher actions that support them.</p>\n<p>The first mathematical goal is for students to explain orally and in writing how equivalent multiplication expressions represent the area of a rectangle with fractional sides greater than one. The second is to explain area-finding strategies orally and represent the result with a multiplication expression. These goals provide the lesson context for examining participation and listening routines. The notes below paraphrase the recorded observation account; descriptions of classroom dialogue are not presented as verbatim quotations.</p>\n<p>At the opening, the teacher displays the day's area-learning goal and invites a student to read it. Several students volunteer by raising their hands. When the reader needs assistance, the teacher reads alongside the student and checks whether glasses are available and the display is visible. She reminds the class that the goal also appears in their individual workbooks. When several students begin speaking together, she returns them to the expectation that one person speaks at a time. This provides an early example of the positive participation routines connected with element 2.6. [CSTP 2.6: The hand-raising expectation and reminder about a single speaker establish the participation routine. Checking access to the displayed goal also supports the conditions students need to join the opening activity.]</p>\n<p>A possible missed opportunity at the opening is to make the relationship with prior learning more explicit. Questions about earlier area work could help students connect familiar representations with the current goal. The teacher could also identify what a successful response will include by the end of the lesson. Stating or developing those criteria with students would give them a clearer basis for recognizing progress and explaining their understanding. The displayed goal identifies the topic, but the connection among earlier work, today's task, and evidence of success could be developed further.</p>\n<p>The teacher then introduces the warm-up on the digital display. Some students begin before the explanation is complete, and she asks them to pause while she clarifies the task. Before the true-or-false activity, she checks the meaning of true and reminds students to raise their hands when responding. She reviews the activity's three parts, gives approximately one minute to begin, and starts a timer. As students work, she circulates, confers, and reminds the class to maintain a quiet working environment. Students ask whether the familiar digital random selector will choose speakers, and she confirms that it will. These established procedures organize independent thinking and subsequent participation. [CSTP 2.6: The teacher combines directions, a timed work period, circulation, and a familiar selection procedure. These routines organize how students begin the task, seek assistance, and prepare to contribute to its review.]</p>\n<p>During the warm-up review, the digital selector identifies four different students to contribute. As each explains, classmates turn toward the speaker, and the teacher records the reasoning on the board. She monitors attention and signals who will speak next. A student at the back becomes noisy and appears disengaged; the teacher explains that the student will be asked to restate a classmate's contribution. When that student says the speaker cannot be heard, she asks the speaker to increase volume and speak confidently. This interaction links the listening expectation with an adjustment that makes listening more possible. [CSTP 2.6: The observation connects an expectation for listening with an adjustment in speaking volume. The teacher responds to a practical barrier so that the student can hear the contribution before being asked to restate it.]</p>\n<p>The next selected student is encouraged to explain the reason for a true-or-false choice. The teacher offers a sentence opening that connects the judgment with its justification. Other students remain relatively quiet and some look toward the speaker. In relation to element 2.6, the evidence concerns the use of selection routines, expectations for attention, and language support to sustain participation. The notes distinguish visible behaviors, such as turning toward a classmate, from an inference that every student is fully engaged. The teacher's response to audibility is also relevant to whether students can meet the listening expectation. [CSTP 2.6: Participation is supported through both selection and a language frame. The teacher gives students a predictable way to enter the discussion while maintaining expectations for classmates to attend to the explanation being shared.]</p>\n<p>Moving into the first main activity, the teacher revisits the lesson goal and refers to the previous day's exit response. She encourages the class to build on that earlier success and notes that another closing assessment will follow today's work. The activity diagram appears on the display, and she holds up the matching workbook page. Students are directed to page 55 and asked to point to the diagram. As she circulates, this pointing response provides a quick check that students have located the material. A volunteer reads the first problem while classmates look at their books and remain quiet. These routines support a shared starting point for the activity. [CSTP 2.6: Locating and pointing to the common diagram helps establish readiness for the next task. The teacher checks that students have the required material before a classmate reads the prompt and discussion continues.]</p>\n<p>The teacher asks questions about the diagram. One student who has already volunteered raises a hand again. After approximately ten seconds of wait time, the teacher uses a paper random-selection tool to invite another response. As students explain, she asks clarifying and facilitative questions and points to relevant parts of the representation. Another student volunteers an explanation, followed by a further contribution that the teacher connects back to the diagram. Classmates appear to attend to the exchange. This sequence provides evidence of attempts to distribute participation and keep mathematical discussion connected to a common visual reference. [CSTP 2.6: Wait time and selection procedures provide alternatives to relying on the first raised hand. Questions and reference to the diagram help sustain a shared focus as different students contribute their mathematical thinking.]</p>\n<p>Before students continue, the teacher explains the task expectations, restates its purpose, and connects it with the previous lesson. She assigns five minutes and starts the timer. She also checks the procedure for requesting help. Students identify the established expectation to consult three classmates. Once work begins, students operate individually and in pairs. The teacher encourages them to ask a precise question rather than make a general request for assistance and suggests rereading the directions to identify what they need. She circulates, listens to conversations, and occasionally confers with an individual. The routine is intended to support productive peer assistance as part of the learning environment. [CSTP 2.6: The work period includes a known help-seeking procedure and expectations for specific questions. The teacher monitors how students use peer support while remaining available for individual clarification during the activity.]</p>\n<p>From the back of the room, the teacher uses the class's familiar call-and-response attention signal. Students respond and turn toward her. She asks a question to clarify the activity directions, and several students volunteer answers. She reminds the class that additional resources in the workbook can support their work. Students raise hands with questions, and she acknowledges them promptly before they return to the task. Work continues both independently and with partners. The teacher notices a student who has already written an expression and uses that observation to encourage the class. These actions show routines for gaining attention, clarifying expectations, and reinforcing productive effort. [CSTP 2.6: A familiar attention signal allows the teacher to clarify directions during work time. Prompt acknowledgement of questions and reminders about available resources support students in returning to the task with clearer expectations.]</p>\n<p>As she continues circulating, the teacher provides individual assistance and checks progress. When one student begins distracting behavior, she moves close, speaks with the student, and redirects attention to the partner. She asks the partner to explain the mathematics being attempted. This response uses proximity and peer interaction to reconnect the student with the activity. The observation focus remains the teacher's use of procedures and positive support, together with the student response. It does not establish that every peer conversation is productive or that the redirection has a lasting effect beyond the observed moment. [CSTP 2.6: The teacher uses proximity, an individual conversation, and a partner connection to redirect participation. The evidence records the immediate support offered and the learning task to which the student is being returned.]</p>\n<p>Near the end of the work period, the teacher gives a thirty-second warning and encourages students to record their thinking. When the timer ends, she repeats the attention signal and students turn toward her. She acknowledges that the task can be challenging and invites a volunteer to share. Noticing a student who has not yet contributed, she asks that student to explain at the digital display. As the student approaches, the teacher reminds classmates that supporting a peer includes listening. She also explains that the random selector will identify the next participant. These transitions connect predictable procedures with expectations for respectful participation. [CSTP 2.6: Advance notice, a timer, and an established attention signal organize the transition. Inviting a student who has not yet shared and reminding classmates to listen connect that procedure with inclusive participation.]</p>\n<p>While the student marks the diagram and explains, the teacher stands near a group at the side of the room and facilitates the exchange. She encourages the class to count with the speaker as parts of the representation are identified. Students join in, and some ask clarifying questions. This provides a way for classmates to participate while one student leads the explanation. In terms of element 2.6, the observation records both the routine for taking turns and the teacher's efforts to make listening active. The mathematical diagram continues to serve as a shared reference for the discussion. [CSTP 2.6: Counting with the presenter gives the audience a defined role in the explanation. The teacher maintains the shared discussion while supporting the student at the display and responding to classmates seeking clarification.]</p>\n<p>At 27:00, the teacher supports written explanation by putting a sentence frame on the board that asks students to connect an expression with the diagram. Several students begin copying it into their workbooks. She notices and names students who are recording the frame. This provides a bridge between the oral discussion and the written reasoning expected in the lesson. The notes capture the availability of the support and some visible uptake, rather than assume every student used the frame or that copying it alone demonstrated understanding. [CSTP 2.6: The written frame and acknowledgement of students using it make a participation expectation visible. This support helps connect the shared oral work with the written activity students are now expected to undertake.]</p>\n<p>At 28:00, students work alone and with partners, and some approach the teacher for conferences. When a student expresses difficulty, she directs attention back to the diagram, notes that a classmate has found a solution, and encourages continued effort. She identifies a student who appears to understand and asks that student to discuss the reasoning with another learner. She also confers with a pair and then an individual. Some students move around the room to consult others. Not all of those conversations appear related to the mathematics, and the teacher comments on the difference between students persisting with the task and students becoming distracted. This moment shows both the intended peer support and a need to maintain its focus.</p>\n<p>At 36:00, the teacher begins a countdown for students to return to their seats. Students move quickly, and a classmate urges others to sit. The teacher indicates that continued talking will affect the class's letter-based behavior system. Two students are at the front annotating the digital diagram while most of the room is quiet. Attention is uneven, however. The teacher points out that some students are looking outside or away from the screen and asks one student to turn toward the presentation. The speakers continue explaining, and she uses questions to clarify their mathematics. She invites an additional student to join the explanation and thanks the students for contributing. The record includes both positive participation support and more corrective management responses. [CSTP 2.6: The transition includes a countdown, reminders, and individual redirection. The record also notes uneven attention, allowing the coaching discussion to consider both the procedures used and the student responses actually observed.]</p>\n<p>At 41:00, a student at the back remains at the pencil sharpener for more than twenty seconds. The teacher reminds the student of the procedure for requesting a replacement pencil. The student asks, and the teacher moves over for a quiet conversation while the presentation continues. She supplies a pencil, and the student returns to the desk. This is an example of using an established materials routine and a brief individual interaction to reduce interruption. It relates to the selected standard through the management of classroom procedures in support of continued learning for the student and classmates. [CSTP 2.6: A materials procedure provides an alternative to an extended interruption at the sharpener. The quiet exchange and replacement pencil allow the student to return to the desk while the class presentation continues.]</p>\n<p>At 42:00, a student continues explaining at the front with the diagram. The teacher invites questions from the class. When a question is too quiet to hear, she requests more volume. When classmates ask for the explanation again, she promptly invites the speaker to repeat it more audibly. Students look toward the screen as the speaker annotates the representation. The teacher then asks the student to label and count the fourteen units being referenced. She confirms how the length and width relate to the multiplication expression and thanks the student. The sequence shows how clarification, audibility, and visual reference can support the listening and participation expectations established for the lesson. [CSTP 2.6: Requests for greater volume and a repeated explanation respond to students' listening needs. The teacher supports clarification while keeping the group connected to the diagram and the mathematical reasoning being presented.]</p>\n<p>At 43:30, the teacher moves the class to another activity and asks students to choose one of two area problems. She has adjusted the task to provide more accessible entry points after observing the preceding work. As students begin, she circulates and monitors. Some take their materials to established groups immediately. The modification concerns instructional access, while the familiar group arrangements provide a routine for beginning the task. The observation can therefore connect the teacher's response to student work with the environment in which students continue learning, without treating all students' responses as identical. [CSTP 2.6: The teacher adjusts the task after observing students' earlier work and continues monitoring established groups. The entry records how familiar arrangements and an accessible task support participation in the next learning activity.]</p>\n<p>At 44:50, the teacher announces that one minute remains for the selected problem. At 46:30, she signals the end of work time and uses a five-count to bring students back to their seats. These time cues make the transition explicit and give students a predictable way to return to whole-class discussion. They are additional examples of the procedures relevant to element 2.6. The record also allows the coaching partners to consider how much transition time is needed and whether students are ready to attend when the next explanation begins. [CSTP 2.6: The time warning and countdown make the change from group work to discussion explicit. These recurring cues provide structure for returning to seats and preparing to listen to classmates' explanations.]</p>\n<p>At 47:00, the teacher asks who has solved an expression and would like to explain. She invites a student who has not yet shared to come to the whiteboard. While that student writes, she monitors the room. When another student is disruptive, she explains that the student will be asked to restate the speaker's reasoning. This connects a behavioral redirection with the listening focus, while also raising a useful coaching question about how restating is framed: as a routine opportunity for everyone to learn from peers or primarily as a response to off-task behavior. [CSTP 2.6: The invitation broadens participation to another student while the teacher monitors the audience. Asking a listener to restate the explanation links the behavioral expectation with attention to a peer's mathematical contribution.]</p>\n<p>At 48:00, the teacher helps the student explaining by placing the student's workbook and diagram under the document camera. The visible representation supports the oral account. She asks the student to clarify how many of a particular fractional length are present, and the student identifies four. She assists with marking the diagram to make the reasoning visible to classmates. This combines an instructional scaffold with support for a student's participation in front of the class. It also provides evidence for discussing whether the questions make the relationship between side lengths and area sufficiently clear. [CSTP 2.6: Displaying the student's own diagram provides support for presenting and listening. The teacher assists the explanation without removing the student from the speaking role, maintaining an opportunity for public participation.]</p>\n<p>At 48:40, the teacher returns to the student who had been asked to listen and restate. She asks for an explanation of how the previous speaker determined the area. The student begins to paraphrase the reasoning, and she thanks the student. A possible missed opportunity is to ask additional questions about how the side lengths were identified and why those quantities were multiplied. Such questions could strengthen the mathematical explanation rather than stop at a retelling of the procedure. The event therefore offers evidence of the listening routine and a separate question about the depth of reasoning it supports. [CSTP 2.6: The follow-up makes the listening expectation observable through a paraphrase. The teacher acknowledges the response, while the accompanying coaching question considers how additional prompts could connect that participation with more explicit mathematical reasoning.]</p>\n<p>At 49:20, another student is invited to explain. The teacher tells the class that someone will be selected to summarize what was heard, maintaining an expectation of active listening. She uses detailed prompts and descriptions to make the student's method visible. After selecting a listener, she asks that student to explain the speaker's approach. The listener reports not hearing it clearly, so the teacher asks for another explanation at a higher volume. The original speaker repeats the account, and the listener then paraphrases it. This gives the coaching partners a specific sequence for examining how participation expectations are paired with support when audibility is a barrier. [CSTP 2.6: The teacher pairs the expectation to summarize with an opportunity to hear the explanation again. Repetition and increased volume support the listener before the student is asked to demonstrate what was understood.]</p>\n<p>At 51:00, the teacher transitions to the cool-down or exit response. Students are asked to take out a separate packet, and she sets a one-minute timer while they locate it. She directs them to page 79. A possible improvement is to establish at the beginning of the lesson which materials students will need and practice having them ready. That could reduce time spent searching and make the transition into assessment more efficient. The issue is connected to classroom procedure rather than a conclusion that students were unwilling to work. [CSTP 2.6: The transition reveals a materials routine that is not yet fully established. Preparing the packet earlier is identified as a possible support for reducing searching and increasing readiness for the independent assessment.]</p>\n<p>At 53:00, the teacher displays the exit task and explains how to complete it. One possible missed opportunity is to ask students what they notice and what the problem requires before supplying all the directions. That could increase their participation in interpreting the task. Another is to return explicitly to the lesson goals and success criteria: ask what students practiced, which strategies helped them determine area, and how those strategies apply to this final response. Such a review could connect the formative assessment with the learning it is meant to reveal. [CSTP 2.6: The closing task offers another opportunity to make expectations clear. Questions about the task, prior strategies, and successful performance could help students take a more active role in preparing for independent work.]</p>\n<p>At 53:38, the teacher points out a similarity with the previous day's task and reminds students to find the area. She explains that students may read quietly when finished. A visible set of finishing options could make that expectation more routine and reduce the need for repeated directions. Students then work on the exit response for approximately the final ten minutes. The observation ends with both evidence of established classroom supports and specific opportunities for refinement: clearer connections among goals and tasks, more explicit preparation of materials, and questions that help students explain the mathematics underlying their participation. [CSTP 2.6: The teacher states what students may do after completing the assessment. Making that expectation visible and familiar could help maintain a predictable working environment as students finish at different times.]</p>\n<p>The observation preserves the sequence of teacher actions, student responses, and possible missed opportunities so the coaching partners can revisit each event in context when discussing the selected standard and the mathematics goals.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Keep time, observed teacher action, student response, and interpretation distinguishable.</li><li>Link each proposed missed opportunity to the lesson learning goal, not just a preference for a different routine.</li><li>Compare responses across different learners and connect the relevant observations to actual work samples.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"G\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"G\"><span id=\"g-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-g\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part G / Student work from the observed lesson</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: During the observation, use an observation tool to collect evidence related to the one or two CSTP elements agreed upon during the pre-observation meeting with the volunteer teacher. In addition to the lesson, collect evidence including student work (process, product, and/or performance) that was the result of the lesson. Submit your notes from the observation and/or forms you used to document the observation as well as the student work product as evidence for Step 3.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 16; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use actual work produced during the lesson and discussed with the teacher. Identify the task, the success criteria, what the work demonstrates, and what remains uncertain. A correct answer, an incorrect answer, and visible reasoning can each provide different information.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Work sample A received full credit, 2/2. Work sample B received partial credit, 1/2. Work sample C received no credit, 0/2, on the same two-item area task.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use anonymous, stable sample labels that also appear in the commentary and observation discussion.</li><li>Apply clear criteria to identify the demonstrated strategy, correct reasoning, and specific misconception.</li><li>State the number of works examined and avoid generalizing a few selected examples to the entire class.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"H\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"H\"><span id=\"h-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-h\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part H / Post-observation meeting video clips</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: From the video of the post-observation meeting where you and the volunteer teacher are seen and heard, select 1 to 3 video clips. Your 1 to 3 video clips may total up to 15 minutes (assessors will only watch and score the first 15 minutes of video recording submitted).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 17; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit one to three clips, totaling up to fifteen minutes, from the joint post-observation meeting. The evidence should show examination of the lesson video and student work, discussion of practice, shared next steps, and teacher feedback on your coaching.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.5: Joint next steps for teacher growth</h3><ul><li>Co-determine professional next steps based on CSTP-related evidence.</li><li>Include resources and additional coaching support, with analysis of your part in the joint decision.</li><li>For Level 4, focus professional growth on individual students' learning needs; Level 5 adds specific evidence-based resources and detailed learning steps.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The reflection references postconference clips unavailable for review. This section provides no substitute video and does not present invented dialogue as recorded coaching evidence.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Check the actual clips against the current handbook's content and duration requirements.</li><li>Verify the evidence of joint viewing, teacher reasoning, shared next steps, and feedback.</li><li>Confirm the commentary's precise clip locations and do not manufacture dialogue to fill a gap.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-i\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 1 / Jointly assessing student learning</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tVolunteer Teacher’s Assessment of Student Learning:</p><p>Describe how you supported the volunteer teacher in assessing student learning during the CSTP-focused lesson.</p><p>Describe how you shared the evidence of CSTP-focused data with the volunteer teacher.</p><p>Describe how you and the volunteer teacher co-analyzed the video of the lesson, student work, and/or other data (if collected) from the lesson.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all three components: how you supported the teacher's assessment of learning, shared CSTP-focused evidence, and jointly analyzed the lesson video and student work. Explain how your coaching helped the teacher interpret evidence, not simply that you looked at it together.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>During the postconference, we considered how the teacher had monitored students' participation while they practiced the mathematics needed for the closing assessment. Her explanation emphasized students counting rectangular side lengths accurately so they could write an appropriate multiplication expression and determine area. She wanted the whole class to use the strategy, not only the student presenting at the board. This connected the selected focus on participation routines with the lesson's mathematical purpose. Engagement mattered because students needed opportunities to practice the reasoning they would later demonstrate independently.</p>\n<p>In the written account of clip 1, at 1:16, the teacher explains that she counted with the class to encourage everyone to attend to the side lengths and prepare to write expressions. At 1:30, I clarify the connection with the exit task: students would need to use that same counting process to show the intended learning. She confirms at 1:36 and 1:38. This exchange checks that the observed strategy is being interpreted in relation to a goal, rather than praised simply because students appeared involved.</p>\n<p>I then ask how that teaching choice appears in the student work collected after the lesson (1:38). At 1:50, the teacher points to two successful cool-down responses. One belongs to the student who had asked for a clearer or more audible explanation during the presentation. The teacher notes that this student both wrote the expression and calculated the area correctly. At 2:09, I connect these features with the lesson goals, and she agrees. The example gives us a particular response to examine, while also requiring care not to infer that one classroom interaction alone caused the successful result.</p>\n<p>As we continue reviewing work at 2:11, the teacher identifies two students who earned full credit. She refers to their expressions and area calculations as evidence that they completed the task. At 2:31, I ask whether the work shows use of the strategy seen in the video. At 2:34, she examines several responses and notes that many students counted eleven fractional pieces along one side. This is a more specific observation than the score alone. It connects markings on the page with the counting approach practiced during instruction and gives us a basis for discussing both correct and incomplete responses.</p>\n<p>The postconference also draws on standards-focused observation notes. We pause the lesson recording at selected moments and discuss events relevant to element 2.6, which concerns routines and positive-behavior supports for an effective learning environment. The notes provide another record of those moments. I use them to identify what I observed, while inviting the teacher to explain her intentions and interpretation. This helps distinguish the event itself from our conclusions about its significance.</p>\n<p>At the opening of clip 1, I ask the teacher to describe the lesson segment we are viewing (0:00-0:03). She explains that one student is presenting at the board while another asks a question from the audience. Because the voices are difficult to hear from the back, she asks both students to speak more audibly. She describes this as practice in a classroom routine for speaking so others can listen. At 0:41, I connect the event with my notes about a support for engagement and positive participation. This gives the standard a concrete example rather than discussing it only in general terms.</p>\n<p>Another note concerns the teacher's movement around the classroom. At approximately 5:03, I describe seeing her circulate and observe the groups during the activity. I connect that monitoring with maintaining conditions in which students can attend to the learning task. The discussion can then consider what she notices and how she responds, rather than equate walking around the room automatically with effective support. Her perspective contributes information about why she approached particular students or groups.</p>\n<p>At 2:48, I also refer to her enlarging the displayed diagram so students could count along with the teacher and classmates. I had recorded this in the observation notes. The change makes the shared representation easier to inspect and supports participation in the counting strategy. It is relevant both to instructional access and to the selected focus on a learning environment where students can contribute. Bringing the note and video segment together gives us a specific action to discuss and a way to connect it with the work students later produced.</p>\n<p>Joint analysis occurs as we move between the recording and the exit responses. At 3:02, I point out students' markings and circles around parts of the diagram. At 3:11, the teacher notices dots that appear to record counting. At 3:14, I relate those markings to the strategy practiced in the lesson. We are not relying only on a correct final answer; we are looking for traces of the process students used. This can help identify partial understanding even when a response is not fully correct and can make subsequent instructional decisions more precise.</p>\n<p>We also discuss an adjustment the teacher made during the lesson. At 3:26, I note that the next activity originally contained four items, and she confirms at 3:34 that these involved expressions. At 3:37, I ask her to explain the change she made to support students. She responds at 3:46 that the original task required students to identify expressions matching the area situation, but she supplied expressions because deriving or selecting them independently would have been too difficult for many learners at that point. This account helps us understand the instructional reasoning behind a visible change in the lesson.</p>\n<p>At 4:12, I ask whether available time also influenced the decision. She confirms at 4:17 and explains at 4:22 that the first activity had already taken approximately forty-four minutes in a mathematics period usually lasting about an hour. The remaining time had to include the closing assessment. At 4:33, I check whether preserving that opportunity was part of her reasoning, and she agrees at 4:39. This exchange places the adjustment within both a learning concern and a practical constraint, rather than interpret it as an unexplained departure from the plan.</p>\n<p>At 4:40, I summarize that the modification gave students a more accessible opportunity to attempt further practice before the exit response. The teacher confirms this interpretation as the discussion continues. Looking back, the conversation combines her account of decision-making, the observed lesson sequence, and the work students produced. Each source contributes something different. The video can show the change, the teacher can explain her intention, and the work can reveal aspects of students' understanding afterward.</p>\n<p>The counting evidence is especially useful because a score can conceal different kinds of understanding. A student may count a side correctly but make an error in the other side or in the final expression. Looking at dots, circles, and written quantities allows the teacher to identify those distinctions. Our discussion begins to connect the strategy practiced aloud with the process visible on the page, rather than assume that every incorrect answer represents the same misconception.</p>\n<p>The audibility discussion also illustrates why student behavior and teacher support should be examined together. A student who asks to hear an explanation again may be trying to participate, not failing to listen. The teacher's decision to request a louder voice responds to that condition and gives the student another opportunity to access the reasoning. Referring to the notes helps make this sequence visible in the coaching conversation.</p>\n<p>The task modification presents a further question about balancing access and demand. Supplying expressions made additional practice possible within the remaining time, but it changed what students were being asked to generate themselves. Understanding the teacher's reason for the adjustment is necessary before considering its implications for the exit response. That is why I ask about both the perceived challenge and the time available rather than interpret the modification from the plan alone.</p>\n<p>The example therefore illustrates a postconference organized around specific evidence rather than a general judgment about the lesson. It also has limits. The written account and observation record were available, but the clips themselves were not available for independent inspection. Scores and selected work samples do not establish the learning of every student or prove the effect of a single strategy. The useful coaching move is to ask what each piece of evidence shows, compare interpretations with the teacher, and use the resulting understanding to identify a more focused next step.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the work sample, criterion, and mathematical inference supporting each claim of learning.</li><li>Examine how the in-the-moment task modification affected what the work can demonstrate.</li><li>Connect the teacher's interpretation with the prior goal and the relevant observation evidence, and explain your coaching contribution.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 2 / Teaching effectiveness and next steps</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tEffectiveness of Teaching Practices Including Next Steps for Volunteer Teacher Growth:</p><p>Describe how you facilitated a joint discussion of the effectiveness of the volunteer teacher’s teaching practices to support student learning, identifying any missed opportunities.</p><p>After discussing the volunteer teacher’s effective teaching practices that supported student learning in this lesson, describe how you and the volunteer teacher co-determined possible next steps of the volunteer teacher’s professional growth in the identified CSTP element(s) (what you will do and what the volunteer teacher will do).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Analyze the joint discussion of successful practices and missed opportunities, then explain the next professional steps you co-determined. Include what the teacher will do, what you will do, the resources or support, and the evidence behind the plan.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Making joint next steps concrete</summary><p>A useful next step connects an identified learning need to a teacher action, your coaching support, appropriate resources, and evidence to revisit. Explain how both adults shaped the decision rather than presenting advice as an agreement.</p><p>Distinguish the next lesson from the observed lesson: a proposed check for understanding has not happened yet. Identify what the teacher will try, what you will help prepare or examine, and how the subsequent discussion could determine whether the change addressed the need.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.5: Joint next steps for teacher growth</h3><ul><li>Co-determine professional next steps based on CSTP-related evidence.</li><li>Include resources and additional coaching support, with analysis of your part in the joint decision.</li><li>For Level 4, focus professional growth on individual students' learning needs; Level 5 adds specific evidence-based resources and detailed learning steps.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>In the postconference, we used the lesson recording, observation notes, and student work to examine teaching that supported learning and opportunities for improvement. I wanted the teacher to reflect on the relationship among participation, positive classroom routines, and the mathematics goals. Questions and prompts were intended to keep her interpretation central while making the evidence specific. Rather than offer only a list of suggestions, I referred to particular events and asked what they revealed about students' access to the lesson.</p>\n<p>One effective practice involved connecting current work with earlier learning. In the written account of clip 2, at approximately 1:37, I refer to my notes while we view a moment where the teacher links the present task with preceding activities. I explain that such a connection can help students use what they already know to approach the new goal. She confirms at 1:44 and 1:54. Bringing the note into the discussion helps identify the practice precisely and connects feedback with an event we are considering together.</p>\n<p>We also examine the transition into the closing assessment. At the beginning of clip 2, I ask what the teacher notices about that transition. At 0:08, she observes that it takes time and does not yet function as an established routine. A student is unsure which material to take out even though the class has reached the end of the lesson. Her observation identifies a specific difficulty: students do not consistently recognize the signal or know how to prepare for the cool-down. This gives the discussion a concrete point for improvement rather than a broad criticism of organization.</p>\n<p>At 0:30, I ask whether the transition could be taught and practiced as a routine. She agrees at 0:31. I then ask how she might establish it for a future lesson (0:33). At 0:40, she considers using a clear signal after the lesson synthesis so students know to get the cool-down ready. She also considers having students prepare it as part of a morning routine. These possibilities come from her reflection on the observed problem. My question supports the movement from noticing a difficulty to identifying an action she could take.</p>\n<p>At 1:05, I add the possibility of displaying the page number in advance so that locating the material becomes more predictable. I connect this with the selected standard's attention to routines that support learning. The teacher explains at 1:18 that she has already organized cool-downs into packets to make them easier to access. She identifies organization as an area she is still working on. I repeat that focus at 1:29 to confirm what I heard. At 1:31, she points to students searching for packets in the recording, providing a visible reason for the proposed next step.</p>\n<p>The conversation then turns to student work. At around 2:06, the teacher examines a response that is close to correct but includes an inaccurate count of the whole units. The student writes an expression based on that count and consequently calculates an incorrect area. She interprets the work as showing some knowledge of how to determine area, with an error in representing the side length. This distinction matters because the next teaching response may differ from the response needed by a student who has no understanding of the multiplication model.</p>\n<p>At 2:23, I ask whether an earlier point in the lesson offered an opportunity to support that part of the strategy more explicitly. The teacher responds at 2:33 by identifying checking work as a routine she wants to strengthen. She describes students who finish quickly and move on without revisiting an answer. She wants them to view returning to a problem and checking their reasoning as a normal part of mathematics rather than a sign that something has gone wrong. This reflection connects a particular error in student work with an ongoing classroom practice she could teach.</p>\n<p>At 3:07, the teacher notices another issue in the recording, and I invite her to explain it at 3:09. She observes at 3:11 that the room is not as quiet as intended during the cool-down. She has explained that this is independent work designed to show each student's understanding, but the expected behavior is not yet consistent. I recognize this as another specific area for development: the class needs clearer, practiced expectations for independent assessment. It relates to the selected standard because the environment affects the kind of evidence the teacher can obtain about individual learning.</p>\n<p>These reflections lead into joint planning. At 3:33, I summarize that several parts of the new mathematics lesson sequence could become more dependable routines. The teacher agrees at 3:50. At 3:53, I ask what she would do to establish them. She responds at 3:59 that students need an explicit account of expectations for the warm-up, main activities, cool-down, and what to do after finishing. This is more specific than a general goal to improve management. It identifies recurring parts of the lesson where students can learn what participation should look like.</p>\n<p>At 4:25, I ask whether something in the physical classroom environment could help make those routines visible. The teacher mentions her frequent use of a timer at 4:32, including timed periods for quiet thinking. I note that this practice was also present in the observation. We discuss it as an existing strength on which to build, rather than assume all routines need to be introduced from the beginning. The timer gives students information about the available time and supports movement through the lesson's stages.</p>\n<p>At 4:47, I acknowledge that the teacher managed to reach the closing assessment after the warm-up and activities. She explains at 5:01 that obtaining this evidence is a priority, even if an earlier activity has to be shortened or omitted. Her reasoning connects time management with assessment: she wants to know what students understood before deciding what to teach next. This also helps us consider the earlier lesson modification in context. The adjustment preserved an opportunity for an independent response, although the quality of that evidence still depends on clear task and behavior expectations.</p>\n<p>As we move into clip 3, I return to the CSTP focus and ask the teacher to summarize the routines she wants to develop. The opening question, at approximately 0:00, connects her proposed actions with classroom procedures and positive support for learning. At 0:34, she identifies expectations across the warm-up, activities, synthesis, cool-down, and the period after students finish. Restating the sequence gives us a shared account of her priority. It also helps distinguish the professional learning goal from isolated corrections to individual student behavior.</p>\n<p>At 0:53, I revisit her earlier concern about materials and invite more detail. At 1:03, she explains that students need the correct mathematics notebook and unit workbook at the start of a lesson. The program contains approximately six to eight units with corresponding workbooks, and the class is currently in the second unit. If students keep every workbook in their desks, locating the current one becomes difficult. She wants completed materials to go home and the current materials to remain easy to find. This explains the organizational problem in terms of the curriculum's materials rather than assume students are simply careless.</p>\n<p>She also explains that cool-down packets should remain in folders, but some students do not store them there and subsequently lose them. At 1:46, I ask how she might support the students who continue to have this difficulty. At 1:51, she considers keeping packets for particular students and points to a duplicate in the work samples as evidence of the problem. At 1:57, I acknowledge that only a few students may need that additional arrangement. This keeps the response proportionate: a targeted support can accompany the general classroom routine rather than require an identical intervention for everyone.</p>\n<p>The next steps therefore include teaching the sequence of lesson expectations, improving materials organization, and establishing checking and independent-work routines. Each is connected with an observed event or a feature of student work. We also identify practices already helping, such as the timer and the teacher's insistence on preserving time for a closing assessment. Considering strengths alongside needs gives the teacher resources to build on and helps keep the discussion developmental.</p>\n<p>My own follow-up is to share the observation notes. At approximately 2:51 in clip 3, I explain that the teacher will receive the detailed record showing the connections with the selected CSTP element. Access to those notes will allow her to revisit examples after the conference and consider them alongside her own reflections. It also makes the basis for feedback transparent. The notes should support her learning rather than function as a private judgment held by the coach.</p>\n<p>Looking back, the discussion moves through a repeated sequence: notice a specific event, ask what it means, consider a possible response, and confirm the next step. The teacher contributes the concern and potential strategies, while I use questions and summaries to organize the inquiry. This supports a collaborative relationship in which improvement is connected to classroom evidence. It also suggests a further coaching need: the teacher's interest in using the assessment results to plan differentiated instruction deserves continued attention beyond routines alone.</p>\n<p>The materials discussion is grounded in the actual organization of this curriculum. A notebook, a current unit workbook, and a separate cool-down packet serve different purposes. Students can have difficulty beginning the correct task when those materials are mixed with completed unit books. The teacher's proposed routine would clarify what remains at school, what goes home, and what should be ready before a lesson begins. This is a practical response to the observed search for materials.</p>\n<p>The checking routine has a different instructional purpose. The student whose count was inaccurate showed enough understanding for the teacher to see how close the response was to a correct solution. Encouraging students to revisit a representation could help them detect such errors before considering the work finished. The teacher wants checking to be normal classroom practice, not an occasional reminder offered only during a test.</p>\n<p>Similarly, expectations for the cool-down need to make its purpose understandable. Independent work allows the teacher to learn what each student can do. If students rely on nearby conversations, the response may be less useful for planning subsequent instruction. Discussing and practicing the routine can therefore improve both the working environment and the quality of the assessment information available afterward.</p>\n<p>These proposed changes share a connection with the selected standards element but address different moments: preparing to learn, participating during instruction, reviewing one's reasoning, and showing understanding independently. Naming those moments helps the teacher decide where to begin and what to observe next. It also keeps the coaching from reducing several distinct needs to a single broad management label. The postconference can then return to particular evidence when considering whether the routines are becoming established.</p>\n<p>The available account is a written description with timestamps, not a verified transcript of clips inspected during this review. Its value is in showing how a postconference can connect teaching decisions, student evidence, and professional learning priorities. The proposed routines have not yet been evaluated for their effects. A later cycle would need to examine whether students can prepare materials more independently, use the checking process, and complete the closing task under conditions that reveal their own understanding. That follow-up would help the teacher judge whether the chosen changes improve both the learning environment and the instructional information she obtains.</p>\n<p>Continued support could connect that follow-up with the teacher's request for resources. Reviewing the next set of work together would provide a practical opportunity to discuss differentiation, identify a specific learning need, and consider how the revised routines support the instruction planned in response.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Distinguish a behavioral or organizational barrier from a mathematical misconception using the work.</li><li>Specify a jointly chosen practice, resource, and coaching action, with responsibilities and a review date.</li><li>Identify how the plan addresses particular students' learning needs and what new evidence will show whether it helps.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"I3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"I\"><span id=\"i-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 3 / Learning from teacher feedback</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tVolunteer Teacher Feedback on Candidate’s Coaching Practices:</p><p>Describe the feedback the volunteer teacher provided regarding your coaching skills during the coaching and observation cycle, including what they found helpful and/or not helpful. Explain how this feedback informs your understanding of your strengths and areas for growth as an instructional coach.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Report what the teacher found helpful and what was missing or unhelpful, then explain what that feedback reveals about your coaching strengths and growth. The current question explicitly asks for your interpretation, not just a transcript of the feedback exchange.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><ul><li>Analyze CSTP-focused coaching strengths and growth using teacher feedback and cycle learning.</li><li>Refer to evidence of how the coaching partnership was established and maintained.</li><li>For Level 4, analyze the teacher's feedback extensively and explain how it will change future coaching.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>At the end of the postconference, I asked the teacher to comment on the coaching itself. In the written account of clip 3, at 2:08, I invite her to identify what was useful and what was less useful. At 2:21, she expresses appreciation for having another adult observe the classroom and consider how to support the students. She explains that someone who has seen the lesson can offer feedback grounded in what is actually happening. Her response identifies the value of a second perspective and direct classroom observation rather than only general advice.</p>\n<p>I then ask what she would have wanted from the cycle that was missing (3:03). She takes time to think and begins a tentative response at 3:18. At 3:23, I offer resources or further professional development as possible examples. She agrees at 3:25, and I ask which areas would be most relevant at 3:27. She identifies classroom management with this particular class and more effective differentiation of the curriculum (3:28). This gives the feedback a more specific direction, although my suggested examples may have influenced the way she framed the response.</p>\n<p>At 3:38, she returns to the cool-down results. Only two students earned full credit, and she asks what to do with that information and how to move instruction forward. This is a significant extension of the conversation: she wants help translating assessment evidence into subsequent teaching, not only improving classroom procedures. I acknowledge that priority at 3:53 and express willingness to work with her on using the data for instructional planning.</p>\n<p>Her request also suggests that the next coaching conversation should include time to sort the work by demonstrated understanding and instructional need. That would respond directly to her question about what follows from the assessment. The present cycle identified the need; continued collaboration would be required to develop and examine the subsequent teaching response.</p>\n<p>At 4:09, she again notes the usefulness of someone entering the classroom with a different perspective. Her comments identify strengths in observation-based feedback and an unmet need for follow-up resources and planning support. For my reflection, I would retain the direct observation and collaborative discussion while creating more space for her to name missing supports before I suggest categories. The written feedback account is informative, but the unavailable clip limits independent review of the exact exchange and the extent to which my prompts shaped it.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Distinguish the teacher's specific feedback from the options suggested by the coach.</li><li>Explain what the comments establish about a coaching strength and an unmet support need.</li><li>State how future coaching will address that need, reserving the fuller reflection for Part J.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J1\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-j\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 1 / Coaching strengths and areas for growth</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tBased on your experience in this coaching cycle, and the feedback you elicited from the volunteer teacher, reflect on your instructional coaching abilities during the post-observation meeting in Step 3. Refer to the volunteer teacher’s feedback/input and evaluate your strengths and areas for growth in coaching and observing a teacher’s practice and providing instructional feedback in relation to the agreed-upon CSTP element(s). Explain why you think you should work on them.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Evaluate your coaching using the teacher's feedback and the cycle evidence. Explain why each strength or growth area matters for the agreed CSTP focus, and how your future approach will change. A hypothetical question is a proposed improvement, not something you actually asked.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><ul><li>Analyze CSTP-focused coaching strengths and growth using teacher feedback and cycle learning.</li><li>Refer to evidence of how the coaching partnership was established and maintained.</li><li>For Level 4, analyze the teacher's feedback extensively and explain how it will change future coaching.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The volunteer teacher's feedback identified a strength in the cycle and several ways I could improve it. She valued having another person observe the class directly and offer a perspective grounded in what occurred. In the written account of clip 3, around 2:08 and the response that follows, she explains that my presence helped make the feedback relevant to the students and the classroom. This suggests that observation-based discussion was useful. It does not yet tell me which particular question, note, or piece of feedback contributed most to her learning.</p>\n<p>Looking back, I would ask more specific follow-up questions. I could invite her to identify the feedback most useful in relation to element 2.6 and the lesson's learning goals. I could also ask which idea she intends to apply next and why that idea seems promising. Such questions would help me move beyond a general expression of appreciation to information about the coaching practices that actually supported her. They could also strengthen our relationship by showing that I want to understand her experience rather than simply receive a positive evaluation.</p>\n<p>In future cycles, I want to make these follow-ups a deliberate part of the closing conversation. A teacher may value observation for several reasons: another adult notices a detail, helps interpret student work, or creates time to reflect. I should not decide which reason applies without asking. Clarifying the contribution of my coaching would help me retain useful practices and change those that are less helpful. It would also provide a more concrete basis for my own professional growth than broad statements about being supportive.</p>\n<p>I also asked what had been missing from the cycle. At approximately 3:03 in clip 3, I invite the teacher to identify support she would have wanted. At 3:28, she names resources for classroom management and further differentiation of the curriculum. I acknowledge the request and allow approximately ten seconds while she examines the student work. At 3:38, she returns to the fact that only two students earned full credit on the cool-down and asks how to use that information to move instruction forward. Her concern extends beyond routines to the interpretation of learning evidence and the planning of subsequent teaching.</p>\n<p>At 3:53, I clarify that she wants support using assessment information to determine instructional next steps and express willingness to help. In reflection, I recognize that we could have addressed part of this need earlier. More time in the preconference to inspect the lesson plan might have helped us anticipate where particular students would require scaffolding or extension. I could have asked which learners were likely to need substantial support, which were ready for additional challenge, and how those needs might appear in each activity.</p>\n<p>Working together, we could have identified precise points in the lesson for scaffolds, planned questions that reveal understanding, or prepared supplementary materials connected with the mathematical goals. This would make differentiation part of the lesson design rather than only a topic raised after the assessment. It would also help connect the selected classroom-environment focus with students' access to the content. The teacher's request therefore provides a concrete direction for improving the depth of future planning conversations.</p>\n<p>Active listening was a strength I want to retain. During the postconference, I used paraphrases, acknowledgement, and clarification to connect the teacher's explanation with the evidence. For example, in clip 1 at 1:16, she describes encouraging all students to count side lengths during the lesson. I ask a clarifying question that connects that strategy with the closing assessment. At 1:50, she begins looking in the exit responses for evidence that students used the approach. The question helps move the discussion from a teaching intention to a feature of student work.</p>\n<p>This illustrates how listening can support analysis rather than merely signal politeness. By reflecting the teacher's point and asking a relevant next question, I create space for her to examine the relationship between a decision and students' responses. I want to continue developing this approach because it keeps her reasoning central and encourages reflection on pedagogy. At the same time, I need to ensure that my paraphrases do not become leading interpretations that she is simply invited to confirm.</p>\n<p>The wait time after her request was useful because she continued thinking while looking at the work. It allowed a broader request for resources to become a more focused question about using assessment information. I want to preserve that space in future conversations so that my response does not arrive before the teacher has finished identifying what she needs.</p>\n<p>My next steps are therefore to ask more precise feedback questions, make more room for differentiated planning, and continue using evidence-focused listening. I would seek specific information about what the teacher wants to develop and what parts of the coaching helped or limited that work. The written account supports these reflections, while the unavailable clips limit independent review of the interactions. The aim is a stronger instructional leadership practice in which feedback about my coaching is as concrete and useful as the feedback I hope to offer the teacher.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each growth area to a specific teacher comment or observable coaching moment.</li><li>Specify the resource or follow-up support that will accompany better questioning.</li><li>Define how feedback from a future cycle will show whether the revised approach is more useful.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J2\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 2 / Sustaining a two-way partnership</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tReflect on and refer to evidence from Steps 1–3 to demonstrate how you were able to facilitate and maintain a two-way conversation throughout the coaching, observation, and instructional feedback cycle. How were you able to encourage teacher voice and ownership of their strengths and areas for growth based on evidence of practice in relation to the CSTP element(s), the video of instruction, and student work product(s)?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use evidence across the cycle to explain how the teacher retained voice and ownership in identifying strengths, needs, and next steps. Asking questions is not sufficient by itself: show how the teacher's thinking influenced the work.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><ul><li>Analyze CSTP-focused coaching strengths and growth using teacher feedback and cycle learning.</li><li>Refer to evidence of how the coaching partnership was established and maintained.</li><li>For Level 4, analyze the teacher's feedback extensively and explain how it will change future coaching.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The investigation of existing practice in Step 1 showed that teachers had relatively few opportunities for regular cycles of instructional feedback. That context made it important to organize this cycle carefully. I needed to understand the purpose of each meeting and prepare questions that would invite the volunteer teacher to share her thinking honestly. Across planning, observation, and reflection, I wanted the process to remain a conversation with the teacher rather than a sequence of judgments delivered to her.</p>\n<p>In the preconference, I used questions to identify lesson goals, classroom context, and students' assets and needs. These topics would help us choose a CSTP element relevant to the actual lesson. They also gave the teacher an opportunity to explain her professional concerns before we discussed what I would observe. Preparing the conversation in this order helped me listen for connections instead of arriving with a predetermined standards focus.</p>\n<p>At approximately 3:39 in the written account of preconference clip 1, I ask about cultural and linguistic strengths and the support students might need. The teacher describes a class eager to participate, then identifies sustained engagement with difficult fraction work as a concern. I recognize that willingness to contribute and the challenge of the content can both inform our observation. Supporting engagement is not simply a matter of asking students to pay attention; it involves the routines and instructional conditions that help them participate in demanding learning.</p>\n<p>I keep this concern in mind when inviting the teacher to choose the standards focus. Around 5:00, we examine the district guide and I ask which elements match the areas where she wants feedback. At 5:12, she explains that, as a newer teacher, she wants to develop classroom management and selects element 2.6. She connects it with positive behavior, including one person speaking at a time. Her choice follows from the earlier discussion of students and the learning environment. This supports agency because the focus is something she identifies as meaningful, not simply an element I assign.</p>\n<p>The discussion also clarifies what evidence could represent that focus. At 5:30, I ask which observable events would best show the selected element, and the teacher identifies listening and participation behaviors. This helps translate the standards language into things we can notice during the lesson. It gives us a common reference for later feedback and reduces the likelihood that the teacher and I will interpret the observation's purpose differently.</p>\n<p>We identify student work separately from those behavioral indicators. At approximately 1:43 in the same clip, I ask about a closing assessment that would reveal the intended learning. The teacher describes an exit response requiring students to write a multiplication expression for a shaded area and determine its value. That product gives us evidence of mathematical understanding alongside the notes about participation. Keeping both sources in the plan helps ensure that the coaching connects the learning environment with the content students are expected to learn.</p>\n<p>In the postconference, I continue to use listening, paraphrasing, acknowledgement, and clarification. These practices are intended to make room for the teacher's interpretation of both strengths and needs. When she notices that students are not consistently following the intended independent-work expectations during the cool-down, I summarize the possible next step as establishing routines within the new curriculum's lesson sequence. In the written account of clip 2, this occurs around 3:33, followed by her confirmation. The summary checks my understanding while keeping the concern connected with what she has observed.</p>\n<p>At 3:53, I ask what she would do to make those parts of the lesson routine. She proposes discussing expectations for each phase, helping students organize materials, and clarifying the differences among the warm-up, activities, synthesis, and cool-down. These ideas are her responses to the problem, developed through the conversation. My facilitation supports her agency by asking for possible actions rather than immediately supply a complete management plan. We can then consider how those actions relate to the selected CSTP element and the evidence that prompted them.</p>\n<p>Another exchange connects her instructional choices with student work. At approximately 1:30 in postconference clip 1, I clarify whether counting side lengths during instruction prepares students for the exit task. She confirms. At 1:38, I ask how that strategy appears in the collected responses. She looks through the samples and notes at 2:34 that many students counted eleven fractional pieces along a side. This is a concrete connection between a teaching decision and visible features of student reasoning. It allows her to examine what students carried into their independent work rather than rely only on how the lesson felt.</p>\n<p>The cycle therefore gave several opportunities for a two-way exchange: the teacher defined a professional interest, identified relevant evidence, interpreted observed events, and proposed subsequent actions. I contributed organization, questions, notes, and summaries. The value of those contributions depends on whether they help her think more clearly, not whether they move the conversation quickly toward my preferred conclusion. Acknowledgement and clarification should remain invitations to examine evidence, with room for the teacher to disagree or add a different interpretation.</p>\n<p>Preparing questions does not mean scripting the teacher's responses. I need to use the planned prompts flexibly, listening for concerns that require a different follow-up. The examples in this cycle show how a discussion of goals can lead to participation routines, and how a discussion of routines can reveal a need for further assessment-informed planning. That responsiveness is part of sustaining a two-way coaching process.</p>\n<p>Looking back, I would strengthen this approach by allowing more time for specific differentiation questions and by following up more precisely on feedback about my coaching. The teacher's interest in using the cool-down results for future instruction shows that the conversation could extend further into assessment-informed planning. The written account describes meaningful participation in decisions, but the unavailable recordings prevent independent confirmation of the balance of voice throughout the cycle. Future practice should continue to connect teacher agency, student evidence, and a clearly defined professional focus, while using the teacher's feedback to improve the coaching relationship itself.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify a teacher-originated interpretation that changed your plan or understanding.</li><li>Analyze one moment where a leading prompt or rapid summary may have limited the conversation.</li><li>Explain how the partnership supported the teacher's capacity to interpret evidence independently.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><article id=\"J3\" class=\"template-review\" data-part=\"J\"><span id=\"j-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 3 / Equity and continuous improvement</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tInformed by a continuous improvement mindset and focus on equitable leadership, reflect on your role (or future role) as an equity-driven school leader. Discuss the potential impact of supporting teacher growth through coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what this cycle taught you about equitable instructional leadership and the potential of coaching to support teacher growth. Connect your leadership stance to actual teacher feedback, classroom evidence, and student access. Potential impact should not be written as a guaranteed outcome.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</h3><ul><li>Reflect on your role as an instructional coach through continuous improvement and equitable leadership.</li><li>Explain the potential impact of coaching, observation, or feedback on teacher growth.</li><li>For Level 4, support the reflection with specific evidence from this cycle; Level 5 adds relevant evidence-based practices or research.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>As a leader concerned with equity, I want to support the learning and well-being of the adults in the school so they can respond more effectively to students each day. Coaching, observation, and instructional feedback offer opportunities for sustained professional learning connected to actual classrooms. Their value lies in ongoing, differentiated support and the opportunity to examine how teaching relates to academic and social-emotional learning.</p>\n<p>Aguilar (2024) describes an equity-centered approach to coaching that develops instructional skill, reflection, effectiveness, and resilience. Collaborative coaching can connect student, teacher, and school goals while giving teachers an active role in deciding what to learn. The cycle uses standards-focused observation and student work as shared evidence for that inquiry. Inviting teachers to name a focus and help construct goals is an important expression of agency, rather than treating professional learning as something arranged entirely for them.</p>\n<p>That partnership also requires attention to adults' professional needs and the conditions that sustain their learning over time.</p>\n<p>This cycle reinforced the importance of understanding why a learning goal matters to the teacher and how it relates to students' needs. An instructional leader can support that understanding through careful listening, focused observation, questions, and joint analysis. Regular opportunities to work in this way can build trust and make professional growth more connected to daily practice. The intended benefit is improved learning for students, but that benefit requires evidence over time. My commitment is to sustain the collaborative process, attend to the teacher's perspective, and use student responses to determine whether the changes we pursue are making a difference.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use a specific teacher comment and student-work finding to explain an equity issue revealed by the cycle.</li><li>Describe how your leadership response would improve access while preserving teacher agency.</li><li>Distinguish the potential benefits of coaching from outcomes demonstrated in this evidence set.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section></article><section id=\"alignment\" class=\"major-section\"><h2>Read the template and handbook together</h2><div class=\"evidence-table-wrap\"><table class=\"audit\"><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Current template</th><th>Current handbook</th><th>Meaning for the student</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CSTPs in Part A</td><td>Section I question 5 explicitly asks how CSTPs guide teacher growth, including a crosswalk if another framework is used.</td><td>The investigation directions discuss CSTPs and an alternative aligned framework in prose; the numbered list on p. 7 contains four questions.</td><td>Answer all five questions in the current template. Read the handbook prose as well as its numbered list.</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-observation evidence</td><td>Parts B and E cover context and four commentary prompts.</td><td>The handbook also requires the teacher's lesson plan and two pre-observation clips, up to six minutes each (pp. 11-14).</td><td>A written explanation does not replace the plan or the visible joint decision-making in the clips.</td></tr><tr><td>CSTP focus</td><td>Part E asks for one or two CSTP elements linked to self-assessment and students' needs.</td><td>The pre-observation directions specify one or two elements within the same domain (p. 12).</td><td>Use the handbook to complete the template's instruction; establish the focus jointly.</td></tr><tr><td>Observation and evidence</td><td>There is no separate narrative template for Parts F and G.</td><td>Conduct a classroom observation of at least 20 minutes, record its full duration, and collect focused evidence during the observation (p. 16).</td><td>Plan to observe live and preserve CSTP-focused notes and actual student work. The handbook treats the observation and later joint video review as distinct steps.</td></tr><tr><td>Post-observation feedback</td><td>Part I question 3 asks what the teacher found helpful or unhelpful and what that reveals about your coaching.</td><td>The handbook repeats this analytical task and connects teacher feedback with reflection (pp. 17-19, 23-24).</td><td>Report the feedback, interpret it, and explain the change it calls for. A thank-you statement alone is not the analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflection format</td><td>Part J is a written template with a four-page maximum.</td><td>The handbook also permits up to six minutes of verbal or ASL reflection (pp. 23-25).</td><td>Use the limit for the chosen format. The three reflection questions still apply.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note\">Page references are printed handbook pages. These are instructional observations, not official CTC corrections.</p></section><section id=\"limits\" class=\"major-section\"><h2>Submission requirements</h2><table class=\"audit\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>A</th><td>School coaching practices and the volunteer teacher</td><td>Up to 5 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>B</th><td>Classroom context, lesson, and observation</td><td>Up to 4 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>C</th><td>Volunteer teacher lesson plan and work-product description</td><td>1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>D</th><td>Two pre-observation meeting clips</td><td>Exactly 2 video files; up to 6 minutes each</td></tr><tr><th>E</th><td>Commentary on the pre-observation meeting</td><td>Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>F</th><td>Observation notes and CSTP-focused evidence</td><td>1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>G</th><td>Student work used in the post-observation meeting</td><td>At least 1 example; 1 file; document or video; no maximum specified</td></tr><tr><th>H</th><td>Post-observation meeting clips</td><td>1-3 clips; each at least 1 minute; up to 15 minutes TOTAL</td></tr><tr><th>I</th><td>Commentary on the post-observation meeting</td><td>Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr><tr><th>J</th><td>Reflective narrative</td><td>Up to 4 written pages OR 6 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Meeting-record templates marked optional may be replaced by an appropriate local format; the required evidence is not optional. Consult the current handbook for recording permissions, technical specifications, and submission requirements.</p></section><section id=\"source-record\" class=\"sources\"><h2>Source notes</h2><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf\">Current Cycle 3 handbook</a>. Official material copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Interpolated example wording is newly drafted for instruction; original source wording is not retained in these app examples. Example references to research are preserved, but those studies and the school records were not independently verified. No assessor score report or video recording was available.</p><ul class=\"template-links\"><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S1_A_CoachingObsFeedbackPracVolTeacher.docx\">Part A template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_B_ClassroomContextLessonObservation.docx\">Part B template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_E_CommentaryPreObservationMeeting.docx\">Part E template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S3_I_CommentaryPostObservationMeeting.docx\">Part I template</a></li><li><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S4_J_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\">Part J template</a></li></ul><p>Companion reviews: <a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Cycle 1</a> | <a href=\"#cycle/c2\">Cycle 2</a>.</p></section><section id=\"scores\" class=\"major-section\">\n            <h2>Interpolated Example Status</h2>\n            <p>The examples on this page are newly worded instructional parallels. They are not official exemplars, not candidate submissions, and not scored responses.</p>\n            <p>Use them to study the kind of evidence and reasoning each prompt calls for, then replace the details with your own school context, records, meetings, and reflections.</p>\n          </section><section id=\"rubric-3-1\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate describe and analyze the current role of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school with the volunteer teacher, and explain the implications of how current practices impact their approach to conducting a coaching cycle?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not provide an explanation of the purpose or processes for coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school. Candidate outlines a plan for the coaching cycle with little or no details OR does not outline a plan.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate lists the teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school, providing limited details about the processes used. The purpose for conducting these processes is unclear. Candidate outlines a vague plan for the coaching cycle with minimal details about implications of each step of the coaching cycle and/or observation based on the volunteer teacher’s professional experience, current teaching assignment, and past experience in relation to coaching.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly describes and analyzes the current role of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback, discussing the purpose and intended goals of the practices for both teachers and students. Candidate clearly describes a plan for and the implications of each step of the coaching cycle and observation based on the volunteer teacher’s professional experience, current teaching assignment, and past experience in relation to coaching.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate describes how teachers participate in the selection and purpose of the instructional feedback practices at the school to meet the unique needs of the teachers and how the practices build trust between the teachers and school administrators.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate discusses relevant evidence-based practices and/or adult learning theory to explain how school practices of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback support ongoing teacher development and benefit students’ learning and well- being.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Source of Evidence: • Part A: Written Narrative: Coaching, Observation, and/or Instructional Feedback Practices at the School and the Volunteer Teacher (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 10.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-3-2\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher to understand the learning goals, classroom context, and student assets (e.g., cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests and learning needs; jointly select with the volunteer teacher one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, including evidence to be collected; and plan for the observation?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate and the volunteer teacher do not discuss classroom context, learning goals, and/or student assets and/or learning needs. Selection of the CSTP element(s) and/or evidence to be collected, including student work, is made by the candidate without input from the volunteer teacher or element(s) are not selected. OR No plan is established for the coaching and/or observation process. OR In the commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis* of their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate and volunteer teacher broadly discuss classroom context, learning goals, and/or student assets and/or learning needs, including student work. Candidate minimally involves the volunteer teacher in the selection of CSTP element(s) and/or evidence to be collected during the observation. It is not clear what aspects of instruction need to be the focus of the observation, and a plan is not specified and/or agreed to by the volunteer teacher OR candidate creates plan for observation without input from the volunteer teacher. In the commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis* of their ability to listen to and/or talk with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate clearly engages the volunteer teacher in a detailed and focused pre-observation discussion about the classroom context, student assets and learning needs, and content- specific learning goals of the lesson. Candidate and the volunteer teacher jointly select the CSTP element(s) and together determine evidence to be collected during the observation, including student work. Candidate and volunteer teacher create a plan for the observation, clearly identifying the focal aspects of instruction. In the commentary, candidate provides an analysis* of their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate uses facilitative questioning strategies to engage in a two-way discussion with the volunteer teacher to draw out their specific areas of need in relation to learning goals, classroom context, student assets, CSTP element(s), and observation plans.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate uses facilitative questioning strategies, including re-directing as appropriate for the conversation, to deepen discussion regarding equitable opportunities for all students in the class to learn.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Note: *Analysis: What the candidate does, why they do it, and the impact their action had Sources of Evidence: • Part B: Written Narrative: Classroom Context, Lesson, and Observation (up to 4 pages) • Part C: Volunteer Teacher’s Lesson Plan, Including Student Work Product Description • Part D: 2 Video Clips of the Pre-Observation Meeting (up to 6 minutes each) • Part E: Commentary of the Pre-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5C</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 15.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-3-3\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate recognize, document, and analyze the volunteer teacher’s teaching practice related to the selected CSTP element(s) and learning goals of the lesson?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate provides observational evidence that is unrelated to the CSTP element(s) and/or student learning goals of the observed lesson. OR Candidate’s observation notes are too vague to provide CSTP- related guidance. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of the volunteer teacher’s qualities of teaching practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate provides evidence of the observed lesson that is mostly unrelated to the CSTP element(s) and/or student learning goals. Candidate documents evidence that may be CSTP related, but evidence is vague and/or too general and may not be useful to the volunteer teacher. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of the volunteer teacher’s qualities of teaching practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate accurately recognizes and documents observational evidence of essential teaching practices for the selected CSTP element(s) and content-specific student learning goals. Candidate’s observation notes are detailed and clearly provide CSTP-specific guidance in a meaningful and useful way to the volunteer teacher. In their commentary, candidate clearly provides a clear analysis of the volunteer teacher’s qualities of teaching practice.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate’s observational evidence documented either a. missed teaching opportunities that would have supported students to meet the content-specific learning goals OR b. successful CSTP-related practices that the volunteer teacher did implement that positively impacted student learning during the lesson.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate documents how the volunteer teacher addresses whole-class, small-group, and individual student learning needs during the lesson and how they adapted or did not adapt their instruction to meet student needs as aligned to the learning goals of the lesson and the selected CSTP element(s).</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) • Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting • Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) • Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 20.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-3-4\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to foster a learning conversation in a post- observation meeting using CSTP-focused observation evidence, lesson observation video, and student work with the volunteer teacher regarding strengths and area(s) for growth?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate provides inaccurate or irrelevant feedback to the volunteer teacher, making limited use of the observation and other collected evidence (e.g., lesson observation video, student work). OR No strengths or area(s) of growth for the volunteer teacher are discussed. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate provides the volunteer teacher with minimal CSTP-focused evidence or feedback, with limited discussion of findings. Candidate vaguely connects observation evidence, lesson observation video, or quality of student work to the volunteer teacher’s strengths or area(s) for growth. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate fosters a thoughtful and productive collaborative conversation with the volunteer teacher, citing the CSTP-specific observation evidence, the jointly viewed lesson observation video, and student work. Jointly with the volunteer teacher, candidate respectfully and supportively identifies teacher's strengths and area(s) for growth based on gathered CSTP evidence and student work. In their commentary, candidate provides a clear analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate makes deep connections with the volunteer teacher among the pre- observation meeting considerations, the CSTP observation evidence, lesson observation video, and student learning as evidenced in the student work produced during the lesson.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate fosters a learning conversation that increases the volunteer teacher’s capacity for self-evaluation of instruction and assessment of student learning.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) • Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting • Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) • Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 21.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-3-5\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.5: Joint next steps for teacher growth</h3><p>Essential Question: In partnership with the volunteer teacher, how does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to co- determine next steps for professional growth, including resources and additional coaching support based on the CSTP-related evidence during the post-observation meeting?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate solely determines the volunteer teacher’s next steps for professional growth. OR There is little to no identification of resources and/or additional coaching support for the volunteer teacher. OR No relationship to CSTP-related evidence for next steps is discussed. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate occasionally allows the volunteer teacher to add their insights or perspective to the discussion on the teacher’s next steps for professional growth. The conversation with the volunteer teacher lacks identification of resources and additional coaching support and/or the inclusion of CSTP- related evidence. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate participates in a collaborative conversation with the volunteer teacher to co- determine next steps for professional growth, including resources and additional coaching support, that are clearly based on the CSTP- related evidence (observation feedback, lesson observation video, and/or student work). In their commentary, candidate provides a clear analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate and the volunteer teacher identify professional growth with specific attention to addressing individual students’ learning needs for CSTP-related professional learning.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides specific evidenced-based resources to support the volunteer teacher’s professional growth and engages the teacher in developing detailed next steps for professional learning and growth.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Sources of Evidence: • Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) • Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting • Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) • Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 22.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-3-6\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate analyze their capacity to conduct a CSTP-focused coaching and observation process, based on their experience in this coaching cycle and feedback from the volunteer teacher, and refer to evidence to demonstrate their ability to facilitate and maintain a coaching partnership?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not provide an analysis of their capacity to support teacher development through CSTP-focused coaching and/or observation processes. OR Candidate does not use the volunteer teacher’s feedback regarding their coaching skills. OR Candidate does not reflect on or refer to evidence of how they facilitated and maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate minimally describes strengths and/or areas for growth in CSTP-focused coaching and observation. Candidate’s analysis provides cursory use of the volunteer teacher’s feedback. Candidate minimally reflects upon and/or vaguely refers to evidence of how they facilitated and/or maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate analyzes CSTP- focused coaching and observation strengths and clearly identifies areas for growth. Candidate’s analysis clearly draws on the volunteer teacher’s feedback and other learning gained throughout the cycle. Candidate clearly reflects upon and refers to evidence of how they facilitated and maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides an extensive analysis of the volunteer teacher’s feedback to them regarding their coaching and observation abilities in planning and conducting the coaching cycle and discusses how they would change their future approach to coaching based on that feedback.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains how evidence-based practices or research related to coaching skills support teacher development and/or adult learning.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Source of Evidence: • Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5C</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 24.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-3-7\" class=\"rubric-block appendix-rubric\"><h3>Rubric 3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate, informed by a continuous improvement mindset and focused on equitable leadership, reflect on their role as an instructional leader acting as an instructional coach and reflect on the potential impact of coaching?</p><div class=\"rubric\"><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 1</h4><p>Candidate does not demonstrate an awareness of the connection between equitable leadership and instructional coaching. OR Candidate does not reflect upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 2</h4><p>Candidate vaguely reflects on and describes a minimal understanding of how instructional coaching and/or equitable leadership inform a continuous improvement mindset. Candidate minimally reflects upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 3</h4><p>Candidate, informed by a continuous improvement mindset and focused on equitable leadership, clearly reflects on their role as a school leader acting as an instructional coach. Candidate clearly reflects upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 4</h4><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate cites specific evidence from the cycle, drawing on the volunteer teacher’s feedback and/or other collected evidence, to support their reflection and analysis of their capacity to be an equitable leader and an instructional coach.</p></section><section class=\"level\"><h4>Level 5</h4><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites evidence-based practices or research that support instructional coaching and observation as a viable and equitable strategy to support teacher growth and benefit students.</p></section></div><p class=\"source-note\">Source of Evidence: • Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5C</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 25.</p></section></div>","search":[{"title":"Part A / I.1 / Analyzing school coaching practices","url":"#cycle/c3/A1","html":"<span id=\"a-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-a\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.1 / Analyzing school coaching practices</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tOutline how often during the year teachers are coached, observed, or provided instructional feedback and what the intended outcomes are for the school, teachers, and students. After reviewing current practices at this school, provide an analysis of the effectiveness of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain frequency, procedures, and intended outcomes for the school, teachers, and students, then evaluate effectiveness using available evidence. Distinguish evaluation, walkthroughs, and voluntary instructional coaching; they may serve different purposes and create different relationships.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Certificated teachers at this school participate in an annual evaluation with a site administrator. The process begins with a pre-observation conference, includes two formal observations, and concludes each observation with a follow-up conference. At the opening meeting, the teacher consults district materials and selects one or two elements of the California Standards for the Teaching Profession as a focus. Those elements guide both the teacher's preparation and the administrator's collection of evidence. One observation occurs in the fall and another in the spring, with a post-observation conference held within five days of each visit.</p>\n<p>During the follow-up conferences, the administrator discusses instructional practice in relation to the selected standards. The stated purposes are to maintain a strong certificated staff capable of advancing student achievement and to help teachers understand and develop their practice. Administrators also conduct several informal walkthroughs during the year. These shorter visits do not appear to have a consistent preconference or postconference attached to them. The formal process therefore provides a clearer sequence for preparation and feedback than the informal visits.</p>\n<p>Recent achievement results raise questions about the school's improvement work. Across grades three through five, the proportion meeting ELA proficiency declined from 34% in 2023 to 18% in 2025. Mathematics proficiency declined from 24% to 17% over the same period. These trends do not isolate the effect of evaluation or coaching, but they indicate that the school cannot assume its existing practices have produced the intended achievement gains. Additional evidence would be needed to understand the relationship between teacher support, instructional changes, and student outcomes.</p>\n<p>This year is the district's first year of implementing instructional coaching. The elementary school is one of four sites assigned a full-time coach. Teachers may volunteer for a coaching cycle, and the process depends on establishing a trusting, collaborative relationship. Rather than begin with an administrator's evaluation requirements, the coach and teacher identify a focus connected to student strengths and needs. They use the CSTPs to help establish goals, plan instruction together, and decide what evidence of student learning to collect.</p>\n<p>The partners also decide how the lesson will be taught and observed. The teacher may teach while the coach observes; the coach may model while the teacher observes; or they may teach together and bring their observations to the later discussion. These options allow the arrangement to respond to the teacher's learning needs. Afterward, they examine student work and observation information, reflect on instruction, make adjustments, acknowledge progress, and identify subsequent steps. The intended emphasis is learning through shared analysis rather than simply receiving a judgment about performance.</p>\n<p>Because the coaching role is new at the site, its effectiveness cannot yet be established. A later review would need to include next year's SBAC ELA and mathematics results together with other quantitative and qualitative evidence. Student outcomes should be considered alongside information about participation, the coaching process, and changes in instruction. This would provide a more useful account than attributing a future score change to coaching alone. The present task is to implement the process thoughtfully and collect evidence that can inform that later analysis.</p>\n<p>Both administrator evaluation and instructional coaching contain practices related to adult learning. Aguilar (2024) emphasizes trust, attention to adults' previous experience, agency in learning, and opportunities to apply knowledge and receive relevant feedback. In the preconference, teachers exercise agency by selecting a standards focus and a content area. They draw on what they know about their students when designing lessons and identifying strengths and needs. An agreed observation focus helps make the evidence relevant to the learning they intend to pursue.</p>\n<p>The contrast between evaluation and voluntary coaching is relevant to how teachers may experience feedback. Both use standards and observation, but the coach must establish a relationship in which the teacher can discuss uncertainty and request help. Jointly deciding whether to observe, model, or co-teach gives the partners a way to match the process to the teacher's learning. The follow-up discussion then provides an opportunity to examine the resulting evidence together and choose an appropriate continuation of that work.</p>\n<p>The postconference provides a setting for timely feedback and reflection. In the administrator process, feedback about observed practice may help a teacher retain new learning and transfer it into everyday instruction. In coaching, discussion centers on student evidence and the reasons a particular instructional problem matters. This fits an adult-learning orientation toward practical problems and meaningful application. The processes are not identical, especially in their evaluative roles, but each can support learning when teachers understand the purpose, contribute to the focus, and have an opportunity to use the feedback. Trust and a clear connection to student needs are especially important as the new coaching model develops.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Separate established evaluation practices from the new coaching model and identify their timelines.</li><li>Describe what the achievement trend establishes and what it cannot establish about coaching effectiveness.</li><li>Use available teacher feedback, observation records, and changes in instruction to evaluate the process more directly.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / I.2 / Explaining processes and documents","url":"#cycle/c3/A2","html":"<span id=\"a-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.2 / Explaining processes and documents</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tDescribe the processes, forms, and/or documents that are used to guide pre-observation meetings, collect observation data, and focus post-observation meetings (e.g., teacher response forms, observation tools, student work products, next steps for professional growth). If the school does not conduct teacher observations and uses a different approach to provide instructional feedback to teachers, provide a detailed description of the process(es) and forms used.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how documents are used before, during, and after observation. Identify what each tool records, how it connects to the CSTP focus, and how teachers use the resulting evidence. Naming a form is not a description of the process.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>For annual evaluation, the teacher and administrator complete a district digital form during the preconference. It records the selected CSTP elements, the observation arrangements, and relevant lesson context. The administrator uses the same district tool to document evidence during the visit. That record then becomes part of the postconference discussion, where the partners review what was observed and the administrator provides feedback on instruction.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Describe the relevant fields and who completes or reviews them at each stage.</li><li>Explain how observational evidence is separated from interpretation or evaluation.</li><li>Show how the tool connects CSTP evidence, student learning, feedback, and next professional steps.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / I.3 / Teacher input and ownership","url":"#cycle/c3/A3","html":"<span id=\"a-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.3 / Teacher input and ownership</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tExplain the level of input teachers have on the focus or purpose of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain which decisions teachers influence, how they exercise that choice, and any limits on it. Distinguish selecting from options offered by an administrator from genuinely shaping the purpose, evidence, and goals of coaching.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>In the formal evaluation process, teachers choose one or two CSTP elements to concentrate the feedback they receive after observation. They then select a subject area, plan and teach a lesson, and identify the student products to be collected. These choices give the observation a defined instructional focus. In a voluntary coaching cycle, the teacher and instructional coach establish the goals together, collaborate on lesson planning, and agree on the focus of feedback. The CSTPs provide a common reference for that discussion, while the partners connect the selected element with the teacher's learning priorities and students' needs. This shared selection helps make the feedback relevant to the planned lesson.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify a concrete example of teacher input changing the observation or feedback focus.</li><li>Explain how confidentiality, role clarity, and teacher choice operate in the local process.</li><li>Connect these practices to trust and teacher-specific needs using available evidence.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / I.4 / Implications for this coaching cycle","url":"#cycle/c3/A4","html":"<span id=\"a-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.4 / Implications for this coaching cycle</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tExplain the implications for this cycle based on the analysis of the current role of coaching practices (at the school) and the background and experience of the volunteer teacher.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what you will retain, adapt, or clarify because of the school's practices and this teacher's experience. The task asks for implications, not merely a statement that the teacher already knows the process.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The volunteer teacher is familiar with a sequence of planning, classroom observation, and follow-up reflection from earlier coaching experiences. She has also selected CSTP elements to focus evidence collection and feedback. This cycle can build on that familiarity while involving her again in choosing and applying the standards that will guide the observation and subsequent discussion of instruction.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify which familiar routines can be used and which features of this cycle need clarification.</li><li>Connect a known teacher experience or need to a specific coaching decision.</li><li>Explain how you will check that the teacher understands and agrees to the purpose and evidence collection.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / I.5 / CSTPs and teacher development","url":"#cycle/c3/A5","html":"<span id=\"a-q5\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / I.5 / CSTPs and teacher development</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>5.\tHow do the CSTPs guide or play a role in teacher growth and development at the school? (If the school uses another set of standards, provide a crosswalk to the CSTPs for the element[s] you have identified.)</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the standards guide ongoing growth: identifying a need, focusing evidence, interpreting practice, and deciding next steps. If a different local framework is used, provide the required crosswalk for the selected elements.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Teachers use selected CSTP elements to guide professional growth in both annual administrator evaluation and voluntary instructional coaching. The standards provide a shared reference for identifying a focus and discussing practice. They focus feedback.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain how the local process uses an element to focus observation evidence and feedback.</li><li>Describe how the same focus informs a professional learning decision.</li><li>Provide a crosswalk only if a different framework is actually used; do not invent one.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.1 / Understanding the volunteer teacher","url":"#cycle/c3/A6","html":"<span id=\"a-q6\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.1 / Understanding the volunteer teacher</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tProvide information about the volunteer teacher’s:</p><p>a.\tprofessional experience as a teacher (including years of service, school site[s], grades and content taught, languages)</p><p>b.\tcurrent assignment (grade level[s], content area, course titles)</p><p>c.\tpast experiences with coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback at the school site, in other previous teaching positions, or during teacher preparation</p><p>d.\tknowledge and use of the CSTP to guide their growth and development as a teacher at the school site, in other previous teaching positions, or during teacher preparation</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all four subparts: professional experience and languages, current assignment, previous coaching experiences, and knowledge and use of the CSTPs. Explain the background accurately enough to support the individualized plan in the next question.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The volunteer has completed two years as a full-time certificated teacher and is entering her third year in the classroom. She currently works at a TK-5 elementary school. In her first year, she taught a combined fourth- and fifth-grade class in another district in an anonymized area. She moved to the present school for her second year and taught fifth grade. She is fluent in English and also speaks and understands Cantonese.</p>\n<p>Her current assignment is a fifth-grade multiple-subject classroom. This means the coaching will take place within a setting she knows from the preceding year, although the students and their learning needs are those of her current class.</p>\n<p>She has participated in several forms of coaching. During student teaching, she met weekly with three other student teachers and an experienced mentor. The group planned lessons and prepared for observations together. Members took turns observing one another with the mentor, followed by discussions of the lesson and instructional feedback. This gave her experience with both observing and being observed in a collaborative setting.</p>\n<p>She also completed two years of beginning-teacher induction, the first in her previous district and the second in the current district. In addition, she participated with her grade-level team in a district science coaching program. That experience included planning and pre-observation meetings, classroom observations, and reflection afterward.</p>\n<p>Across these settings, she has encountered the full sequence of preparation, observation, and reflection. That background means the cycle can connect with established experience while still attending to the particular classroom-management focus she identifies for this year.</p>\n<p>The CSTPs have been part of her development throughout these experiences. She used them during student teaching and during her first two certificated years at both school sites. She will continue using them this year in the annual evaluation process with the administrator. Her previous experience therefore provides a foundation for selecting a standards focus and discussing evidence, while the present cycle offers another opportunity to connect that process with the needs of her fifth-grade students.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Clarify total teaching experience separately from years at the current school.</li><li>Include the teacher's account of what prior coaching helped with and what remained a need.</li><li>Use the background to inform the next answer rather than assuming experience eliminates the need for orientation.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.2 / Planning the coaching cycle","url":"#cycle/c3/A7","html":"<span id=\"a-q7\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.2 / Planning the coaching cycle</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tExplain how the information you gathered about the school context and the volunteer teacher’s experiences informs your plans for conducting a coaching cycle, including how you will:</p><p>a.\torient the volunteer teacher to participate in your coaching process</p><p>b.\tparticipate in a pre-observation meeting</p><p>c.\tprepare for the observation and collection of evidence (including student work)</p><p>d.\tjointly conduct (with the volunteer teacher) the post-observation meeting</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the school and teacher information shapes four connected steps: orientation, pre-observation meeting, observation and evidence collection, and the joint post-observation meeting. State the purpose of each step and the decisions you will make together.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I will introduce the coaching process during our first conversation about participation and work with the volunteer teacher to arrange the pre-observation meeting. I will explain the three connected stages: planning before the lesson, observing the lesson, and discussing evidence afterward. Because she already has experience with coaching, I will ask questions that help us connect what she has found useful in earlier cycles with this one. The orientation will also cover the practical commitments. For example, I will confirm that the preconference will take approximately thirty minutes and explain the planned recording of that conversation so she understands and agrees to the arrangements.</p>\n<p>At the preconference, we will meet at a mutually workable time to discuss the lesson and the students who will participate. I will invite the teacher to describe student strengths, assets, and needs, then use clarifying and facilitative prompts to help us identify one or two CSTP elements. We will also agree on the student work products that can show learning during the lesson. These decisions will guide what I collect and what we later examine together. Before closing, I will summarize the selected standards and evidence so that we have the same understanding of the observation focus.</p>\n<p>We will review permissions and recording arrangements for the classroom lesson, confirm the observation's duration, and schedule the postconference. I will explain how I will provide a secure link in a shared digital folder so the teacher can view the lesson recording before that meeting. Giving her access in advance will allow her to reflect on the lesson and identify moments she wants to discuss. The recording is one source of evidence alongside observation notes and student work, not a replacement for either.</p>\n<p>I will also explain the intended postconference conversation. We will consider the teacher's self-assessment of instruction and assessment, examine the effectiveness of particular teaching practices, and agree on possible next steps. Those steps may involve resources, professional learning, or further coaching. I want the teacher to know from the beginning that the conversation will involve her interpretation and choices. I will also ask for feedback on the coaching process so I can identify what helped her and what I should improve.</p>\n<p>Before the observation, we will reconfirm the date, time, and length of the live fifth-grade lesson. We will ensure the necessary permissions and protocols are in place for everyone included in the recording. I will prepare a tool for collecting evidence tied to the agreed CSTP focus. Having that tool ready will help me attend to the selected practices and student responses without trying to record every classroom event equally. We will be clear about the student products to retain for analysis.</p>\n<p>During the post-observation meeting, we will review the recording and discuss instructional strengths and needs using the standards-specific notes, lesson events, and student work. I will use clarifying, facilitative, and reflective questions to support a productive conversation. The teacher's perspective will help us understand her intentions, while the evidence will help us examine what students actually did. We will compare those sources when considering teaching effectiveness rather than rely only on a general impression of the lesson.</p>\n<p>The orientation will also help us distinguish the purpose of each stage. The preconference establishes intentions and evidence, the observation records what happens during the live lesson, and the postconference supports interpretation and professional learning. Reviewing those purposes in advance can help the teacher prepare and make the later conversation more collaborative. It also gives us a shared basis for deciding whether the evidence we collect answers the questions we intended to examine.</p>\n<p>Together, we will identify a manageable next step and the resources or additional support that could help the teacher pursue it. We will also examine the assessment evidence to consider what students understood and what requires further instruction. At the end, I will ask the teacher to explain which aspects of my coaching were useful and which were less helpful. Her feedback will contribute to my own reflection on strengths and growth needs. Across the cycle, my role is to organize a focused inquiry with the teacher, maintain a clear connection among goals and evidence, and support professional learning through shared analysis.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each major coaching choice to the teacher's background or a stated need.</li><li>Jointly define what evidence will address both the CSTP focus and lesson learning goals, including student work.</li><li>Confirm live observation, duration, recording permissions, secure handling, and shared review of the evidence.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part A / II.3 / Anticipating challenges","url":"#cycle/c3/A8","html":"<span id=\"a-q8\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part A / II.3 / Anticipating challenges</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tDescribe any challenges you may face in conducting a coaching cycle with the volunteer teacher and provide strategies for addressing these challenges.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part A template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Identify plausible challenges for this teacher and context, explain why each may occur, and give a practical response. Tie the strategy to the challenge rather than listing generic good coaching practices.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: School practices and coaching plan</h3><ul><li>Analyze the purpose and goals of current coaching, observation, and feedback for teachers and students.</li><li>Explain a plan for each coaching step grounded in the volunteer teacher's experience and assignment.</li><li>For Level 4, describe teacher input that meets unique needs and how school practices build trust with administrators.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>One likely challenge in the preconference is turning a broad concern into a sufficiently clear observation goal. I will prepare questions that help us identify measurable learning intentions, students' strengths and assets, and the work that could demonstrate understanding. We will connect those decisions with the selected CSTP elements so the observation has a manageable focus.</p>\n<p>During the lesson, collecting precise evidence while many events occur at once may be difficult. I will use a prepared observation tool organized around the one or two elements we agreed to examine. This should help me record relevant teacher actions and student responses, along with the work products selected in advance.</p>\n<p>In the postconference, the challenge may be bringing several forms of evidence into one useful learning conversation. I will prepare questions and notes that link particular observation moments, video segments, and student work with the standards focus. Those references can support discussion of both strengths and areas for development. The aim is to make the evidence accessible enough for joint interpretation and a practical next step, while leaving space for the teacher's own reflections.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Ground each anticipated challenge in the school context or teacher's reported experience.</li><li>Specify an observable goal and the evidence fields that will keep the observation focused.</li><li>Plan how to invite disagreement and revise interpretations jointly without taking ownership away from the teacher.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part B / 1 / Classroom context and learning goals","url":"#cycle/c3/B","html":"<span id=\"b-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-b\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part B / 1 / Classroom context and learning goals</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tClassroom context, including:</p><p>a.\ttopic of lesson, learning and instructional goals, students’ prior knowledge, and what was taught in the prior lesson</p><p>b.\tclassroom setting (face-to-face, online, or hybrid) and organization for learning (whole class, small groups, individual practice, hybrid)</p><p>c.\tdescription of students served, including their assets and learning needs (e.g., grade level, demographics, student assets [cultural and/or linguistic] and interests, learning supports and adaptations)</p><p>d.\tmaterials and/or resources used during the lesson by the teacher or students to enhance or extend learning</p><p>e.\tother relevant classroom context information (e.g., time of day for the lesson, time of year, support staff that are in the room during the lesson, materials used, safety issues, bell schedule, parent support)</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part B template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all five components: the lesson and prior learning; the setting and organization; students' assets and learning needs; materials; and other relevant context. Explain information that affects instruction and observation, not just demographic totals.</p><p>Distinguish class enrollment from attendance in the observed lesson and distinguish student engagement from achievement of the mathematical goal.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>What each form of evidence contributes</summary><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">What it establishes</th><th scope=\"col\">Its limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Lesson plan</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">Intended learning, instruction, supports, and student work.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">It does not establish what happened during instruction.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Observation notes and lesson recording</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">Specific teaching actions and student responses.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">Events require interpretation; participation alone does not establish learning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Student work</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">What particular learners demonstrated on the task.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">A few examples do not establish a whole-class pattern or the cause of a result.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Meeting clips and commentary</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">The coaching interaction and an explanation of leadership decisions.</td><td data-label=\"Its limit\">Commentary cannot establish an interaction absent from the submitted evidence.</td></tr></tbody></table></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The observed lesson is Lesson 11 in the adopted fifth-grade mathematics program's second unit, which addresses fractions as quotients and fraction multiplication. Its focus is finding the area of rectangles when a side is a fraction greater than one. Students are expected to describe, orally and in writing, how equivalent multiplication expressions represent the area of such a rectangle. They are also expected to explain their area-finding strategies orally and represent the area with a multiplication expression. These goals combine mathematical reasoning with opportunities to communicate that reasoning in more than one form.</p>\n<p>Earlier lessons involved multiplying fractions by whole numbers with visual models and finding rectangular areas when a fractional side was less than one. Students had practiced using diagrams to explain their thinking both aloud and in writing. For instance, a rectangle model could show the equivalence of two-thirds multiplied by four and the product of two times four multiplied by one-third. The new lesson extends that knowledge to a side greater than one. Students use area diagrams, prior reasoning, and familiar strategies to investigate the new situation within the Number and Operations-Fractions standards.</p>\n<p>The lesson took place in person in a self-contained classroom with the whole class working together. Seventeen students were present. Desks were arranged in pairs and faced a digital display used for lesson materials and images from the document camera. This arrangement supported both partner interaction and attention to shared representations during discussion.</p>\n<p>The class enrollment is 22 students, identified in the scenario as 13 boys and 9 girls. The teacher described them as friendly, interested in participating, and enthusiastic about classroom and school activities. Seven students have individualized education programs and receive push-in or pull-out assistance from specialist staff. Fourteen are identified as multilingual learners or English learners. The class includes 19 Hispanic or Latine students, one multiracial student, one Pacific Islander student, and one Filipino student. Their cultural and linguistic backgrounds are varied, and the teacher identified willingness to contribute ideas as an important class asset.</p>\n<p>For presentation, the teacher used a large television connected to a computer and document camera. Each student also had an individual consumable workbook containing the lesson's materials. Students could therefore refer to their own diagrams and tasks while viewing the corresponding whole-class display.</p>\n<p>The difference between total enrollment and the number present matters when describing this lesson. The class context includes all 22 students, while the observation records the participation of the 17 attending that morning. Similarly, the two paraprofessionals were supporting particular accommodations; their presence should not be interpreted as an identical level of assistance for every learner in the room.</p>\n<p>The observation occurred in early October during the fall semester, from 9:30 to 10:35 in the morning. Two paraprofessionals were present to support three students with accommodations and other assistance specified in their IEPs. The day followed a shortened schedule for parent-teacher conferences, but there were no major schedule changes, significant disruptions, or safety incidents during the lesson. These conditions provide context for interpreting participation and the classroom routines recorded in the observation.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify specific assets and learning needs relevant to explaining fractional area, using teacher or student evidence.</li><li>Explain the planned language supports, adaptations, and adult roles without inferring needs from demographic categories.</li><li>Maintain the distinction between 22 enrolled, 17 present, and the number of work samples actually analyzed.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part C / Teacher lesson plan and student-work description","url":"#cycle/c3/C","html":"<span id=\"c-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-c\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part C / Teacher lesson plan and student-work description</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Collect and submit a copy of the volunteer teacher’s lesson plan, including the student work product description.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 11; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Provide the volunteer teacher's actual plan and a description of the student work that will show learning. Explain how the work relates to the lesson goals. An adopted curriculum plan establishes intended instruction, not proof of what occurred.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The teacher used the district-adopted fifth-grade plan for Unit 2, Lesson 11, on rectangular areas with fractional sides greater than one. The source packet contains the lesson materials across eight reproduced pages following the description. Those pages locate the lesson within the unit, identify its mathematical purpose, and provide the activities the teacher used during the live observation.</p>\n<p>The selected student product is the end-of-lesson cool-down, a brief formative assessment associated with California standard 5.NF.B.4.b. It asks students to use a shaded area representation in two ways: first, express the area as a multiplication problem; second, determine the value of that area. The work is intended to show whether students can connect the visual model with multiplication and an area calculation after the lesson.</p>\n<p>The lesson sequence moves from a warm-up into diagram-based area activities and then to the independent closing response. That sequence provides the context for discussing where the teacher modeled, adjusted a task, or checked understanding during the observation.</p>\n<p>The materials include the opening lesson information, warm-up, instructional activities, and the closing assessment. The same cool-down appears on the final page of the supplied lesson packet. Its role in the coaching cycle is distinct from the observation notes: it provides a student response to the mathematics task, while the notes describe teaching and participation during the lesson. Together, the plan and work-product description establish what was intended, what evidence would be collected, and how that evidence connects with the lesson goals.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the actual lesson adaptations agreed with the teacher and distinguish them from the published plan.</li><li>Specify what successful mathematical reasoning would look or sound like.</li><li>Connect the planned work and observation evidence to both the learning goals and the selected CSTP element.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part D / Pre-observation meeting video clips","url":"#cycle/c3/D","html":"<span id=\"d-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-d\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part D / Pre-observation meeting video clips</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: Select and submit 2 video clips, each up to 6 minutes, that include the following:</p><p>Video Clip 1: Teacher Needs, Lesson Plan Review, and CSTP Selection (up to 6 minutes) Select a video clip that demonstrates: • Your coaching, including asking prompting and clarifying questions, that leads to the volunteer teacher’s self-assessment of instructional needs and challenges based on the lesson plan they intend to teach and the student work product that will be the outcome of the lesson; student assets and needs; and classroom context</p><p>Supporting the teacher to then identify one or two elements of one of the six CSTP domains for which they would like observation data collected to guide the post-observation meeting • Video Clip 2: Observation Process and CSTP Data (up to 6 minutes) Select a video clip that demonstrates:  Your review of the coaching and observation process with the teacher • Your review of the CSTP–element-focused data you will collect during the observation to share and discuss in the post-observation meeting, in addition to the student work products and other relevant data shared by the teacher</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 12 and following page; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit two clips, up to six minutes each, showing the required pre-observation discussion. The first concerns teacher needs, the lesson, and CSTP selection; the second concerns the observation process and evidence to collect. Joint decisions must be visible in the actual exchange.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The written account refers to preconference recordings that were unavailable for inspection. No replacement recording or invented transcript is presented as evidence of those coaching conversations.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Verify the two clips against the distinct functions and six-minute limits in the current handbook.</li><li>Ensure the teacher's input and the jointly agreed focus and evidence are visible and audible.</li><li>Check every commentary timestamp against the submitted clips and keep the interaction authentic.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / 1 / Teacher self-assessment","url":"#cycle/c3/E1","html":"<span id=\"e-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-e\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 1 / Teacher self-assessment</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tVolunteer Teacher’s Self-Assessment: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher determined their instructional needs and challenges based on the CSTP element(s).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain how the teacher identified instructional needs and challenges and how your listening and questioning helped connect them to the CSTP focus. Analyze the teacher's thinking and the effect of your coaching, not only the questions you planned to ask.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>I wanted the preconference to help the teacher identify an instructional focus connected to a CSTP element. I began with questions about the lesson's intended learning so that the standards discussion would grow from the work students would do. In the account of clip 1, from 0:00 to 0:42, I ask the teacher to describe the main goals and then paraphrase her explanation to check my understanding. She identifies mathematical learning that includes speaking, listening, and writing. I note that these different forms of participation may matter when we later discuss students' needs and the observation focus.</p>\n<p>This opening supports a two-way conversation because the teacher supplies the goals and I clarify their meaning rather than select a focus in advance. As she explains the lesson, I listen for connections among the intended mathematics, the ways students will communicate, and the classroom conditions needed for participation. Those connections can help us decide what evidence would be useful. The initial questions therefore serve more than an introductory purpose; they establish a foundation for later choices about observation and feedback.</p>\n<p>I next invite the teacher to describe students' strengths and needs. Around 3:39-4:20, the commentary describes her identifying enthusiasm for participation as a class asset and sustained engagement with challenging fraction work as a need. She sees students as willing to contribute but also recognizes that the content can be demanding. This distinction matters because the coaching should build on their readiness to participate while examining the supports that help participation continue. It gives us a reason to consider teaching practices that maintain engagement when students encounter difficulty.</p>\n<p>I connect that concern with the standards element we select later. Supporting students through challenging mathematics involves more than explaining the content; it also involves routines that help them listen, contribute, and learn from classmates. Active listening during the early part of the conversation allows me to notice that relationship. Looking back, I see that the questions about goals, assets, needs, and context helped the teacher develop the rationale for the focus rather than simply agree with a suggestion from me.</p>\n<p>The classroom context provides another connection. In clip 1 at approximately 4:21-4:28, I ask about the learning environment and classroom culture. The teacher then describes her work on students raising hands and listening while one person speaks. These are specific behaviors she wants to strengthen, not a general statement that the class needs better management. Identifying them gives the observation something concrete to attend to and helps link the teacher's professional interest with students' opportunities to hear and respond to mathematical ideas.</p>\n<p>At approximately 4:38, I invite her to choose a CSTP element on which she would like feedback. We look at the district guide together. At 5:04, I ask which element matches the aspect of teaching she wants to examine in this lesson. At 5:12, she identifies element 2.6, explaining that as a newer teacher she wants to continue developing classroom management and support positive behavior, including one speaker at a time. Her selection follows directly from the needs described earlier in the meeting.</p>\n<p>My role is to help make those connections explicit. I use questions, paraphrases, and confirmation to relate the standards language to the routines and behaviors the teacher has named. This keeps her self-assessment central while also ensuring the focus is observable. Rather than treat the CSTP as a label added after planning, we use it to organize what we will look for and why it matters for students' access to the lesson.</p>\n<p>The teacher's interest in classroom management is therefore connected with access to mathematics rather than treated as a separate concern about order. Students need to hear an explanation, ask a question, and contribute their own reasoning when working with challenging fraction concepts. The routines she identifies can support those opportunities. My responsibility is to help us identify evidence of that relationship without assuming that a quiet room alone shows learning or that enthusiasm alone guarantees understanding. This distinction keeps the observation connected with both the selected standard and the lesson goals.</p>\n<p>In reflection, I see this sequence as a strength of the coaching conversation. The teacher identified learning intentions, student assets, engagement challenges, and classroom routines before choosing the element. I listened for relationships among those topics and helped turn them into an observation plan. The written account also requires care: its references to clips are not independently verified because the recordings were unavailable. What it offers is a described example of how facilitative questions can support a teacher's choice of focus and connect that choice with student participation in demanding content.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Correct clip references by checking the recordings, rather than inferring the intended timestamp.</li><li>Identify the teacher's specific self-assessed need and show how it influenced the jointly selected focus.</li><li>Explain the impact of a facilitative question using the teacher's resulting reasoning, not merely agreement.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / 2 / Reviewing the lesson together","url":"#cycle/c3/E2","html":"<span id=\"e-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 2 / Reviewing the lesson together</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tLesson Plan Review: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher reviewed the lesson plan. Think about:</p><p>a.\tstudent work product,</p><p>b.\tclassroom context, and</p><p>c.\tstudents’ assets and learning needs.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Connect the student work, classroom context, and student assets and needs to the lesson review. Show how your questions helped the teacher clarify what students should learn and how the planned evidence will reveal it.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The choice of a student work product began with clarifying the lesson goals. In the first clip's written account, I ask at 0:00-0:10 what the teacher intends students to learn and what they should be able to demonstrate by the end. She describes two related goals: explain orally and in writing how equivalent multiplication expressions represent the area of a rectangle with fractional sides greater than one, and describe area-finding strategies orally while representing the result as a multiplication expression. This establishes both the mathematics and the forms of communication that evidence should address.</p>\n<p>At approximately 0:37, I paraphrase the goals as including speaking and writing, and the teacher confirms that interpretation at 0:42. I then ask how Lesson 11 connects with earlier learning (0:48). She explains that students have already multiplied fractions by whole numbers and found rectangle areas with one fractional side less than one (0:58). This connection helps us see what students can draw on and what is new in the observed lesson. It also makes the intended evidence more specific: we are looking for students to extend familiar area reasoning to a different kind of side length.</p>\n<p>At 1:12, I ask how she will recognize that students have met the goals and which products could make that learning visible. At 1:23, she describes listening to students use sentence frames in conversation and examining their written multiplication expressions for rectangle diagrams. I recognize the alignment with oral and written goals, but I want to clarify the particular work we can retain and analyze after the observation. Discussion during the lesson can reveal reasoning, while a collected product gives us something to examine together in the postconference.</p>\n<p>This is a moment where active listening matters. I am trying to distinguish an intended student behavior from a specific piece of evidence. The teacher has described what she hopes students will do, and my follow-up helps identify where that response will appear in the lesson materials. I do not want to assume that any written page will address the goals equally well. The selected work should allow us to consider the connection among the diagram, multiplication expression, and area calculation.</p>\n<p>At 1:43, I ask whether the lesson includes an exit response or cool-down that can show the intended outcomes. She confirms that it does at 1:52, and I invite her to explain it at 1:53. At 1:55, she describes the closing task: students write a multiplication expression for the area of a shaded region and determine the area. This gives us an identified formative assessment tied to the lesson plan. It also clarifies that the written product primarily captures mathematical representation and calculation, while oral explanation will require attention to the classroom discussion as well.</p>\n<p>The classroom setting is another part of the evidence plan. At approximately 4:21, I invite the teacher to describe this year's classroom culture and learning environment. She responds at 4:30 that students are practicing raising hands when they wish to contribute and listening to one speaker at a time. These are positive participation routines still under development. I see a connection between those routines and the oral learning goal: students need opportunities to speak, but also to hear and respond to the mathematical thinking of others. This context helps us identify what to observe rather than consider the work product in isolation.</p>\n<p>The discussion of assets and needs adds further context. At 3:39, I ask about students' cultural and linguistic strengths and whether any students require substantial scaffolding. At 3:53, the teacher describes a class with many ideas and a strong desire to participate. At 4:05, she identifies maintaining engagement during challenging fraction work as an area needing support. Her response provides a useful starting point, although it is broader than a detailed account of specific cultural or linguistic resources. It identifies willingness to contribute as an asset and sustained engagement as a need.</p>\n<p>I connect these observations with our later choice of a standard concerning an effective learning environment. The teacher's interest in participation routines, the challenge of the mathematics, and the lesson's communication goals all point toward examining how students are supported to listen and contribute. This gives the CSTP focus a practical relationship to the lesson rather than making it an abstract selection from a guide.</p>\n<p>The selected assessment also helps define the limits of what we can infer. A written expression and area answer can show aspects of mathematical understanding, but they do not capture every speaking and listening goal described at the beginning. That is why the observation of discussion remains relevant. The teacher and I need to consider the evidence sources together when we return to the lesson goals in the postconference. Our planning conversation begins that alignment by identifying the product, the expected response, and the classroom conditions under which students will complete it.</p>\n<p>Looking back, the conversation helped us identify several complementary sources of evidence. The cool-down would provide a written response to the area task. Observation notes would describe teaching routines and student participation. The recording, when available to the coaching partners, could help revisit particular interactions. Together these sources could support a richer analysis than any one alone. The written commentary preserves the sequence and its timestamps, while the absence of the recordings in the review means the interactions themselves cannot be independently confirmed.</p>\n<p>The evidence plan keeps the oral and written components visible throughout the cycle.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Explain the success criteria for the work and the evidence needed for the oral as well as written goals.</li><li>Draw out specific relevant student assets and supports instead of treating demographic labels or enthusiasm as sufficient.</li><li>Show how a contextual detail changed the lesson discussion or observation plan.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / 3 / Jointly selecting the CSTP focus","url":"#cycle/c3/E3","html":"<span id=\"e-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 3 / Jointly selecting the CSTP focus</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tCSTP Selection: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher selected one or two CSTP elements based on the volunteer teacher’s self-assessment and students’ assets and learning needs.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain why the selected element fits the teacher's self-assessment and students' needs, and how the selection was genuinely joint. The current handbook specifies one or two elements within the same CSTP domain.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>We used the district's detailed CSTP guide to connect the teacher's self-assessment with an observation focus. I invited her to identify one or two elements relevant to her interests and the students' strengths and learning needs. In the written account of clip 1, at approximately 4:38, I explain that we are moving from discussion of the lesson into choosing the teaching practices she would like to explore or receive feedback on. This gives her a clear role in setting the direction.</p>\n<p>At around 5:00, we examine the printed guide together. I ask which element best matches the feedback she wants from this particular lesson. At 5:12, she points to element 2.6 and explains that, as a newer teacher, she wants to keep improving classroom management. She connects that interest with supporting positive behavior, especially listening while one person speaks. The choice therefore draws on both her professional learning priority and a concrete student participation need described earlier.</p>\n<p>In reflection, I see how the order of the conversation supported this selection. We first discussed learning goals, student assets and needs, and the classroom environment. Those topics gave us a basis for interpreting the standards language. Inviting the teacher to choose an element afterward allowed her to explain why it fit rather than simply select a number from the guide.</p>\n<p>The guide is useful here because it gives both partners access to the same language. Pointing to the element and discussing its relationship to the lesson makes the selection visible and open to clarification rather than leaving the focus implicit.</p>\n<p>The resulting focus connects the teacher's instructional strategies with the environment students need to access learning. It also identifies a basis for collecting evidence and providing feedback during the observation. My facilitation helped organize that connection, while the teacher's own account established its relevance. The written commentary describes this collaborative sequence; the unavailable recording limits confirmation of the precise interaction.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Link the selected element to the specific lesson goals and student needs discussed earlier.</li><li>Identify observable teaching practices and student responses that will provide relevant evidence.</li><li>Preserve the teacher's rationale and agency while jointly testing whether the focus is sufficiently precise.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part E / 4 / Agreeing on observation evidence","url":"#cycle/c3/E4","html":"<span id=\"e-q4\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part E / 4 / Agreeing on observation evidence</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>4.\tCoaching and Observation Process: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher collaboratively reviewed the coaching and observation process, and the CSTP–element-focused data to be collected during the lesson.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part E template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain the jointly agreed process, duration, focus, and evidence collection. Include teacher practices, student responses, and student work. Analyze how the conversation produced shared understanding rather than presenting logistics as a checklist.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Pre-observation coaching</h3><ul><li>Discuss context, student assets and needs, and content-specific goals in a focused two-way conversation.</li><li>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, observation evidence including student work, and an observation plan.</li><li>Analyze what you did, why, and its impact. For Level 4, use facilitative questions to draw out specific needs across those topics.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>At the end of the preconference, I wanted to confirm the observation arrangements and the specific evidence we had agreed to collect. In the written account of clip 2, from 0:00 to 0:18, I explain that we will review the coaching sequence before closing and ask how long the lesson is expected to take. At 0:19, the teacher consults her plan and estimates approximately an hour to an hour and ten minutes. I record that information and acknowledge it at 0:27. Clarifying the duration helps us plan the observation and recording realistically.</p>\n<p>I then summarize the discussion so far. At approximately 0:31, I refer to the lesson's instructional components, the students' goals, assets, and needs, and our agreement about the focus of data collection. The teacher is described as confirming these points as I review them. My purpose is to check that we have a common understanding and that no important detail has been overlooked while moving from the instructional conversation into logistics. A summary gives the teacher an opportunity to correct or add to my interpretation before we proceed.</p>\n<p>At 0:48, I point to element 2.6 in the district guide and ask whether it is the element we selected. The teacher confirms. At 0:53, I review its focus on routines, procedures, shared expectations, and support for positive behavior that allow all students to learn. Referring to the guide keeps the formal focus visible while we connect it with the classroom behaviors she has already described. We are not adding a new concern at this point; we are clarifying the relationship between the selected element and the planned observation.</p>\n<p>Next, I ask what observable evidence would represent that focus. At approximately 1:02, I paraphrase her earlier emphasis on students demonstrating positive listening behaviors. The teacher confirms at about 1:18. I then ask her to restate the particular behaviors she wants us to notice. This follow-up is important because a broad phrase such as positive behavior could otherwise lead us to attend to different things. Naming the behaviors in advance helps make the observation more focused and the later discussion more useful.</p>\n<p>At 1:23, the teacher identifies raising hands, clarifying classmates' ideas, and adding to a contribution rather than simply repeating it. She also explains that when students repeat an idea, they can acknowledge the classmate whose thinking they are restating. These details connect listening with participation in a shared discussion. They suggest evidence beyond silence or compliance: students can show that they have attended to another person's reasoning by accurately representing or extending it.</p>\n<p>I listen and acknowledge the response while continuing to record the agreed focus. At 1:38, the teacher mentions using a random selection tool to broaden participation and help ensure that everyone has a voice. This adds a teacher action to the student behaviors we have identified. It gives us a way to examine how the teacher creates opportunities for contribution, while also observing how students respond. The distinction matters because the selected standard concerns the teacher's use of routines and supports, not simply whether students behave as expected.</p>\n<p>At approximately 1:44, I summarize that I will attend to both sides of that relationship: students' listening and participation, and the instructional strategies used to encourage those behaviors. The teacher is described as agreeing. I connect this again with the effective learning environment represented by element 2.6. At around 2:00, we both refer to the district guide while I identify the element. Her confirmation at 2:04 and my acknowledgement at 2:05 close this part of the review. These checks help ensure that the observation plan reflects what we developed together.</p>\n<p>I then explain what will happen after the classroom observation. At approximately 2:07, I describe reviewing the lesson video during the postconference along with student products and other evidence related to the selected standard. The teacher confirms the plan at 2:27. This connects collection with its later use: we are gathering information to interpret together, not merely completing an observation form. It also clarifies that the postconference will involve several sources and that the teacher will participate in the analysis.</p>\n<p>The earlier clip contains another part of the same planning process. At around 5:30 in clip 1, I ask which observation evidence would best represent the selected element while pointing to it in the guide. At 5:40, the teacher identifies hand-raising, avoiding simultaneous talk, and repeating classmates' contributions as ways to examine listening. These initial indicators are then refined and confirmed in the closing discussion described above. The two moments together show how an observation focus can develop through questions, clarification, and a final shared summary.</p>\n<p>The review also helps separate an observation indicator from a conclusion. Hand-raising, a request for clarification, or a student's paraphrase can be recorded as an event. Whether those events show that the routine supports learning is something we will discuss with the other evidence afterward. Agreeing on indicators gives us a more reliable starting point for that conversation.</p>\n<p>The teacher's description of acknowledging a classmate when repeating an idea adds a relational dimension to listening. It asks students to recognize another person's contribution rather than present the same idea as their own. Together with the use of a selection tool, this provides specific classroom practices we can examine when considering opportunities for different students to have a voice.</p>\n<p>Looking back, my coaching decisions aimed to make the process predictable and the evidence specific. Confirming duration addressed the practical arrangement; reviewing the standard clarified the professional focus; naming behaviors established what we would observe; and explaining the postconference showed how the evidence would support learning. The teacher's confirmations are described in the commentary, but the recordings were unavailable for independent review. The example therefore illustrates the planned relationship among goals, observation, and feedback without presenting paraphrased dialogue as a verified transcript.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>State the agreed evidence categories and how the observation tool will capture them.</li><li>Identify a teacher contribution that changed or refined the plan.</li><li>Confirm the live observation and subsequent joint video/work review, and explain how the plan addresses the mathematical goals.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part F / CSTP-focused observation notes","url":"#cycle/c3/F","html":"<span id=\"f-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-f\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part F / CSTP-focused observation notes</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: During the observation, use an observation tool to collect evidence related to the one or two CSTP elements agreed upon during the pre-observation meeting with the volunteer teacher. In addition to the lesson, collect evidence including student work (process, product, and/or performance) that was the result of the lesson. Submit your notes from the observation and/or forms you used to document the observation as well as the student work product as evidence for Step 3.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 16; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Collect specific evidence during the live observation and connect it to the agreed CSTP element and lesson goals. Separate observed actions and student responses from your interpretation or proposed alternative. Useful notes allow the teacher to examine a claim, not merely accept your judgment.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>From observation to a coaching question</summary><p>Keep three moves distinct: describe an event, offer a cautious interpretation tied to the agreed focus, and invite the teacher to examine it. A question should allow a genuine alternative interpretation.</p><table class=\"audit teaching-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Observed event</th><th scope=\"col\">Cautious interpretation</th><th scope=\"col\">Question for joint examination</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Observed event\">Illustrative only: a student reports finishing, but the collected response states an answer without explaining the reasoning.</td><td data-label=\"Cautious interpretation\">Completion did not establish understanding in this instance. The record does not establish the student's effort or the teacher's intention.</td><td data-label=\"Question for joint examination\">What does this response show about understanding, and what further evidence would help us decide?</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching demonstration, not an event from the interpolated example or a substitute for observation notes. Connect your actual evidence to the CSTP element and lesson goal selected with the teacher.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Observation focus: CSTP 2, the creation and maintenance of effective learning environments, with attention to element 2.6. The selected element concerns classroom routines, procedures, expectations, and positive-behavior supports that make learning possible for all students. The teacher's self-selected professional focus is classroom management. Agreed indicators include one speaker at a time, students raising hands, listening while classmates speak, and showing understanding by clarifying or restating another student's contribution. The observation will attend to both student responses and the teacher actions that support them.</p>\n<p>The first mathematical goal is for students to explain orally and in writing how equivalent multiplication expressions represent the area of a rectangle with fractional sides greater than one. The second is to explain area-finding strategies orally and represent the result with a multiplication expression. These goals provide the lesson context for examining participation and listening routines. The notes below paraphrase the recorded observation account; descriptions of classroom dialogue are not presented as verbatim quotations.</p>\n<p>At the opening, the teacher displays the day's area-learning goal and invites a student to read it. Several students volunteer by raising their hands. When the reader needs assistance, the teacher reads alongside the student and checks whether glasses are available and the display is visible. She reminds the class that the goal also appears in their individual workbooks. When several students begin speaking together, she returns them to the expectation that one person speaks at a time. This provides an early example of the positive participation routines connected with element 2.6. [CSTP 2.6: The hand-raising expectation and reminder about a single speaker establish the participation routine. Checking access to the displayed goal also supports the conditions students need to join the opening activity.]</p>\n<p>A possible missed opportunity at the opening is to make the relationship with prior learning more explicit. Questions about earlier area work could help students connect familiar representations with the current goal. The teacher could also identify what a successful response will include by the end of the lesson. Stating or developing those criteria with students would give them a clearer basis for recognizing progress and explaining their understanding. The displayed goal identifies the topic, but the connection among earlier work, today's task, and evidence of success could be developed further.</p>\n<p>The teacher then introduces the warm-up on the digital display. Some students begin before the explanation is complete, and she asks them to pause while she clarifies the task. Before the true-or-false activity, she checks the meaning of true and reminds students to raise their hands when responding. She reviews the activity's three parts, gives approximately one minute to begin, and starts a timer. As students work, she circulates, confers, and reminds the class to maintain a quiet working environment. Students ask whether the familiar digital random selector will choose speakers, and she confirms that it will. These established procedures organize independent thinking and subsequent participation. [CSTP 2.6: The teacher combines directions, a timed work period, circulation, and a familiar selection procedure. These routines organize how students begin the task, seek assistance, and prepare to contribute to its review.]</p>\n<p>During the warm-up review, the digital selector identifies four different students to contribute. As each explains, classmates turn toward the speaker, and the teacher records the reasoning on the board. She monitors attention and signals who will speak next. A student at the back becomes noisy and appears disengaged; the teacher explains that the student will be asked to restate a classmate's contribution. When that student says the speaker cannot be heard, she asks the speaker to increase volume and speak confidently. This interaction links the listening expectation with an adjustment that makes listening more possible. [CSTP 2.6: The observation connects an expectation for listening with an adjustment in speaking volume. The teacher responds to a practical barrier so that the student can hear the contribution before being asked to restate it.]</p>\n<p>The next selected student is encouraged to explain the reason for a true-or-false choice. The teacher offers a sentence opening that connects the judgment with its justification. Other students remain relatively quiet and some look toward the speaker. In relation to element 2.6, the evidence concerns the use of selection routines, expectations for attention, and language support to sustain participation. The notes distinguish visible behaviors, such as turning toward a classmate, from an inference that every student is fully engaged. The teacher's response to audibility is also relevant to whether students can meet the listening expectation. [CSTP 2.6: Participation is supported through both selection and a language frame. The teacher gives students a predictable way to enter the discussion while maintaining expectations for classmates to attend to the explanation being shared.]</p>\n<p>Moving into the first main activity, the teacher revisits the lesson goal and refers to the previous day's exit response. She encourages the class to build on that earlier success and notes that another closing assessment will follow today's work. The activity diagram appears on the display, and she holds up the matching workbook page. Students are directed to page 55 and asked to point to the diagram. As she circulates, this pointing response provides a quick check that students have located the material. A volunteer reads the first problem while classmates look at their books and remain quiet. These routines support a shared starting point for the activity. [CSTP 2.6: Locating and pointing to the common diagram helps establish readiness for the next task. The teacher checks that students have the required material before a classmate reads the prompt and discussion continues.]</p>\n<p>The teacher asks questions about the diagram. One student who has already volunteered raises a hand again. After approximately ten seconds of wait time, the teacher uses a paper random-selection tool to invite another response. As students explain, she asks clarifying and facilitative questions and points to relevant parts of the representation. Another student volunteers an explanation, followed by a further contribution that the teacher connects back to the diagram. Classmates appear to attend to the exchange. This sequence provides evidence of attempts to distribute participation and keep mathematical discussion connected to a common visual reference. [CSTP 2.6: Wait time and selection procedures provide alternatives to relying on the first raised hand. Questions and reference to the diagram help sustain a shared focus as different students contribute their mathematical thinking.]</p>\n<p>Before students continue, the teacher explains the task expectations, restates its purpose, and connects it with the previous lesson. She assigns five minutes and starts the timer. She also checks the procedure for requesting help. Students identify the established expectation to consult three classmates. Once work begins, students operate individually and in pairs. The teacher encourages them to ask a precise question rather than make a general request for assistance and suggests rereading the directions to identify what they need. She circulates, listens to conversations, and occasionally confers with an individual. The routine is intended to support productive peer assistance as part of the learning environment. [CSTP 2.6: The work period includes a known help-seeking procedure and expectations for specific questions. The teacher monitors how students use peer support while remaining available for individual clarification during the activity.]</p>\n<p>From the back of the room, the teacher uses the class's familiar call-and-response attention signal. Students respond and turn toward her. She asks a question to clarify the activity directions, and several students volunteer answers. She reminds the class that additional resources in the workbook can support their work. Students raise hands with questions, and she acknowledges them promptly before they return to the task. Work continues both independently and with partners. The teacher notices a student who has already written an expression and uses that observation to encourage the class. These actions show routines for gaining attention, clarifying expectations, and reinforcing productive effort. [CSTP 2.6: A familiar attention signal allows the teacher to clarify directions during work time. Prompt acknowledgement of questions and reminders about available resources support students in returning to the task with clearer expectations.]</p>\n<p>As she continues circulating, the teacher provides individual assistance and checks progress. When one student begins distracting behavior, she moves close, speaks with the student, and redirects attention to the partner. She asks the partner to explain the mathematics being attempted. This response uses proximity and peer interaction to reconnect the student with the activity. The observation focus remains the teacher's use of procedures and positive support, together with the student response. It does not establish that every peer conversation is productive or that the redirection has a lasting effect beyond the observed moment. [CSTP 2.6: The teacher uses proximity, an individual conversation, and a partner connection to redirect participation. The evidence records the immediate support offered and the learning task to which the student is being returned.]</p>\n<p>Near the end of the work period, the teacher gives a thirty-second warning and encourages students to record their thinking. When the timer ends, she repeats the attention signal and students turn toward her. She acknowledges that the task can be challenging and invites a volunteer to share. Noticing a student who has not yet contributed, she asks that student to explain at the digital display. As the student approaches, the teacher reminds classmates that supporting a peer includes listening. She also explains that the random selector will identify the next participant. These transitions connect predictable procedures with expectations for respectful participation. [CSTP 2.6: Advance notice, a timer, and an established attention signal organize the transition. Inviting a student who has not yet shared and reminding classmates to listen connect that procedure with inclusive participation.]</p>\n<p>While the student marks the diagram and explains, the teacher stands near a group at the side of the room and facilitates the exchange. She encourages the class to count with the speaker as parts of the representation are identified. Students join in, and some ask clarifying questions. This provides a way for classmates to participate while one student leads the explanation. In terms of element 2.6, the observation records both the routine for taking turns and the teacher's efforts to make listening active. The mathematical diagram continues to serve as a shared reference for the discussion. [CSTP 2.6: Counting with the presenter gives the audience a defined role in the explanation. The teacher maintains the shared discussion while supporting the student at the display and responding to classmates seeking clarification.]</p>\n<p>At 27:00, the teacher supports written explanation by putting a sentence frame on the board that asks students to connect an expression with the diagram. Several students begin copying it into their workbooks. She notices and names students who are recording the frame. This provides a bridge between the oral discussion and the written reasoning expected in the lesson. The notes capture the availability of the support and some visible uptake, rather than assume every student used the frame or that copying it alone demonstrated understanding. [CSTP 2.6: The written frame and acknowledgement of students using it make a participation expectation visible. This support helps connect the shared oral work with the written activity students are now expected to undertake.]</p>\n<p>At 28:00, students work alone and with partners, and some approach the teacher for conferences. When a student expresses difficulty, she directs attention back to the diagram, notes that a classmate has found a solution, and encourages continued effort. She identifies a student who appears to understand and asks that student to discuss the reasoning with another learner. She also confers with a pair and then an individual. Some students move around the room to consult others. Not all of those conversations appear related to the mathematics, and the teacher comments on the difference between students persisting with the task and students becoming distracted. This moment shows both the intended peer support and a need to maintain its focus.</p>\n<p>At 36:00, the teacher begins a countdown for students to return to their seats. Students move quickly, and a classmate urges others to sit. The teacher indicates that continued talking will affect the class's letter-based behavior system. Two students are at the front annotating the digital diagram while most of the room is quiet. Attention is uneven, however. The teacher points out that some students are looking outside or away from the screen and asks one student to turn toward the presentation. The speakers continue explaining, and she uses questions to clarify their mathematics. She invites an additional student to join the explanation and thanks the students for contributing. The record includes both positive participation support and more corrective management responses. [CSTP 2.6: The transition includes a countdown, reminders, and individual redirection. The record also notes uneven attention, allowing the coaching discussion to consider both the procedures used and the student responses actually observed.]</p>\n<p>At 41:00, a student at the back remains at the pencil sharpener for more than twenty seconds. The teacher reminds the student of the procedure for requesting a replacement pencil. The student asks, and the teacher moves over for a quiet conversation while the presentation continues. She supplies a pencil, and the student returns to the desk. This is an example of using an established materials routine and a brief individual interaction to reduce interruption. It relates to the selected standard through the management of classroom procedures in support of continued learning for the student and classmates. [CSTP 2.6: A materials procedure provides an alternative to an extended interruption at the sharpener. The quiet exchange and replacement pencil allow the student to return to the desk while the class presentation continues.]</p>\n<p>At 42:00, a student continues explaining at the front with the diagram. The teacher invites questions from the class. When a question is too quiet to hear, she requests more volume. When classmates ask for the explanation again, she promptly invites the speaker to repeat it more audibly. Students look toward the screen as the speaker annotates the representation. The teacher then asks the student to label and count the fourteen units being referenced. She confirms how the length and width relate to the multiplication expression and thanks the student. The sequence shows how clarification, audibility, and visual reference can support the listening and participation expectations established for the lesson. [CSTP 2.6: Requests for greater volume and a repeated explanation respond to students' listening needs. The teacher supports clarification while keeping the group connected to the diagram and the mathematical reasoning being presented.]</p>\n<p>At 43:30, the teacher moves the class to another activity and asks students to choose one of two area problems. She has adjusted the task to provide more accessible entry points after observing the preceding work. As students begin, she circulates and monitors. Some take their materials to established groups immediately. The modification concerns instructional access, while the familiar group arrangements provide a routine for beginning the task. The observation can therefore connect the teacher's response to student work with the environment in which students continue learning, without treating all students' responses as identical. [CSTP 2.6: The teacher adjusts the task after observing students' earlier work and continues monitoring established groups. The entry records how familiar arrangements and an accessible task support participation in the next learning activity.]</p>\n<p>At 44:50, the teacher announces that one minute remains for the selected problem. At 46:30, she signals the end of work time and uses a five-count to bring students back to their seats. These time cues make the transition explicit and give students a predictable way to return to whole-class discussion. They are additional examples of the procedures relevant to element 2.6. The record also allows the coaching partners to consider how much transition time is needed and whether students are ready to attend when the next explanation begins. [CSTP 2.6: The time warning and countdown make the change from group work to discussion explicit. These recurring cues provide structure for returning to seats and preparing to listen to classmates' explanations.]</p>\n<p>At 47:00, the teacher asks who has solved an expression and would like to explain. She invites a student who has not yet shared to come to the whiteboard. While that student writes, she monitors the room. When another student is disruptive, she explains that the student will be asked to restate the speaker's reasoning. This connects a behavioral redirection with the listening focus, while also raising a useful coaching question about how restating is framed: as a routine opportunity for everyone to learn from peers or primarily as a response to off-task behavior. [CSTP 2.6: The invitation broadens participation to another student while the teacher monitors the audience. Asking a listener to restate the explanation links the behavioral expectation with attention to a peer's mathematical contribution.]</p>\n<p>At 48:00, the teacher helps the student explaining by placing the student's workbook and diagram under the document camera. The visible representation supports the oral account. She asks the student to clarify how many of a particular fractional length are present, and the student identifies four. She assists with marking the diagram to make the reasoning visible to classmates. This combines an instructional scaffold with support for a student's participation in front of the class. It also provides evidence for discussing whether the questions make the relationship between side lengths and area sufficiently clear. [CSTP 2.6: Displaying the student's own diagram provides support for presenting and listening. The teacher assists the explanation without removing the student from the speaking role, maintaining an opportunity for public participation.]</p>\n<p>At 48:40, the teacher returns to the student who had been asked to listen and restate. She asks for an explanation of how the previous speaker determined the area. The student begins to paraphrase the reasoning, and she thanks the student. A possible missed opportunity is to ask additional questions about how the side lengths were identified and why those quantities were multiplied. Such questions could strengthen the mathematical explanation rather than stop at a retelling of the procedure. The event therefore offers evidence of the listening routine and a separate question about the depth of reasoning it supports. [CSTP 2.6: The follow-up makes the listening expectation observable through a paraphrase. The teacher acknowledges the response, while the accompanying coaching question considers how additional prompts could connect that participation with more explicit mathematical reasoning.]</p>\n<p>At 49:20, another student is invited to explain. The teacher tells the class that someone will be selected to summarize what was heard, maintaining an expectation of active listening. She uses detailed prompts and descriptions to make the student's method visible. After selecting a listener, she asks that student to explain the speaker's approach. The listener reports not hearing it clearly, so the teacher asks for another explanation at a higher volume. The original speaker repeats the account, and the listener then paraphrases it. This gives the coaching partners a specific sequence for examining how participation expectations are paired with support when audibility is a barrier. [CSTP 2.6: The teacher pairs the expectation to summarize with an opportunity to hear the explanation again. Repetition and increased volume support the listener before the student is asked to demonstrate what was understood.]</p>\n<p>At 51:00, the teacher transitions to the cool-down or exit response. Students are asked to take out a separate packet, and she sets a one-minute timer while they locate it. She directs them to page 79. A possible improvement is to establish at the beginning of the lesson which materials students will need and practice having them ready. That could reduce time spent searching and make the transition into assessment more efficient. The issue is connected to classroom procedure rather than a conclusion that students were unwilling to work. [CSTP 2.6: The transition reveals a materials routine that is not yet fully established. Preparing the packet earlier is identified as a possible support for reducing searching and increasing readiness for the independent assessment.]</p>\n<p>At 53:00, the teacher displays the exit task and explains how to complete it. One possible missed opportunity is to ask students what they notice and what the problem requires before supplying all the directions. That could increase their participation in interpreting the task. Another is to return explicitly to the lesson goals and success criteria: ask what students practiced, which strategies helped them determine area, and how those strategies apply to this final response. Such a review could connect the formative assessment with the learning it is meant to reveal. [CSTP 2.6: The closing task offers another opportunity to make expectations clear. Questions about the task, prior strategies, and successful performance could help students take a more active role in preparing for independent work.]</p>\n<p>At 53:38, the teacher points out a similarity with the previous day's task and reminds students to find the area. She explains that students may read quietly when finished. A visible set of finishing options could make that expectation more routine and reduce the need for repeated directions. Students then work on the exit response for approximately the final ten minutes. The observation ends with both evidence of established classroom supports and specific opportunities for refinement: clearer connections among goals and tasks, more explicit preparation of materials, and questions that help students explain the mathematics underlying their participation. [CSTP 2.6: The teacher states what students may do after completing the assessment. Making that expectation visible and familiar could help maintain a predictable working environment as students finish at different times.]</p>\n<p>The observation preserves the sequence of teacher actions, student responses, and possible missed opportunities so the coaching partners can revisit each event in context when discussing the selected standard and the mathematics goals.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Keep time, observed teacher action, student response, and interpretation distinguishable.</li><li>Link each proposed missed opportunity to the lesson learning goal, not just a preference for a different routine.</li><li>Compare responses across different learners and connect the relevant observations to actual work samples.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part G / Student work from the observed lesson","url":"#cycle/c3/G","html":"<span id=\"g-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-g\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part G / Student work from the observed lesson</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: During the observation, use an observation tool to collect evidence related to the one or two CSTP elements agreed upon during the pre-observation meeting with the volunteer teacher. In addition to the lesson, collect evidence including student work (process, product, and/or performance) that was the result of the lesson. Submit your notes from the observation and/or forms you used to document the observation as well as the student work product as evidence for Step 3.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 16; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use actual work produced during the lesson and discussed with the teacher. Identify the task, the success criteria, what the work demonstrates, and what remains uncertain. A correct answer, an incorrect answer, and visible reasoning can each provide different information.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>Work sample A received full credit, 2/2. Work sample B received partial credit, 1/2. Work sample C received no credit, 0/2, on the same two-item area task.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use anonymous, stable sample labels that also appear in the commentary and observation discussion.</li><li>Apply clear criteria to identify the demonstrated strategy, correct reasoning, and specific misconception.</li><li>State the number of works examined and avoid generalizing a few selected examples to the entire class.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part H / Post-observation meeting video clips","url":"#cycle/c3/H","html":"<span id=\"h-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-h\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part H / Post-observation meeting video clips</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>Directions: From the video of the post-observation meeting where you and the volunteer teacher are seen and heard, select 1 to 3 video clips. Your 1 to 3 video clips may total up to 15 minutes (assessors will only watch and score the first 15 minutes of video recording submitted).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current handbook, printed page 17; no separate narrative template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Submit one to three clips, totaling up to fifteen minutes, from the joint post-observation meeting. The evidence should show examination of the lesson video and student work, discussion of practice, shared next steps, and teacher feedback on your coaching.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.5: Joint next steps for teacher growth</h3><ul><li>Co-determine professional next steps based on CSTP-related evidence.</li><li>Include resources and additional coaching support, with analysis of your part in the joint decision.</li><li>For Level 4, focus professional growth on individual students' learning needs; Level 5 adds specific evidence-based resources and detailed learning steps.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The reflection references postconference clips unavailable for review. This section provides no substitute video and does not present invented dialogue as recorded coaching evidence.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Check the actual clips against the current handbook's content and duration requirements.</li><li>Verify the evidence of joint viewing, teacher reasoning, shared next steps, and feedback.</li><li>Confirm the commentary's precise clip locations and do not manufacture dialogue to fill a gap.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 1 / Jointly assessing student learning","url":"#cycle/c3/I1","html":"<span id=\"i-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-i\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 1 / Jointly assessing student learning</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tVolunteer Teacher’s Assessment of Student Learning:</p><p>Describe how you supported the volunteer teacher in assessing student learning during the CSTP-focused lesson.</p><p>Describe how you shared the evidence of CSTP-focused data with the volunteer teacher.</p><p>Describe how you and the volunteer teacher co-analyzed the video of the lesson, student work, and/or other data (if collected) from the lesson.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Address all three components: how you supported the teacher's assessment of learning, shared CSTP-focused evidence, and jointly analyzed the lesson video and student work. Explain how your coaching helped the teacher interpret evidence, not simply that you looked at it together.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Observation evidence and teaching practice</h3><ul><li>Document accurate, detailed evidence tied to the selected CSTP element and lesson learning goals.</li><li>Use the notes and commentary to analyze teaching practice in a useful way.</li><li>For Level 4, document missed opportunities to support the learning goals or successful CSTP-related practices that positively affected learning.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>During the postconference, we considered how the teacher had monitored students' participation while they practiced the mathematics needed for the closing assessment. Her explanation emphasized students counting rectangular side lengths accurately so they could write an appropriate multiplication expression and determine area. She wanted the whole class to use the strategy, not only the student presenting at the board. This connected the selected focus on participation routines with the lesson's mathematical purpose. Engagement mattered because students needed opportunities to practice the reasoning they would later demonstrate independently.</p>\n<p>In the written account of clip 1, at 1:16, the teacher explains that she counted with the class to encourage everyone to attend to the side lengths and prepare to write expressions. At 1:30, I clarify the connection with the exit task: students would need to use that same counting process to show the intended learning. She confirms at 1:36 and 1:38. This exchange checks that the observed strategy is being interpreted in relation to a goal, rather than praised simply because students appeared involved.</p>\n<p>I then ask how that teaching choice appears in the student work collected after the lesson (1:38). At 1:50, the teacher points to two successful cool-down responses. One belongs to the student who had asked for a clearer or more audible explanation during the presentation. The teacher notes that this student both wrote the expression and calculated the area correctly. At 2:09, I connect these features with the lesson goals, and she agrees. The example gives us a particular response to examine, while also requiring care not to infer that one classroom interaction alone caused the successful result.</p>\n<p>As we continue reviewing work at 2:11, the teacher identifies two students who earned full credit. She refers to their expressions and area calculations as evidence that they completed the task. At 2:31, I ask whether the work shows use of the strategy seen in the video. At 2:34, she examines several responses and notes that many students counted eleven fractional pieces along one side. This is a more specific observation than the score alone. It connects markings on the page with the counting approach practiced during instruction and gives us a basis for discussing both correct and incomplete responses.</p>\n<p>The postconference also draws on standards-focused observation notes. We pause the lesson recording at selected moments and discuss events relevant to element 2.6, which concerns routines and positive-behavior supports for an effective learning environment. The notes provide another record of those moments. I use them to identify what I observed, while inviting the teacher to explain her intentions and interpretation. This helps distinguish the event itself from our conclusions about its significance.</p>\n<p>At the opening of clip 1, I ask the teacher to describe the lesson segment we are viewing (0:00-0:03). She explains that one student is presenting at the board while another asks a question from the audience. Because the voices are difficult to hear from the back, she asks both students to speak more audibly. She describes this as practice in a classroom routine for speaking so others can listen. At 0:41, I connect the event with my notes about a support for engagement and positive participation. This gives the standard a concrete example rather than discussing it only in general terms.</p>\n<p>Another note concerns the teacher's movement around the classroom. At approximately 5:03, I describe seeing her circulate and observe the groups during the activity. I connect that monitoring with maintaining conditions in which students can attend to the learning task. The discussion can then consider what she notices and how she responds, rather than equate walking around the room automatically with effective support. Her perspective contributes information about why she approached particular students or groups.</p>\n<p>At 2:48, I also refer to her enlarging the displayed diagram so students could count along with the teacher and classmates. I had recorded this in the observation notes. The change makes the shared representation easier to inspect and supports participation in the counting strategy. It is relevant both to instructional access and to the selected focus on a learning environment where students can contribute. Bringing the note and video segment together gives us a specific action to discuss and a way to connect it with the work students later produced.</p>\n<p>Joint analysis occurs as we move between the recording and the exit responses. At 3:02, I point out students' markings and circles around parts of the diagram. At 3:11, the teacher notices dots that appear to record counting. At 3:14, I relate those markings to the strategy practiced in the lesson. We are not relying only on a correct final answer; we are looking for traces of the process students used. This can help identify partial understanding even when a response is not fully correct and can make subsequent instructional decisions more precise.</p>\n<p>We also discuss an adjustment the teacher made during the lesson. At 3:26, I note that the next activity originally contained four items, and she confirms at 3:34 that these involved expressions. At 3:37, I ask her to explain the change she made to support students. She responds at 3:46 that the original task required students to identify expressions matching the area situation, but she supplied expressions because deriving or selecting them independently would have been too difficult for many learners at that point. This account helps us understand the instructional reasoning behind a visible change in the lesson.</p>\n<p>At 4:12, I ask whether available time also influenced the decision. She confirms at 4:17 and explains at 4:22 that the first activity had already taken approximately forty-four minutes in a mathematics period usually lasting about an hour. The remaining time had to include the closing assessment. At 4:33, I check whether preserving that opportunity was part of her reasoning, and she agrees at 4:39. This exchange places the adjustment within both a learning concern and a practical constraint, rather than interpret it as an unexplained departure from the plan.</p>\n<p>At 4:40, I summarize that the modification gave students a more accessible opportunity to attempt further practice before the exit response. The teacher confirms this interpretation as the discussion continues. Looking back, the conversation combines her account of decision-making, the observed lesson sequence, and the work students produced. Each source contributes something different. The video can show the change, the teacher can explain her intention, and the work can reveal aspects of students' understanding afterward.</p>\n<p>The counting evidence is especially useful because a score can conceal different kinds of understanding. A student may count a side correctly but make an error in the other side or in the final expression. Looking at dots, circles, and written quantities allows the teacher to identify those distinctions. Our discussion begins to connect the strategy practiced aloud with the process visible on the page, rather than assume that every incorrect answer represents the same misconception.</p>\n<p>The audibility discussion also illustrates why student behavior and teacher support should be examined together. A student who asks to hear an explanation again may be trying to participate, not failing to listen. The teacher's decision to request a louder voice responds to that condition and gives the student another opportunity to access the reasoning. Referring to the notes helps make this sequence visible in the coaching conversation.</p>\n<p>The task modification presents a further question about balancing access and demand. Supplying expressions made additional practice possible within the remaining time, but it changed what students were being asked to generate themselves. Understanding the teacher's reason for the adjustment is necessary before considering its implications for the exit response. That is why I ask about both the perceived challenge and the time available rather than interpret the modification from the plan alone.</p>\n<p>The example therefore illustrates a postconference organized around specific evidence rather than a general judgment about the lesson. It also has limits. The written account and observation record were available, but the clips themselves were not available for independent inspection. Scores and selected work samples do not establish the learning of every student or prove the effect of a single strategy. The useful coaching move is to ask what each piece of evidence shows, compare interpretations with the teacher, and use the resulting understanding to identify a more focused next step.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify the work sample, criterion, and mathematical inference supporting each claim of learning.</li><li>Examine how the in-the-moment task modification affected what the work can demonstrate.</li><li>Connect the teacher's interpretation with the prior goal and the relevant observation evidence, and explain your coaching contribution.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 2 / Teaching effectiveness and next steps","url":"#cycle/c3/I2","html":"<span id=\"i-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 2 / Teaching effectiveness and next steps</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tEffectiveness of Teaching Practices Including Next Steps for Volunteer Teacher Growth:</p><p>Describe how you facilitated a joint discussion of the effectiveness of the volunteer teacher’s teaching practices to support student learning, identifying any missed opportunities.</p><p>After discussing the volunteer teacher’s effective teaching practices that supported student learning in this lesson, describe how you and the volunteer teacher co-determined possible next steps of the volunteer teacher’s professional growth in the identified CSTP element(s) (what you will do and what the volunteer teacher will do).</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Analyze the joint discussion of successful practices and missed opportunities, then explain the next professional steps you co-determined. Include what the teacher will do, what you will do, the resources or support, and the evidence behind the plan.</p><details class=\"teaching-support\"><summary>Making joint next steps concrete</summary><p>A useful next step connects an identified learning need to a teacher action, your coaching support, appropriate resources, and evidence to revisit. Explain how both adults shaped the decision rather than presenting advice as an agreement.</p><p>Distinguish the next lesson from the observed lesson: a proposed check for understanding has not happened yet. Identify what the teacher will try, what you will help prepare or examine, and how the subsequent discussion could determine whether the change addressed the need.</p></details></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.4: Evidence-based learning conversation</h3><ul><li>Use CSTP notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive collaborative conversation.</li><li>Jointly identify teaching strengths and growth needs and analyze how your coaching supported the conversation.</li><li>For Level 4, make deep connections among pre-observation considerations, observation evidence, video, and learning demonstrated in the work.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p><h3>Rubric 3.5: Joint next steps for teacher growth</h3><ul><li>Co-determine professional next steps based on CSTP-related evidence.</li><li>Include resources and additional coaching support, with analysis of your part in the joint decision.</li><li>For Level 4, focus professional growth on individual students' learning needs; Level 5 adds specific evidence-based resources and detailed learning steps.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>In the postconference, we used the lesson recording, observation notes, and student work to examine teaching that supported learning and opportunities for improvement. I wanted the teacher to reflect on the relationship among participation, positive classroom routines, and the mathematics goals. Questions and prompts were intended to keep her interpretation central while making the evidence specific. Rather than offer only a list of suggestions, I referred to particular events and asked what they revealed about students' access to the lesson.</p>\n<p>One effective practice involved connecting current work with earlier learning. In the written account of clip 2, at approximately 1:37, I refer to my notes while we view a moment where the teacher links the present task with preceding activities. I explain that such a connection can help students use what they already know to approach the new goal. She confirms at 1:44 and 1:54. Bringing the note into the discussion helps identify the practice precisely and connects feedback with an event we are considering together.</p>\n<p>We also examine the transition into the closing assessment. At the beginning of clip 2, I ask what the teacher notices about that transition. At 0:08, she observes that it takes time and does not yet function as an established routine. A student is unsure which material to take out even though the class has reached the end of the lesson. Her observation identifies a specific difficulty: students do not consistently recognize the signal or know how to prepare for the cool-down. This gives the discussion a concrete point for improvement rather than a broad criticism of organization.</p>\n<p>At 0:30, I ask whether the transition could be taught and practiced as a routine. She agrees at 0:31. I then ask how she might establish it for a future lesson (0:33). At 0:40, she considers using a clear signal after the lesson synthesis so students know to get the cool-down ready. She also considers having students prepare it as part of a morning routine. These possibilities come from her reflection on the observed problem. My question supports the movement from noticing a difficulty to identifying an action she could take.</p>\n<p>At 1:05, I add the possibility of displaying the page number in advance so that locating the material becomes more predictable. I connect this with the selected standard's attention to routines that support learning. The teacher explains at 1:18 that she has already organized cool-downs into packets to make them easier to access. She identifies organization as an area she is still working on. I repeat that focus at 1:29 to confirm what I heard. At 1:31, she points to students searching for packets in the recording, providing a visible reason for the proposed next step.</p>\n<p>The conversation then turns to student work. At around 2:06, the teacher examines a response that is close to correct but includes an inaccurate count of the whole units. The student writes an expression based on that count and consequently calculates an incorrect area. She interprets the work as showing some knowledge of how to determine area, with an error in representing the side length. This distinction matters because the next teaching response may differ from the response needed by a student who has no understanding of the multiplication model.</p>\n<p>At 2:23, I ask whether an earlier point in the lesson offered an opportunity to support that part of the strategy more explicitly. The teacher responds at 2:33 by identifying checking work as a routine she wants to strengthen. She describes students who finish quickly and move on without revisiting an answer. She wants them to view returning to a problem and checking their reasoning as a normal part of mathematics rather than a sign that something has gone wrong. This reflection connects a particular error in student work with an ongoing classroom practice she could teach.</p>\n<p>At 3:07, the teacher notices another issue in the recording, and I invite her to explain it at 3:09. She observes at 3:11 that the room is not as quiet as intended during the cool-down. She has explained that this is independent work designed to show each student's understanding, but the expected behavior is not yet consistent. I recognize this as another specific area for development: the class needs clearer, practiced expectations for independent assessment. It relates to the selected standard because the environment affects the kind of evidence the teacher can obtain about individual learning.</p>\n<p>These reflections lead into joint planning. At 3:33, I summarize that several parts of the new mathematics lesson sequence could become more dependable routines. The teacher agrees at 3:50. At 3:53, I ask what she would do to establish them. She responds at 3:59 that students need an explicit account of expectations for the warm-up, main activities, cool-down, and what to do after finishing. This is more specific than a general goal to improve management. It identifies recurring parts of the lesson where students can learn what participation should look like.</p>\n<p>At 4:25, I ask whether something in the physical classroom environment could help make those routines visible. The teacher mentions her frequent use of a timer at 4:32, including timed periods for quiet thinking. I note that this practice was also present in the observation. We discuss it as an existing strength on which to build, rather than assume all routines need to be introduced from the beginning. The timer gives students information about the available time and supports movement through the lesson's stages.</p>\n<p>At 4:47, I acknowledge that the teacher managed to reach the closing assessment after the warm-up and activities. She explains at 5:01 that obtaining this evidence is a priority, even if an earlier activity has to be shortened or omitted. Her reasoning connects time management with assessment: she wants to know what students understood before deciding what to teach next. This also helps us consider the earlier lesson modification in context. The adjustment preserved an opportunity for an independent response, although the quality of that evidence still depends on clear task and behavior expectations.</p>\n<p>As we move into clip 3, I return to the CSTP focus and ask the teacher to summarize the routines she wants to develop. The opening question, at approximately 0:00, connects her proposed actions with classroom procedures and positive support for learning. At 0:34, she identifies expectations across the warm-up, activities, synthesis, cool-down, and the period after students finish. Restating the sequence gives us a shared account of her priority. It also helps distinguish the professional learning goal from isolated corrections to individual student behavior.</p>\n<p>At 0:53, I revisit her earlier concern about materials and invite more detail. At 1:03, she explains that students need the correct mathematics notebook and unit workbook at the start of a lesson. The program contains approximately six to eight units with corresponding workbooks, and the class is currently in the second unit. If students keep every workbook in their desks, locating the current one becomes difficult. She wants completed materials to go home and the current materials to remain easy to find. This explains the organizational problem in terms of the curriculum's materials rather than assume students are simply careless.</p>\n<p>She also explains that cool-down packets should remain in folders, but some students do not store them there and subsequently lose them. At 1:46, I ask how she might support the students who continue to have this difficulty. At 1:51, she considers keeping packets for particular students and points to a duplicate in the work samples as evidence of the problem. At 1:57, I acknowledge that only a few students may need that additional arrangement. This keeps the response proportionate: a targeted support can accompany the general classroom routine rather than require an identical intervention for everyone.</p>\n<p>The next steps therefore include teaching the sequence of lesson expectations, improving materials organization, and establishing checking and independent-work routines. Each is connected with an observed event or a feature of student work. We also identify practices already helping, such as the timer and the teacher's insistence on preserving time for a closing assessment. Considering strengths alongside needs gives the teacher resources to build on and helps keep the discussion developmental.</p>\n<p>My own follow-up is to share the observation notes. At approximately 2:51 in clip 3, I explain that the teacher will receive the detailed record showing the connections with the selected CSTP element. Access to those notes will allow her to revisit examples after the conference and consider them alongside her own reflections. It also makes the basis for feedback transparent. The notes should support her learning rather than function as a private judgment held by the coach.</p>\n<p>Looking back, the discussion moves through a repeated sequence: notice a specific event, ask what it means, consider a possible response, and confirm the next step. The teacher contributes the concern and potential strategies, while I use questions and summaries to organize the inquiry. This supports a collaborative relationship in which improvement is connected to classroom evidence. It also suggests a further coaching need: the teacher's interest in using the assessment results to plan differentiated instruction deserves continued attention beyond routines alone.</p>\n<p>The materials discussion is grounded in the actual organization of this curriculum. A notebook, a current unit workbook, and a separate cool-down packet serve different purposes. Students can have difficulty beginning the correct task when those materials are mixed with completed unit books. The teacher's proposed routine would clarify what remains at school, what goes home, and what should be ready before a lesson begins. This is a practical response to the observed search for materials.</p>\n<p>The checking routine has a different instructional purpose. The student whose count was inaccurate showed enough understanding for the teacher to see how close the response was to a correct solution. Encouraging students to revisit a representation could help them detect such errors before considering the work finished. The teacher wants checking to be normal classroom practice, not an occasional reminder offered only during a test.</p>\n<p>Similarly, expectations for the cool-down need to make its purpose understandable. Independent work allows the teacher to learn what each student can do. If students rely on nearby conversations, the response may be less useful for planning subsequent instruction. Discussing and practicing the routine can therefore improve both the working environment and the quality of the assessment information available afterward.</p>\n<p>These proposed changes share a connection with the selected standards element but address different moments: preparing to learn, participating during instruction, reviewing one's reasoning, and showing understanding independently. Naming those moments helps the teacher decide where to begin and what to observe next. It also keeps the coaching from reducing several distinct needs to a single broad management label. The postconference can then return to particular evidence when considering whether the routines are becoming established.</p>\n<p>The available account is a written description with timestamps, not a verified transcript of clips inspected during this review. Its value is in showing how a postconference can connect teaching decisions, student evidence, and professional learning priorities. The proposed routines have not yet been evaluated for their effects. A later cycle would need to examine whether students can prepare materials more independently, use the checking process, and complete the closing task under conditions that reveal their own understanding. That follow-up would help the teacher judge whether the chosen changes improve both the learning environment and the instructional information she obtains.</p>\n<p>Continued support could connect that follow-up with the teacher's request for resources. Reviewing the next set of work together would provide a practical opportunity to discuss differentiation, identify a specific learning need, and consider how the revised routines support the instruction planned in response.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Distinguish a behavioral or organizational barrier from a mathematical misconception using the work.</li><li>Specify a jointly chosen practice, resource, and coaching action, with responsibilities and a review date.</li><li>Identify how the plan addresses particular students' learning needs and what new evidence will show whether it helps.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part I / 3 / Learning from teacher feedback","url":"#cycle/c3/I3","html":"<span id=\"i-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part I / 3 / Learning from teacher feedback</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tVolunteer Teacher Feedback on Candidate’s Coaching Practices:</p><p>Describe the feedback the volunteer teacher provided regarding your coaching skills during the coaching and observation cycle, including what they found helpful and/or not helpful. Explain how this feedback informs your understanding of your strengths and areas for growth as an instructional coach.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part I template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Report what the teacher found helpful and what was missing or unhelpful, then explain what that feedback reveals about your coaching strengths and growth. The current question explicitly asks for your interpretation, not just a transcript of the feedback exchange.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><ul><li>Analyze CSTP-focused coaching strengths and growth using teacher feedback and cycle learning.</li><li>Refer to evidence of how the coaching partnership was established and maintained.</li><li>For Level 4, analyze the teacher's feedback extensively and explain how it will change future coaching.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>At the end of the postconference, I asked the teacher to comment on the coaching itself. In the written account of clip 3, at 2:08, I invite her to identify what was useful and what was less useful. At 2:21, she expresses appreciation for having another adult observe the classroom and consider how to support the students. She explains that someone who has seen the lesson can offer feedback grounded in what is actually happening. Her response identifies the value of a second perspective and direct classroom observation rather than only general advice.</p>\n<p>I then ask what she would have wanted from the cycle that was missing (3:03). She takes time to think and begins a tentative response at 3:18. At 3:23, I offer resources or further professional development as possible examples. She agrees at 3:25, and I ask which areas would be most relevant at 3:27. She identifies classroom management with this particular class and more effective differentiation of the curriculum (3:28). This gives the feedback a more specific direction, although my suggested examples may have influenced the way she framed the response.</p>\n<p>At 3:38, she returns to the cool-down results. Only two students earned full credit, and she asks what to do with that information and how to move instruction forward. This is a significant extension of the conversation: she wants help translating assessment evidence into subsequent teaching, not only improving classroom procedures. I acknowledge that priority at 3:53 and express willingness to work with her on using the data for instructional planning.</p>\n<p>Her request also suggests that the next coaching conversation should include time to sort the work by demonstrated understanding and instructional need. That would respond directly to her question about what follows from the assessment. The present cycle identified the need; continued collaboration would be required to develop and examine the subsequent teaching response.</p>\n<p>At 4:09, she again notes the usefulness of someone entering the classroom with a different perspective. Her comments identify strengths in observation-based feedback and an unmet need for follow-up resources and planning support. For my reflection, I would retain the direct observation and collaborative discussion while creating more space for her to name missing supports before I suggest categories. The written feedback account is informative, but the unavailable clip limits independent review of the exact exchange and the extent to which my prompts shaped it.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Distinguish the teacher's specific feedback from the options suggested by the coach.</li><li>Explain what the comments establish about a coaching strength and an unmet support need.</li><li>State how future coaching will address that need, reserving the fuller reflection for Part J.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 1 / Coaching strengths and areas for growth","url":"#cycle/c3/J1","html":"<span id=\"j-q1\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><span id=\"part-j\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 1 / Coaching strengths and areas for growth</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>1.\tBased on your experience in this coaching cycle, and the feedback you elicited from the volunteer teacher, reflect on your instructional coaching abilities during the post-observation meeting in Step 3. Refer to the volunteer teacher’s feedback/input and evaluate your strengths and areas for growth in coaching and observing a teacher’s practice and providing instructional feedback in relation to the agreed-upon CSTP element(s). Explain why you think you should work on them.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Evaluate your coaching using the teacher's feedback and the cycle evidence. Explain why each strength or growth area matters for the agreed CSTP focus, and how your future approach will change. A hypothetical question is a proposed improvement, not something you actually asked.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><ul><li>Analyze CSTP-focused coaching strengths and growth using teacher feedback and cycle learning.</li><li>Refer to evidence of how the coaching partnership was established and maintained.</li><li>For Level 4, analyze the teacher's feedback extensively and explain how it will change future coaching.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The volunteer teacher's feedback identified a strength in the cycle and several ways I could improve it. She valued having another person observe the class directly and offer a perspective grounded in what occurred. In the written account of clip 3, around 2:08 and the response that follows, she explains that my presence helped make the feedback relevant to the students and the classroom. This suggests that observation-based discussion was useful. It does not yet tell me which particular question, note, or piece of feedback contributed most to her learning.</p>\n<p>Looking back, I would ask more specific follow-up questions. I could invite her to identify the feedback most useful in relation to element 2.6 and the lesson's learning goals. I could also ask which idea she intends to apply next and why that idea seems promising. Such questions would help me move beyond a general expression of appreciation to information about the coaching practices that actually supported her. They could also strengthen our relationship by showing that I want to understand her experience rather than simply receive a positive evaluation.</p>\n<p>In future cycles, I want to make these follow-ups a deliberate part of the closing conversation. A teacher may value observation for several reasons: another adult notices a detail, helps interpret student work, or creates time to reflect. I should not decide which reason applies without asking. Clarifying the contribution of my coaching would help me retain useful practices and change those that are less helpful. It would also provide a more concrete basis for my own professional growth than broad statements about being supportive.</p>\n<p>I also asked what had been missing from the cycle. At approximately 3:03 in clip 3, I invite the teacher to identify support she would have wanted. At 3:28, she names resources for classroom management and further differentiation of the curriculum. I acknowledge the request and allow approximately ten seconds while she examines the student work. At 3:38, she returns to the fact that only two students earned full credit on the cool-down and asks how to use that information to move instruction forward. Her concern extends beyond routines to the interpretation of learning evidence and the planning of subsequent teaching.</p>\n<p>At 3:53, I clarify that she wants support using assessment information to determine instructional next steps and express willingness to help. In reflection, I recognize that we could have addressed part of this need earlier. More time in the preconference to inspect the lesson plan might have helped us anticipate where particular students would require scaffolding or extension. I could have asked which learners were likely to need substantial support, which were ready for additional challenge, and how those needs might appear in each activity.</p>\n<p>Working together, we could have identified precise points in the lesson for scaffolds, planned questions that reveal understanding, or prepared supplementary materials connected with the mathematical goals. This would make differentiation part of the lesson design rather than only a topic raised after the assessment. It would also help connect the selected classroom-environment focus with students' access to the content. The teacher's request therefore provides a concrete direction for improving the depth of future planning conversations.</p>\n<p>Active listening was a strength I want to retain. During the postconference, I used paraphrases, acknowledgement, and clarification to connect the teacher's explanation with the evidence. For example, in clip 1 at 1:16, she describes encouraging all students to count side lengths during the lesson. I ask a clarifying question that connects that strategy with the closing assessment. At 1:50, she begins looking in the exit responses for evidence that students used the approach. The question helps move the discussion from a teaching intention to a feature of student work.</p>\n<p>This illustrates how listening can support analysis rather than merely signal politeness. By reflecting the teacher's point and asking a relevant next question, I create space for her to examine the relationship between a decision and students' responses. I want to continue developing this approach because it keeps her reasoning central and encourages reflection on pedagogy. At the same time, I need to ensure that my paraphrases do not become leading interpretations that she is simply invited to confirm.</p>\n<p>The wait time after her request was useful because she continued thinking while looking at the work. It allowed a broader request for resources to become a more focused question about using assessment information. I want to preserve that space in future conversations so that my response does not arrive before the teacher has finished identifying what she needs.</p>\n<p>My next steps are therefore to ask more precise feedback questions, make more room for differentiated planning, and continue using evidence-focused listening. I would seek specific information about what the teacher wants to develop and what parts of the coaching helped or limited that work. The written account supports these reflections, while the unavailable clips limit independent review of the interactions. The aim is a stronger instructional leadership practice in which feedback about my coaching is as concrete and useful as the feedback I hope to offer the teacher.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Connect each growth area to a specific teacher comment or observable coaching moment.</li><li>Specify the resource or follow-up support that will accompany better questioning.</li><li>Define how feedback from a future cycle will show whether the revised approach is more useful.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 2 / Sustaining a two-way partnership","url":"#cycle/c3/J2","html":"<span id=\"j-q2\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 2 / Sustaining a two-way partnership</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>2.\tReflect on and refer to evidence from Steps 1–3 to demonstrate how you were able to facilitate and maintain a two-way conversation throughout the coaching, observation, and instructional feedback cycle. How were you able to encourage teacher voice and ownership of their strengths and areas for growth based on evidence of practice in relation to the CSTP element(s), the video of instruction, and student work product(s)?</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Use evidence across the cycle to explain how the teacher retained voice and ownership in identifying strengths, needs, and next steps. Asking questions is not sufficient by itself: show how the teacher's thinking influenced the work.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on the coaching partnership</h3><ul><li>Analyze CSTP-focused coaching strengths and growth using teacher feedback and cycle learning.</li><li>Refer to evidence of how the coaching partnership was established and maintained.</li><li>For Level 4, analyze the teacher's feedback extensively and explain how it will change future coaching.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>The investigation of existing practice in Step 1 showed that teachers had relatively few opportunities for regular cycles of instructional feedback. That context made it important to organize this cycle carefully. I needed to understand the purpose of each meeting and prepare questions that would invite the volunteer teacher to share her thinking honestly. Across planning, observation, and reflection, I wanted the process to remain a conversation with the teacher rather than a sequence of judgments delivered to her.</p>\n<p>In the preconference, I used questions to identify lesson goals, classroom context, and students' assets and needs. These topics would help us choose a CSTP element relevant to the actual lesson. They also gave the teacher an opportunity to explain her professional concerns before we discussed what I would observe. Preparing the conversation in this order helped me listen for connections instead of arriving with a predetermined standards focus.</p>\n<p>At approximately 3:39 in the written account of preconference clip 1, I ask about cultural and linguistic strengths and the support students might need. The teacher describes a class eager to participate, then identifies sustained engagement with difficult fraction work as a concern. I recognize that willingness to contribute and the challenge of the content can both inform our observation. Supporting engagement is not simply a matter of asking students to pay attention; it involves the routines and instructional conditions that help them participate in demanding learning.</p>\n<p>I keep this concern in mind when inviting the teacher to choose the standards focus. Around 5:00, we examine the district guide and I ask which elements match the areas where she wants feedback. At 5:12, she explains that, as a newer teacher, she wants to develop classroom management and selects element 2.6. She connects it with positive behavior, including one person speaking at a time. Her choice follows from the earlier discussion of students and the learning environment. This supports agency because the focus is something she identifies as meaningful, not simply an element I assign.</p>\n<p>The discussion also clarifies what evidence could represent that focus. At 5:30, I ask which observable events would best show the selected element, and the teacher identifies listening and participation behaviors. This helps translate the standards language into things we can notice during the lesson. It gives us a common reference for later feedback and reduces the likelihood that the teacher and I will interpret the observation's purpose differently.</p>\n<p>We identify student work separately from those behavioral indicators. At approximately 1:43 in the same clip, I ask about a closing assessment that would reveal the intended learning. The teacher describes an exit response requiring students to write a multiplication expression for a shaded area and determine its value. That product gives us evidence of mathematical understanding alongside the notes about participation. Keeping both sources in the plan helps ensure that the coaching connects the learning environment with the content students are expected to learn.</p>\n<p>In the postconference, I continue to use listening, paraphrasing, acknowledgement, and clarification. These practices are intended to make room for the teacher's interpretation of both strengths and needs. When she notices that students are not consistently following the intended independent-work expectations during the cool-down, I summarize the possible next step as establishing routines within the new curriculum's lesson sequence. In the written account of clip 2, this occurs around 3:33, followed by her confirmation. The summary checks my understanding while keeping the concern connected with what she has observed.</p>\n<p>At 3:53, I ask what she would do to make those parts of the lesson routine. She proposes discussing expectations for each phase, helping students organize materials, and clarifying the differences among the warm-up, activities, synthesis, and cool-down. These ideas are her responses to the problem, developed through the conversation. My facilitation supports her agency by asking for possible actions rather than immediately supply a complete management plan. We can then consider how those actions relate to the selected CSTP element and the evidence that prompted them.</p>\n<p>Another exchange connects her instructional choices with student work. At approximately 1:30 in postconference clip 1, I clarify whether counting side lengths during instruction prepares students for the exit task. She confirms. At 1:38, I ask how that strategy appears in the collected responses. She looks through the samples and notes at 2:34 that many students counted eleven fractional pieces along a side. This is a concrete connection between a teaching decision and visible features of student reasoning. It allows her to examine what students carried into their independent work rather than rely only on how the lesson felt.</p>\n<p>The cycle therefore gave several opportunities for a two-way exchange: the teacher defined a professional interest, identified relevant evidence, interpreted observed events, and proposed subsequent actions. I contributed organization, questions, notes, and summaries. The value of those contributions depends on whether they help her think more clearly, not whether they move the conversation quickly toward my preferred conclusion. Acknowledgement and clarification should remain invitations to examine evidence, with room for the teacher to disagree or add a different interpretation.</p>\n<p>Preparing questions does not mean scripting the teacher's responses. I need to use the planned prompts flexibly, listening for concerns that require a different follow-up. The examples in this cycle show how a discussion of goals can lead to participation routines, and how a discussion of routines can reveal a need for further assessment-informed planning. That responsiveness is part of sustaining a two-way coaching process.</p>\n<p>Looking back, I would strengthen this approach by allowing more time for specific differentiation questions and by following up more precisely on feedback about my coaching. The teacher's interest in using the cool-down results for future instruction shows that the conversation could extend further into assessment-informed planning. The written account describes meaningful participation in decisions, but the unavailable recordings prevent independent confirmation of the balance of voice throughout the cycle. Future practice should continue to connect teacher agency, student evidence, and a clearly defined professional focus, while using the teacher's feedback to improve the coaching relationship itself.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Identify a teacher-originated interpretation that changed your plan or understanding.</li><li>Analyze one moment where a leading prompt or rapid summary may have limited the conversation.</li><li>Explain how the partnership supported the teacher's capacity to interpret evidence independently.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"},{"title":"Part J / 3 / Equity and continuous improvement","url":"#cycle/c3/J3","html":"<span id=\"j-q3\" class=\"anchor-alias\"></span><h2>Part J / 3 / Equity and continuous improvement</h2><section class=\"question\"><div class=\"prompt\"><p>3.\tInformed by a continuous improvement mindset and focus on equitable leadership, reflect on your role (or future role) as an equity-driven school leader. Discuss the potential impact of supporting teacher growth through coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback.</p></div><p class=\"source-note\">Current Part J template</p></section><section class=\"meaning\"><h3>What this asks</h3><p>Explain what this cycle taught you about equitable instructional leadership and the potential of coaching to support teacher growth. Connect your leadership stance to actual teacher feedback, classroom evidence, and student access. Potential impact should not be written as a guaranteed outcome.</p></section><section class=\"rubric-requirements\"><h3>Rubric 3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</h3><ul><li>Reflect on your role as an instructional coach through continuous improvement and equitable leadership.</li><li>Explain the potential impact of coaching, observation, or feedback on teacher growth.</li><li>For Level 4, support the reflection with specific evidence from this cycle; Level 5 adds relevant evidence-based practices or research.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Teaching summary. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-7\">Complete official rubric</a>.</p></section><section class=\"example-response\"><h3>Interpolated Example</h3><div class=\"interpolated-example\"><p>As a leader concerned with equity, I want to support the learning and well-being of the adults in the school so they can respond more effectively to students each day. Coaching, observation, and instructional feedback offer opportunities for sustained professional learning connected to actual classrooms. Their value lies in ongoing, differentiated support and the opportunity to examine how teaching relates to academic and social-emotional learning.</p>\n<p>Aguilar (2024) describes an equity-centered approach to coaching that develops instructional skill, reflection, effectiveness, and resilience. Collaborative coaching can connect student, teacher, and school goals while giving teachers an active role in deciding what to learn. The cycle uses standards-focused observation and student work as shared evidence for that inquiry. Inviting teachers to name a focus and help construct goals is an important expression of agency, rather than treating professional learning as something arranged entirely for them.</p>\n<p>That partnership also requires attention to adults' professional needs and the conditions that sustain their learning over time.</p>\n<p>This cycle reinforced the importance of understanding why a learning goal matters to the teacher and how it relates to students' needs. An instructional leader can support that understanding through careful listening, focused observation, questions, and joint analysis. Regular opportunities to work in this way can build trust and make professional growth more connected to daily practice. The intended benefit is improved learning for students, but that benefit requires evidence over time. My commitment is to sustain the collaborative process, attend to the teacher's perspective, and use student responses to determine whether the changes we pursue are making a difference.</p></div></section><section class=\"critique\"><h3>Reading the Interpolated Example</h3><h4>Questions for review</h4><ol class=\"improvements\"><li>Use a specific teacher comment and student-work finding to explain an equity issue revealed by the cycle.</li><li>Describe how your leadership response would improve access while preserving teacher agency.</li><li>Distinguish the potential benefits of coaching from outcomes demonstrated in this evidence set.</li></ol><section class=\"score-judgment\"><h4>Instructional status</h4><p>This rewritten teaching example preserves the scenario and its evidence for discussion. It is not an official exemplar, candidate submission, or scored response.</p></section></section>"}],"anchors":["top","preparation","foundation","A1","a-q1","part-a","A2","a-q2","A3","a-q3","A4","a-q4","A5","a-q5","A6","a-q6","A7","a-q7","A8","a-q8","B","b-q1","part-b","C","c-q1","part-c","D","d-q1","part-d","E1","e-q1","part-e","E2","e-q2","E3","e-q3","E4","e-q4","F","f-q1","part-f","G","g-q1","part-g","H","h-q1","part-h","I1","i-q1","part-i","I2","i-q2","I3","i-q3","J1","j-q1","part-j","J2","j-q2","J3","j-q3","alignment","limits","source-record","scores","rubric-3-1","rubric-3-2","rubric-3-3","rubric-3-4","rubric-3-5","rubric-3-6","rubric-3-7"],"aliases":{"part-a":"A1","a-q1":"A1","a-q2":"A2","a-q3":"A3","a-q4":"A4","a-q5":"A5","a-q6":"A6","a-q7":"A7","a-q8":"A8","part-b":"B","b-q1":"B","part-c":"C","c-q1":"C","part-d":"D","d-q1":"D","part-e":"E1","e-q1":"E1","e-q2":"E2","e-q3":"E3","e-q4":"E4","part-f":"F","f-q1":"F","part-g":"G","g-q1":"G","part-h":"H","h-q1":"H","part-i":"I1","i-q1":"I1","i-q2":"I2","i-q3":"I3","part-j":"J1","j-q1":"J1","j-q2":"J2","j-q3":"J3","foundation":"preparation","rubric-map":"scores","final-check":"scores"}}};
